   141	  "findings": []
   142	}
   143	```
   144	
   145	`attestation.policy.hash` identifies the authored rule artifact. `evidence`
   146	records the observed OpenClaw state used by the checks, and
   147	`workspace.hash` identifies that evidence payload. `findingsHash` identifies
   148	the exact finding set. `checkedAt` records when the check ran.
   149	`attestationHash` identifies the stable claim (policy hash, evidence hash,
   150	findings hash, and clean/dirty state) and deliberately excludes `checkedAt`,
   151	so the same policy state always produces the same attestation hash. Together
   152	these four values form the audit tuple for one policy check.
   153	
   154	If a gateway or supervisor uses policy to block, approve, or annotate a
   155	runtime action, it should record the attestation hash from the last clean
   156	check. `checkedAt` stays in JSON output for audit logs but is not part of the
   157	stable hash.
   158	
   159	Lifecycle for accepting policy state:
   160	
   161	1. Author or review `policy.jsonc`.
   162	2. Run `openclaw policy check --json`.
   163	3. If clean, record `attestation.policy.hash` as `expectedHash`.
   164	4. Record `attestation.attestationHash` as `expectedAttestationHash`.
   165	5. Re-run `openclaw doctor --lint` in CI or release gates.
   166	
   167	If policy rules change intentionally, update both accepted hashes from a
   168	clean check. If only workspace settings change (policy stays the same),
   169	typically only `expectedAttestationHash` changes.
   170	
   171	Enabling or upgrading `agents.workspace` rules adds `agentWorkspace` evidence
   172	to the workspace hash and attestation hash; review the new evidence and
   173	refresh accepted attestation hashes after enabling. Enabling or upgrading
   174	tool posture rules adds `toolPosture` evidence the same way.
   175	
   176	`openclaw policy watch` re-runs the check and reports when current evidence no
   177	longer matches `expectedAttestationHash`:
   178	
   179	```bash
   180	openclaw policy watch --json
   181	openclaw policy watch --agent ops --json
   182	```
   183	
   184	Use `--once` in CI or scripts that need a single drift evaluation. Without
   185	`--once`, it polls every two seconds by default; use `--interval-ms` to change
   186	the interval.
