     2	summary: "The Full Release Validation umbrella, release publish, and Docker Release dispatch"
     3	read_when:
     4	  - You are dispatching or rerunning Full Release Validation
     5	  - You are publishing a release or a Docker image
     6	title: "Full Release Validation"
     7	sidebarTitle: "Full Release Validation"
     8	---
     9	
    10	The Full Release Validation umbrella, release publish, and Docker Release dispatch. Part of the [Release validation workflows](/ci/release-validation) index.
    11	
    12	## Full Release Validation
    13	
    14	`Full Release Validation` is the manual release umbrella. Every run binds an
    15	exact Validation SHA + Tooling SHA tuple and rejects an `expected_sha` mismatch
    16	before child dispatch. Validation SHA maps to the Code SHA for product
    17	validation or the Release SHA for changelog-only validation; it is not a third
    18	release identity. Beta-publish maps to `release_profile=beta` with
    19	`run_release_soak=false`. Regular stable releases use `release_profile=stable`.
    20	
    21	See [Full release validation](/reference/full-release-validation) for the
    22	stage matrix, exact workflow job names, profile differences, the `npm-beta-v1`
    23	and `npm-stable-v1` coverage policies, artifacts, and focused rerun handles.
    24	
    25	The `normal_ci` child dispatches `ci.yml` with the exact target and release scope,
    26	without `release_gate`. Complete campaigns (`rerun_group=all`) retain QA Smoke's
    27	full scenario profile and Control UI performance independently of changed paths.
    28	Docker seed runs all six lanes in every ordinary manual/release scope:
    29	`cron-mcp-cleanup`, `fleet-cache`, `mcp-channels`, `mcp-code-mode-gateway`,
    30	`published-upgrade-survivor`, and `update-channel-switch`. This includes
    31	`npm-beta` and `npm-stable` qualification. The survivor uses `legacy-operator-state`
    32	with `auto-auth`, so the published driver must update
    33	the running managed Gateway. Every admitted canonical main run retains this
    34	exact combination; PRs omit Docker seed and QA Smoke. Manual/release CI builds
    35	the full declaration-complete package. Main's smoke package instead uses the
    36	existing `ciArtifacts` profile and canonical packer with `--skip-build`, retaining
    37	the runtime, public SDK declarations, and unchanged tarball integrity check.
    38	Hosted manual CI splits QA Smoke into six parts; normal hybrid first attempts use four parts
    39	with the same coverage.
    40	
    41	For targets with [test runtime selection](/ci/pipeline#test-runtime-selection),
    42	`normal_ci` retains the complete Node test inventory and also runs each admitted
    43	Bun-compatible selection on Bun. Both results are required; they share existing
    44	jobs and execute sequentially within each worker slot. Older targets without this
    45	capability retain Node-only testing.
    46	This includes the Control UI config when the target's runtime owner admits it;
    47	its Bun pass excludes two GC-sensitive files retained in the full Node pass.
    48	An older unit-only runtime owner retains the UI's Node pass.
    49	
    50	Package Acceptance separately retains expanded published-upgrade scenarios:
    51	current unpublished candidates include native operator state, and stable/full
    52	profiles force the `reported-issues` soak. Its ordinary survivor restart mode
    53	and separate `update-restart-auth` base scenario do not replace the exact Docker
    54	seed combination. `Full Release Artifacts` and `Full Release Candidate` prepare
    55	the immutable package/image inputs; candidate-phase release checks consume them
    56	without moving or weakening that coverage.
    57	
    58	Existing frozen-target contracts still apply: Docker seed requires its declared
    59	capability, and targets without the Docker tier selector retain the survivor
    60	fallback. QA Smoke requires a supported harness, and historical performance
    61	checks retain their availability handling. Focused reruns select their requested
    62	groups, and validated evidence reuse can reuse completed proof. Regressions
    63	outside the remaining automatic owner paths, or in the five release-only Docker
    64	seed lanes, can first surface in this manual/release tier.
    65	
    66	The live/E2E selected-ref validator fetches the complete commit and ref history
    67	with a sparse checkout. Ancestry and release-ref checks remain unchanged, while
    68	historical file contents stay out of this metadata-only job. Build and test jobs
    69	check out their own complete source trees.
    70	
    71	`OpenClaw Release Publish` is the manual mutating release workflow. Dispatch
    72	regular beta and stable publishes from a protected lightweight
    73	`release-publish/<tooling-sha12>-<epoch>` tag at the frozen Tooling SHA after the
    74	release tag exists and after the OpenClaw npm preflight has succeeded (the preflight runs
    75	`pnpm plugins:sync:check` among its checks). The tag still selects the exact
    76	release commit, including a commit on `release/YYYY.M.PATCH`; Tideclaw alpha
    77	publishes keep using their matching alpha branch. For current validation runs,
    78	set `preflight_run_id` and `full_release_validation_run_id` to the same successful
    79	Full Release Validation run ID and pin `full_release_validation_run_attempt`.
    80	The publisher resolves the independent `Full Release Artifacts` producer from
    81	that validation manifest's sealed `publicationArtifacts.npmPreflight` descriptor.
    82	The producer ID alone does not carry Full Release Validation authorization.
    83	
    84	`Docker Release` requests the `docker-release` environment approval in a
    85	separate job after source identity and image preparation succeed (or prepared
    86	artifacts are supplied). Approval waits stay outside `docker-release-publish`;
    87	only the approved publisher enters that global registry/alias lock. It then
    88	revalidates the immutable source, prepared artifacts, attestations and alias
    89	state before writing. Docker Hub credentials remain required caller-provided
    90	secrets. Failed preparation, denied approval and cancellation cannot publish.
    91	
    92	Historical recovery may still supply a separate successful `OpenClaw NPM Release`
    93	preflight run ID alongside the matching successful Full Release Validation run
    94	and attempt. Create the tooling tag with the [release publish commands](/reference/RELEASING#regular-release-publish-automation);
    95	real core npm, plugin npm, or ClawHub publication from `main` is rejected before
    96	child dispatch. Docker-only recovery may still use `main`.
    97	
    98	The publisher dispatches `Plugin NPM Release` for all
    99	publishable plugin packages, dispatches `Plugin ClawHub Release` for the same
   100	release SHA, then dispatches `OpenClaw NPM Release` after plugin npm succeeds.
   101	Stable Windows promotion is optional: supply both an exact `windows_node_tag`
   102	and candidate-approved `windows_node_installer_digests` to dispatch its signed
   103	installers after GitHub release finalization. Omit both to skip Windows.
   104	For npm-stable evidence, when the tagged `apps/android/version.json` matches
   105	the stable tag's base version, a separate native qualification job starts full
   106	CI for the exact release SHA with Android enabled. A successful result is revalidated
   107	after core publication before the separate Android job creates its existing
   108	approval receipt and dispatches the tag-owned APK workflow. This keeps frozen
   109	release tags usable without allowing narrower npm evidence to authorize an
   110	unqualified native build. Native failure remains visible and prevents Android
   111	approval; core npm and GitHub release finalization do not wait for it. The whole
   112	parent can remain active after core publication while native qualification
   113	finishes. Existing full evidence and macOS's independent validation retain their
   114	native qualification contracts. A mismatched Android pin skips both native
   115	qualification and APK publication, with the pin, release train, and shared
   116	mobile cutter (`scripts/mobile-release-version.ts --prepare`) remedy recorded
   117	in the parent summary and release proof.
   118	Focused plugin-only repairs use `plugin_publish_scope=selected` with a nonempty
   119	package list. Plugin-only `all-publishable` runs require the same immutable npm
   120	preflight and Full Release Validation evidence as a core publish.
   121	
   122	```bash
   123	PUBLISH_REF="release-publish/<tooling-sha12>-<epoch>"
   124	FRV_RUN_ID="<successful-full-release-validation-run-id>"
   125	FRV_RUN_ATTEMPT="<successful-full-release-validation-run-attempt>"
   126	gh workflow run openclaw-release-publish.yml \
   127	  --ref "$PUBLISH_REF" \
   128	  -f tag=vYYYY.M.PATCH-beta.N \
   129	  -f preflight_run_id="$FRV_RUN_ID" \
   130	  -f full_release_validation_run_id="$FRV_RUN_ID" \
   131	  -f full_release_validation_run_attempt="$FRV_RUN_ATTEMPT" \
   132	  -f npm_dist_tag=beta
   133	```
   134	
   135	For pinned commit proof on a fast-moving branch, use the helper instead of
   136	`gh workflow run ... --ref main -f ref=<sha>`:
   137	
   138	```bash
   139	TOOLING_SHA="<recorded-full-main-ancestor-sha>"
   140	VALIDATION_SHA="<full-release-candidate-sha>"
   141	PUBLICATION_SELECTION='{"route":"normal","npmDistTag":"latest","publishOpenclawNpm":true,"pluginPublishScope":"all-publishable","plugins":[]}'
   142	pnpm ci:full-release \
   143	  --sha "$VALIDATION_SHA" \
   144	  --target-ref release/YYYY.M.PATCH \
   145	  --workflow-sha "$TOOLING_SHA" \
   146	  -f validation_purpose=publish \
   147	  -f publication_selection_json="$PUBLICATION_SELECTION"
   148	```
   149	
   150	Choose `npmDistTag=beta` for a beta and `route=prepared` only for an intended
   151	prepared-button consumer. Source admission verifies committed metadata; fresh
   152	publish runs also retain separate selected npm and ClawHub registry admission
   153	before fanout. Neither grants publication authority. See
   154	[Dispatch](/reference/full-release-validation/dispatch) for both contracts.
   155	For nonpublish work, explicitly select
   156	`diagnostic`, `main-qualification`, or `postpublish-confidence` and omit the
   157	publication selection; profile and filters still select the actual coverage.
   158	
   159	GitHub workflow dispatch refs must be branches or tags, not raw commit SHAs. The
   160	helper pushes a temporary `release-ci/<sha>-...` branch at a trusted Tooling
   161	SHA, passes the requested Validation SHA through `ref` and `expected_sha`, reuses
   162	strict exact-target evidence when available, and verifies every child workflow
   163	`headSha` matches the Tooling SHA. Record that Tooling SHA once and never refresh
   164	it from moving `main`. Regular release branches accept only their final package
   165	version or a matching beta prerelease; Tideclaw alpha validation uses its exact
   166	alpha tag and matching alpha branch.
   167	
   168	`release_profile` controls live/provider breadth passed into release checks. The
   169	manual release workflows default to `stable`; use `full` only when you
   170	intentionally want the broad provider/media matrix. Stable and full
   171	release checks always run the exhaustive live/E2E and Docker release-path soak;
   172	the beta profile can opt in with `run_release_soak=true`.
   173	
   174	`fail_fast` defaults to `false`: the umbrella waits for each dispatched child
   175	workflow and reports its independent failures together. Set `fail_fast=true`
   176	only when cancelling a child after its first failed job is more useful than the
   177	complete failure inventory. In Release Checks, this also enables the Matrix QA
   178	CLI's own first-scenario cancellation.
   179	
   180	- `beta` keeps the fastest OpenAI/core release-critical lanes.
   181	- `stable` adds the stable provider/backend set.
   182	- `full` runs the broad provider/media matrix.
   183	
   184	The umbrella records dispatched child run ids, and `Verify full validation`
   185	checks them during that parent attempt. Parent cancellation or timeout leaves
   186	adopted exact children running; cancel one explicitly when it is no longer
   187	needed.
   188	
   189	For recovery, classify product, harness/tooling/provenance,
   190	infrastructure/credential, and wrapper failures before editing. Only confirmed
   191	product failure changes the Code SHA. Diagnose and fix the owning defect before an explicit narrow `rerun_group`
   192	validation run; never retry a failed test automatically or widen to `all`. Narrow evidence is not publish authorization by itself.
   193	
   194	`OpenClaw Release Checks` uses the trusted workflow ref to resolve the selected ref once into a `release-package-under-test` tarball, then passes that artifact to cross-OS checks and Package Acceptance, plus the live/E2E release-path Docker workflow when soak coverage runs. That keeps the package bytes consistent across release boxes and avoids repacking the same candidate in multiple child jobs. For the Codex npm-plugin live lane, release checks either pass a matching published plugin spec derived from `release_package_spec`, pass the operator-supplied `codex_plugin_spec`, or leave the input blank so the Docker script packs the selected checkout's Codex plugin.
   195	
   196	Full Release Validation concurrency is keyed by Validation SHA, Tooling SHA,
   197	rerun group, release profile, and effective soak coverage with
   198	`cancel-in-progress: false`. Release Checks uses the same coverage identity in
   199	each phase, so beta, stable, and full requests do not queue behind each other.
   200	Stable/full always include soak; setting their soak flag explicitly does not
   201	create another concurrency group. Parent cancellation does not cancel adopted
   202	children.
   203	
   204	In the canonical repository's `hybrid` runner mode, target resolution, evidence
   205	reuse, candidate discovery, candidate binding, and candidate resolution use
   206	the small Blacksmith runner pool. These serial jobs otherwise compound hosted
   207	runner admission delays before tests can start. Other modes and noncanonical
   208	repositories retain GitHub-hosted runners; the reusable harness also honors
   209	its explicit hosted-runner override. Long-running decision and diagnostic
   210	collectors remain hosted.
   211	---
   212	summary: "Install Smoke coverage, the local Docker E2E aggregate, and release-path Docker chunks"
   213	read_when:
   214	  - You are running or debugging Docker E2E lanes
   215	  - You need the local Docker E2E tunables or the release-path chunk names
   216	title: "Install smoke and Docker E2E"
   217	sidebarTitle: "Install smoke and Docker E2E"
   218	---
   219	
   220	Install Smoke coverage, the local Docker E2E aggregate and its tunables, the reusable live/E2E workflow, and release-path chunks. Part of the [Release validation workflows](/ci/release-validation) index.
   221	
   222	## Install smoke
   223	
   224	The `Install Smoke` workflow no longer runs on pull requests or `main` pushes. Its nightly/manual wrapper and release validation both call the read-only `install-smoke-reusable.yml` core, and every run takes the full install-smoke path on GitHub-hosted runners:
   225	
   226	- The root Dockerfile smoke image is built once per target SHA, bound to the workflow revision and producer attempt in an immutable artifact, then loaded by the CLI smoke, agents delete shared-workspace CLI smoke, container gateway-network E2E, and bundled `matrix` plugin build-arg smoke. The plugin smoke verifies runtime dependency install mirroring and that the plugin loads without entry-escape diagnostics.
   227	- QR package install and the installer/update Docker smokes (including Rocky Linux installer lanes and an update lane against a configurable `update_baseline_version` npm baseline) run as separate jobs so installer work does not wait behind the root image smokes.
   228	
   229	The slow Bun global install and runtime smoke is separately gated by `run_bun_global_install_smoke`. It installs the candidate with trusted lifecycle scripts, then verifies representative CLI, local-agent, and Gateway paths under Bun 1.4 or newer. It runs on the nightly schedule, defaults on for workflow calls from release checks, and manual `Install Smoke` dispatches can opt into it. Normal PR CI still runs the fast Bun launcher regression lane for Node-relevant changes. QR and installer Docker tests keep their own install-focused Dockerfiles.
   230	
   231	## Local Docker E2E
   232	
   233	`pnpm test:docker:all` prebuilds one shared live-test image, packs OpenClaw once as an npm tarball, and builds two shared `scripts/e2e/Dockerfile` images:
   234	
   235	- a bare Node/Git runner for installer/update/plugin-dependency lanes;
   236	- a functional image that installs the same tarball into `/app` for normal functionality lanes.
   237	
   238	Docker lane definitions live in `scripts/lib/docker-e2e-scenarios.mts`, planner logic lives in `scripts/lib/docker-e2e-plan.mts`, and the runner only executes the selected plan. The scheduler selects the image per lane with `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_E2E_BARE_IMAGE` and `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_E2E_FUNCTIONAL_IMAGE`, then runs lanes with `OPENCLAW_SKIP_DOCKER_BUILD=1`. Live lanes that use these package images do not require the separate source live-test image; model/backend lanes that consume the source image still prepare it.
   239	
   240	### Tunables
   241	
   242	| Variable                               | Default | Purpose                                                                                       |
   243	| -------------------------------------- | ------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   244	| `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_PARALLELISM`      | 10      | Main-pool slot count for normal lanes.                                                        |
   245	| `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_TAIL_PARALLELISM` | 10      | Provider-sensitive tail-pool slot count.                                                      |
   246	| `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_LIVE_LIMIT`       | 9       | Concurrent live lane cap so providers do not throttle.                                        |
   247	| `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_NPM_LIMIT`        | 5       | Concurrent npm install lane cap.                                                              |
   248	| `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_SERVICE_LIMIT`    | 7       | Concurrent multi-service lane cap.                                                            |
   249	| `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_START_STAGGER_MS` | 2000    | Stagger between lane starts to avoid Docker daemon create storms; set `0` for no stagger.     |
   250	| `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_LANE_TIMEOUT_MS`  | 7200000 | Per-lane fallback timeout (120 minutes); selected live/tail lanes use tighter caps.           |
   251	| `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_DRY_RUN`          | unset   | `1` prints the scheduler plan without running lanes.                                          |
   252	| `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_LANES`            | unset   | Comma-separated exact lane list; skips cleanup smoke so agents can reproduce one failed lane. |
   253	
   254	A lane heavier than its effective cap can still start from an empty pool, then runs alone until it releases capacity. The local aggregate preflights Docker, removes stale OpenClaw E2E containers, emits active-lane status, persists lane timings for longest-first ordering, and stops scheduling new pooled lanes after the first failure by default.
   255	
   256	### Reusable live/E2E workflow
   257	
   258	Repository E2E runs as nine independent jobs: four duration-weighted Gateway shards, four
   259	duration-weighted Control UI shards, and the standalone agent-plugin Gateway
   260	test. Two independent producers build the selected source once per profile:
   261	the full private-QA build for Gateway package/type checks, and the CI artifact
   262	build for UI and agent-plugin tests. Consumers restore exact producer artifacts,
   263	including generated plugin assets and local build metadata, and install their
   264	own Chromium and sandbox prerequisites. Each group has four test slots, so long
   265	UI shards start together without waiting for Gateway declarations or tests.
   266	A failed producer blocks its own consumers; other diagnostics continue.
   267	
   268	Gateway shards retain the existing
   269	four fresh-process boundaries and two-worker limit. Each UI shard runs its
   270	bundled files with up to two workers, then its private-server, real-Gateway, and
   271	runtime-budget files serially. The root sequencer assigns files across both
   272	projects to the same four weighted shards. No tests are filtered out, and the
   273	existing 90-minute job deadline is unchanged. Local `pnpm test:e2e` still runs
   274	its suite commands sequentially; each UI command uses the same project policy.
   275	
   276	This removes seven builds per invocation and raises peak test concurrency from
   277	six to eight. Release checks route the full Gateway build and four Gateway test
   278	shards to the existing `blacksmith-32vcpu-ubuntu-2404` profile through
   279	`gateway_repo_e2e_use_github_hosted_runners: false`. This uses five Blacksmith
   280	registrations per campaign, with at most four Gateway test runners at once;
   281	UI, plugin, and other live-suite routing stays unchanged. Other callers default
   282	the Gateway option to true and retain their overall hosted-runner choice.
   283	A standalone Blacksmith invocation can register eleven runners: two producers
   284	and nine test jobs. Producer artifact identities
   285	survive consumer-only retries; consumers never select an artifact by their own
   286	current attempt number.
   287	
   288	The reusable live/E2E workflow asks `scripts/test-docker-all.mjs --plan-json` which package, image kind, live image, lane, and credential coverage is required. `scripts/docker-e2e.mjs` then converts that plan into GitHub outputs and summaries. It either packs OpenClaw through `scripts/package-openclaw-for-docker.mjs`, downloads a current-run package artifact, or downloads a package artifact from `package_artifact_run_id`, then validates the tarball inventory. The default `no-push-artifact` path builds package-digest-tagged bare/functional images through Blacksmith's Docker layer cache, packs the exact image bytes into an immutable workflow artifact, and has each consumer verify and load that artifact. `existing-only` instead requires explicit `docker_e2e_bare_image`/`docker_e2e_functional_image` GHCR refs and never builds or pushes. Those registry pulls use a bounded 180-second per-attempt timeout so a stuck stream retries quickly instead of consuming most of the CI critical path. After successful scheduled validation, `openclaw-scheduled-live-checks.yml` passes the immutable tested-image manifest to the separate package-write publisher; read-only release and prerelease callers never traverse that writer.
   289	
   290	### Release-path chunks
   291	
   292	Release Docker coverage runs smaller chunked jobs with `OPENCLAW_SKIP_DOCKER_BUILD=1` so each chunk verifies and loads only the artifact-backed image kind it needs (or pulls it under explicit `existing-only` reuse) and executes multiple lanes through the same weighted scheduler:
   293	
   294	- `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_PROFILE=release-path`
   295	- `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_CHUNK=core | package-update-openai | package-update-onboarding | package-update-migrations | package-update-self-upgrade | plugins-runtime-plugins | plugins-runtime-services | plugins-runtime-install-a..h | openwebui`
   296	
   297	Current release Docker chunks are `core`, `package-update-openai`, `package-update-onboarding`, `package-update-migrations`, `package-update-self-upgrade`, `plugins-runtime-plugins`, `plugins-runtime-services`, `plugins-runtime-install-a` through `plugins-runtime-install-h`, and `openwebui`. `package-update-openai` includes the live Codex plugin package lane, which installs the candidate OpenClaw package, installs the Codex plugin from `codex_plugin_spec` or a same-ref tarball with explicit Codex CLI install approval, runs Codex CLI preflight and same-session agent turns, then runs a zero-retry medium-thinking turn that sends progress, reads randomized workspace inputs, writes their exact artifact, and sends completion. `plugins-runtime-core`, `plugins-runtime`, and `plugins-integrations` remain aggregate plugin/runtime aliases. The `install-e2e` lane alias remains the aggregate manual rerun alias for both provider installer lanes.
   298	
   299	The stable/full Docker `core` chunk includes `live-anthropic-cache`. It sends eight
   300	bounded requests through the candidate package's Anthropic provider and managed
   301	transport, checking conversation cache reuse while temporary runtime context
   302	moves past two real tool results and a subsequent user turn. Missing credentials,
   303	incorrect cache markers, changed cached prefixes, or repeated history writes fail
   304	the lane without retries. This supplements the source-based live cache floors;
   305	it does not exercise Gateway session scheduling.
   306	The scheduler declares `anthropic-api-key` for this lane, and both full-chunk and
   307	targeted-lane preflights require `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` specifically; OAuth credentials
   308	remain accepted for the other Anthropic lanes that support them.
   309	
   310	Provider-neutral package checks run in three balanced rows: onboarding and install switching, channel/published migrations, and self-upgrades. This avoids serializing eight npm-heavy lanes behind one runner's npm resource limit. The aggregate `package-update-core` and `package-update` names remain available for manual runs. The `package-update-openai` row also runs root-managed VPS upgrade and authenticated update restart proof. Scheduler resource limits remain unchanged. Credential preflight failures remain blocking while the following diagnostic pool drains non-live lanes; earlier setup failures and cancellation still prevent execution.
   311	
   312	OpenWebUI runs as a standalone `openwebui` chunk on a dedicated large-disk Blacksmith runner whenever stable or full release-path coverage requests it, even when the reusable workflow routes supported jobs to GitHub-hosted runners. Keeping the external image pull separate prevents the large image from competing with the shared package and plugin images in `plugins-runtime-services`; legacy aggregate plugin/runtime chunks still include OpenWebUI for compatible manual reruns. Bundled-channel update lanes retry once for transient npm network failures.
   313	
   314	Each chunk uploads `.artifacts/docker-tests/` with lane logs, timings, `summary.json`, `failures.json`, phase timings, scheduler plan JSON, slow-lane tables, and per-lane rerun commands. The workflow `docker_lanes` input runs selected lanes against images prepared for that run instead of the chunk jobs, which keeps failed-lane debugging bounded to one targeted Docker job; if a selected lane is a live Docker lane, the targeted job builds the live-test image locally for that rerun. The rerun helper validates the failure artifact's exact selected target SHA and manual dispatch repacks that ref, because the internal reusable-workflow package tuple is not part of the `workflow_dispatch` schema. Generated commands include prepared image inputs and `shared_image_policy=existing-only` only when those inputs are GHCR-backed; runner-local artifact tags are omitted so a fresh runner rebuilds them. An explicit target override drops recovered GHCR image refs unless the artifact proves they match the override. Artifact-generated workflow-definition refs are also omitted because full-release temporary branches are deleted; dispatch uses the repository default branch unless the operator explicitly overrides it.
   315	
   316	```bash
   317	pnpm test:docker:rerun <run-id>      # download Docker artifacts and print combined/per-lane targeted rerun commands
   318	pnpm test:docker:timings <summary>   # slow-lane and phase critical-path summaries
   319	```
   320	
   321	The scheduled live/E2E workflow runs the full release-path Docker suite daily and, after it succeeds, invokes the explicit publisher for the exact tested image artifacts.
   322	---
   323	summary: "Named live and E2E shards in the release live/E2E child workflow"
   324	read_when:
   325	  - You are rerunning a failed live or E2E shard
   326	  - You need the shard names for a manual one-shot run
   327	title: "Live and E2E shards"
   328	sidebarTitle: "Live and E2E shards"
   329	---
   330	
   331	The named live and E2E shards the release live/E2E child runs, and what each shard covers. Part of the [Release validation workflows](/ci/release-validation) index.
   332	
   333	## Live and E2E shards
   334	
   335	The release live/E2E child keeps broad native `pnpm test:live` coverage, but it runs it as named shards through `scripts/test-live-shard.mjs` instead of one serial job:
   336	
   337	- `native-live-src-agents` and `native-live-src-agents-zai-coding`
   338	- `native-live-src-gateway-core`
   339	- provider-filtered `native-live-src-gateway-profiles` jobs
   340	- `native-live-src-gateway-backends`
   341	- `native-live-src-infra`
   342	- `native-live-test`
   343	- `native-live-extensions-a-k`
   344	- `native-live-extensions-l-n`
   345	- `native-live-extensions-moonshot`
   346	- `native-live-extensions-openai`
   347	- `native-live-extensions-o-z-other`
   348	- `native-live-extensions-xai`
   349	- split media audio/video shards and provider-filtered music shards
   350	
   351	That keeps the same file coverage while making slow live provider failures easier to rerun and diagnose. The aggregate `native-live-src-gateway`, `native-live-extensions-o-z`, `native-live-extensions-media`, and `native-live-extensions-media-music` shard names remain valid for manual one-shot reruns.
   352	
   353	Stable/full release validation includes the configless `agent exec --auth-env-only` Code Mode smoke in `native-live-test`. The test runner builds the runtime before starting workers. The smoke copies that built distribution outside the source checkout, applies the package's plugin exclusions, and reuses installed dependencies. It supplies only `OPENAI_API_KEY` to a fresh CLI environment, runs `openai/gpt-5.6-luna` without a runtime override, and verifies Code Mode engagement, nested tool calls, and an exact read-to-write artifact. This proves built-distribution behavior; Package Acceptance owns tarball installation proof. The shard requires passing evidence from this test; a missing key or skipped test cannot satisfy the release gate.
   354	
   355	Gateway-profile shards and shards containing the image-tool provider or OpenAI plugin live tests prepare the `sourcePerformance` build profile before starting Vitest. This supplies executable provider and agent runtime artifacts without building declarations or the Control UI. Provider requests, assertions, and test deadlines remain unchanged; gateway diagnostic environment settings apply only to gateway-profile shards. Cold source-plugin Jiti import cost remains a separate performance follow-up, not live provider latency.
   356	
   357	Stable/full release runs explicitly enable OpenAI AgentSession repeated compaction in `native-live-src-agents` with `OPENCLAW_LIVE_OPENAI_COMPACTION=1` and `OPENCLAW_LIVE_OPENAI_COMPACTION_FULL=0`. This uses the bounded 48k context profile and requires multiple compactions plus durable-marker recall. Manual shard runs retain the explicit opt-in; once enabled, a skipped compaction test fails the shard's pass-evidence gate. The separate 922k full-context stress profile remains a manual opt-in.
   358	
   359	The native live media shards run in `ghcr.io/openclaw/openclaw-live-media-runner:ubuntu-24.04`, built by the `Live Media Runner Image` workflow. That image preinstalls `ffmpeg` and `ffprobe`; media jobs only verify the binaries before setup. Keep Docker-backed live suites on normal Blacksmith runners — container jobs are the wrong place to launch nested Docker tests.
   360	
   361	Docker-backed live model/backend shards use a separate shared `ghcr.io/openclaw/openclaw-live-test:<sha>-<extensions>` image per selected commit. The live release workflow builds and pushes that image once, then the Docker live model, provider-sharded gateway, CLI backend, ACP bind, and Codex harness shards run with `OPENCLAW_SKIP_DOCKER_BUILD=1`. Gateway Docker shards carry explicit script-level `timeout` caps below the workflow job timeout so a stuck container or cleanup path fails fast instead of consuming the whole release-check budget. If those shards rebuild the full source Docker target independently, the release run is misconfigured and will waste wall clock on duplicate image builds.
   362	---
   363	summary: "Package Acceptance jobs, candidate sources, suite profiles, and dispatch examples"
   364	read_when:
   365	  - You are validating an installable OpenClaw package
   366	  - You are debugging a failed package acceptance run
   367	title: "Package Acceptance"
   368	sidebarTitle: "Package Acceptance"
   369	---
   370	
   371	Package Acceptance jobs, candidate sources, suite profiles, compatibility windows, and dispatch examples. Part of the [Release validation workflows](/ci/release-validation) index.
   372	
   373	## Package Acceptance
   374	
   375	Use `Package Acceptance` when the question is "does this installable OpenClaw package work as a product?" It is different from normal CI: normal CI validates the source tree, while package acceptance validates a single tarball through the same Docker E2E harness users exercise after install or update.
   376	
   377	### Jobs
   378	
   379	1. `resolve_package` checks out `workflow_ref`, resolves one package candidate, writes `.artifacts/docker-e2e-package/openclaw-current.tgz`, writes `.artifacts/docker-e2e-package/package-candidate.json`, uploads both as the `package-under-test` artifact, and prints the source, workflow ref, package ref, version, SHA-256, and profile in the GitHub step summary.
   380	2. `package_integrity` downloads the `package-under-test` artifact and enforces the public package tarball contract with `scripts/check-openclaw-package-tarball.mjs`.
   381	3. `npm_12_install_sh` installs that exact artifact through the public Linux installer under npm 12 in an isolated home/prefix, then verifies the CLI version and lifecycle-completion guard.
   382	4. `docker_acceptance` calls `openclaw-live-and-e2e-checks-reusable.yml` with the resolved package source SHA (falling back to `workflow_ref`) and `package_artifact_name=package-under-test`. The reusable workflow downloads that artifact, validates the tarball inventory, prepares package-digest Docker images when needed, and runs the selected Docker lanes against that package instead of packing the workflow checkout. When a profile selects multiple targeted `docker_lanes`, the reusable workflow prepares the package and shared images once, then fans those lanes out as parallel targeted Docker jobs with unique artifacts.
   383	5. `package_telegram` optionally calls `NPM Telegram Beta E2E`. It runs when `telegram_mode` is not `none` and installs the same `package-under-test` artifact when Package Acceptance resolved one; standalone Telegram dispatch can still install a published npm spec.
   384	6. `summary` fails the workflow if package resolution, integrity, npm 12 installer acceptance, Docker acceptance, or the optional Telegram lane failed. Selected lanes keep their first failure; callers cannot downgrade a failing test to a warning.
   385	
   386	### Candidate sources
   387	
   388	- `source=npm` accepts only `openclaw@extended-stable`, `openclaw@beta`, `openclaw@latest`, or an exact OpenClaw release version such as `openclaw@2026.9.5`. Use this for published extended-stable, prerelease, or stable acceptance.
   389	- `source=ref` packs a trusted `package_ref` branch, tag, or full commit SHA. The resolver fetches OpenClaw branches/tags, verifies the selected commit is reachable from repository branch history or a release tag, installs deps in a detached worktree, and packs it with `scripts/package-openclaw-for-docker.mjs`.
   390	- `source=url` downloads a public HTTPS `.tgz`; `package_sha256` is required. This path rejects URL credentials, non-default HTTPS ports, private/internal/special-use hostnames or resolved IPs, and redirects outside the same public safety policy.
   391	- `source=trusted-url` downloads an HTTPS `.tgz` from a named trusted-source policy in `.github/package-trusted-sources.json`; `package_sha256` and `trusted_source_id` are required. Use this only for maintainer-owned enterprise mirrors or private package repositories that need configured hosts, ports, path prefixes, redirect hosts, or private-network resolution. If the policy declares bearer auth, the workflow uses the fixed `OPENCLAW_TRUSTED_PACKAGE_TOKEN` secret; URL-embedded credentials are still rejected.
   392	- `source=artifact` downloads one `.tgz` from `artifact_run_id` and `artifact_name`; `package_sha256` is optional but should be supplied for externally shared artifacts.
   393	
   394	Keep `workflow_ref` and `package_ref` separate. `workflow_ref` is the trusted workflow/harness code that runs the test. `package_ref` is the source commit that gets packed when `source=ref`. This lets the current test harness validate older trusted source commits without running old workflow logic.
   395	
   396	### Suite profiles
   397	
   398	- `smoke` — `npm-onboard-channel-agent`, `gateway-network`, `config-reload`
   399	- `package` — `npm-onboard-channel-agent`, `doctor-switch`, `update-channel-switch`, `skill-install`, `update-corrupt-plugin`, `upgrade-survivor`, `published-upgrade-survivor`, `root-managed-vps-upgrade`, `update-restart-auth`, `plugins-offline`, `plugin-update`
   400	- `product` — the `package` set with live `plugins` coverage instead of `plugins-offline`, plus `mcp-channels`, `cron-mcp-cleanup`, `openai-web-search-minimal`, `openwebui`
   401	- `full` — full Docker release-path chunks with OpenWebUI
   402	- `custom` — exact `docker_lanes`; required when `suite_profile=custom`
   403	
   404	The `package` profile uses offline plugin coverage so published-package validation is not gated on live ClawHub availability. The optional Telegram lane reuses the `package-under-test` artifact in `NPM Telegram Beta E2E`, with the published npm spec path kept for standalone dispatches.
   405	
   406	For the dedicated update and plugin testing policy, including local commands,
   407	Docker lanes, Package Acceptance inputs, release defaults, and failure triage,
   408	see [Testing updates and plugins](/help/testing-updates-plugins).
   409	
   410	Release checks call Package Acceptance with `source=artifact`, the prepared release package artifact, `suite_profile=custom`, `docker_lanes='doctor-switch update-channel-switch skill-install update-corrupt-plugin upgrade-survivor published-upgrade-survivor root-managed-vps-upgrade update-restart-auth plugins-offline plugin-update plugin-binding-command-escape'`, and `telegram_mode=mock-openai`. This keeps package migration, update, live ClawHub skill install, stale-plugin-dependency cleanup, configured-plugin install repair, offline plugin, plugin-update, and Telegram proof on the same resolved package tarball. Set `release_package_spec` on Full Release Validation or OpenClaw Release Checks after publishing a beta to run the same matrix against the shipped npm package without rebuilding; set `package_acceptance_package_spec` only when Package Acceptance needs a different package from the rest of release validation. Cross-OS release checks still cover OS-specific onboarding, installer, and platform behavior; package/update product validation should start with Package Acceptance.
   411	
   412	Upgrade-survivor assertion ownership follows the selected target's release train,
   413	read from its immutable package metadata after source-identity validation.
   414	Extended-stable targets retain their shipped scenario runner, assertions, and
   415	fixtures; regular targets keep the trusted scenario together, including its
   416	serving-turn/post-inference assertions.
   417	The historical baseline does not select the assertion owner. Invalid target
   418	versions and unsupported extended-stable correction versions fail before Docker.
   419	
   420	Docker seed CI resolves an exact published stable predecessor of the selected source package version before running `published-upgrade-survivor`. It uses the release baseline resolver and selected release context, so publishing `latest` never turns the first upgrade into an already-current operation. Missing predecessors fail before Docker starts; the separate already-current control remains unchanged.
   421	
   422	The `published-upgrade-survivor` Docker lane validates one published package baseline per scenario. In Package Acceptance, the resolved `package-under-test` tarball is always the candidate and `published_upgrade_survivor_baseline` selects the fallback published baseline, defaulting to `openclaw@latest`; failed-lane rerun commands preserve that baseline. Current source release checks set `published_upgrade_survivor_baselines=supported-lines` for `legacy-operator-state`: npm's current `latest`, the preceding stable version, `extended-stable` when that tag exists, and the documented oldest supported baseline `2026.6.34`. The resolver reads `npm view openclaw versions` and `npm view openclaw dist-tags` at run time, pins exact versions before fanout, and deduplicates overlapping lines. Normal current-source release checks retain `base` and add `legacy-operator-state` and `custom-plugin-siblings`; release soak selects `reported-issues`, including these and the existing issue-shaped fixtures. The sibling-source scenario uses baselines from 2026.9.4 onward and requires actual custom-plugin Doctor contract execution from the private update canary, plus intact source files and plugin loading after the update.
   423	
   424	Before targeted Docker fanout, the trusted group planner installs each distinct published baseline in a throwaway npm prefix and checks `openclaw --version` plus `openclaw config set gateway.mode local` against synthetic isolated state. The config write loads the CLI setup path because version/help and config reads can use fast paths. An installed CLI that exits unsuccessfully is recorded as an **unusable published baseline**, with its skipped scenarios and captured error in the job summary and `upgrade-baseline-checks-*` artifact. Skipped scenarios are never counted as successful upgrades. Install errors, probe timeouts, and process-launch failures fail planning. Candidate installs and upgrades retain their existing failure gates.
   425	
   426	When a prerelease registry is mounted, the baseline package install and the
   427	initial manual baseline Gateway start use the configured published upstream.
   428	Published and candidate packages can share an exact version while containing
   429	different bytes; preserving published dist-tags alone does not isolate them.
   430	After those baseline commands, candidate installs keep using the verified
   431	candidate registry, including its exact-version dependencies.
   432	
   433	The `legacy-operator-state` companion fixture preserves the published archive
   434	for its package name/version across both registry phases.
   435	When the candidate companion has that same version, installation assertions
   436	require the retained published bytes. A different version selects and verifies
   437	the prepared candidate archive. This keeps same-version core update proof from
   438	simulating an npm republish; testing changed companion bytes requires a distinct
   439	companion version.
   440	
   441	Expanded release qualification requires the candidate's `YYYY.M.PATCH` base version
   442	to be at least the trusted workflow package's base version, ignoring prerelease
   443	suffixes for this comparison. It then reads immutable source-directory metadata for
   444	the operator-state harness. Older source targets and extended-stable contexts
   445	or branches keep the validated candidate-relative predecessor. Published candidates retain that predecessor and the preexisting synthetic
   446	scenario inventory because their qualification path does not prepare the
   447	registry required by the operator-state fixture.
   448	A separate `package_acceptance_package_spec` override resolves its predecessor
   449	from the override's actual package version inside Package Acceptance.
   450	
   451	Within the supported-lines release profile, `custom-plugin-siblings` always uses
   452	the exact published `openclaw@2026.9.4` regression driver. This retains the
   453	reported first hop when the source candidate has the same version; base and
   454	legacy-operator scenarios keep their existing baseline selection.
   455	
   456	The child workflow prepares or reuses the prerelease plugin registry required
   457	by the new scenario's artifact assertions, so that scenario runs only for
   458	qualifying unpublished candidates. Published requalification retains `base`, or every preexisting
   459	reported-issue scenario for soak, because its package path does not prepare
   460	that registry. Historical qualification likewise excludes only the newly added
   461	operator-state scenario. Existing frozen-target compatibility checks and the
   462	explicit scenario-omission opt-in remain unchanged; candidate source code is
   463	never executed to choose this profile.
   464	
   465	For the standalone `supported-lines` selector, the group planner runs every
   466	preexisting synthetic scenario only against that separately resolved
   467	predecessor, and runs `legacy-operator-state` against each supported baseline.
   468	It merges overlapping groups and retains every requested fixture; a missing
   469	or moving-tag predecessor fails planning. Comma and whitespace delimiters and
   470	repeated standalone selectors use the resolver's normal token grammar. Explicit version lists and mixed selector/version lists retain the
   471	full Cartesian baseline/scenario matrix for deliberate manual proof. The
   472	selector provenance travels only as internal reusable-workflow metadata;
   473	there is no new manual-dispatch input.
   474	
   475	Expanded published-upgrade survivor and update-migration selections are split by baseline into groups of at most three scenarios, with at most 32 targeted Docker jobs active per matrix. Grouping shares the execution planner's baseline-compatibility policy, so every supported scenario runs exactly once without creating empty shards for old baselines. Each scenario owns a fresh container and the unchanged npm resource limit; package and image identities remain shared across the matrix. `Update Migration` runs weekly on Sunday at 03:17 UTC and on manual dispatch. It defaults to `supported-lines` with both `plugin-deps-cleanup` and `legacy-operator-state`, keeps the existing cleanup coverage, and forwards no provider secrets. The weekly run keeps cleanup on the candidate-relative predecessor and exercises native operator state on each supported baseline. A planning allowance of 12 minutes per scenario plus 30 minutes for shared package/image preparation and controls gives about 78 runner-minutes weekly with three distinct baselines, or 90 with four; actual timing artifacts determine the observed cost.
   476	
   477	Pass `baselines=all-since-2026.6.1` for exhaustive historical cleanup; `last-stable-4`, `release-history`, and exact historical versions remain explicit manual selections. Local aggregate runs can pass the resolved exact specs through `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_BASELINE_SPECS`, keep a single lane with `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_BASELINE_SPEC`, or set `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_SCENARIOS` for the scenario matrix. Existing scenarios retain their baked `openclaw config set` recipes and summary records. The new operator-state scenario instead uses the baseline's own agent, exec-approvals, cron, and plugin CLIs, then verifies preserved state and a mock-provider turn after upgrade. Gateway probes include `/healthz`, `/readyz`, and RPC status. See [Testing updates and plugins](/help/testing-updates-plugins) for the preserved-state and successful-upgrade requirements.
   478	
   479	All supported baseline rows require successful updates. Existing synthetic
   480	`base` and reported-issue fixtures retain their success assertions and run once
   481	on the candidate-relative predecessor. The lane does not run an extra Doctor or
   482	omit those fixtures to turn a failed schema upgrade into a pass.
   483	
   484	Current cross-OS tooling runs packaged fresh-install and upgrade checks on both
   485	Node 24.19.0 and the Node 26.1.0 support floor across Linux, Windows, and macOS.
   486	Windows packaged fresh-install retains Node 24.16.0 for its Node 24 cell because
   487	of the later libuv file-watcher regression. Both runtime variants consume the
   488	same prepared candidate tarball. A focused `suite_filter` selects both Node
   489	variants; the Node 26 jobs and artifacts have distinct names. Installer and
   490	source-update lanes keep their existing Node 24 coverage. Explicitly selecting
   491	older `workflow_ref` tooling retains that revision's historical matrix.
   492	
   493	The Windows packaged and installer fresh lanes also verify that an installed package can import a browser-control override from a raw absolute Windows path. The OpenAI cross-OS agent-turn smoke defaults to `OPENCLAW_CROSS_OS_OPENAI_MODEL` when set, otherwise `openai/gpt-5.6-luna`, so the install and gateway proof uses the lower-cost GPT-5.6 test tier.
   494	
   495	### Legacy compatibility windows
   496	
   497	Current upgrade-survivor execution requires a published baseline of `2026.6.1`
   498	or newer. Historical selectors filter out earlier releases; `release-history`
   499	selects the six most recent supported stable releases. Exact pre-June baselines
   500	are rejected, including when a dist-tag resolves to one. Use matching historical
   501	`workflow_ref` tooling to replay older upgrades. Existing historical receipts
   502	remain readable.
   503	
   504	Package Acceptance no longer relaxes assertions for pre-June 2026 candidates.
   505	Missing inventory entries, shipped local build metadata, missing service-wrapper
   506	support, and incomplete update or plugin install-record persistence fail the
   507	current contracts. Current package validators also require both npm lockfile
   508	formats to be absent from new tarballs. To reproduce historical acceptance
   509	results, select the matching historical `workflow_ref` tooling.
   510	
   511	### Examples
   512	
   513	```bash
   514	# Validate the current beta package with product-level coverage.
   515	gh workflow run package-acceptance.yml \
   516	  --ref main \
   517	  -f workflow_ref=main \
   518	  -f source=npm \
   519	  -f package_spec=openclaw@beta \
   520	  -f suite_profile=product \
   521	  -f telegram_mode=mock-openai
   522	
   523	# Validate the published extended-stable package with package coverage.
   524	gh workflow run package-acceptance.yml \
   525	  --ref main \
   526	  -f workflow_ref=main \
   527	  -f source=npm \
   528	  -f package_spec=openclaw@extended-stable \
   529	  -f suite_profile=package \
   530	  -f telegram_mode=mock-openai
   531	
   532	# Pack and validate a release branch with the current harness.
   533	gh workflow run package-acceptance.yml \
   534	  --ref main \
   535	  -f workflow_ref=main \
   536	  -f source=ref \
   537	  -f package_ref=release/YYYY.M.PATCH \
   538	  -f suite_profile=package \
   539	  -f telegram_mode=mock-openai
   540	
   541	# Validate a tarball URL. SHA-256 is mandatory for source=url.
   542	gh workflow run package-acceptance.yml \
   543	  --ref main \
   544	  -f workflow_ref=main \
   545	  -f source=url \
   546	  -f package_url=https://example.com/openclaw-current.tgz \
   547	  -f package_sha256=<64-char-sha256> \
   548	  -f suite_profile=smoke
   549	
   550	# Validate a tarball from a named trusted private mirror policy.
   551	gh workflow run package-acceptance.yml \
   552	  --ref main \
   553	  -f workflow_ref=main \
   554	  -f source=trusted-url \
   555	  -f trusted_source_id=enterprise-artifactory \
   556	  -f package_url=https://packages.example.internal:8443/artifactory/openclaw/openclaw-current.tgz \
   557	  -f package_sha256=<64-char-sha256> \
   558	  -f suite_profile=smoke
   559	
   560	# Reuse a tarball uploaded by another Actions run.
   561	gh workflow run package-acceptance.yml \
   562	  --ref main \
   563	  -f workflow_ref=main \
   564	  -f source=artifact \
   565	  -f artifact_run_id=<run-id> \
   566	  -f artifact_name=package-under-test \
   567	  -f suite_profile=custom \
   568	  -f docker_lanes='install-e2e plugin-update'
   569	```
   570	
   571	When debugging a failed package acceptance run, start at the `resolve_package` summary to confirm the package source, version, and SHA-256. Then inspect the `docker_acceptance` child run and its Docker artifacts: `.artifacts/docker-tests/**/summary.json`, `failures.json`, lane logs, phase timings, and rerun commands. Prefer rerunning the failed package profile or exact Docker lanes instead of rerunning full release validation.
   572	---
   573	summary: "The separate Plugin Prerelease workflow, its batching limits, and when it runs"
