     8	
     9	## Integrity checks
    10	
    11	| When                                        | Check                                                                                                                                           |
    12	| ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
    13	| Every open                                  | Validate the `schema_meta` table and primary metadata row                                                                                       |
    14	| Writable agent open and Gateway readiness   | Run full integrity and foreign-key checks when neither current runtime proof nor a clean same-version restart receipt is available              |
    15	| Same-process agent reopen                   | Reuse current file-bound runtime proof without another integrity or quick check; recheck owner, version, schema, and canonical indexes          |
    16	| Clean same-version agent restart            | Recheck owner, version, schema, and canonical indexes; queue a child-process `quick_check` and foreign-key check after the Gateway is listening |
    17	| Before a pending migration                  | Run a full integrity, foreign-key, role, schema, and index scan                                                                                 |
    18	| Gateway background verifier                 | Run the full scan about once daily and log results                                                                                              |
    19	| Doctor, backup verification, and compaction | Run the full scan before accepting or rewriting the database                                                                                    |
    20	
    21	The existing quarantine store keeps a reconstructible `agent_integrity_verifications`
    22	record: canonical database path, device, inode, OpenClaw version, verification time, and
    23	integer `clean_close`. It does not hash database contents. Agent lease admission
    24	durably clears cleanliness before opening; only the last graceful lease release
    25	can restore it after a successful WAL checkpoint and native close. Forced worker
    26	exit, failed cleanup, or uncertain ownership invalidates the record. This uses
    27	the existing single shared-state lease owner; independent Gateways must not share
    28	mutable agent databases across state directories.
    29	
    30	Within a live lifecycle, an admitted owner can still lend its revocable,
    31	file-bound runtime proof to another handle when no foreign or unknown process
    32	holds a writer lease. This includes reopening after the last local lease closes.
    33	This proof does not require the persisted restart receipt. Peer leases
    34	with matching process ID and start time do not consume or block publication of
    35	that receipt; each handle retains its own lease until cleanup finishes.
    36	Explicit invalidation revokes shared runtime proof as well as durable metadata,
    37	including stale admission and unsettled Worker cleanup. A successful native close
    38	with a reader-blocked checkpoint removes restart metadata but preserves live
    39	runtime proof; failed close or uncertain storage errors revoke both. Cold opens and restarts
    40	still require matching clean-close metadata or a full check.
    41	Cleanup workers and native agent execution workers borrow that proof under their
    42	existing writer admission. Cleanup workers return new verification to the Gateway
    43	after they finish.
    44	Reclamation retains one Worker connection per database, so alternating agents
    45	reuse their admitted handles. Requests still share the archive FIFO. Each Worker
    46	retires after 30 idle minutes, on database close, or when idle under critical
    47	memory pressure; failed cleanup retains its original lease until settlement.
    48	Integrity revocation, schema checks, and update behavior are unchanged.
    49	Native execution workers can also borrow retained host proof after the host handle
    50	closes or is evicted. The receiving opener rechecks the physical file identity and
    51	shared revocation cell; a closed handle alone does not discard valid proof.
    52	When native execution establishes the first runtime proof, it returns that proof
    53	through its existing admission so later cleanup workers can reuse it without a
    54	host SQLite open. The host accepts it only for the admitted physical file and
    55	unchanged validation state; revocation during the open rejects the handoff.
    56	
    57	Opens borrowing a clean restart receipt queue checks in the existing Gateway
    58	verifier. Reopens borrowing current runtime proof do not queue another check.
    59	Background success is logged; only the full-check lease owner
    60	publishes verification metadata. Confirmed corruption uses the existing quarantine
    61	path and prevents the next open. Ordinary writes do not invalidate the file identity. Same-inode damage
    62	introduced after a clean close can therefore be detected after readiness by the
    63	quick check, SQLite operations, the daily full verifier, or explicit Doctor.
    64	`openclaw doctor` retains full checks and `doctor --fix` clears verification
    65	metadata with quarantine. A failed durable dirty-marker write refuses that open
    66	rather than leaving stale clean proof reusable after a crash.
    67	
    68	The table is additive in the quarantine store; agent and shared-state schema
    69	versions do not change. An update to a different OpenClaw version runs the full
    70	gate, and older builds ignore the new table and retain their full checks. Pending
    71	migrations, index repairs, shared-state readiness, and explicit copied-file
    72	preflight still perform their existing full checks. Snapshot-based agent
    73	readiness also conservatively retains its full gate.
    74	
    75	Startup certifies each database without a canonical-validation receipt once,
    76	including an empty session source with an empty pending-validation queue.
    77	Successful canonical validation records `session_key_contract.canonical_ready`
    78	in the final authorized batch transaction. This nullable `TEXT` column is added
    79	on first certification without changing the schema version. Its receipt binds
    80	the agent and physical file generation, including device, inode, and birth time.
    81	On later boots, unchanged empty and populated stores reuse that first proof and inspect
    82	the pending queue; ordinary canonical writes still mark changed rows for
    83	validation. Exact invalidation triggers remain required. Copies and replaced
    84	files need their own first proof, even when their imported pending queue is empty.
    85	The receipt does not certify physical integrity, replace the integrity policy
    86	above, or override explicit process-local revocation. Older readers can
    87	ignore the nullable column; backup and rollback retain its existing row lifetime.
    88	
    89	Database replacement, quarantine, and failed admission discard applicable
    90	verification. Doctor maintenance discards remembered runtime verification after
    91	draining agent connections and before raw maintenance can run. Pending migrations
    92	still run full checks, and canonical index
    93	repairs verify their result before committing. Schema, ownership, and current
    94	write authority are never borrowed from the integrity result.
    95	
    96	Shared-state runtime opens and automatic startup preparation converge supported
    97	schema additions and preserve atomic upgrades from older schema versions. A
    98	newly added supported column receives its required content transformation in the
    99	same transaction. Opens do not rerun historical row backfills for columns already
   100	present when the application version changes. Run
   101	`openclaw doctor --fix` during update maintenance to repair historical accounting
   102	or legacy payload fields. A current-schema database that still contains the
   103	retired `cron_run_logs` table requires Doctor before runtime can open it; Doctor
   104	imports its retained history into task runs atomically before removing the table.
   105	Shared-state integrity, schema, version, and ownership checks remain in place.
   106	
   107	Schema compatibility preflight can read agent schema headers without a full integrity scan. For ordinary rollback-mode agent databases and complete WAL families, a read-only child reads the schema version and optional writer build in one fresh SQLite transaction, including committed WAL changes, without copying unrelated database contents. Its source-reader lease stays held through native close; cancellation and timeout wait for child closure. Parent-side diagnostics do not open or close the live agent file, preserving the parent's SQLite locks. As with the previous online-backup reader, native SQLite may update SHM read marks or rebuild existing SHM after a quiescent family reopens; the database and WAL contents remain unchanged. The Gateway carries successful header facts from admission to its later compatibility preflight only while the database, WAL, and rollback-journal files are unchanged. Changed or uncertain files are inspected again. Full readiness and writable admission retain their existing validation and fresh authority checks.
   108	
   109	Private snapshots remain necessary inside owner-held source-exclusion scopes, for incomplete WAL families whose inspection would create source sidecars, and for rollback journals requiring private recovery. Those cases use the existing snapshot owner and deadline; ordinary inspection errors do not trigger a full-copy fallback. Shared-state preflight is unchanged. `openclaw database preflight` performs the release-local shape comparison for an explicit copied file. The background verifier also scans already-open databases about once daily.
   110	
   111	Concurrent asynchronous requests for the same physical live database share one
   112	snapshot operation. When the canonical runtime already owns an open SQLite
   113	connection, that owner supplies SQLite's online backup instead of reopening or
   114	copying the live database family. Each caller retains an independent cleanup
   115	lease, and cancellation detaches only that caller while the shared operation and
   116	remaining leases keep their original owner and cleanup authority.
   117	
   118	Ordinary observed config loads, including runtime reload preparation, read pending
   119	plugin migration obligations through the existing live shared-state reader or
   120	worker rather than copying the database and WAL. Each read sees current committed
   121	rows; it does not cache obligations or grant publication authority. Unobserved
   122	inspection and inherited artifact-preserving scopes retain private snapshots.
   123	Migration publication still rechecks the current generation under its coordinator.
   124	
   125	Runtime config publication, including model-catalog worker generations, also reads
   126	Claw consent provenance through the live shared-state reader instead of copying
   127	the database and WAL for every generation. Each publication refreshes committed
   128	provenance; config-digest checks, failure handling, and publication admission remain
   129	unchanged. Explicit provenance inspection and inherited artifact-preserving scopes
   130	still use private snapshots. Neither optimization changes schemas, stored records,
   131	retention, or update migrations.
   132	
   133	Live snapshots use SQLite's online-backup owner and a read transaction to pin
   134	committed pages while writers continue. Native readers may update existing SHM
   135	read marks, so artifact-preserving planning and Doctor scopes use raw copies
   136	instead. A WAL copy captures main first, then a bounded WAL prefix, and verifies
   137	both within the same pinned WAL generation. Appended frames are allowed; resets,
   138	replacements, and changes to captured bytes require another attempt. SQLite
   139	interprets committed frames in the private copy. Source SHM stays untouched.
   140	Only raw copies reuse a scoped IPC child; native backups remain one-shot to avoid
   141	Node 26 completion stalls with persistent IPC. Incomplete WAL families, rollback
   142	crash residue, and owner-excluded sources also retain private copying and recovery.
   143	Existing WAL and rollback-journal files can coexist without write activity;
   144	inspection copies and verifies both before SQLite recovers the private family.
   145	It does not discard committed WAL pages, repair the source, or change plan identity.
   146	Snapshot debug telemetry reports operation and owner,
   147	main and WAL sizes, copied bytes, attempt, duration, and outcome.
   148	
   149	Synchronous CLI snapshots also pause between source-change retries, so a brief
   150	write burst does not exhaust all ten attempts immediately. These retries only
   151	repeat private snapshot preparation; they do not resend Gateway commands.
   152	
   153	Private snapshot files remain temporary artifacts: the creator registers cleanup
   154	before copying and publishes the finished copy by rename. Graceful shutdown
   155	drains existing shutdown owners and joins snapshot workers before cleanup. Cleanup
   156	keeps every token until copied data is removed, so partial removal remains recoverable.
   157	Native termination and hard kills can skip that drain. Each staging directory holds
   158	an open SQLite transaction as its lifetime token. Reclamation obtains exclusive
   159	tokens for the parent and every nested worker before inspecting or removing the
   160	copy, independent of PID namespaces. Worker admission checks the parent's token;
   161	retirement is committed before handles close so a late worker cannot restart it.
   162	The Gateway schedules abandoned-copy reclamation after startup, once foreground
   163	root work is idle, then revisits every 15 minutes after a completed pass. Each
   164	pass yields to foreground work and rechecks ownership and age, so copies skipped
   165	as recent or over budget can become eligible without restarting the Gateway.
   166	Snapshot allocation only creates and registers its own token;
   167	neither synchronous nor asynchronous allocation waits for a reclamation pass.
   168	Reclamation runs in a SQLite worker, keeping directory traversal and removal off
   169	the Gateway event loop, and logs the copied-data byte count. Concurrent cleanup
   170	requests for one root share a pass. Reclamation requires verifiable inactive
   171	owner and worker tokens, applies a 15-minute grace period to current staging
   172	directories, and uses a 512 MiB copied-byte budget per pass. The first reclaimed
   173	directory may exceed that budget, after the same ownership and age checks; the
   174	pass then stops, so oversized interrupted copies can make progress one at a time.
   175	Active, recent, over-budget, or
   176	structurally unknown directories remain untouched. Shutdown and the existing
   177	reclamation deadline stop at directory boundaries, after removal and token
   178	release settle together. Reclamation worker failures warn without preventing
   179	later snapshot allocation.
   180	Legacy directories use a 24-hour age threshold, including legacy children under a
   181	current parent. Updaters also mark staging for a selected installation as legacy-compatible
   182	before launching workers that may predate tokens. Current workers fence admission
   183	inside an existing legacy parent, including the intervening `openclaw` cache
   184	directory created by released workers. Reclamation understands both generations
   185	in that layout and applies the same token and age rules to inner staging directories.
   186	A read-only scan checks all legacy activity before token I/O; validation
   187	repeats under locks before deletion. Copies that are still too recent keep their existing timestamps.
   188	Coordination files do not count as copied data or legacy activity. Live or unverified tokens, recent legacy copies,
   189	unknown contents, and symlinks are left alone with a warning. Canonical databases
   190	and backups are never reclaimed by this owner.
   191	
   192	Schema-only agent inspections during Doctor and restart checks read metadata in
   193	a child process, within one SQLite read transaction, without copying the whole
   194	database. Empty files, rollback journals, incomplete WAL sidecars, and
   195	owner-provided snapshots retain the private snapshot path. Startup readiness also
   196	performs the full integrity and foreign-key checks described below.
   197	
   198	Doctor also shares one private shared-state snapshot across a synchronous
   199	workspace-alias check. The next check reads fresh state, so committed repairs are
   200	visible without taking a separate snapshot for every configured workspace.
   201	
   202	Memory search and maintenance managers borrow the verified per-agent connection. Acquisition does not reopen or rescan a healthy shared handle. Native and transformed plugin modules share the same process-owned connection lifecycle, query cache, and commit observers. Nested synchronous writes use SQLite savepoints on that connection. A manager retains that exact connection against cache eviction until its work drains, then releases its borrow without closing the database. Explicit quarantine and disposal still revoke it. Full memory rebuilds use separate temporary shadow databases and publish their derived tables in one synchronous transaction. Read-only memory status keeps its separate diagnostic connection and does not create or migrate a missing database.
   203	
   204	If nested rollback or savepoint cleanup fails, the transaction owner preserves the original failure, discards staged state and post-commit observers, and closes the connection. Catching that failure cannot resume writes on the abandoned handle. A later operation must acquire a fresh connection through its database owner. Doctor plugin-state imports retain earlier committed batches; an aborted batch cannot commit its prefix. Ordinary row refusals that successfully roll back their savepoint still commit the successful prefix for resumable imports.
   205	
   206	The shared cache targets 64 handles, but live borrows, synchronous transactions, and incognito state are not evicted. After owners release them, the next new connection trims idle handles back to that target.
   207	
   208	Concurrent runs normally share the cached writer for an agent database on the main thread. Workers and diagnostics can open additional connections to the same file; the connection count is operation-dependent. Canonical agent connections set SQLite's busy timeout before use. A timeout cannot resolve a worker holding a write transaction while waiting for a blocked main thread: synchronous transcript appends do not join the asynchronous session write queue. Transaction callbacks must finish synchronously, and a competing writer must not depend on the main event loop to release its lock.
   209	
   210	Periodic agent maintenance uses passive WAL checkpoints and bounded incremental vacuum. Session reclamation keeps deletion on a separate worker write connection and uses a passive checkpoint and bounded vacuum after commit; long deletion transactions can still contend with other writers. Full compaction belongs to offline Doctor maintenance. Run errors naming the Gateway state database retain a safe SQLite diagnosis; see [storage failure troubleshooting](/gateway/troubleshooting#agent-run-failed-with-a-storage-error).
   211	
   212	After an admitted periodic PASSIVE checkpoint completes, the WAL owner makes one
   213	zero-lock-wait TRUNCATE attempt if the observed WAL still exceeds its existing
   214	64 MiB recycling limit. A concurrent reader or writer can defer recycling to the
   215	next maintenance pass. The connection's busy timeout is restored afterward, and
   216	the existing checkpoint health records the result. This does not change
   217	durability, transaction contents, reader lifetimes, schemas, or history retention;
   218	it never unlinks a live WAL. Upgrades need no state migration, and older binaries
   219	can continue reading the same databases.
   220	
   221	Quarantine decisions live only in a dedicated `openclaw-quarantine.sqlite` store, so they survive damage to the databases being quarantined. Verification results are logged.
   222	
   223	Background verification errors retain the original name and message and append bounded Node `code` and SQLite `errcode` values from up to eight cause-chain nodes. These diagnostics do not change the verdict: I/O failures remain inconclusive, while proven corruption is reconfirmed by the database owner before quarantine. A generic `disk I/O error` (`errcode=10`) does not establish disk exhaustion.
   224	
   225	The background verifier retains its child through native exit and IPC disconnect,
   226	including when sending work fails. Failed native launches settle after closure
   227	without requiring an exit event. If the operating system refuses a termination
   228	request, the verifier logs the failure and keeps waiting for native exit; an
   229	undelivered signal does not mean the child has stopped. The original worker or
   230	IPC error remains the reported failure even if termination also fails.
   231	
   232	Agent database maintenance fences other writers with a 60-second lease in the shared state database. A dedicated worker renews that lease during synchronous integrity scans and migration phases. Maintenance still checks the exact persisted owner before mutations and commit, and stops if the heartbeat fails or ownership expires or changes. Finishing or cancelling maintenance stops renewal before releasing the lease; process death leaves at most the remaining lease duration.
   233	
   234	Before draining heartbeats for a file capture, each state-lease owner attempts a final ordinary renewal. Capture remains bounded by the shortest durable expiry read after drainage; it cannot renew while files are excluded or revive an expired owner.
   235	
   236	Asynchronous agent-database admission runs the first full-file integrity check in a read-only child process when that check is outside a write transaction. Later ordinary opens reuse remembered verification. Maintenance retains its independent full check. The connection and owning scope remain held until the native reader closes; cancellation and timeout wait for process exit. Schema changes, index repairs, and compaction retain their synchronous phases.
   237	
   238	An agent maintenance lease reuses one integrity-check process across its queued
   239	checks. Each request opens and closes its own database, reads fresh file identity,
   240	and rechecks the current maintenance owner. Results and database handles are never
   241	cached between requests. Failures retire the process before the caller resumes,
   242	and the lease joins all queued checks and the child before releasing ownership.
   243	For reused children, worker lifetime timing measures each request through native
   244	database close; one-shot checks include process exit.
   245	
   246	The integrity child allows SQLite to cache up to about 64 MiB of database pages
   247	while checking indexes and foreign keys. SQLite allocates those pages as needed,
   248	and the cache ends when that database closes; retained Gateway connections keep their
   249	existing cache settings. Full integrity and foreign-key checks still run.
   250	
   251	Explicit session-maintenance finalization uses this asynchronous admission if its writable handle was evicted during archive or deletion preparation. It keeps its place in the session writer queue and rechecks maintenance and deletion authority before committing. Automatic maintenance retires when its original handle closes instead of reopening it.
   252	
   253	The integrity child and both asynchronous and synchronous read-only snapshot workers share a size-derived lifetime budget. It includes a five-minute startup and shutdown allowance, then budgets four file-sized IO passes with tenfold headroom below the 32 MiB/s reference rate for older disks. A verified raw copy reads the source and writes a private file, then compares both; other inspection modes use the same conservative allowance. Sizing includes the main database, WAL, SHM, and rollback journal.
   254	
   255	A 2 GiB database gets 2,860 seconds, and workers finish as soon as their work completes. The size-derived allowance has only the runtime's timer-representability ceiling. Budgets above the startup allowance are logged once per call at debug level with the operation, path, measured size, and applied budget. If the snapshot worker cannot stat the source, it uses the startup allowance and lets the child report the underlying error.
   256	
   257	Update schema inspection and candidate snapshots use this same allowance as an inactivity watchdog. Larger caller budgets remain available, and observed private-copy progress renews the deadline. See [How updates run](/cli/update/how-updates-run).
   258	
   259	Live snapshots use the online-backup worker. Artifact-preserving scopes and synchronous snapshot copies keep source bytes unchanged, including WAL coordination state.
   260	
   261	Full startup readiness checks agent ownership, integrity, foreign keys, and schema
   262	in one fresh read-only transaction in a disposable child. Complete WAL families
   263	and rollback-mode databases without journals do not need a full private copy.
   264	Empty files, incomplete WAL families, rollback recovery, and source-exclusion
   265	scopes retain private snapshot inspection. The parent waits
   266	for native close before accepting the result or releasing its scope. The source
   267	database and WAL remain unchanged; native WAL readers may update SHM read marks.
   268	Admission before the migration lease and the fresh check before migration writes
   269	remain separate, with no cached readiness result shared between them.
   270	
   271	### Startup on multi-agent hosts
   272	
   273	Current development builds already limit startup agent-database checks and
   274	session startup maintenance to two databases at a time. Each inspection's
   275	size-derived foreground allowance starts when its scheduled inspection begins, so waiting for a
   276	slot does not consume it. For example, a 267.5 MiB database without sidecars gets
   277	635 seconds. These concurrency and budget improvements precede the background
   278	startup recovery described here; installed releases can have shorter budgets
   279	and different concurrency.
   280	
   281	Session startup certification reuses up to two worker threads for databases that
   282	need fresh canonical proof. Valid receipts retain their existing fast path. Each
   283	certification task has fresh admission, its own commit gate, and full canonical
   284	validation. The task closes its database handles and leases and waits
   285	for the parent's close request to finish before releasing the thread for another
   286	database. If native cleanup is uncertain, writer admission and cleanup custody
   287	remain held until execution ends.
   288	
   289	During startup, reaching the inspection's foreground deadline records a warning
   290	and marks that agent **degraded** while the Gateway continues with healthy agents.
   291	Its sessions remain unavailable, and its database is excluded from automatic
   292	migration and ordinary writes. The inspection continues in the background within
   293	the same concurrency limit. Expiring the wait does not establish corruption.
   294	
   295	A successful inspection alone does not make the agent available. The Gateway
   296	first refreshes its credentials and completes that agent's session validation,
   297	transcript preparation, and model preparation, with current database and runtime ownership checked before
   298	publication. Only then does it clear the pending refusal. A failed inspection or
   299	preparation leaves the agent degraded with the recorded reason; it does not stop
   300	healthy agents. Shared-state database failures retain their existing startup
   301	checks.
   302	
   303	Inspect `openclaw gateway call agents.list --json` or Gateway logs for the affected
   304	agent and reason. If the check fails, follow that reason's repair guidance; stop the Gateway
   305	before running `openclaw doctor --fix` against the same state directory or
   306	restoring the affected database from a verified backup. Restart after repair.
   307	Gateway shutdown cancels and joins pending inspections and preparation before
   308	releasing their owners. A result arriving during shutdown cannot readmit an agent.
   309	
   310	Integrity-child timeout and incomplete-exit errors include `lastObservedPhase`:
   311	
   312	| Value             | Last observation                                                                          |
   313	| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   314	| `starting`        | The parent has not received a child phase.                                                |
   315	| `opening`         | The child announced file-identity checks and opening a read-only connection.              |
   316	| `checking`        | The connection opened, and the child announced the full integrity and foreign-key checks. |
   317	| `closing`         | The child announced connection cleanup after checking or an error.                        |
   318	| `result-received` | The parent received a final result and is waiting for child closure.                      |
   319	
   320	These phases describe messages the parent received, not the child's exact current location or native CPU time. `checking` does not distinguish the integrity check from the foreign-key check. A final result can report failure; phase messages never establish successful validation or release ownership.
   321	
   322	Slow asynchronous agent-database opens include optional wall-time measurements:
   323	
   324	| Field                       | Measured interval                                                                                                                     |
   325	| --------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   326	| `integrityWorkerCheckMs`    | Full integrity and foreign-key checks inside the child, excluding opening and closing the connection.                                 |
   327	| `integrityWorkerLifetimeMs` | Parent-observed time from forking the child through its close event, including startup, IPC, cleanup and event delivery.              |
   328	| `integrityOutsideWorkerMs`  | The integrity gate's remaining time outside that child lifetime, including parent preparation, scheduling and admission revalidation. |
   329	
   330	Missing measurements stay absent, including a child check killed before reporting
   331	its duration. These fields are distinct from the calling driver's synchronous
   332	`integrityCheckSyncMs` and `integrityOutsideCheckMs`. None measures CPU time or
   333	isolates storage waiting. The parent still waits for child closure and revalidates
   334	the database and current authority before admission continues.
   335	
   336	Startup errors containing `state lease heartbeat did not become ready` include `phase=startup`, the settlement trigger (`timeout` or `message`), and the status observed before the parent marks failure. `status=starting` distinguishes readiness still pending from `status=lost`, where loss was already recorded. `elapsedMs` measures monotonic time since heartbeat startup began; `timeoutMs` is the startup wait budget, capped at five seconds and the latest confirmed durable lease expiry. The live state-lease owner renews during startup until the worker takes over. Expired or replaced owners cannot renew, and host renewal never extends the five-second startup cap. These fields do not establish why startup stalled or ownership was lost.
   337	
   338	The heartbeat proves ownership, not migration progress. A live but stuck maintenance process can keep its lease; stop that process before retrying Doctor.
   339	
   340	## Troubleshooting
   341	
   342	`SQLite read-only worker` failures append `code` and numeric SQLite `errcode` diagnostics when the underlying error supplies valid values, including through a bounded cause chain. Report the full code suffix when investigating a failure. Snapshot and integrity-child timeout errors include the applied budget and source file size; snapshot timeouts report an unknown size if the source stat failed. Integrity-child timeouts also retain `lastObservedPhase`. A generic `disk I/O error` or `SQLITE_IOERR` alone does not prove the disk is full.
   343	
   344	### The state database is busy
   345	
   346	Wait for the other OpenClaw process to finish its database work, then retry the
   347	command. `state-lifecycle` contention normally clears after startup, a write, or
   348	maintenance finishes. `gateway-lifecycle` protects a running Gateway's ownership,
   349	and `state-handles` protects open database connections; those can remain held
   350	while the Gateway runs.
   351	
   352	If contention persists, run `openclaw gateway status` with the same profile and
   353	state-directory settings, and check for other OpenClaw processes using that state
   354	directory. Stop the blocking Gateway through its service manager or original
   355	terminal before retrying an operation that needs exclusive access. Prefer plain
   356	status here: `--deep` adds database preflight. Doctor also needs state coordination,
   357	so running it while the lock is held can fail with the same contention.
   358	
   359	### Database paths cannot be compared
   360	
   361	`Cannot determine whether database paths alias` means OpenClaw could not safely
   362	compare paths that do not yet exist. Check permission to create and remove entries
   363	under the nearest existing parent directory, then retry. Comparisons use bounded
   364	filesystem probes: each missing suffix permits up to 8,192 UTF-16 code units, with
   365	at most 32,768 forward filesystem observations. Simplify unusually long paths if
   366	those limits are exceeded. Incomplete probe cleanup never becomes a cached
   367	path-identity result.
   368	
   369	### A mount probe times out while opening a local database
   370	
   371	On macOS, native filesystem inspection can confirm APFS after mount enumeration
   372	times out. For a canonical database directory, OpenClaw then keeps WAL enabled
   373	instead of attempting a rollback-mode transition that conflicts with other open
   374	connections. Unknown filesystems, failed native inspection, and aliased paths
   375	retain the conservative rollback policy. The existing rules for network and
   376	cross-VM filesystems, including the refusal to write through SSHFS, still apply.
   377	
   378	### A legacy Workshop index prevents shared-state reads
   379	
   380	The `legacy-workshop-review-index` error requires `openclaw doctor --fix`.
   381	Ordinary Gateway reads and automatic migration do not enter the legacy catalog
   382	repair path. Healthy reads retain their prepared SQLite queries.
   383	
   384	With OpenClaw 2026.9.4, run Doctor before retrying `openclaw update`: the installed
   385	updater checks database integrity before it can launch the target version.
   386	
   387	Doctor checks database versions and active owners before repairing the exact
   388	known index. It restores catalog readability before loading dependent config
   389	and plugin state, then continues its normal migration and verification flow.
   390	The readability repair preserves review rows and schema-version markers;
   391	unrecognized damage and newer databases remain refused.
   392	
   393	### The shared-state WAL keeps growing
   394	
   395	The running Gateway records the result of its existing WAL maintenance pass,
   396	normally every 30 minutes. `openclaw status --deep` and Doctor show a **SQLite
   397	WAL** warning after two consecutive blocked checkpoints, or after one blocked
   398	checkpoint when the WAL exceeds both twice the database size and the existing
   399	64 MiB journal-size limit. Checkpoint errors warn immediately. A later complete
   400	checkpoint clears the warning; a large WAL alone does not mean a checkpoint is
   401	blocked. File-size observation failures are recorded and logged separately from
   402	SQLite's completion result; they do not turn a completed checkpoint into a failure.
   403	
   404	Shared-state maintenance waits up to 350 ms for lifecycle coordination. Periodic
   405	maintenance yields between acquisition attempts so the Gateway event loop can
   406	continue. It rechecks the same database owner and physical file before proceeding;
   407	retirement cancels and joins pending admission. Explicit synchronous checkpoint
   408	and close operations retain their existing contract. A refused
   409	periodic attempt retries once after one second, then waits for the next interval.
   410	Contention is recorded as blocked. Status and Doctor warn after two consecutive
   411	refusals; maintenance logs once per five. A completed checkpoint resets that count and clears the history
   412	eviction gate. On Linux, `blockingOwner` includes the observed kernel lock holder's
   413	PID, process start time (boot ticks), command, and coordinator family when procfs
   414	is available. This best-effort snapshot is diagnostic only; the SQLite lock still
   415	owns exclusion. Other platforms and unavailable observations report `unknown`.
   416	Coordinator files remain write-free, and updates require no state migration.
   417	
   418	The warning includes observed WAL and database sizes, checkpointed and total WAL
   419	frames, the last observed complete checkpoint, the consecutive blocked count,
   420	the observation time, and up to eight process-local active reader owners when
   421	the blocking connection uses OpenClaw's tracked query helpers. Reader diagnostics
   422	contain only the bounded operation label, main/worker owner kind, optional worker
   423	actor id, age, and idle time; they never include SQL, bindings, or row contents.
   424	SQLite can report `busy=0` for an incomplete PASSIVE checkpoint; fewer checkpointed
   425	frames than total frames still records a blocked checkpoint. An absent reader list
   426	means that the blocker is untracked or belongs to another process, not that no
   427	reader exists.
   428	
   429	Shared-state SQLite worker actors inspect their already-open WAL connection after
   430	60 seconds without an active operation. An admitted PASSIVE checkpoint that
   431	positively inspects a healthy connection keeps the actor until 30 minutes after
   432	its last real operation; the inspection does not extend that deadline. Another
   433	connection's reader can prevent a complete checkpoint without making this actor
   434	unhealthy. A local native reader that refuses the checkpoint, an unavailable
   435	inspection, or an actor without an inspectable WAL connection retains the
   436	60-second retirement behavior. Inspection never opens a database for an
   437	artifact-preserving reader. Retirement closes the native database borrow before
   438	a later request opens a replacement actor. An actor that returns from an
   439	operation with a tracked reader still active fails settlement and retires
   440	immediately.
   441	
   442	Observations belong to the open database handle in the Gateway process. They
   443	reset when that handle is replaced or the Gateway restarts. Status and Doctor
   444	read the recorded observation through the existing status RPC; they do not run
   445	a checkpoint or open a diagnostic database. Before the first observation, or
   446	when an older Gateway supplies no observations, this warning is absent.
   447	
   448	If the warning persists, capture `openclaw status --deep` output and restart the
   449	Gateway gracefully with `openclaw gateway restart`. Report the captured output
   450	if the warning returns. Do not delete the WAL: it can contain committed data
   451	that has not reached the main database file.
   452	
   453	### Doctor reports orphan task delivery rows
   454	
   455	If `foreign_key_check` names `task_delivery_state` referencing `task_runs`,
   456	stop the Gateway and run `openclaw doctor --fix`. Doctor can recover this known
   457	relation when the database is structurally intact and has no unrelated
   458	foreign-key violations or unrecognized delivery-table schema or triggers.
   459	
   460	Before removing any orphan rows, Doctor preserves a complete WAL-aware database
   461	copy and a lossless row export in a private `openclaw-task-delivery-recovery-*`
   462	directory beside the shared database. Its report names that directory. It contains:
   463	
   464	- `database.sqlite`: the pre-repair database, including the orphan rows.
   465	- `orphan-rows.jsonl`: the original delivery payload, with SQLite integers encoded
   466	  as decimal strings to avoid precision loss.
   467	- `manifest.json`: file hashes, row count, and preservation metadata.
   468	
   469	Doctor removes only delivery rows whose task no longer exists, within the existing
   470	repair transaction, and requires clean integrity and foreign-key checks before
   471	commit. It does not fabricate tasks or replay deliveries. If preservation or a
   472	later repair step fails, row removal rolls back and any recovery artifacts remain.
   473	A manifest proves preservation, not that the repair committed; rerun Doctor to
   474	verify completion.
   475	
   476	Keep these files private: they can contain delivery destinations and other user
   477	data. Retain them until the updated Gateway is verified and any needed payload
   478	has been recovered. Do not overwrite newer runtime state with this original
   479	copy. This recovery does not establish which writer created the orphan rows.
   480	
   481	### Why you cannot go back after updating to 2026.7.2
   482	
   483	Every release through `v2026.7.1` used agent schema 1 and state schema 1. The 2026.7.2 release train (starting with `v2026.7.2-beta.1`) migrates your databases forward on first start. That migration is one-way: the data is rewritten into the newer schema, and installing an older OpenClaw afterwards does not undo it. The older build refuses to start with a `newer schema version` error that names the build that owns the database.
   484	
   485	Some older packages omit schema metadata. The updater recognizes the schema-1
   486	contract for plain 2026 stable releases through `2026.7.1` and checks it before
   487	activation, including when updating from a local package tarball. An incompatible
   488	target is refused while the current installation remains in place.
   489	
   490	Downgrading the binary never downgrades the data. Use the managed recovery path
   491	or restore the verified pre-update backup with its matching release. Retain
   492	migration recovery originals until you have verified the upgrade; they do not
   493	replace a complete backup. See [Downgrade](/install/updating#downgrade).
   494	
   495	### The Gateway refuses to start with a newer schema version error
   496	
   497	A newer OpenClaw build wrote your databases, and the running build is older. The error names the refusing install — release version, commit, and install root — plus the schema it supports and the schema it found.
   498	
   499	Act on the install root, not the version. One release version string spans many `main` commits, schema levels, and same-version schema shapes, so two installs can both call themselves `2026.7.2` and still disagree about a database. A prerelease version may not exist on the `latest` npm tag at all: check `npm view openclaw dist-tags` before reinstalling, because the tag carrying the schema you need may be `beta`, and reinstalling from `latest` can move you further away.
   500	
   501	When a Gateway runs from a linked source checkout, its status and schema-refusal diagnostics report the commit captured when `dist/` was built, not the checkout's current Git HEAD. If that build identity is unknown, rebuild the checkout (`pnpm build`) before concluding the version is wrong.
   502	
   503	Open the database with a build that supports its schema, or point the older build at a separate `OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR`. Do not edit the database to silence the error.
   504	
   505	Config reads also save health fingerprints to this database. If that write fails,
   506	`Config health-state write failed` reports the first failure for that database
   507	in the current process. Repeated identical failures are suppressed while writes
   508	continue to be attempted. A different error, or a failure after a successful
   509	health-state write, is reported again. Suppressing duplicates does not resolve
   510	the underlying database error.
   511	
   512	### A database is quarantined after integrity verification failed
   513	
   514	The background verifier proved the file is corrupt, and every open now fails fast instead of rescanning. Restore the database from a backup or repair it, then run `openclaw doctor --fix` to clear the quarantine record. Doctor reports an explicit error if the quarantine record itself cannot be cleared; rerun it until it reports clean.
   515	
   516	For shared-state or per-agent index-only corruption, `openclaw doctor --fix` is
   517	the supported repair. Doctor requires every `integrity_check` finding to name missing,
   518	non-unique, or incorrectly counted index entries, verifies the table data without
   519	using the damaged indexes, and preserves the damaged database in an
   520	`openclaw-index-recovery-*` directory beside it before running `REINDEX`.
   521	It prints the backup path and a warning naming every rebuilt index, then requires
   522	clean integrity and foreign-key checks before clearing quarantine. Table rows
   523	are preserved. Page or b-tree damage, unreadable table data, and other integrity
   524	failures remain a refusal: preserve the database and its WAL, then restore a
   525	verified backup or use SQLite recovery. Runtime and startup never perform this
   526	repair automatically.
   527	
   528	<a id="downgrades-are-unsupported" />
   529	
