    35	Version 3 was an unshipped development step folded into version 4.
    36	
    37	### Compact agent payload storage
    38	
    39	Agent schema **23** changes the transcript and memory storage representations.
    40	The [storage design](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/153618) records
    41	the migration scope and required proof. Shared-state schema remains 17.
    42	
    43	Each transcript event retains its original JSON in exactly one representation:
    44	identity `TEXT` or a checksummed level-1 Zstd `BLOB`. Compression applies only to
    45	eligible UTF-8 events from 1 KiB through 4 MiB, and only when the frame plus its
    46	navigation metadata saves at least 64 bytes and 10 percent. Small, oversized,
    47	malformed, and exceptional Unicode records retain identity storage. UTF-16
    48	databases retain identity storage and their existing native byte accounting.
    49	The metadata holds navigation projections, report-selection facts, and exact
    50	context-budget sizes. Report deduplication and database size statistics use these
    51	facts without decoding unrelated bodies; selected body reads reconstruct the
    52	original text. No extension or new
    53	SQLite file format is required. A runtime without Zstd can write identity rows,
    54	but refuses to decode an existing compressed row.
    55	
    56	Memory chunks and embedding caches use little-endian Float64 vectors, preserving
    57	the provider's finite numbers without a Float32 precision change. The optional
    58	vector accelerator remains derived Float32 storage. Chunks keep their logical
    59	IDs and gain a stable integer identity used by full-text maintenance triggers.
    60	Malformed legacy vectors preserve chunk text and provenance and record existing
    61	source/vector rebuild debt. Usage rollups also move to a metadata envelope plus
    62	an identity or compressed body in the existing cache table; obsolete or invalid
    63	derived caches can be rebuilt.
    64	
    65	Transcript full-text search keeps its existing content and rowids. A derived row
    66	map indexes session/message ownership so cleanup and reconciliation delete
    67	selected FTS rowids without scanning the full text table. Duplicate and null
    68	message IDs remain valid. Migration copies existing rowids without rebuilding
    69	or retokenizing text. Both schema 21 and the deployed schema 22 migrate directly
    70	to schema 23. Schema 22's lazy `(session_id, fts_rowid)` map can be empty or
    71	incomplete, so the new `(id, session_id, message_id)` map is populated from
    72	existing FTS content. The migration retires `fts_row_count` after retaining its
    73	unknown or incomplete state as `needs_rebuild`. Clean mappings remain clean;
    74	existing rebuild claims, cursors, active-path rows, and canonical-validation
    75	pending rows are preserved. The unpublished compressed schema-22 draft is not
    76	a supported predecessor.
    77	
    78	The admitted migration converts one transcript record at a time, verifies each
    79	compressed frame against its original bytes, preserves row identities and
    80	timestamps, and commits table replacements with both schema markers. Unknown
    81	columns or dependencies that a rebuild would discard cause a refusal. Earlier
    82	supported schemas run their prerequisite migrations first. Conversion needs
    83	temporary space for old and replacement tables, journal/WAL activity, and the
    84	verified backup. Freed pages are reusable; a smaller payload does not by itself
    85	shrink the database file. Existing maintenance owns physical reclamation.
    86	
    87	Stop all writers and take a verified WAL-aware backup before upgrading. Supported
    88	updaters run the candidate's Doctor under the existing maintenance owner. The
    89	2026.9.2 package updater rehearses on private copies before its post-core Doctor
    90	verifies recovery-backup coverage and performs the live migration. Unverified
    91	authority or backup coverage retains the refusal and
    92	[manual recovery instructions](/install/updating#updating-from-2026.9.2-across-a-schema-bump).
    93	Interrupted conversion rolls back its transaction. Earlier prerequisite migrations
    94	can already be committed; keep writers stopped and resume Doctor with the
    95	compatible build. Older builds refuse schema 23. Rollback requires the
    96	pre-upgrade backup and matching build; lowering markers cannot restore the old
    97	payload representation.
    98	
    99	### Transcript FTS row ownership
   100	
   101	Agent schema **22** adds `session_transcript_fts_rows` and the nullable
   102	`session_transcript_index_state.fts_row_count`. The transcript projection owner
   103	records every inserted FTS rowid in the same transaction as its FTS row. An index
   104	on `session_id` makes deletion proportional to the session's indexed rows even
   105	when different sessions' appends are interleaved. These are derived search facts;
   106	raw transcript bytes, visibility, retention and synchronous rebuild limits stay
   107	unchanged.
   108	
   109	Migration creates an empty mapping and marks existing index state
   110	`needs_rebuild = 1`, with `fts_row_count = NULL`. It does not scan or backfill FTS
   111	content. On the next reconcile, unknown or incomplete ownership takes the legacy
   112	session-filtered delete during that first rebuild and publishes exact mappings
   113	with their count. Worker rebuilds retain bounded delete chunks, so a legacy
   114	projection may need a fallback scan per chunk until that first rebuild finishes.
   115	Subsequent deletes use exact rowids. Synchronous and worker reconciliation,
   116	suffix replacement, deletion and cold restoration maintain the same ownership.
   117	There is no foreign-key cascade on the mapping: deletion needs those rowids even
   118	after the session window has been removed; the projection owner removes them
   119	with their FTS rows.
   120	
   121	Both schema version markers advance through the existing maintenance owner in
   122	the same transaction. Stop writers and take a verified WAL-aware backup before
   123	running the compatible build's `openclaw doctor --fix`. Older builds refuse
   124	schema 22 because their writes cannot maintain row ownership. Rollback requires
   125	the pre-migration backup and matching build; lowering version markers is unsafe.
   126	The existing [older-updater contract](/reference/database-schemas/versioning#schema-bumps-and-older-updaters)
   127	applies, including private rehearsal and verified backup coverage for supported
   128	2026.9.2 package updates.
   129	
   130	### Incremental canonical-session validation
   131	
   132	The [accepted storage design](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/149323)
   133	owns this projection's invalidation, certification, migration and rollback contract.
   134	
   135	Agent schema **21** adds `session_canonical_validation_pending`, a derived set
   136	of session keys requiring canonical validation. Required triggers mark node
   137	identity, JSON, validity and lineage changes, changes to retained-window
   138	associations, and main-key policy changes. Node deletion and renaming clean up
   139	the old key without depending on foreign-key enforcement. The table stores no
   140	permission grants or copied session payloads.
   141	
   142	The existing canonical validator certifies final rows before their markers are
   143	removed in the same transaction. Rollback restores the data and pending work
   144	together. A connection opened before migration still fires the new triggers;
   145	its older validity flag cannot clear the pending marker. Read-only inspection
   146	does not create or repair the projection, and missing or drifted required
   147	definitions do not count as a clean database.
   148	
   149	The physical database owner requires a full canonical proof on first admission;
   150	an imported empty pending table is not sufficient. The existing mutation worker
   151	seeds all keys and validates bounded batches before publishing that proof.
   152	Ordinary connection close and eviction preserve it, while physical replacement,
   153	registry invalidation and native deserialization revoke it. Read-only callers
   154	without an admitted proof retain full validation and never create a writer.
   155	Each native reader keeps the existing admission contract for its current
   156	main-key policy and physical owner. Already-admitted metadata readers retain
   157	their established raw-row parser behavior; a fresh reader, policy change or
   158	owner replacement must cross admission again. Pending keys make that admission
   159	incremental without caching session identity or permission results.
   160	
   161	Typed worker reads can continue an existing committed reader admission for one
   162	retained request. The continuation expires with its source admission or connection
   163	and remains bound to the physical file, main-key policy, and readiness. It cannot
   164	admit an unrelated pooled read or replace strict validation when that proof is
   165	missing, transactional, changed, or revoked.
   166	
   167	Gateway startup reuses valid canonical receipts for the same physical generation;
   168	they do not replace integrity checks. Stores needing fresh proof are certified up
   169	to two at a time, using the same disk-work bound as database preflight. Two
   170	execution workers serve separate
   171	per-database tasks. Each task retains its worker across validation batches, then
   172	closes the exact database and lease under the parent's coordinated close request
   173	before downstream maintenance or another task can proceed. Uncertain native
   174	termination retains writer admission and cleanup custody. A refusal stops new
   175	admissions and drains active work before startup fails; the startup owner joins
   176	its execution workers before returning. Ordinary archive work keeps its global
   177	FIFO; certification retains per-database write ordering and fresh physical-owner
   178	checks.
   179	
   180	Transcript-index reconciliation shares one worker across agent databases, including
   181	repairs scheduled by dashboard title reads. Each task retains its own message
   182	channel, source snapshot, and deletion lease. Successful reuse follows read-handle
   183	close, parent write settlement, and exact lease release. After a native worker
   184	failure, recovery joins termination and accepted parent writes before releasing
   185	the failed task's lease. Final Gateway shutdown closes admission, drains accepted
   186	repairs and lease recovery, then joins worker exit before shared-state retirement.
   187	
   188	The 20-to-21 migration installs the table and triggers and marks every existing
   189	node pending without parsing, repairing or certifying session contents. Both
   190	schema version markers advance in the same maintenance transaction. Earlier
   191	supported schemas retain their existing prerequisite migrations. Preserve
   192	malformed rows for Doctor and keep writers stopped if migration is interrupted.
   193	
   194	Older builds refuse schema 21 and do not recognize its triggers. Take and verify
   195	a WAL-aware backup before migration. Rollback restores that backup with its
   196	matching build; removing the derived objects or lowering the version markers
   197	does not provide a supported lossless downgrade. Updates driven by 2026.9.2
   198	require the candidate's private rehearsal and verified recovery-backup path;
   199	see [older updaters](/reference/database-schemas/versioning#schema-bumps-and-older-updaters)
   200	for supported migration and refusal conditions.
   201	
   202	### Cold transcript storage
   203	
   204	Agent schema **20** adds `session_transcript_cold_archives`. Session windows
   205	remain the identity owner; each cold row records the transcript generation,
   206	immutable archive name, compressed SHA-256, event and byte counts, last
   207	sequence, and archive time. Archives on disk use `storage: "file"` with no
   208	database blob. Supported backups embed verified compressed bytes in their
   209	private copies as `storage: "sqlite"`. Existing reset/deletion archives keep
   210	their separate table and behavior.
   211	
   212	The schema bump protects history semantics: an older reader would mistake
   213	extracted event rows for an empty transcript even though it could ignore the
   214	new table. Both `PRAGMA user_version` and `schema_meta.schema_version` advance
   215	through the existing migration owner. The supported updater runs the target
   216	build's Doctor phase under maintenance authority; ordinary active readers
   217	must not perform this migration. Migration creates the schema without
   218	extracting any transcripts. Extraction is disabled until
   219	`session.maintenance.coldStorage.enabled` is set.
   220	
   221	Take and verify a backup before upgrading. If migration fails, keep writers
   222	stopped and finish Doctor with the compatible build before restarting. The
   223	2026.9.2 updater follows the candidate's private rehearsal and verified
   224	recovery-backup path; see
   225	[older updaters](/reference/database-schemas/versioning#schema-bumps-and-older-updaters)
   226	for supported migration and refusal conditions.
   227	Older builds refuse schema 20. Rollback requires the pre-upgrade backup and
   228	its matching build; do not lower version markers or drop the cold archive
   229	table. Restoring cold events alone does not make the newer schema a supported
   230	downgrade.
   231	
   232	After an interrupted update, the agent database maintenance lease can remain
   233	valid for up to 60 seconds. If Doctor reports a maintenance lease timeout,
   234	keep writers stopped, allow that lease to expire, then run
   235	[`openclaw update repair`](/cli/update/repair-and-recovery#update-repair)
   236	from the compatible installation. The package may already have been replaced
   237	even when the schema transaction rolled back. Do not delete lease records or
   238	change schema markers to bypass recovery.
   239	
   240	See [cold transcript storage](/reference/session-management-compaction/maintenance#cold-transcript-storage)
   241	for retention, missing-file recovery, and physical space reclamation, and
   242	[cold transcript backups](/install/backups#cold-transcript-backups) for portable
   243	restoration without the source archive directory.
   244	
   245	### Creator namespace migration
   246	
   247	Agent schema **19** and shared-state schema **14** add a source discriminator to human creator actors in the existing session and cron JSON records. No table, sidecar, or separate identity ledger is added. The session node remains the immutable creator owner; mutable owner assignments and explicit sharing grants are unchanged.
   248	
   249	Historical human creators stamped directly by `operator` or `run` creation become `profile`; channel creation becomes `channel`. Origin-losing cron, inherited spawn or Talk, legacy `createdBy`, and missing-source history remain `unknown`. The migration preserves IDs, attribution, creation times, content, and existing sandbox restrictions. A UUID, profile lookup, participant, current route, or required sandbox never supplies missing creator authority. Recovery from incomplete physical projections also produces unknown human attribution.
   250	
   251	Before upgrading, stop the Gateway and all other writers, then [create and verify a WAL-aware backup](/cli/backup). Run `openclaw doctor --fix` with the new build. The agent migration retains the stopped-writer maintenance gate and runs after the schema-18 participant migration, without rebuilding already migrated participant rows. Canonical data and both schema markers commit in the owning database transaction. Shared-state and agent databases are separate transactions; if one fails, keep writers stopped and rerun Doctor before starting the Gateway.
   252	
   253	Older builds refuse the new versions. For rollback, stop all writers and restore the verified pre-upgrade backups with their matching older build. Do not decrement either schema marker: an older writer cannot maintain the creator-source contract. Unknown historical provenance is irrecoverable from the stored ID alone. Administrators retain sharing management access; assigning responsibility does not restore an implicit creator grant.
   254	
   255	Required sandbox resources keep their existing keys for proven profile creators. Channel and unknown creators instead use canonical-session isolation, with no new persisted principal field. Their old ambiguous resources are left untouched by migration, not automatically adopted or copied; operators must recover needed files explicitly before ordinary retention or cleanup. See [sandbox scope and recovery](/gateway/sandboxing#modes-scope-and-backend).
   256	
   257	### Participant identity migration
   258	
   259	Agent schema 18 rebuilds `session_participants` with the unique key `(session_key, identity_namespace, actor_id)`. The raw actor ID remains separate from its namespace. This replaces the old `(session_key, actor_type, actor_id)` key; it is not a same-version additive change. Both schema markers advance together. No companion table or per-input ledger is added.
   260	
   261	Before upgrading existing data, take a verified, WAL-aware backup and stop the Gateway and other agent-database writers. Run `openclaw doctor --fix` with the new build. The migration uses the existing maintenance lease to reject active writers and fence new claims. Ordinary runtime opens refuse the old participant schema rather than migrating it behind active readers. Earlier structural and media migrations run in their historical order before participant convergence. Explicit Doctor repair exits nonzero if an existing configured, default-layout, or registered database still fails runtime schema readiness, including when a live writer or an unknown table dependency blocks this migration. Readiness uses the same target discovery as migration without registering, pruning, or creating stores. Archive migration warnings remain advisory when required database schemas are ready.
   262	
   263	Membership and recorded contribution aggregates survive. Historical profile timestamps are unknown because earlier source promotion could contaminate them even when a contribution count was present. Supported agent and channel-only observation times remain; an unresolved historical channel domain stays unresolved. Migration does not invent missing channel rows or inspect transcripts to reconstruct identities. New observations do not turn an unknown first input time into a claimed first-ever time.
   264	
   265	The rebuild, data copy, version markers, and foreign-key validation commit atomically. Unknown table shapes or database-local dependents are refused. A failed migration rolls back rather than leaving a partial replacement table. Older builds refuse schema 18; do not decrement either version marker or restore the old unique key. Downgrade recovery requires the verified pre-migration backup.
   266	
   267	Normal admission remains bounded at 32 identities. Same-store alias repair sums aggregates; retryable cross-store copies retain the larger recorded aggregate. Repairs preserve already-retained histories above the admission bound. Reset retains logical-session participation, while deletion removes it with the session node.
   268	---
   269	summary: "Shared state database schema versions, their changes, and their first releases"
   270	read_when:
   271	  - "Looking up which release first shipped a state schema version"
   272	  - "Reading the per-version notes for a recent state schema change"
   273	title: "State schema history"
   274	---
   275	
   276	## State schema history
   277	
   278	Doctor completes recognized schema-1 databases that predate the audit ledger before later workspace and agent checks run. Missing ownership metadata or a missing audit ledger at schema 2 or newer still prevents repair.
   279	
   280	| Version | Change                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | First release       |
   281	| ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |
   282	| 1       | Initial shared state database                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                   | `v2026.5.30-beta.1` |
   283	| 2       | Metadata-only message audit events ([#103903](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/103903))                                                                                                                                                                                                                                | `v2026.7.2-beta.1`  |
   284	| 3       | `STRICT` tables and schema-drift hardening ([#108663](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/108663))                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | `v2026.7.2-beta.2`  |
   285	| 4       | Session watch provenance replaces encoded sentinel rows                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Unreleased          |
   286	| 5       | Durable cloud-worker result references on pending workspace fences ([`7a7d6bb`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/7a7d6bb51f42bd896de2b8a4df2ee66f3dce0a21), [#110952](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/110952))                                                                                             | `v2026.7.2-beta.4`  |
   287	| 6       | Every committed shared-state table becomes part of the canonical runtime schema ([`509a5f0`](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/commit/509a5f03737642fec4a940e6d605887f7957ddc8), [#113473](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/113473))                                                                                | `v2026.7.2-beta.5`  |
   288	| 7       | Retired inferred-commitment storage removed                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     | Unreleased          |
   289	| 8       | Cloud-worker placement execution modes and mode-aware turn claims                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               | Unreleased          |
   290	| 9       | In-root agent database registry paths stored relative to the state directory                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                    | Unreleased          |
   291	| 10      | Six dead tables retired (agent_model_catalogs, android_notification_recent_packages, command_log_entries, diagnostic_stability_bundles, media_blobs, model_capability_cache)                                                                                                                                                    | Unreleased          |
   292	| 11      | Legacy skill curator lifecycle table and never-read proposal origin-run projection retired                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      | Unreleased          |
   293	| 12      | Thirteen singleton/cache tables retired; durable state folded into config_machine_state                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         | Unreleased          |
   294	| 13      | State consolidation: cron jobs and subagent runs become JSON-canonical (113 projection columns, five unused indexes removed); installed_plugin_index and shared auth-profile singletons fold into config_machine_state; workspace_attestations merges into workspace_setup_state; gateway origin device tokens become canonical | Unreleased          |
   295	| 14      | Source-qualified cron creator capture; historical human job creators remain unknown                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | Unreleased          |
   296	| 15      | Conversation bindings use exact target keys; redundant agent/session projections removed                                                                                                                                                                                                                                        | Unreleased          |
   297	| 16      | Skill Workshop ownership moves from workspace/provenance columns to per-agent directory containment                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | Unreleased          |
   298	| 17      | Prepared worker lifecycle facts and one-use node workspace bindings                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             | Unreleased          |
   299	| 18      | Original requesting authority retained with shared GitHub publication receipts                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                  | Unreleased          |
   300	| 19      | Durable original channel-owner authorization and revocation continuity                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          | Unreleased          |
   301	
   302	### State schema 19
   303	
   304	Schema 19 adds nullable `authorization_id TEXT` and
   305	`authorization_basis_json TEXT` to the existing `user_profile_identities` rows.
   306	The profile owner reuses an uninterrupted channel link's opaque UUID and records
   307	only its original access-policy grant reference (or JSON `null`). It does not
   308	copy channel identities into another store. A reference is versioned JSON
   309	`{ "version": 1, "id": "<uuid>" }`, not a credential: recovery resolves it through
   310	the profile read worker and rechecks current roles or identity scopes and the original plugin grant.
   311	Unknown versions, malformed references, missing rows, or corrupt grant facts do
   312	not authorize work. This schema supplies the owner contract; consumers must
   313	retain and revalidate their own effect authority.
   314	
   315	Profile authority mutations clear these fields in the same transaction as their
   316	role, alias, or ownership change. Unlinking removes the row. Restoring a role or
   317	link cannot restore a retired reference. The existing config machine-state store
   318	records the activated `gateway.roles` and `gateway.auth.identityScopes` policy
   319	snapshot under `operator.channelPolicy`. Identity-scope-only owners use the same
   320	reference contract; removing and restoring their configured scopes cannot revive
   321	a retired reference. Under the secrets activation lock, changes to either policy
   322	retire existing references durably before
   323	publishing the exact successor snapshot. Rollback uses the same operation with
   324	the merged target; publication failure reconciles the surviving active policy.
   325	No agent schema changes are involved.
   326	
   327	Startup and Doctor add the nullable columns without assigning authority to legacy
   328	rows; unused profile tables remain absent until first use. The canonical schema
   329	and the profile owner's lazy ensure share the table and index definitions.
   330	Both published schema markers normally advance to 19. During the existing
   331	[older-updater publication deferral](/reference/database-schemas/versioning#schema-bumps-and-older-updaters),
   332	content can be upgraded while older published markers remain. References cannot
   333	be issued or recovered until version 19 is published.
   334	
   335	Version 18 writers cannot preserve revocation history, so this is a versioned
   336	permission contract even though the added columns are nullable. Their normal
   337	worker admission checks foreign version changes under the state coordinator and
   338	refuses further writes, including deferred content newer than their support. Older readers also refuse schema 19.
   339	Create a verified, WAL-aware backup before upgrading. Downgrade requires restoring
   340	the matching pre-upgrade backup in a separate state directory; never lower the
   341	markers or remove authority columns. Restoring a backup loses later revocations
   342	and receipts and does not undo external effects; reconcile those with a compatible
   343	build before rollback.
   344	
   345	### State schema 18
   346	
   347	Schema 18 adds nullable `requester_authority_json TEXT` columns to
   348	`github_publication_session_lifecycles` and `github_repository_publication_requests`.
   349	Shared publication admission records its original requester, scope ceiling, and
   350	any required plugin grant identity and original alias-binding IDs in the existing request transaction. The
   351	snapshot survives deferral and restart; it does not replace current role, grant,
   352	session, or execution authority checks. Publisher selection, repository routing,
   353	human attribution, and receipt retention are unchanged.
   354	
   355	Startup and `openclaw doctor --fix` add the columns to existing tables without
   356	rebuilding or rewriting their rows. Historical values stay `NULL`: migration
   357	does not infer a requester from the publisher, session creator, or current
   358	assignee. Unproven pending requests cannot begin new effects. Terminal receipts
   359	remain readable, and observing an already-dispatched GitHub result does not
   360	authorize another operation. Unused publication tables remain absent until their
   361	normal first write, which creates the canonical schema.
   362	
   363	The profile schema owner adds nullable `binding_id TEXT` to the existing
   364	`user_profile_emails` table and initializes missing IDs in its own transaction.
   365	Each email-to-profile binding has an opaque UUID. Creation or an actual ownership
   366	change starts a new binding; same-owner refreshes retain it. Removing and later
   367	restoring an alias cannot restore its former ID. Publication snapshots retain
   368	only those original IDs, without copying email addresses or updating accepted
   369	receipt bytes. Initializing existing aliases does not backfill missing authority
   370	into historical publication requests.
   371	
   372	The publication column additions and version facts commit in one schema transaction; failure
   373	rolls them back together. Both published markers normally advance to 18. The
   374	existing [older-updater publication deferral](/reference/database-schemas/versioning#schema-bumps-and-older-updaters)
   375	can retain earlier published markers while recording applied content version 18.
   376	Reopening uses that content version and does not repeat migration.
   377	
   378	Older readers validate these optional tables exactly, so bare nullable columns
   379	do not make this a same-version addition. Builds supporting state schema 17 or
   380	earlier refuse schema 18. Stop older writers and create a verified, WAL-aware
   381	backup before upgrading. Rollback requires the matching build and pre-upgrade
   382	backup in a separate state directory, not lowered version markers or deleted
   383	authority fields. Keep the newer database and reconcile accepted GitHub effects
   384	with a compatible build before rollback: restoring a backup loses later local
   385	receipts and does not undo pushed commits or pull requests.
   386	
   387	### State schema 17
   388	
   389	Schema 17 adds the complete prepared-worker storage contract. The nullable
   390	`worker_environments` columns `preparation_key`, `preparation_demand_at_ms`,
   391	`preparation_expires_at_ms`, and `preparation_consumed_at_ms` form one constrained
   392	tuple for one-use capacity. The separate nullable `last_activated_at_ms` column
   393	stores successful activation time. Ready-pool admission writes the preparation
   394	tuple; consuming that capacity and assigning a session placement share one
   395	transaction. Successful placement activation records its demand time in the
   396	same transaction. Consumption survives failed attachment, placement retirement,
   397	and reopen, while terminal rows retain unexpired demand and uncertain cleanup.
   398	Migration leaves all five values `NULL` on existing workers and does not infer
   399	demand, activation, or unused capacity from historical rows.
   400	
   401	Dedicated nodes register fixed build paths in the first-use
   402	`node_worker_prepared_workspaces` table. It records the exact environment and
   403	completed `preparation_key`, a required `cache_key` identifying compatible
   404	project/runtime contents, and fixed workspace and HOME paths. Separate required
   405	`source_manifest_ref` and `prepared_manifest_ref` digests identify the clean Git
   406	baseline and completed setup tree. Initial binding verifies the completed tree;
   407	replay preserves the bound session's edits. Binding records the session and
   408	owner epoch once. Retirement keeps that binding until machine teardown, so
   409	retired paths cannot be claimed by another session.
   410	
   411	Registration creates the node-only table inside its existing synchronous write
   412	transaction. An aborted registration rolls back that DDL, and a later attempt
   413	can create it again. Ordinary database open and migration leave the table
   414	absent. Per-agent schemas and native companion tables do not change; native
   415	clients can continue validating and reading their existing owned tables at
   416	state schema 17 without performing migrations.
   417	
   418	Startup and `openclaw doctor --fix` apply the schema-16 Skill Workshop migration
   419	before the prepared-worker migration when opening a schema-15 database. A
   420	schema-16 database receives only the prepared-worker migration. The tuple
   421	constraint belongs to the last added column, so migration scans existing
   422	environments once and preserves the table instead of rebuilding it. Leases,
   423	credentials, placements, inference turns, unknown additive data, and uncertain
   424	cleanup remain intact. No provider operation runs in the migration transaction.
   425	
   426	Migration content and its version facts commit together; failure rolls back all
   427	database changes from that attempt. Both published markers normally advance to
   428	17 in that transaction. When an older updater still owns trailing ledger reads,
   429	the existing [version-publication deferral](/reference/database-schemas/versioning#schema-bumps-and-older-updaters)
   430	keeps the published markers at their earlier version and records applied content
   431	17 separately. Reopening does not repeat that content migration. Workshop
   432	directory relocation runs afterward, outside the schema transaction.
   433	
   434	Before upgrading, stop older writers and create a verified, WAL-aware backup.
   435	Builds supporting state schema 16 or earlier refuse the published schema 17.
   436	To roll back, complete cloud cleanup with a compatible build, stop all writers,
   437	then restore the pre-upgrade backup into a separate state directory. Restoring
   438	does not retain changes made after that backup. Do not lower either version
   439	marker or erase a consumed binding. Unresolved provider cleanup can continue
   440	incurring charges until deletion is confirmed.
   441	
   442	### State schema 16
   443	
   444	Schema 16 removes `workspace_dir` and `claim_released_time` from
   445	`skill_workshop_proposals`. It also removes `workspace_dir` and
   446	`idx_skill_workshop_collection_reviews_workspace_time` from collection review
   447	history and adds `owner_agent_id` plus its owner/time index. Proposal rows remain intact. A proposal whose claim a
   448	collection review had released becomes `stale` with a status reason, so the
   449	skill path it once created stays user-owned and Doctor never relocates it.
   450	
   451	Skill Workshop ownership is now the physical
   452	`<state-dir>/agents/<agentId>/agent/workshop-skills` directory. Startup and `openclaw doctor --fix`
   453	drop the retired columns and index in the shared schema transaction. Both then
   454	run the same migration to relocate applied legacy Workshop creates to the
   455	inferred owner agent and retarget eligible pending creates. Conflicts and ambiguous ownership become
   456	stale proposals and leave the legacy directories unchanged. Review history rows
   457	map to a unique owner agent when possible; otherwise the schema migration discards them as
   458	cache-class state.
   459	
   460	If a database reports schema 16 but still has recognized schema-15 Workshop
   461	columns, update preflight identifies the pending migration and Doctor runs it
   462	before rebuilding canonical indexes. Proposal-only legacy columns are handled
   463	the same way. The repair preserves attributable review rows and unrelated
   464	indexes; unrecognized column sets or dependencies on retired columns still
   465	refuse and roll back the transaction.
   466	
   467	Skill-only workspace relocation uses the existing `migration_runs` and
   468	`migration_sources` tables to save pre-move directory identity, file hashes,
   469	and the workspace attestation timestamp. After relocation, only matching
   470	attestation-only state is retired; setup state, path aliases, and newer
   471	attestations remain intact. Interrupted migrations reuse the saved pre-move
   472	facts rather than inferring them from an empty directory. Workspace reset
   473	removes pending workspace-scoped receipts. No additional schema version or
   474	table is required.
   475	
   476	### State schema 15
   477	
   478	Schema 15 removes `target_agent_id` and `target_session_id` from `current_conversation_bindings`. The target index uses the complete `target_session_key` and remains non-unique: several conversations may point at the same destination. This lets plugin-owned targets persist without inventing an OpenClaw agent owner. Channel/account isolation, plugin approvals, binding identifiers, target keys, JSON metadata, expiry, and detach behavior are unchanged.
   479	
   480	Startup and `openclaw doctor --fix` run the migration in the existing exclusive write transaction. They remove only the two projections and replace the target index, preserving all other row values. A dependent trigger, index, or failed schema check rolls the transaction back; migration does not discard an unknown dependency to force the upgrade. Column removal rewrites the binding table, so upgrade cost scales with its size.
   481	
   482	Stop older writers and create a verified, WAL-aware backup before upgrading. Builds supporting shared-state schema 14 or earlier refuse the migrated database. To return to an older build, restore that pre-upgrade backup into a separate state directory; do not lower the version markers or reconstruct an agent projection. See [Downgrade](/install/updating#downgrade) for the general recovery contract.
   483	
   484	### State schema 13
   485	
   486	Schema 13 makes `cron_jobs.job_json`, `cron_jobs.state_json`, and `subagent_runs.payload_json` the canonical records. Physical columns remain only where production queries, ordering, or runtime-only updates require them. Cron jobs shrink from 75 columns to 15, and subagent runs shrink from 59 columns to six. Migration preserves failure-destination fields explicitly configured as undefined by encoding them as JSON `null`; it also normalizes legacy run-status aliases into `state_json` before removing the redundant projections.
   487	
   488	Workspace attestations merge into `workspace_setup_state`. Migration preserves attestation timestamps and generated bootstrap hashes when older databases have no `workspace_path_aliases` table. Attestation-only workspaces retain a null path until the workspace is encountered again.
   489	
   490	The shared-state `auth_profile_stores` and `auth_profile_state` singletons move into `config_machine_state` under `authProfiles.store` and `authProfiles.state`; per-agent auth tables remain unchanged. Because these rows contain credentials, secret-redacted Git backups omit the `authProfiles.` machine-state prefix.
   491	
   492	### State schema 11
   493	
   494	Schema 11 removes the `skill_lifecycle` and `skill_workshop_proposal_origin_runs` tables. Archived-skill lifecycle state is discarded during the upgrade: previously archived Workshop skills return to the active collection, where weekly collection review judges them by content. The origin-run rows were a never-read projection; canonical proposal provenance stays in `skill_workshop_proposals.record_json`. Recorded skill usage and collection-review state are preserved.
   495	
   496	### State schema 9
   497	
   498	Schema 9 stores an `agent_databases.path` value relative to the state directory when the registered agent database is inside that directory. During migration, a foreign default-layout row is re-anchored to the in-root counterpart when that file exists. It is deleted only when the same agent already holds its in-root registration, because dual default-layout registrations cannot produce a valid combined session list. Otherwise, the absolute row is preserved, so genuine external registrations are never deleted. This keeps a copied state directory self-contained without dropping supported external database paths.
   499	---
   500	summary: "Where personal GitHub connections, personal model accounts, and Apple companion journals live"
   501	read_when:
