   503	  - "Understanding Apple companion delivery journals and their migration behaviour"
   504	title: "Per-person and companion storage"
   505	---
   506	
   507	## Personal GitHub connections and publication
   508	
   509	Personal GitHub connection state uses the existing `secret_store_entries` identity scope, with the canonical authenticated profile as `scope_id` and the fixed private name `github-connection`. It is not a generic identity-secret API or a profile preference. One bounded record owns selection, pending device authorization, and refresh recovery. Personal managed CLI credentials use a separate `credentials/github/personal/<opaque-profile-id>` directory, outside older system/agent cleanup roots.
   510	
   511	Personal publication uses the lazy, same-version `github_personal_publication_requests` table. It records the requesting profile, selected connection generation and account, immutable target/workspace snapshot, idempotency, and outcome; it contains no tokens. Reading status does not create the table. Existing system and agent requests remain in their original table.
   512	
   513	Local shared and personal publication records use the first-use `github_publication_session_lifecycles` companion table to bind each request to its admitted session lifecycle revision. The key is the publication kind and request ID; the binding commits in the same transaction as the request. An explicit `NULL` records that the session had no revision at admission. A missing binding cannot authorize unfinished publication and is never filled from the current session. Terminal receipt history remains readable.
   514	
   515	The companion table leaves both existing local request-table definitions and their receipt digests unchanged. State schema 18 adds `requester_authority_json` to this companion and to repository publication receipts so shared requests retain their original requesting authority. Historical rows stay unbound; terminal receipts remain readable. These optional tables require a version bump because shipped schema validators reject additional columns even when nullable. See [state schema 18](/reference/database-schemas/state-schema-history#state-schema-18) for migration and rollback.
   516	
   517	Older builds ignore both the personal request table and identity-scoped credential rows instead of executing a personal request as System. Re-upgrade still enforces original authorization expiry. Unfinished personal publication requires fresh confirmation by the same authenticated owner after a Gateway restart; remote-result reconciliation reuses the original request markers.
   518	
   519	Disconnect removes usable local credentials and retains a secret-free disconnected selection to fence stale work. Profile merges preserve target state, including an explicit disconnection; a source connection transfers only when the target has no state, with new selection authority. Credentials stranded by a profile merge performed on an older build require reconnect, not runtime adoption through aliases.
   520	
   521	Personal publication receipts remain for the logical session's lifetime. Archive/reset preserves receipts and invalidates incompatible unfinished work. An already-dispatched GitHub operation can still record its observed result, without gaining authority for another operation. Permanent session deletion fences execution and removes its personal receipts and lifecycle bindings. There is no timed idempotency expiry, and deleting local state does not undo an already-created GitHub commit or pull request.
   522	
   523	See the accepted [personal GitHub ownership and publication design](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/133590) and the operator-facing [GitHub connections guide](/concepts/user-model#github-connections).
   524	
   525	## Personal model accounts
   526	
   527	Personal model accounts use the existing `secret_store_entries` identity scope, keyed by the canonical Gateway profile. A versioned `model-accounts` record owns provider selections, while each `model-account:<profile-id>` record owns one inline OAuth or token credential and its usage state. Each record retains the existing 64 KiB secret-store limit; connecting more accounts or merging profiles does not combine credentials under one size limit. This adds no table, column, index, or schema version. Generic secret-list/read methods and profile preferences do not expose these records.
   528	
   529	The credential and its selected link commit in one synchronous transaction after the Gateway revalidates the initiating authorization. Runtime loads only an explicitly selected credential and routes refresh and usage updates to that same owner. Shared and agent-local auth saves exclude the reserved personal-profile namespace, including runtime snapshots and CLI mirrors.
   530	
   531	Unlink records an explicit disconnected selection and retains credentials used by existing session pins. A verified identity merge transfers only the live source's records, preserving the target's selections and disconnections while retaining old credential IDs for pinned sessions. Credentials stranded on an alias by an older build are not adopted at runtime. A compatible downgrade leaves private records outside the older shared-account pool; re-upgrade can use retained records, while accounts stranded by older identity merges need reconnecting.
   532	
   533	See [Per-person model accounts](/concepts/multi-user#per-person-model-accounts) for connection, cancellation, session billing, and unlink behavior.
   534	
   535	## Apple companion delivery journals
   536	
   537	Companion Watch chat has separate app-local storage. It does not change the
   538	Gateway control-plane or per-agent database schema, and `openclaw doctor`
   539	does not migrate it. Open the updated iPhone and Watch apps to use the new
   540	delivery protocol. See [Watch voice and chat](/platforms/ios#apple-watch-voice-and-chat)
   541	for delivery statuses and recovery.
   542	
   543	The iPhone's existing `client-state.sqlite` owns `watch_message_journal`.
   544	The named GRDB migration `client-state-watch-message-journal-v9` adds that table
   545	and a nullable `watch_route_generation TEXT` column to
   546	`gateway_routing_identity`. The generation changes after Forget and re-pairing;
   547	a late callback or queued command from the old pairing cannot become new work.
   548	Admission, accepted run identity and terminal receipt state share one journal
   549	owner, separate from the general chat outbox.
   550	The journal's nullable `command_fingerprint BLOB` stores SHA-256 of each
   551	admitted command's canonical bytes. Dismiss preserves this hash, so reusing an
   552	ID with changed content or submission time cannot return the original result
   553	after its command text is cleared. The hash expires with the row or is removed
   554	by Forget; legacy imports have no command fingerprint.
   555	The migration is registered by shared Apple client storage, so the Mac client
   556	also sees the additive schema; it does not process companion Watch delivery.
   557	
   558	The additive `client-state-watch-message-legacy-receipts-v1` migration creates
   559	`watch_message_legacy_imports`. It stores SHA-256 hashes of exact legacy command
   560	IDs and imported content, never the text or Gateway ID. A nullable content hash
   561	records the older app's ID-only recent-message suppression policy; it is not
   562	proof of a matching body or successful execution.
   563	
   564	Old Watch UserDefaults are decoded and reconciled in one SQLite transaction
   565	whenever the phone prepares its journal. Imported rows and their hash receipts
   566	commit together before cleanup checks that both source blobs are unchanged.
   567	This also recovers messages written by an older app after downgrade. Unprovable
   568	queued text becomes **Needs review**, never an automatic send. Conflicting IDs
   569	or unseen messages associated with a previously forgotten Gateway preserve the
   570	source and surface a recovery error instead of discarding or retargeting text.
   571	
   572	Imported text remains until explicit discard or Gateway Forget. Its hash-only
   573	receipt has no timed expiry and survives both actions, so an identical old
   574	snapshot cannot resurrect deleted text. This storage grows per legacy ID and is
   575	removed only by a full onboarding reset, which clears the old UserDefaults
   576	before deleting client state. New commands and their reply replay instead have
   577	an immutable 48-hour deadline. Dismiss hides a completed card without changing
   578	its receipt, acknowledgment state or deadline; active deliveries cannot be
   579	discarded or dismissed.
   580	Expired copies are pruned when delivery state is next used, including opening
   581	the phone's delivery list. An idle or suspended app does not promise immediate
   582	wall-clock erasure.
   583	
   584	The Watch owns its outbound commands and received results in its own SQLite
   585	journal. A 90-second speech timeout does not remove this delivery state or
   586	cancel the remote run. Both apps commit before issuing their application-level
   587	admission or terminal receipt. A permanent rejection is explicitly not an
   588	admission and creates no phone journal row. If dispatch became ambiguous before an accepted run was recorded,
   589	recovery reports uncertainty rather than automatically executing the message
   590	again. The phone retains its current WAL policy: this is app-termination
   591	recovery, not a claim of power-loss durability.
   592	
   593	Forget removes phone journal rows in the existing irreversible removal
   594	transaction, including rows imported without a routing parent. The phone first
   595	accounts for retained legacy source and refuses removal if that cannot be done
   596	safely. The additive
   597	schema leaves the old reader's explicit routing updates intact, and a deletion
   598	trigger keeps its Forget path effective after downgrade. An older app cannot
   599	offer the new receipt protocol. Do not remove migration markers or reset
   600	`client-state.sqlite` to downgrade: that file also contains other user-owned
   601	client state.
   602	
   603	The [accepted design](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/136617) records
   604	the schema, migration, ownership, retention and validation boundaries.
   605	---
   606	summary: "OpenClaw SQLite database locations, schema versions, integrity checks, and downgrade recovery"
