   708	summary: "How OpenClaw records schema versions, when a bump is required, and how updaters cross one"
   709	read_when:
   710	  - "Deciding whether a storage change needs a schema-version bump"
   711	  - "Diagnosing a refused update or a newer schema version error"
   712	title: "Versioning contract"
   713	---
   714	
   715	## Versioning contract
   716	
   717	Each database records its published schema in two places:
   718	
   719	- `PRAGMA user_version` is the SQLite schema version.
   720	- The primary `schema_meta` row records `role`, `agent_id`, `schema_version`, and `app_version`. `app_version` is the OpenClaw build that last wrote the schema metadata.
   721	
   722	OpenClaw applies forward-only migrations when it opens an older supported database. It refuses a database whose `user_version` is newer than the running build and reports a `newer schema version` error. The Gateway checks all registered databases before startup. [`openclaw update`](/cli/update) also refuses a package or source target whose declared schema support is older than an on-disk database. Known stable releases published before schema metadata was added are checked against their shipped schema-1 contract. Updates driven by the 2026.9.2 release line can temporarily defer publication of a shared-state schema version while the old updater finishes; see [Schema bumps and older updaters](#schema-bumps-and-older-updaters).
   723	
   724	When Gateway startup encounters a newer database schema, it exits with status 78 so the generated systemd service does not restart it repeatedly. On macOS, it also parks its managed LaunchAgent to stop `KeepAlive` retries. This applies to failures during CLI bootstrap as well as server startup and does not depend on the database-backed crash counter. Start the Gateway with a build that supports the existing schemas. The older install cannot repair them with `doctor --fix`; run Doctor from the compatible install if further migration is required, then restart through the service or deployment owner.
   725	
   726	Changes may stay at the same schema version only when downgraded readers remain safe. New tables qualify because older builds ignore them. An explicitly compatible column on an existing table qualifies only when its declaration is exactly one bare nullable SQLite `STRICT` datatype: `ANY`, `BLOB`, `INT`, `INTEGER`, `REAL`, or `TEXT`. The declaration cannot have a default, `NOT NULL`, a primary or unique key, a check, a reference, a collation, a generated expression, or another suffix. Constrained existing-table additions require a schema-version bump or a companion table instead.
   727	
   728	Matching numeric versions are necessary but not sufficient. A release can add a lazy or startup-repairable table, column, index, or trigger without advancing `user_version`, so two databases at the same version can still have different shapes. OpenClaw validates the canonical table definitions, constraints, indexes, triggers, virtual tables, and table options owned by the running release.
   729	
   730	Admitted agent and cached shared-state handles retain their schema version and
   731	table facts. The handle owner revokes these facts after local DDL or transaction
   732	rollback. A fresh `PRAGMA data_version` probe observes foreign commits on the next
   733	unpinned read, even within the same event-loop turn. On a foreign commit, the owner
   734	compares `schema_version` and `user_version` in one pinned snapshot and retains
   735	facts and their revision when both are unchanged. Data-only commits therefore
   736	avoid table and column scans while version-only changes still trigger refusal
   737	when the stored version is newer than the running build. Actual
   738	SQLite read snapshots retain their view until they end; the next read then observes
   739	committed changes. Canonical session validation uses the same schema revision.
   740	Unchanged versions reuse parsed schema facts and prepared statements without
   741	repeating schema scans. Migration and snapshot consistency checks remain fresh reads.
   742	This changes no stored schema, migration, durability, or update behavior.
   743	
   744	The nullable requester-authority columns on GitHub publication lifecycle and
   745	repository receipts require [state schema 18](/reference/database-schemas/state-schema-history#state-schema-18).
   746	Shipped readers validate these optional tables exactly and reject additional
   747	columns even when bare and nullable. Migration preserves historical rows with
   748	unknown requester authority; the version bump also prevents older publishers
   749	from reopening requests without the new authority checks.
   750	
   751	Session label lookups use a nonunique partial index on
   752	`session_nodes(label, session_key)` for non-null labels, without changing agent
   753	schema 20. The existing writable schema owner installs and repairs the index;
   754	read-only startup accepts its absence until that owner opens the database. A
   755	present but noncanonical definition still fails strict offline validation;
   756	Gateway startup admits canonical index repairs to the same writable schema owner
   757	before readiness and logs the rebuilt indexes and elapsed time. Missing tables
   758	and incompatible column definitions remain refusals. Canonical
   759	session JSON, label uniqueness checks, and retention remain unchanged. Older
   760	same-version readers can ignore the extra index, so binary rollback leaves it
   761	intact. The accepted design is recorded in the
   762	[session label index decision](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/147837#issuecomment-5658783288).
   763	
   764	Task and maintenance lookups add nonunique indexes without changing state schema
   765	17 or agent schema 21: task requester sessions, worker placements by environment,
   766	and session entries whose
   767	validity is not yet confirmed. Existing task matching, stored rows, retention,
   768	and ownership checks are unchanged. Read-only admission accepts missing indexes;
   769	the canonical writable schema owner installs or repairs them. Initial construction
   770	uses time and temporary disk proportional to the affected tables, and subsequent
   771	writes maintain the added indexes. Older same-version readers can ignore them,
   772	so binary rollback preserves both rows and indexes. See the
   773	[accepted index design](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/153533).
   774	
   775	Task execution ownership uses three bare nullable columns on `task_runs`:
   776	`execution_owner_host TEXT`, `execution_owner_pid INTEGER`, and
   777	`execution_owner_start_identity INTEGER`. The first task write ensures them
   778	idempotently; read-only inspection does not add them. They are declared in the
   779	canonical schema and included in the existing additive migration path, without
   780	changing the schema version. Older readers ignore these columns. Legacy rows
   781	remain unknown until an execution owner explicitly records its identity; restore
   782	never guesses their owner. Confirmed process-exit settlement uses existing task
   783	terminal fields and retention rules. Downgrading code does not undo a terminal
   784	outcome already recorded by restore.
   785	
   786	Node worker recovery uses the private `node_worker_launch_cleanup` companion
   787	table in the existing launch journal. The launch owner adds it on first use and
   788	records the selected process-group or owned-anchor transport in `cleanup_mode`,
   789	in the same transaction as the worker identity, before allowing execution. An owned anchor
   790	can record `lineage_settled = 1` only for its exact current running identity after its root exits
   791	and its inherited lineage reaches positive EOF. Recovery also verifies that the
   792	recorded process group has disappeared before releasing capacity. A missing or
   793	ambiguous lineage result remains unknown; an empty anchor group alone cannot
   794	prove that descendants in other groups have stopped.
   795	
   796	The [node recovery repair](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/149158)
   797	keeps the journal as the sole durable owner. Cleanup records contain no launch
   798	descriptor or credentials, do not enter public receipts, and share the launch's
   799	existing 24-hour terminal receipt retention through a cascading foreign key.
   800	The schema version stays unchanged; older readers ignore the new companion
   801	table without changing their launch-table contract. Missing cleanup records preserve the
   802	released `2026.9.4` process-group contract without backfilling guessed identities.
   803	Untagged intermediate builds that used unmarked anchors must drain their workers
   804	on that original build before replacement. Active modern workers must also drain
   805	before downgrade or rollback to an older writer, which cannot interpret anchor
   806	lineage completion.
   807	
   808	Notification ownership uses bare nullable `TEXT` columns at the same schema
   809	version: `session_watch_cursors.watcher_store_path`,
   810	`subagent_runs.requester_store_path`, and `subagent_runs.controller_store_path`.
   811	Their writers ensure them idempotently on first use; reads do not install them.
   812	Older readers ignore the columns. NULL remains unknown, so Gateway notification
   813	delivery does not assign historical records to a current parent by key alone.
   814	
   815	Cron standing-grant definition generations use three bare nullable projections on
   816	`cron_jobs`: `grant_definition_revision`, `grant_definition_generation`, and
   817	`grant_definition_updated_at`. The canonical job remains `job_json`. Current
   818	writers update the projections atomically with it, advance the generation for a
   819	substantive definition change (including edit-and-restore), and preserve the
   820	generation across disable and re-enable.
   821	
   822	The released `operator_approval_standing_grants` table keeps its exact shape. A
   823	first-use companion table, `operator_approval_standing_grant_generations`, binds
   824	each newly minted grant to its job generation and cascades with the grant. Older
   825	same-version readers ignore the companion and the bare nullable job columns, so
   826	they can reopen the database. After re-upgrade, a grant without a companion row
   827	is treated as legacy and requires approval again; it is never assigned a
   828	generation retroactively. A job recreation advances past retained companion
   829	generations, including when an older writer deleted the job row.
   830	
   831	An older writer does not maintain these projections. Its edits make the
   832	projection stale, so a current reader fails closed after re-upgrade. While the
   833	older build is running it cannot enforce generation binding, and changes that
   834	preserve every observable job value and timestamp cannot be reconstructed later.
   835	No backfill or schema-version bump is required. The accepted design and rollback
   836	contract are recorded in [#142153](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/142153).
   837	
   838	Retained ACP imports use the same-version additive-column exception for the bare
   839	nullable `session_nodes.legacy_acp_migration_json TEXT` column. Legacy session
   840	import ensures it on first use and records exact source-component provenance;
   841	ordinary session edits preserve it, and canonical-key repairs carry it with the
   842	session. Canonical ACP initialization or closure consumes those components in
   843	the existing shared-state migration ledger, atomically with the ACP mutation.
   844	A later Doctor retry reads that completion fact instead of treating an absent
   845	ACP row as permission to restore legacy metadata. Missing provenance remains
   846	unknown; readers do not create the column or reconstruct it from legacy files.
   847	The column follows its session's lifetime, while completed receipts retain the
   848	existing migration-ledger lifecycle. No schema-version bump is required.
   849	
   850	Older same-version readers can ignore the nullable column and open the database.
   851	Older ACP writers do not record this supersession; complete pending migrations
   852	before returning to an older writer when that protection is needed.
   853	
   854	[Cold transcript storage](/reference/database-schemas/agent-schema-history#cold-transcript-storage)
   855	requires agent schema 20 even though it adds a companion table. Older readers
   856	would interpret extracted transcript rows as missing history and cannot safely
   857	ignore the new representation. The supported updater's Doctor phase performs
   858	the schema migration; changing the cold-storage age setting afterward needs no
   859	Gateway restart. These are separate operations: live configuration reload does
   860	not authorize an active schema migration.
   861	
   862	Agent schema 21 makes the canonical-validation pending table and its node,
   863	window and main-key invalidation triggers required. This needs a version bump:
   864	older schema inspectors reject unexpected triggers on canonical tables. The
   865	maintenance migration marks existing nodes pending without rewriting their
   866	contents; readiness and Doctor own validation. Already-open older connections
   867	leave pending markers when they change canonical inputs. Reopening with older
   868	code is refused. Rollback uses the verified pre-migration backup and matching
   869	build, not marker changes or removal of the derived table alone. See
   870	[incremental canonical-session validation](/reference/database-schemas/agent-schema-history#incremental-canonical-session-validation).
   871	
   872	Agent schema 22 introduced exact transcript FTS row ownership with a nullable
   873	completeness count and lazy backfill. Schema 23 accepts that deployed shape as
   874	well as schema 21. It rebuilds the ownership map from existing FTS content,
   875	preserves pending reconciliation, and retires the old completeness counter.
   876	
   877	Agent schema 23 changes existing payload representations: transcript events can
   878	use Zstd BLOBs, memory embeddings use Float64 BLOBs, and memory full-text
   879	maintenance uses stable integer chunk identities. Older writers cannot preserve
   880	these contracts, so this requires a bump despite retaining logical event and
   881	chunk IDs. Shared-state schema remains 17. The usage-rollup cache format changes
   882	with this migration but is independently rebuildable. See
   883	[compact agent payload storage](/reference/database-schemas/agent-schema-history#compact-agent-payload-storage)
   884	for conversion, runtime requirements, and recovery.
   885	
   886	Agent schema 19 records collected input consumption in the nullable
   887	`session_pending_inputs.consumed_event_id TEXT` column. Doctor and the feature's
   888	first-use ensure add it when needed; the schema version stays 19. The column
   889	shipped in 2026.8.2 ([#133457](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/133457)),
   890	so the supported beta upgrade runs Doctor from 2026.8.2 or newer. Intermediate builds that
   891	already validate the optional pending-input table may reject the added column
   892	despite sharing version 19. Consumed source receipts remain until their session
   893	window is deleted, so rewriting a transcript cannot make an old input runnable again.
   894	
   895	Cron run receipts use the optional `cron_run_trigger_state_retirements` companion
   896	without changing state schema 17 or the released receipt table's shape. Its only
   897	column is `receipt_id`, a primary key referencing the existing receipt with
   898	`ON DELETE CASCADE`. A committed condition, script-payload, or shared-state edit
   899	creates a retirement row in the same transaction as the job edit. This includes
   900	an exact receipt already closed by an agent-owner edit but still awaiting run
   901	reconciliation. Normal completion and restart recovery preserve the replacement's
   902	state while retaining the old run's history. The first eligible edit creates the
   903	table; queued edits do not retire a future evaluation. The job's private runtime
   904	state retains the exact running receipt ID until scheduler reconciliation, including
   905	when an agent-owner edit closes the receipt first. Recovery and later state edits
   906	use that association even when run timestamps collide. Receipt pruning preserves
   907	that pending receipt; ordinary history retains its existing 64-receipt bound and
   908	deletes retirement rows with their receipts.
   909	
   910	Rows written before this association was recorded retain their legacy recovery
   911	fallback. The association adds no SQL table, column, or schema version. Current
   912	builds omit it from public job state and the public state-patch schema.
   913	
   914	A missing table or row means no recorded retirement; earlier edits cannot be
   915	reconstructed from the final job definition. Older compatible readers ignore the
   916	companion but do not enforce this protection. To preserve edited watcher state,
   917	complete active runs and pending scheduler reconciliation on the current build
   918	before downgrading. A terminal task or receipt can still leave job state
   919	unreconciled.
   920	
   921	Scheduling edits made while a run awaits reconciliation record a private
   922	`runningScheduleChangeId` in the existing job runtime state, in the same
   923	transaction as the edit. The fresh value distinguishes successive committed
   924	edits even when a passive editor's snapshot spans two runs. Completion and
   925	recovery preserve the edited scheduling state; a new run and pending-run cleanup
   926	clear the marker. This adds no table, column, or public job field.
   927	
   928	Pending runs without this marker retain their previous recovery behavior.
   929	Edits acknowledged by older builds cannot be reconstructed reliably from
   930	timestamps or the final schedule. New edits to those pending jobs record the
   931	marker normally. Older compatible readers ignore it; finish pending runs before
   932	downgrading if their edited cadence must be preserved.
   933	
   934	Worker preparation uses the same-version rule for the bare nullable
   935	`worker_environments.preparation_purpose TEXT` column in the shared state
   936	database. Shared state database startup repair adds it without changing state schema 17.
   937	New admissions write `reserve` or `build`; existing preparation rows retain
   938	`NULL` and read as `reserve`, without backfilling demand or changing expiry.
   939	Older readers ignore the column and apply their existing reserve policy to all
   940	prepared workers; stop pending builds before downgrading if they must complete.
   941	Reopening preserves purpose, consumption, demand, and cleanup ownership.
   942	
   943	The placement-move table uses this same-version rule for its bare nullable
   944	`abandon_source INTEGER`, `target_machine_class TEXT`, and `target_os TEXT`
   945	columns. The feature ensures these columns only on first move use; database
   946	startup does not add them, and the schema version remains unchanged.
   947	`target_machine_class` and `target_os` retain explicit profile-target overrides;
   948	`NULL` means no override. For `abandon_source`, `NULL` means ordinary
   949	reconcile-first movement; `1` records the operator's explicit offline-device
   950	abandonment decision so restart recovery cannot accidentally resume remote
   951	reconciliation. Older readers ignore the added columns and can reopen the same
   952	database safely; they do not implement the newer operating-system override.
   953	
   954	Conversation associations use the same rule for the nullable bare
   955	`route_context_json TEXT` column. The database-open repair ensures the column
   956	for updated binaries. Older readers ignore it and can reopen and update the
   957	same database safely; their association update invalidates context captured by
   958	a newer writer so it cannot be replayed after re-upgrade.
   959	
   960	Conversation progress continuations reuse the agent database's `cache_entries`
   961	table with scope `conversation-progress` and the delivery operation ID as the key.
   962	No table, column, schema-version change, or migration is required. A missing cache
   963	entry means no retained presentation; older receipts are not backfilled.
   964	
   965	The receipt owns the known platform message identity and delivery status.
   966	Adoption records that evidence and its bounded, data-only prepared snapshot in
   967	one guarded transaction. Later updates write only the snapshot cache, leaving
   968	the receipt unchanged: desired presentation is not proof that a platform edit
   969	was delivered or that work completed. Snapshots are limited to 64 KiB of JSON,
   970	4,096 characters per string, 128 rolling lines, and 64 checklist steps or prepared
   971	blocks. Invalid optional snapshots are ignored without hiding delivery evidence.
   972	
   973	Reopening restores cached presentation only under the existing task and
   974	requester checks; the snapshot never grants authority. Older builds ignore the
   975	cache scope and cannot resume the newer presentation flow. Canonical session
   976	repair carries snapshots with their receipt identities. The existing session
   977	delivery cleanup removes matching snapshot keys with their receipts, with no new
   978	expiry policy, cleanup loop, or completion owner.
   979	
   980	Transcript context eligibility uses a bare nullable
   981	`session_transcript_active_events.context_eligible INTEGER` column without
   982	changing agent schema 18. Database open installs the column and a non-unique
   983	partial index of unclassified rows. `1` includes an entry in bounded context
   984	acquisition, `0` excludes display-only activity, and `NULL` means the projection
   985	still needs reconciliation. Bootstrap control markers remain eligible; history
   986	counts, positions, and cursors do not change. Raw transcript JSON stays canonical.
   987	
   988	Older same-version writers can append or rebuild without supplying eligibility.
   989	The existing transcript reconciler detects their `NULL` rows even when its
   990	sequence watermark is current, then rebuilds from raw events before publishing
   991	readiness. Readers return a retryable projection-unavailable result while this
   992	work is pending; they do not parse every payload or guess eligibility. Initial
   993	index creation scans projection metadata once, and startup awaits reconciliation
   994	with off-thread parsing and bounded write chunks. Total rebuild cost remains
   995	proportional to history. Rewrites invalidate or rebuild the projection in their
   996	own transaction, and transcript deletion removes its eligibility rows. Downgrade
   997	leaves the additive column and index intact; re-upgrade reconciles unknown rows.
   998	
   999	Multi-account person profiles add the bare nullable
  1000	`user_profiles.primary_github_account_id INTEGER` column on first profile use,
  1001	without changing the shared-state schema version. Existing single-account profiles
  1002	have an unambiguous primary; explicit merges retain all verified account rows and
  1003	keep the target primary. This deliberately accepts a downgrade limitation:
  1004	older single-account writers can discard secondary account links or split a linked
  1005	person again. Re-upgrading cannot reconstruct discarded links. Keep a backup
  1006	before downgrading, and explicitly relink affected profiles after upgrading.
  1007	The version number does not certify preservation of multi-account relationships.
  1008	
  1009	User profiles use the same rule for the nullable bare `user_profiles.role TEXT`
  1010	column in state schema 9. Operator-role assignment lazily ensures the column on
  1011	first use. Older readers ignore the column and can reopen the same database
  1012	safely.
  1013	
  1014	Web Push subscription ownership uses the same rule for nullable bare
  1015	`web_push_subscriptions.device_id TEXT`, `user_profile_id TEXT`, and
  1016	`preferences_json TEXT` columns. Web Push lazily ensures all three columns on
  1017	first use. Existing rows remain unbound and test-only until the browser
  1018	reconnects; older readers ignore the columns and continue reading or updating
  1019	the endpoint and key fields safely.
  1020	
  1021	Approval-notification cleanup uses the same-version additive
  1022	`web_push_approval_deliveries` table. It records the approval/subscription
  1023	identifiers plus the request-time device/profile binding for notifications that
  1024	may have reached a browser. A terminal or restarted Gateway sends only when the
  1025	current subscription still has that binding. The table is lazily created on
  1026	first use, rows cascade away with their approval or subscription, and older
  1027	readers ignore it safely.
  1028	
  1029	Installing OpenClaw manually through npm bypasses the updater guard. Database open checks still refuse an incompatible build.
  1030	
  1031	Structured [Goal controls](/tools/goal#gateway-requests-and-retries) use a lazy
  1032	per-agent `session_goal_operations` table without changing the schema version.
  1033	Goal start/resume commits the Goal transition, input turn, run lifecycle, and
  1034	operation receipt in one transaction. Management operations commit the Goal
  1035	transition and receipt together. Older readers ignore the added table.
  1036	Receipts survive Goal clear and session reset/deletion until their 24-hour
  1037	validity expires; later Goal writes prune expired rows. They retain the
  1038	original result and a keyed request fingerprint, not a second raw request.
  1039	There is no backfill or configuration switch. Downgrading preserves the table
  1040	but disables the new structured controls; upgrading can read retained receipts.
  1041	
  1042	### Schema bumps and older updaters
  1043	
  1044	OpenClaw 2026.9.2 introduced the update ledger but reopens it with old code after
  1045	running the target's Doctor, including a final read after recording its terminal
  1046	outcome. The shared state database runner lets this updater finish by applying
  1047	migration content first and publishing the new schema version later. This rule
  1048	applies to every writable open, including Doctor, the restarted Gateway, and
  1049	other CLI processes.
  1050	
  1051	The runner records the applied content version in the existing
  1052	`config_machine_state` key `state.schema.contentVersion`. While publication is
  1053	deferred, new code uses that content version, and both `PRAGMA user_version` and
  1054	`schema_meta.schema_version` retain the previous published version. Content and
  1055	its marker commit together. Reopening skips migration steps already covered by
  1056	the marker, including the schema-16 Skill Workshop rebuild; it does not infer
  1057	completion from table shape or repeat the rebuild. This requires no new table,
  1058	configuration option, or environment override.
  1059	
  1060	Current content is ready for readers even while its version is unpublished.
  1061	Ordinary CLI commands can run alongside the Gateway throughout this window;
  1062	publication alone does not trigger schema repair or require stopping the Gateway.
  1063	
  1064	A subsequent update can run during this window. Its migration verification and
  1065	rollback checks compare applied content versions from private database snapshots.
  1066	Publishing already-applied content is not another migration; applying new content
  1067	still blocks rollback even when the published number has not changed. Managed
  1068	service stop, activation, and Doctor maintenance keep their normal ownership rules.
  1069	
  1070	Publication waits until **every** update row whose `before.version` identifies
  1071	the 2026.9.2 release line meets its applicable condition:
  1072	
  1073	- A terminal row's `finished_at_ms` is at least five minutes old.
  1074	- A running row's `updated_at_ms` is more than 30 minutes old. The runner treats
  1075	  that driver as abandoned for publication purposes; it does not rewrite the
  1076	  run's outcome.
  1077	
  1078	A missing ledger or no affected rows permits immediate publication. Deadlines
  1079	come from the rows' timestamps, never the observing process's start time. The
  1080	new Gateway's ledger watcher schedules publication at the applicable deadline
  1081	without jitter. Publication holds the Gateway lifecycle fence: the owning Gateway
  1082	can publish, and a later writable open can publish when no Gateway owns the state
  1083	directory. Other processes silently leave publication to that owner.
  1084	Publication rereads the content marker and all affected rows inside one
  1085	synchronous write transaction before advancing both published schema markers.
  1086	A new or refreshed running row blocks publication again. Restarting the Gateway
  1087	does not shorten or restart the grace period.
  1088	
  1089	The five-minute grace accommodates 2026.9.2's trailing ledger reads; that release
  1090	records no driver process identity that would prove those reads have finished.
  1091	An old CLI blocked for more than five minutes after committing its terminal row,
  1092	for example on a stalled stdout pipe, can still fail its final render after
  1093	publication. By then the package swap, any requested service restart, and terminal ledger
  1094	outcome are complete. Downgrade protection for the 2026.9.2 line is delayed by the
  1095	same grace, or by the 30-minute abandoned-driver bound. The retained version is
  1096	not permission to run older code against migrated feature tables. Do not
  1097	manually lower either version marker or delete the content marker.
  1098	
  1099	Update-time Doctor checks shared and registered agent databases before other
  1100	repairs. A state-only migration proceeds with deferred publication and reports
  1101	`schema content applied; version publication deferred until update run <id> finishes`.
  1102	Publication still observes the five-minute grace after that run finishes.
  1103	Agent schema versions are not deferred or relabeled. For a supported 2026.9.2
  1104	package update, the early Doctor runs schema repair on private database copies
  1105	while the old driver can still roll back its package installation. It reports
  1106	success only after the private Doctor and its children settle successfully;
  1107	live agent databases remain unchanged. Known pending agent databases without a
  1108	registered canonical backup owner still refuse during this rollback window.
  1109	
  1110	After the shipped driver commits the package and enters its fresh post-core
  1111	phase, the current updater delegates Doctor under its executor and maintenance
  1112	ownership. Doctor creates and verifies a retained recovery archive covering every
  1113	pending agent database before normal live migration. Coverage requires matching
  1114	agent owners and physical file identities, including registered custom paths;
  1115	opaque archived bytes are not a verified SQLite snapshot. Authority and live
  1116	file identities are checked again after backup work and at the versioned schema
  1117	write and commit boundaries. Doctor reports the retained
  1118	archive path; it does not automatically restore that archive on a later failure.
  1119	
  1120	Doctor keeps the typed `update-schema-bump-unfenced` refusal when the phase or
  1121	current owner cannot be verified, recoverable backup coverage is missing, or the
  1122	required `config_machine_state` table is absent. Private rehearsal and backup
  1123	failures leave live agent schemas unchanged. A failed content transaction rolls
  1124	back. The refusal includes the affected database versions, driving updater
  1125	version, and [manual update commands](/install/updating#updating-from-2026.9.2-across-a-schema-bump).
  1126	Package rollback cannot reverse a migration that already happened; recovery
  1127	after live migration requires the verified backup and a matching build.
  1128	
  1129	The driver check requires a valid semantic version and includes 2026.9.2
  1130	rebuilds. Earlier updaters, including 2026.9.1, have no ledger and keep normal
  1131	publication behavior. Builds from 2026.9.3 onward, including prereleases, use
  1132	transactional updates that fence old-process ledger access and let candidate
  1133	code finish after migration; they also keep normal publication behavior.
  1134	Same-schema repairs and ordinary Doctor runs remain available.
  1135	
  1136	### Profile-owned skill library
  1137	
  1138	[Personal and team skills](/tools/skills#personal-skills-on-a-shared-gateway) use four first-use tables in the shared state database without changing its schema version: `skill_library_entries`, `skill_library_revisions`, `skill_library_events`, and `skill_library_uploads`. Ordinary workspace skills and unused-library discovery do not create these tables. Ownership, sharing, the current revision pointer, portable file manifests, and publication events are canonical SQLite data. Session selections remain in the existing per-agent session store; inherited cron selections remain in the existing private job record.
  1139	
  1140	Complete skill bundles are product artifacts under `<state-dir>/skill-library/<skill-id>/revisions/<revision-hash>/`. Publication writes and verifies an immutable bundle before committing its current pointer and event in one synchronous database transaction. Concurrent edits require the expected revision. A crash before that commit can leave an unreferenced complete bundle, but not a pointer to partially written content. Sharing and transfer change metadata without moving revision files.
  1141	
  1142	Removing a skill excludes it from future selections; existing sessions retain their selected revisions. Published history and complete orphan revisions are retained conservatively. Expired upload records are pruned when another upload begins; clearly abandoned staging directories are cleaned during later publication. Back up both the state databases and the skill-library directory, not just the current revision pointers.
  1143	
  1144	Older same-schema readers ignore the new tables but cannot provide managed-library selection or authoring. Keep the tables and bundle directory intact when changing builds; do not lower schema markers or delete revisions to disable the feature. The accepted storage and ownership decision is recorded in [the profile-owned skills design issue](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/133602).
