$ cat -n /tmp/claude-0/-home-user-trade/2da9385f-4fe4-506a-be3d-78153c549662/scratchpad/cat8/venvs/8-026/openclaw/docs/reference/database-schemas/worker-access.md
     1	---
     2	summary: "Move Gateway database access into existing workers while preserving admission, revisions, and publication"
     3	read_when:
     4	  - Adding or migrating runtime database access
     5	  - Removing SQLite work from the Gateway main thread
     6	  - Reviewing worker result publication or database lifecycle ownership
     7	title: "Database access in workers"
     8	---
     9	
    10	Runtime database access belongs in workers. The Gateway main thread owns live
    11	projections, caches, and caller authority; it awaits prepared facts and installs
    12	committed results. Synchronous boot admission, migrations, Doctor/CLI one-shots,
    13	and lock/lease primitives are the limited exceptions. Existing synchronous runtime
    14	paths are migration debt, not a pattern to extend. The
    15	[migration inventory](/reference/database-schemas/worker-access-inventory) separates
    16	candidate main-thread paths from SQL already executing in workers.
    17	
    18	## Keep one store owner
    19	
    20	Move an existing domain operation across its worker boundary instead of creating
    21	a second store, generic SQL service, or cache manager. Read-only operations use the
    22	existing read-only worker scope and the relevant domain reader. Shared-state
    23	fixed reads, session transcript/history reads, and task registry reads retain
    24	their established adapters and cleanup owners. A Promise around synchronous SQL,
    25	or `withOpenClawAgentDatabaseReadOnly` alone, does not move execution off thread.
    26	`readWithCanonicalSessionAdmission` validates session reads on the executing
    27	thread; invoke it inside the worker's admitted reader.
    28	
    29	Writers use the SQLite worker broker's `state.write` or `agent.write` operation
    30	through their existing domain adapter, such as
    31	`runOpenClawStateWorkerOperation`. The connection-bound Kysely kernel and
    32	transaction callback remain synchronous **inside the worker**. Complete
    33	asynchronous planning first, then reread authoritative rows inside the admitted
    34	transaction. Preserve FIFO order, coordinator custody, transaction/commit grants,
    35	and settlement of accepted write-capable work.
    36	
    37	Worker authority requests wait for the retained host owner's grant or refusal;
    38	host scheduling delays do not expire that authority. The host still checks current
    39	authority before granting, and broker failure joins worker exit before releasing
    40	custody. Coordinator-lock and broker-capacity admission keep their own deadlines.
    41	
    42	Each SQLite broker worker admits up to 128 running and queued requests. A busy
    43	worker's admission queue does not consume another worker's request capacity;
    44	independent workers continue serving their databases. Requests on the same worker
    45	retain FIFO order, including callers waiting for capacity. All workers still share
    46	the 256 MiB retained-input budget, and a caller waiting for request capacity can
    47	time out after ten seconds. Individual commands up to 64 MiB retain their full
    48	serialized size while queued. Larger commands still require an idle worker and
    49	reserve a 32 MiB transport window; they never wait in the input queue. These are
    50	internal resource bounds, not configuration settings. These scheduling and budget
    51	changes preserve database ownership, transaction authority, schemas, and update
    52	behavior.
    53	
    54	## Carry facts, publish after commit
    55	
    56	Memory session preparation retains only export text, provenance, timestamps, and
    57	classification/reset facts from each decoded SQLite event. Full-message observers
    58	retain their original snapshot, and callbacks run after its read transaction closes.
    59	Conversation-recall reset checks use the existing reset navigation projection in
    60	the same background reader pool, without hydrating message bodies. Both paths
    61	retain the transcript read fence and raw line ordinals. Full indexing still scans
    62	the transcript; stored data, exported content, hashes, and update behavior are unchanged.
    63	
    64	Before yielding, capture the physical store target, source/admission scope,
    65	request identity, and the owning projection revision. The lifecycle owner retains
    66	that source until reader cleanup or write settlement completes. Workers return
    67	plain prepared rows, domain results, and the revision/identity evidence already
    68	owned by that operation. Database connections and live authority stay with their
    69	owners; serialized tokens or prepared rows do not grant permission.
    70	
    71	After an awaited read, revalidate the captured lifecycle and current caller
    72	access before disclosing data. Install results only if the owner's revision still
    73	matches; otherwise use its existing invalidation/refresh path. Preserve
    74	identity-keyed sharing caches, bounded reuse, ordering, and byte-stable codecs.
    75	Reuse published facts through the request rather than reopening SQLite for each
    76	viewer or row. Do not add an independent freshness clock or cache lifecycle.
    77	
    78	A writer publishes projections, revision changes, and observer notifications only
    79	after the committed result is acknowledged. A delayed reply cannot replace a
    80	newer native or worker publication. If result delivery is uncertain, retain the
    81	existing reconciliation custody: do not replay the write. Cancellation before
    82	dispatch can refuse work; cancellation after execution must still join its native
    83	settlement. Close and shutdown join accepted work and cleanup before releasing
    84	the store or replacing its generation.
    85	
    86	Session-reclamation retirement honors settled cleanup reported by its worker,
    87	including after a failed request. After an unsettled native exit, the shared-state
    88	cleanup worker releases the exact retained lease. Retirement joins lease deletion and cleanup
    89	store close, keeping those writes off the host connection used by live snapshots.
    90	Automatic process-exit cleanup makes one attempt. A failed attempt retains worker
    91	and lease custody for an explicit lifecycle retry instead of repeatedly scheduling
    92	cleanup whenever the event loop drains. Revocation removes only pending writer
    93	admissions from the existing FIFO. A worker waiting for its first or next permit
    94	receives a refusal and settles cleanup without waiting behind the foreground
    95	callback that requested close. Already admitted write-capable work retains its
    96	permit through native settlement; cancellation never releases it early.
    97	
    98	Physical page reclamation releases the session writer permit between vacuum units,
    99	so queued foreground writers receive their FIFO turn before the next unit. Each
   100	connection starts with eight-page units and adjusts toward a 25 ms hold target,
   101	capped at 512 pages. Periodic and cold reclamation retain their existing total
   102	page budgets. Archive selection, file
   103	removal, and row deletion retain their existing shared permit, with disk pressure
   104	rechecked after admission. Page limits do not bound checkpoint copying or storage
   105	latency. Slow transaction diagnostics include commit and rollback time on both
   106	the main thread and workers, naming the database and operation when supplied.
   107	
   108	Watched human-turn signals and upstream observations use the shared-state writer,
   109	including their watcher probe and pruning. Producers await settlement and recheck
   110	current session authority; upstream observations compare the captured source in
   111	the committing transaction. Goal events share that recording command. Synchronous
   112	creation, compaction, terminal-event, watch, reset, and deletion callbacks remain
   113	separate migration work.
   114	
   115	Durable session entry replacement reads its detached snapshot in the history
   116	worker and commits through the existing agent database executor. The transaction
   117	rereads comparison bytes and current rows, and the host rechecks caller authority
   118	at admission and commit. Exact database locators reserve their existing writer
   119	FIFO before asynchronous schema-owner discovery; unresolved logical stores first
   120	select their physical target without borrowing another store's queue. Committed
   121	receipts invalidate retained entry projections and publish sharing facts before
   122	observers. Missing databases are prepared by the same worker owner. Incognito
   123	stores, already executing workers, Doctor maintenance,
   124	and prepared native deletion rollback closures retain their synchronous kernels.
   125	Schemas, retained bytes, configuration, and update behavior are unchanged.
   126	
   127	Durable trajectory flushes use the same agent database executor for sequence
   128	allocation, event insertion, and retention. The recorder captures its pending
   129	prefix inside the physical store's writer FIFO and retains the host metadata
   130	handle while its live source authority is checked at transaction admission and
   131	commit. It joins native settlement before releasing that FIFO turn: a retained
   132	commit receipt retires the prefix even if the reply is lost, a proven rollback
   133	leaves it retryable, and an unknown outcome fences replay. Events recorded during
   134	the write remain queued for the next flush. Incognito and maintenance scopes and
   135	already executing workers keep their native kernel. Event bytes, ordering,
   136	retention limits, schemas, and update behavior are unchanged.
   137	
   138	Disk-budget historical discovery reads reference, recent-history, and admitted-key
   139	protection in the existing maintenance read worker. It returns candidate IDs;
   140	the host captures live admission identities and rechecks their protection before
   141	archive preparation and deletion. Node references are rechecked in the reclamation
   142	worker transaction before archive persistence or deletion, without a redundant
   143	host reference scan per candidate. A newly referenced candidate may undergo archive
   144	preparation, but the transaction preserves its history and publishes no archive.
   145	A deferred WAL checkpoint still blocks another discovery
   146	pass until a newer completed checkpoint. Exact lifecycle removal and logical
   147	maintenance planning limit reference results to the generations they might
   148	delete. No new cache, index, schema, retention policy, or update step is required.
   149	
   150	## Migrate a caller
   151	
   152	1. Trace the registered request, event, or timer through the store owner. Check
   153	   whether a worker adapter already exists; separate durable databases from
   154	   process-held incognito stores, which cannot be reopened by path in another
   155	   isolate. An unresolved in-memory path remains explicit migration debt, not a
   156	   new synchronous exception.
   157	2. Put the smallest complete read or mutation in that adapter, preserving its
   158	   row codecs, missing-store behavior, snapshot/canonical admission, and error
   159	   contract. Move all affected runtime callers together; never fall back to host
   160	   SQLite after a worker failure.
   161	3. Await the domain operation, check current authority, and install the prepared
   162	   result through the existing projection owner. Retain existing revisions and
   163	   sharing identities. Remove the superseded main-thread call path.
   164	4. Compare serialized results against the original entry point on representative
   165	   fixtures. Exercise stale replies, close/cancellation, sharing changes, and
   166	   committed-write visibility where relevant. Measure main-thread time separately
   167	   from total latency; worker startup and transfer costs still affect users.
   168	
   169	For an example, ordinary durable pages in
   170	`src/gateway/server-methods/chat-history-pages.ts` already await
   171	`readSessionHistoryPageInWorker`. Raw cursor delta reads now use that same worker
   172	for SQLite, JSON parsing, and the subagent source/run visibility facts needed by
   173	the bounded delta. The main thread retains display/profile projection, byte
   174	budgets, and fresh sharing checks against the originally admitted sources. A
   175	failed visibility lookup joins worker retirement before its partial facts return;
   176	the host observes that failure only if projection reaches the lookup before a
   177	history reset. Pending inputs and receipts, retained
   178	transcript-session keys, and SSE inline subagent visibility reads remain migration
   179	debt. Process-held incognito databases and the existing
   180	CLI-import history path still need their owner/lifetime migration; they are not
   181	new synchronous exceptions or fallbacks for a failed durable worker read.
   182	
   183	Exact message membership reads for managed attachments also use the history
   184	worker. The worker validates the entire visible JSON range on every lookup,
   185	including unchanged projection revisions, and returns only matching messages.
   186	Cold archive decoding and restoration retain the existing archive worker and
   187	host generation/commit authorization; transcript read fences still bind the
   188	subsequent read. No validation cache or new restoration owner is introduced.
   189	
   190	Single-message lookups and display-message counts use that same history worker.
   191	Session-message broadcasts await the stored content and sequence in their existing
   192	per-transcript queue, then recheck the live session before publishing. Message
   193	lookup keeps its current-only, byte-limit, and reset-archive behavior; counts keep
   194	their projection-readiness retry. Process-held incognito transcripts remain with
   195	their in-memory owner. Schemas, retained data, and update behavior are unchanged.
   196	
   197	Exact transcript-event matching also uses the history worker for disk discovery,
   198	payload decoding, and selection. Callers supply a serializable selection for the
   199	latest event, visible final result, idempotency key, or active assistant message.
   200	The host captures the physical source before yielding and rechecks its admission
   201	before returning the result. Cold archives retain their existing restoration
   202	owner. Native transaction callbacks and process-held incognito transcripts retain
   203	their synchronous reader; worker failures never fall back to host disk reads.
   204	
   205	The asynchronous transcript-search facade similarly moves durable FTS reads for
   206	all four Gateway/tool callers through the existing worker lifecycle. Each caller
   207	rechecks current scope and authorization after awaiting. Warm `sessions.list`
   208	selects resident projection rows without host Kysely reads. Background refreshes
   209	prepare up to 64 dirty persistent rows in the history worker: entry metadata,
   210	board presence, and activity-summary watermarks share one read snapshot per
   211	physical store. Membership comes from the worker-maintained compact projection,
   212	which also retains participant display facts for per-viewer reads. The projection
   213	retains each store through consumption and rejects replies after stored-fact or
   214	registry invalidation. Runtime owners classify their exact run, capacity, and
   215	Swarm notifications separately, so current display and activity changes do not
   216	discard an unchanged database read. The same projection prepares current runtime
   217	facts before consumption; explicit stored facts, membership changes, and unknown
   218	notifications retain their invalidation checks. Rows replaced or
   219	refreshed by direct reads while a reply is pending keep their newer facts; a dirty
   220	replacement retries under its own generation. Related rows use resident facts and
   221	existing invalidations to converge across batches.
   222	
   223	Dirty resident row refreshes also prepare ACP metadata in the shared-state read
   224	worker. Explicit absence travels with the row facts, so presentation does not
   225	repeat ACP lookups or their schema admission checks. ACP publications invalidate
   226	the existing row revision, and entry lifecycle matching still rejects stale
   227	runtime metadata. Optional preview and terminal-message facts use the retained
   228	history worker, with foreground priority and row-generation checks before
   229	publication. The host evaluates fallback notices using its current runtime plugin
   230	aliases; configuration and model policy do not travel to the read worker.
   231	
   232	Catalog-only replacement reuses complete accepted database facts for live resident
   233	rows while rebuilding their model presentation. Stored-data, configuration,
   234	physical-store, and lifecycle invalidations revoke those facts. Transcript updates
   235	revoke watermarks immediately even inside a coalesced presentation window. Cold
   236	archives retain no complete snapshot; exact archive reads remain bounded by the
   237	existing materialization cache. Schema, persisted data, and update behavior are
   238	unchanged.
   239	
   240	Durable keyed RPCs prepare only their selected dirty or archived rows through the
   241	worker before synchronous presentation; placement waits recheck that preparation.
   242	`sessions.get` selects session metadata from the row projection and reads raw
   243	recent messages in the history worker. They recheck the current config,
   244	sharing policy, and session identity before responding. Hot transcript reads use
   245	the atomic reader's cold marker; restoration runs only after a cold rejection and
   246	retains the bounded retry for a concurrent rearchive.
   247	
   248	`chat.history` and `chat.startup` also select entries and participant facts from
   249	the row projection. They prepare the requested row before selection and recheck
   250	current sharing and the captured store and session generation after awaited
   251	history reads, publishing the response in that synchronous frame. Retained task
   252	history keeps its recorded transcript when the live session advances. Responses
   253	own their nested metadata independently of resident rows. Pending-input
   254	reconciliation remains a separate synchronous owner; this change does not alter
   255	storage, migrations, configuration, or update behavior.
   256	
   257	A missing resident row gets a bounded worker sharing read before history treats
   258	it as absent. This preserves refusal for durable entries marked incognito, which
   259	are intentionally excluded from the resident roster. The sharing owner retains
   260	negative reads through response publication, invalidating them when the selected
   261	key, physical source, or route changes. Unrelated catalog refreshes do not reject
   262	empty history. Excluded metadata never grants transcript access or enters resident
   263	rows.
   264	
   265	Bulk hydration, stored parent links, inherited model lookups, and ACP metadata
   266	also retain qualified stored addresses when main aliases or global scope change.
   267	Request aliases still follow current configuration; preparing history never
   268	rekeys an existing row or redirects its stored lineage.
   269	
   270	Startup/topology hydration, internal synchronous keyed and archived reads, and
   271	process-held incognito stores remain migration debt. Preserve the
   272	projection and its identity/revision invalidation instead of replacing it with
   273	another per-request store scan. See the
   274	[inventory baseline](/reference/database-schemas/worker-access-inventory#profile-priority-and-current-cutover-status)
   275	for measurements and the next owners to migrate.
   276	
   277	Scheduled task maintenance and asynchronous task-status summaries read exact
   278	backing-session keys through the existing session reader worker. Each bounded
   279	batch returns only identity and subagent recovery facts; retained session history
   280	is not materialized. Recovery hooks trigger fresh backing reads before the task
   281	owner rechecks the current record. A concurrent session publication invalidates
   282	prepared facts, so uncertain backing state keeps the task alive for a later pass.
   283	Synchronous operator inspection uses the same selected-row reader. An unavailable
   284	schema refuses the read rather than reporting missing backing sessions. Canonical
   285	admission, malformed-row handling, retention, and update behavior are unchanged.
   286	
   287	Cron retention discovery uses a separate, single-worker maintenance lane within the
   288	same session database lifecycle owner. Foreground history and exact-entry reads
   289	keep their own queue while full-store validation runs. Both lanes retain the same
   290	admission, revocation, cleanup, and idle-retirement rules; a database close joins
   291	every lane that holds it. The additional worker is created on demand and retires
   292	on idle timeout or critical memory pressure. Discovery validates the
   293	complete physical store's metadata and participants in one read snapshot. Its
   294	existing full-row decoder streams JSON once and retains prompt snapshots only
   295	for expired cron runs belonging to the logical agent. The
   296	host retains pending-media, descendant-settlement, and busy-session checks; the
   297	lifecycle mutation still compares each complete expected entry and rechecks its
   298	commit guard. Shared-store ownership, retention, schemas, and update behavior
   299	are unchanged. Discovery closes every matching retained SQLite reader before
   300	releasing its captured alias ownership, allowing successful Node reads to keep
   301	the existing worker warm. Failed reads, uncertain native cleanup, and Bun retain
   302	worker retirement; idle retirement remains unchanged.
   303	
   304	Shared GitHub publication prepares canonical profile identity and alias-binding
   305	lifetimes through the existing profile catalogue and read worker. Alias writers
   306	publish their committed binding facts before observers; worker creation and
   307	lost-reply reconciliation use the same catalogue publication owner. Final
   308	profile identity checks read those retained facts before and after policy callbacks,
   309	without a synchronous database fallback. Unsettled profile mutations keep publication
   310	pending until the mutation owner confirms its outcome. Store replacement invalidates the
   311	retained identity. Doctor alias repairs use exclusive Gateway maintenance, and
   312	the next Gateway prepares facts from the resulting store.
   313	Grant resumption reads the current assigned role and email aliases from that
   314	retained owner on each assertion. The requester resolves its role ceiling from
   315	those supplied facts through the shared role-policy owner.
   316	
   317	Internal operator run admission retains the same prepared profile owner before
   318	accepting work. Current authority reads the exact profile and assigned role from
   319	committed resident facts, without scanning aliases or querying SQLite on the
   320	Gateway thread. Role, source, device, and Gateway revocation remain live through
   321	retained continuations; benign aliases added to the target profile do not revoke
   322	it. Callers revalidate after preparation, and assertions reread profile facts
   323	after source callbacks. External plugin authority callbacks retain their existing
   324	synchronous contract and may have their own storage dependencies.
   325	
   326	Session metadata and membership facts are prepared through the existing session
   327	worker. Their canonical writers publish committed changes before observers, and
   328	unknown or unavailable facts leave publication recovery pending until preparation
   329	succeeds. Incognito sessions retain facts from their existing in-memory writer
   330	lifetime. The requester evaluates these facts with the current role and profile
   331	aliases before and after policy callbacks.
   332	
   333	For writes, shared-state domain operations registered by
   334	`src/state/openclaw-state-worker-runtime.ts` reuse the broker and publish results
   335	through their original store/projection owner.
   336	
   337	Subagent completion, recovery, and delivery settlement use the task registry's
   338	worker transition owner. Accepted run updates retain FIFO order through preparation,
   339	commit, and publication. The worker rereads exact task and backing records, while
   340	the host rechecks the captured runtime, registry entry, and execution authority at
   341	admission. Delivery callbacks await settlement before mirroring or cleanup. The
   342	shipped synchronous detached-task SDK remains a separate compatibility adapter.
   343	
   344	Asynchronous completion waits, kill reconciliation, delivery, and cleanup select
   345	tasks through the existing prepared registry reader. Each poll shares one accepted
   346	read, preserves preferred-run and backing-record selection, and rechecks abort,
   347	runtime ownership, and lifecycle authority after awaiting. Synchronous permission,
   348	kill, and requester-wake commits retain their native boundary, as do shipped custom
   349	runtime hooks. Those boundaries do not provide a fallback for worker read failures.
   350	Other native task mutation callers remain migration debt. Slow main-thread
   351	coordinator warnings include the caller stack as well as the operation label,
   352	captured only after a wait exceeds 100 ms. Schemas, retention, and update behavior
   353	are unchanged.
   354	
   355	Background exec registration and terminal writes use the existing task creation
   356	receipt and worker. A command that exits during registration joins its running
   357	and terminal publications in order. The process keeps its cleanup owner until
   358	task settlement and notification error recovery finish, so scope closure cannot
   359	restore or delete its environment while a write is pending. Registered synchronous
   360	V1 runtimes retain their captured adapter. Command redaction, task data, schemas,
   361	retention, and update behavior are unchanged.
   362	
   363	Worktree run-lease cleanup deletes the exact token and reads the Git unlock target
   364	through the shared-state worker. Failed deletions yield between bounded retries,
   365	retaining the original database admission and Git guard until deletion settles.
   366	Process exit retains its best-effort synchronous deletion because it cannot await
   367	a worker. Git-guard admission reads its registry target through the same retained
   368	worker used by cleanup. Deferred context maintenance publishes its running state
   369	through the task worker before entering the engine, preparing the existing writer
   370	and reader owners for completion. It still awaits task completion and failure
   371	settlement before disposing its engine or releasing its process owner; its progress
   372	timer ends before terminal persistence. Cold worker startup belongs to admission;
   373	normal idle retirement and memory-pressure eviction remain in effect. Detached
   374	worker opening evaluates live admission guards in their captured caller context,
   375	then releases that capture after native opening settles.
   376	Worktree run admission writes, task creation and progress, and the remaining native
   377	cron transitions still need migration. This
   378	cutover preserves schemas, stored bytes, retention, configuration, and update behavior.
   379	
   380	Native cron receipt guards read deletion authority through their transaction's
   381	admitted connection. Other synchronous current-authority readers may reuse that
   382	same thread's coordinated write transaction, including its pending lifecycle rows;
   383	ordinary discovery reads retain committed-state isolation. This avoids preparing
   384	a child-process snapshot while holding the shared-state write coordinator. Agent
   385	database admission refusals remain with their in-memory admission owner. Schemas,
   386	retention, configuration, and update behavior are unchanged.
   387	
   388	Cron activation, exact reservation cleanup, and stale-family removal use typed
   389	commands through the existing worker mutation owner. The host retains the
   390	partition lock, reservation identity, live policy, and runner settlement. The
   391	worker rereads durable receipt and deletion guards before committing. Publication
   392	uses the matching committed receipt once; a lost reply never causes a replay.
   393	Deferred receipt finishing retains the captured physical worker context through
   394	settlement. Reservation creation and remaining manual or timer finalizers retain
   395	their native implementation as migration debt. Schemas, retention, configuration,
   396	and update behavior are unchanged.
   397	
   398	Streaming assistant and tool-result completion events use the session manager's
   399	existing SQLite writer domain. The host retains extension hooks, redaction, and
   400	tool-result custody; the worker validates the prepared parent, appends the exact
   401	storage bytes, and returns the committed version and any required view reload.
   402	The manager adopts that receipt before publishing pending-tool changes. Each
   403	event still commits before the runtime advances; bulk transcript imports reuse
   404	their transaction-local append cursor. Root checks read metadata without saved
   405	prompt payloads. No cross-transaction root cache is introduced.
   406	
   407	Runtime report navigation and writes use the same broker's agent database owner.
   408	Custom report selectors consume prepared facts on the host, and the worker
   409	compares the transcript version before appending. Only a definite version conflict
   410	repeats selection; uncertain writes are never replayed. Startup orphan repair
   411	retains its native transaction so session settlement and the report remain atomic.
   412	Process-held incognito databases, user-input custody, custom-message writes, and
   413	the shipped synchronous SessionManager SDK remain separate migration work.
   414	Schemas, stored bytes, retention, and update behavior are unchanged.
   415	
   416	Channel identity administration, profile role assignments, email linking, and
   417	HTTP/WebSocket sign-in acquisition use that writer and the existing read worker.
   418	Worker commit receipts publish affected profile, alias, and display facts through
   419	the profile owner; warm sign-in ensures avoid unnecessary write transactions.
   420	Channel ingress prepares exact identity and role facts in the read worker, then
   421	retains the profile owner's physical-store and mutation revisions. Final owner
   422	checks read those revisions and current configuration without querying SQLite.
   423	Relevant identity or role mutations revoke prior authority before publication;
   424	closing or replacing the store invalidates its retained authority. Display caches
   425	and discovery snapshots do not grant permission.
   426	
   427	Secret-store expiry runs in that worker for scheduled Gateway cleanup and
   428	post-mutation cleanup. The caller captures the database and expiry cutoffs before
   429	yielding; the worker retains the existing SQL and expiry rules and returns only
   430	the deleted count. Scheduled sweeps coalesce while one is active, and Gateway
   431	shutdown stops scheduling and joins accepted cleanup. Ordinary secret-store
   432	set/delete operations remain separate synchronous migration debt.
   433	
   434	Placement change reporting reads its before/after snapshots in the shared-state
   435	read worker using the placement store's row codec. It transfers only session
   436	identity, state, generation, and update time to the Gateway. The reconciliation
   437	coordinator reserves and admits its sweep before awaiting reporting, preserving
   438	dispatch ordering and request coalescing. Reporting failures preserve the original
   439	operation outcomes. Placement
   440	writes, current-authority checks, and workspace retention retain their existing
   441	owners; these reporting snapshots grant no execution or deletion authority.
   442	
   443	Machine-catalog notifications coalesce pending profile changes and select their
   444	correlated placements through the same read worker. The Gateway publishes keyed
   445	session invalidations after the read and drains pending reporting on shutdown.
   446	Each batch reads current placement and environment facts; it retains no placement
   447	cache and does not scan the placement inventory on the main thread.
   448	
   449	This execution cutover does not change schemas, stored bytes, retention, config,
   450	or update behavior. A change to those contracts follows the
   451	[storage review checkpoint](/reference/database-schemas/storage-changes#review-checkpoint-for-material-changes).
   452	
   453	iMessage resource authorization reads uncached message-to-chat membership through
   454	its existing read-only Messages database worker and joins reader cleanup before
   455	returning. The resource owner retains local executable attestation, exclusive
   456	account binding, and conversation matching; reply sends recheck live caller
   457	authority after the read. Missing or failed reads retain the existing delegated
   458	refusal and direct-operator behavior, without falling back to host SQLite.
   459	
   460	Administrative skill archive uploads use the shared-state worker for staging,
   461	expiry cleanup, commit, installation claims, lease renewal, and consumption. The
   462	host retains per-upload locks and temporary archive materialization. Installation
   463	completion joins accepted renewals before consuming or releasing the exact owner
   464	lease; database close joins the callback and its retained worker cleanup. Cleanup
   465	refuses a replacement physical database and cannot delete a successor's lease.
   466	Upload formats, expiry limits, installation permissions, and update behavior are
   467	unchanged.
   468	
   469	Discord thread-binding startup and bundled mutations use the existing plugin-state
   470	worker. Inbound and outbound activity, binding changes, lifecycle settings, thread
   471	deletion, and expiry await their mutations. The existing registry serializes writes,
   472	checks the live manager and registry revision at worker admission, and joins accepted
   473	binds and writes before shutdown retires the manager. Manager and session-wide
   474	mutations share account ordering, but Discord network preparation stays outside the
   475	shared persistence queue. Session-wide operations reserve their selected accounts
   476	before waiting, so later unbinds include an earlier admitted bind. Activity-write
   477	failures are reported without suppressing inbound dispatch whose original abort
   478	and policy authority remains current. A later synchronous SDK update rebases on
   479	committed rows; delayed acknowledgements preserve that newer projection.
   480	If the native read fails before observing a pending target, compatibility calls leave
   481	its projection unchanged for the worker result. Accepted metadata uses the existing
   482	JSON codec to capture nested values before queue waits. An interrupted full-map
   483	save reports its acknowledged prefix without replaying it, and an acknowledged
   484	target mutation remains successful. Full-map
   485	registration preserves cross-account persistence and bounded eviction recency;
   486	activity retains its 15-second coalescing. Unavailable persistence retains the
   487	existing in-memory fallback, while revoked authority refuses publication. Stored
   488	records, namespace bounds, schema, and update behavior are unchanged. The public
   489	Discord SDK's synchronous list, touch, lifecycle setter, and unbind compatibility
   490	paths remain under the same owner, deprecated for removal at the next Plugin SDK
   491	major. Bundled callers use the awaited variants. ACP startup session reads are a
   492	separate worker migration.
