   476	Asynchronous mutable cron-store loads run in the shared-state worker, including
   477	the existing retired-job deletion and runtime-authority repairs. The connection-bound
   478	load kernel preserves their separate transactions, partition keys, and fingerprints.
   479	Completed repair facts invalidate host scheduler snapshots before the load settles,
   480	including when a later load stage fails. A snapshot retains the host revision captured
   481	before loading; intervening writes leave it stale for the next load. An unavailable
   482	worker result or a failed load without a reported repair also invalidates the cached
   483	revision without replaying the operation. Error causes used by Doctor diagnostics
   484	cross the same closed-field error graph, without changing ordinary broker errors.
   485	Unguarded cron saves without transaction hooks also execute in that worker, using the same
   486	connection-bound kernels as native hook-bearing transactions. Full replacement,
   487	runtime-only updates, quarantine changes, and changed-row merges retain their
   488	existing transaction boundaries. Save results publish committed or uncertain
   489	invalidation before settlement. Internal service callers receive an operation-bound
   490	revision; intervening host writes leave the returned snapshot conservatively stale.
   491	Evicted revision entries fall back to the existing global publication sequence,
   492	and stale save receipts use a negative marker that cannot match a current revision.
   493	Public save signatures and return values are unchanged. Service mutations with
   494	commit guards, one-use authority capture, or caller preconditions retain their
   495	synchronous call-through to the native kernels; their worker admission remains
   496	separate work. Receipt-coupled transaction hooks, Doctor metadata callbacks, and synchronous diagnostic reads
   497	retain their current owners and execution paths.
