  1370	data-version invalidation, without changing schemas, stored retention, or update
  1371	behavior. The idle window is an internal constant, not a configuration option.
  1372	
  1373	Canonical lock coordinators use the same idle window after releasing their locks.
  1374	Independent active shared leases keep separate physical custody; caller-owned
  1375	temporary directories and explicit exclusions still force native close. Explicit
  1376	artifact-preserving inspections, private snapshots, and extension-enabled or nested
  1377	transaction reads retain their isolated connection and cleanup contracts.
  1378	
  1379	Read-only callbacks made while a cached agent writer holds a transaction use a
  1380	separate read-only companion connection. Each call rereads committed rows and
  1381	checks the current schema, agent owner, and physical file identity. The companion
  1382	retains prepared statements, never an authorization result or an open read
  1383	transaction. On connections whose owner enables statement caching, schema-version
  1384	checks reuse the prepared `PRAGMA user_version` statement but read its current
  1385	value on every call. Authorizer changes, database replacement, and close retain
  1386	the existing statement-cache invalidation rules.
  1387	Canonical validation belongs to the admitted physical database:
  1388	first admission requires full proof, then the schema-21 pending-key projection
  1389	records changes independently of connection lifetime. Startup and initial Gateway
  1390	authorization of an unadmitted reader use the existing mutation worker for pending
  1391	validation and recheck live authority after awaiting it. Concurrent runtime readers
  1392	on the same native database owner share its active validation drain. Each waiter
  1393	retains its own lifecycle claim and rechecks physical ownership and canonical proof
  1394	afterward; completion or failure removes the shared drain. Startup's scoped workers
  1395	retain their own batch lifecycle. Schemas, stored data, and update behavior are unchanged.
  1396	Native readers preserve
  1397	their existing main-key admission and raw-row parser behavior; each new reader
  1398	checks pending keys without rescanning unrelated certified entries. Synchronous commit guards still read committed
  1399	rows. See [incremental canonical validation](/reference/database-schemas/agent-schema-history#incremental-canonical-session-validation)
  1400	for migration and rollback behavior. The companion retires with its writer's
  1401	native close, disposal, or replacement, including eviction and update cleanup.
  1402	Cold session search retains one read-only connection while synchronously listing
  1403	entries and checking their current visibility. Unscoped role-filtered searches
  1404	group exact metadata reads per physical store, retaining failures in the original
  1405	key order. Each group uses the exact reader's cold-admission snapshot; warm groups
  1406	retain ordinary committed reads. Incognito checks remain individual lookups.
  1407	The entry accessor closes the connection before transcript search, including on
  1408	errors; inherited async callbacks fall back to ordinary fresh reads. Prepared
  1409	metadata stays within the synchronous request and never caches visibility decisions.
  1410	Other cold readers outside the history worker, including
  1411	extension-capable readers, remain one-shot; incognito reads retain their existing
  1412	process-local owner.
  1413	
  1414	SQLite and Git worker replies transfer owned byte buffers to the caller; shared
  1415	or partial views are copied into an exact owned buffer before transfer.
  1416	
  1417	The history worker retains up to 64 read-only connections across requests, rechecking
  1418	schema, agent owner, and physical file identity before reuse. Every request keeps
  1419	its own snapshot and current admission checks. Switching databases reuses their
  1420	connections; admitting another retained connection evicts the least recently used
  1421	one. Missing databases consume no retained slot. The parent keeps custody of all
  1422	retained targets and retires the worker after 30 minutes without pending history reads; database cleanup revokes admission and joins native worker
  1423	exit before closing the database. Cold restoration carries the request's same
  1424	authority through queue waits and its native commit, so a revoked read cannot
  1425	restore rows after database cleanup. These lifetimes change no schema or
  1426	migration requirement.
  1427	
  1428	RPC and HTTP history pages, cursor deltas, recent messages, and exact message lookups prepare their
  1429	physical target asynchronously and read cold-archive metadata through that same
  1430	retained history worker only after a typed cold read requires restoration. Hot
  1431	reads keep the atomic reader's existing cold check without a metadata preflight.
  1432	Initial metadata
  1433	probes share only in-flight work; every queued restoration rereads the metadata
  1434	after earlier cold operations settle. The existing restoration owner still
  1435	verifies and materializes the archive and retains the 24-hour hot-history cooldown.
  1436	Write-side callers keep their existing native metadata preparation and writer
  1437	admission rather than competing for foreground history capacity. Cold maintenance
  1438	inventory and mutation control SQL also remain with their current owners.
  1439	
  1440	Correlated conversation replies retain their original store and state environment
  1441	while waiting for write admission. Capture rechecks the live reply claim and
  1442	session lifecycle before recording a replayable reply. Cancellation or a changed
  1443	session leaves the message for ordinary inbound dispatch. The durable reply is
  1444	recorded before its optional side audit artifact and before completing the waiter;
  1445	an audit failure does not discard an already recorded reply.
  1446	
  1447	Outbound queue work captures its state root and external-supervisor mode before
  1448	asynchronous preparation. Enqueue, media custody, claims, completion and cleanup
  1449	retain that context; SDK reconnect requests capture it before waiting for Gateway
  1450	admission or loading the delivery runtime. A recovery root applies to an existing
  1451	queue entry, while fresh sends use their selected default root. This context stays
  1452	internal and is not added to durable payloads or plugin callback inputs.
  1453	
  1454	Gateway lifecycle notices retain their original shared-state directory and
  1455	supervisor mode through asynchronous modifying hooks, media staging, and queue
  1456	publication. Immediate delivery, settlement, and retry recovery use that same
  1457	captured context. Startup carries it through the sentinel read, revision-checked
  1458	cleanup, enqueue, and delivery; public plugin send arguments cannot select this
  1459	private context. Runtime retry services and delayed startup callbacks capture
  1460	their state at registration and retain it across retries, session lookups, and
  1461	update-ledger writes. Current configuration and delivery authority are still
  1462	checked when each retry runs.
  1463	
  1464	Standalone session-delivery queue operations run in the shared-state worker.
  1465	Producers, recovery, generated-media preparation, and the retry scheduler carry
  1466	one captured database context through enqueue, retry bookkeeping, and settlement.
  1467	The scheduler stops admission and joins its reads and active drains before the
  1468	database closes. Queue payloads retain their JSON serialization boundary before
  1469	worker transport. Compound task/subagent admission and settlement retain their
  1470	existing synchronous transaction owner. Outbound dead-letter health counts use
  1471	the existing grouped-count kernel in the shared-state worker. Health collection
  1472	captures its original worker admission before awaiting configuration and other
  1473	health work; cached health replies await the count while retaining cached ingress
  1474	pressure.
  1475	
  1476	Outbound ACK settlement also runs in that worker. It captures the selected state
  1477	root and options before admission, preserves exact attempt ownership checks,
  1478	and returns committed media paths before host cleanup. A lost worker reply
  1479	fails the ACK without replaying it or inferring success from an absent row;
  1480	pre-send best-effort fallback therefore cannot authorize a provider send after
  1481	an unacknowledged settlement. Media stays available for existing orphan cleanup.
  1482	Other outbound queue operations and media custody remain separate migration work.
  1483	Schemas, retained receipts, update behavior, and cleanup policy are unchanged.
  1484	
  1485	Completed same-session `sessions_send` replies use the existing outbound queue
  1486	table under `outbound-session-generation-v1`. Each result retains its original
  1487	route and exact agent, session store, session key, physical session ID, and
  1488	nullable lifecycle revision. Ordinary subsequent turns do not supersede these
  1489	rows. Live delivery and recovery prepare generation facts through the session
  1490	owner and check them immediately before dispatch; reset, deletion, or replacement
  1491	rejects an undispatched result. Already-dispatched sends keep the existing
  1492	confirmed or uncertain settlement rules. This queue does not preserve unfinished
  1493	model execution or an in-memory completion observer across restart.
  1494	
  1495	The namespace isolates these rows from older readers without changing database
  1496	schema versions or disabling ordinary queues. Its media uses `g1-`-prefixed names
  1497	under the existing spool owner, limits, and cleanup policy. Older readers leave
  1498	those files alone, including unfinished stage files; their cleanup resumes on a
  1499	supporting version. A full state backup includes these artifacts, while a
  1500	database-only backup still excludes media. Existing snapshot sanitization removes
  1501	pending delivery rows, so backup restoration does not resume these replies and
  1502	unreferenced media remains subject to orphan cleanup. An in-place restart or
  1503	downgrade/reopen retains queue custody. The recorded store and media paths remain
  1504	exact; moving raw state does not rewrite delivery bindings.
  1505	
  1506	Outbound lookup, attempt reservation, failure transitions, and restoration run
  1507	through the existing shared-state worker alongside enqueue, producer claims, and
  1508	ACK. Executable namespaces share stable-intent conflict checks and pending-order
  1509	inventory; each mutation retains the entry's namespace and exact attempt owner.
  1510	
  1511	Outbound producer claims and lease renewals run in the shared-state worker. The
  1512	existing write transaction rereads the pending row, exact owner, and expiry on
  1513	the executing worker. Callers await claim publication; lease stop joins accepted
  1514	renewals before cancellation cleanup or acknowledgement can retire custody. An
  1515	unavailable claim result leaves its row and media with recovery rather than
  1516	replaying the mutation or starting a provider send. The lease period, heartbeat,
  1517	retry budget, namespaces, stored payloads, and update behavior are unchanged.
  1518	The final provider-dispatch fence and queue settlement retain their existing owners.
  1519	
  1520	Pending outbound failure settlement runs in the shared-state worker with the
  1521	captured entry bytes and state context. Its existing exact-row and optional
  1522	claim checks decide settlement before cleanup facts return to the host. Only a
  1523	confirmed failure releases media; a lost worker reply never triggers a replay
  1524	or infers success from an absent row. Unguarded calls still validate terminal
  1525	entries before storage opens, and unmatched guarded claims remain no-ops.
  1526	
  1527	Conversation sends, turns, and queue completion retain their logical agent and
  1528	physical store while waiting for agent write admission. Retry validation reads
  1529	existing operations without recreating them; the queue owner records custody
  1530	before transport I/O and reconciles accepted outcomes on that same store. New
  1531	conversation bindings reread source policy from the original store after route
  1532	preparation and retain the destination owner through the final authority check.
  1533	
  1534	Board mutations, snapshots, and widget document reads expose asynchronous
  1535	contracts. Ordinary disk data mutations run their existing synchronous kernels on the
  1536	canonical per-agent worker connection, shared with other admitted domains.
  1537	Inputs are captured before queued work, and the caller's current authority is
  1538	checked at transaction entry and commit. Committed session changes return to the
  1539	existing host publisher before the result is exposed; rollback publishes nothing,
  1540	and unknown outcomes conservatively invalidate the exact original session without
  1541	replaying the write. Gateway callers await persistence before publishing board
  1542	changes or replies. Existing-session preflight, source-handle acquisition,
  1543	schema/bootstrap/migration, cold `hasBoard` projection, and board reads remain native. Incognito writes retain
  1544	their process-held connection.
  1545	HTML widget capability actions and protected publication run in the store's immediate
  1546	continuation after its authoritative read and current ticket, session, and grant checks.
  1547	Database ownership is released before awaiting external work; no Promise handoff separates
  1548	the final authorization from its use. Board and progress-card writes capture their physical
  1549	database and state environment before joining the canonical agent writer queue. Cold opens
  1550	use its asynchronous integrity admission, and request authority is checked again before
  1551	schema setup and mutation. A changed route, closed request, or revoked session cannot
  1552	publish a queued write. SQLite kernels remain synchronous inside their native transactions, with existing
  1553	revision, grant, session-existence, and transaction semantics.
  1554	
  1555	Progress-card GET resolves its captured session store through the existing target
  1556	preparation owner and reads the card on the session transcript worker. Reads do
  1557	not create missing databases or unused card tables. The same worker custody joins
  1558	native cleanup and rejects retired owners; Gateway authorization is rechecked
  1559	before returning a delayed card. Incognito reads retain their process-held owner.
  1560	Progress-card writes and reset clears keep their existing transaction owners.
  1561	
  1562	MCP App pinning retains its existing source-interaction checks. A delayed adapter must
  1563	revalidate that source authority at its actual write admission; checking view registration
  1564	alone cannot replace the supported asynchronous interaction policy.
  1565	The SQLite owner refreshes that policy after cold-open preparation while holding the
  1566	destination writer admission. A revoked source downgrades the pin to read-only and removes
  1567	its declared tools before the synchronous write. Request authority is checked again after
  1568	the policy wait, so cancellation cannot persist even a downgraded pin. Retiring the admitted
  1569	database during that wait refuses the operation without reopening it.
  1570	
  1571	Backup outcome recording and freshness reads expose asynchronous operations from
  1572	the shared-state owner. Archive, SQLite snapshot, and Git backup commands await
  1573	recording before reporting completion; a recording failure remains a warning and
  1574	does not change the backup result. Status and Doctor await freshness before
  1575	formatting it. Outcome recording executes its insertion-and-pruning transaction
  1576	in the shared-state worker, preserving the 200-row limit and leaving absent
  1577	databases absent. Freshness reads still execute synchronous SQLite internally
  1578	and remain non-creating.
  1579	
  1580	Explicit session deletion, lifecycle-artifact cleanup, and history disk-budget
  1581	eviction prepare their plans inside the session writer queue. When the parent database handle is cold, its
  1582	existing asynchronous admission owner runs the full integrity and foreign-key
  1583	checks in a read-only child, moving those full checks off the main thread while
  1584	retaining that queue position. A supplied caller guard is rechecked before the open
  1585	resumes into index repair, schema work, or registration, and before that caller
  1586	uses the admitted handle. Coalesced callers retain their own guards. History
  1587	eviction also uses this admission when reopening after archive materialization,
  1588	then rereads candidate protection before preparing reclamation.
  1589	
  1590	Artifact cleanup resolves session paths only when its file inventory contains
  1591	candidate transcript, compaction checkpoint, or trajectory files. Prompt-reference
  1592	projection runs only when prompt blobs exist. Age, exclusion, and containment
  1593	checks still govern every removal.
  1594	
  1595	Automatic session-entry maintenance first checks the unarchived count and
  1596	store-scoped age facts. Writes below the existing cap high-water mark skip
  1597	candidate and protection-key reads until pruning or dashboard archiving could
  1598	change an entry. A plan records the next age boundary and a 30-minute recheck
  1599	deadline under the current age policy. Every maintenance entry point rejects
  1600	expired facts, including inline replacement and lifecycle writes that have no
  1601	maintenance timer. Ordinary entry writes only tighten the age boundary; entry-cache
  1602	revision changes and unrelated external commits do not discard it. Backdated
  1603	replacements, archive restores, imports, and Doctor rewrites invalidate it
  1604	explicitly. Rollback and connection replacement also discard reuse. Key-inherent
  1605	protection does not keep an old primary or external conversation permanently due.
  1606	Already-aged entries with dynamic protection wait for the next age boundary or
  1607	periodic recheck instead of requiring fresh planning on every write.
  1608	
  1609	Age-fact refreshes use prepared ordered timestamp probes and dashboard key
  1610	ranges across every agent namespace in a shared store. Activity probes reuse
  1611	the canonical maximum of the recorded activity fields and stop when later
  1612	timestamps cannot improve the next deadline. Uncertified rows retain the key
  1613	decoder's alias handling; older maintenance readers without the pending
  1614	projection keep the full row path. Fresh ordinary stores avoid a full timestamp
  1615	projection; dashboard and recent-activity-heavy stores can still require scans.
  1616	Archived or protected index prefixes can also add work. Existing count and
  1617	invalid-row queries remain separate costs; this is not a constant-work guarantee
  1618	for every maintenance pass.
  1619	
  1620	The parent owns age facts and their tracked-write invalidation. Each planning
  1621	request carries the current fact to the retained worker, replacing any fact from
  1622	an earlier request. Commit authorization checks the captured parent state; after
  1623	settlement, the parent adopts the returned fact only if that state is still
  1624	current, before publication and writer release. A newer write keeps its own
  1625	state. Rolled-back planning does not publish a fact.
  1626	
  1627	The coalesced maintenance kick wakes at the earlier of the age boundary and the
  1628	same periodic deadline for released work protection and external changes.
  1629	Ordinary writes do not postpone that deadline. Its timer retires with
  1630	the exact database connection. Planning still reads its protection-key inventory
  1631	only when age or cap candidates exist. Archives and final deletion retain their
  1632	existing post-writer lifecycle checks. Retention rules, cap buffering, forced cleanup,
  1633	and active-work, ancestor, and lifecycle protection remain unchanged. No schema
  1634	or migration change is required.
  1635	
  1636	After archive preparation, session deletion rereads its target before admitting
  1637	the final reclamation worker. A missing or changed target returns the existing
  1638	entry-mismatch result without starting that worker, while preserving archives
  1639	already committed by the deletion. Admitted workers still recheck the target
  1640	and current authority inside their deletion transaction.
  1641	
  1642	Prepared session-store updates, entry replacements, and lifecycle upserts use
  1643	the same admission for cold snapshot reads and actual commits, retaining their
  1644	existing writer position. Warm update callbacks remain direct. Result-only
  1645	no-op commits do not reopen a disposed handle. Native deletion and archive
  1646	preparation still run outside the writer; the subsequent commit rechecks its
  1647	native owner's authority after any awaited admission.
  1648	
  1649	Automatic entry maintenance captures its policy at writer admission, then plans
  1650	on the existing reclamation worker. Only a pass with retention candidates requests
  1651	protected session identities, after rolling back candidate discovery and before
  1652	a fresh planning transaction. The parent captures those identities under the
  1653	writer. Protection includes runtime providers, active work, and active lifecycle
  1654	mutations; the parent rechecks these owners and the write generation before planning commits.
  1655	Changed inputs roll back that planning pass before a fresh pass begins. Bounded
  1656	finalization preserves changed entries and publishes removals only for committed
  1657	entries. Transcript sizing and empty-transcript validation run on archive workers;
  1658	planner statistics retain the existing deletion threshold and bounded analysis.
  1659	After worker analysis commits, an existing parent connection reloads its planner
  1660	statistics locally without rescanning tables; this remains necessary until its
  1661	query owners move to workers.
  1662	Compound projection and replacement transactions keep their synchronous kernels.
  1663	Candidate-only preservation providers, incognito databases, prepared native
  1664	deletion hooks, commit-authorization joins, archive publication bookkeeping, and
  1665	repository/worktree cleanup retain their existing parent-side owners.
  1666	
  1667	Session reclamation keeps its deletion transaction on a worker connection.
  1668	The worker opens its database under the session writer, then releases that writer
  1669	while any required first full integrity and foreign-key checks run on the same
  1670	connection. Unrelated session writes can continue during those checks. Workers
  1671	can borrow the Gateway's remembered verification for the same physical agent
  1672	database under live write admission. The worker reacquires the writer and
  1673	revalidates current authority before index repair, schema work, or deletion.
  1674	The process retains at most one validated reclamation worker connection and lease,
  1675	with a 30-minute idle retirement. Each deletion keeps its own transaction, retained
  1676	parent claim, numbered write admission, and current-authority checks in its own
  1677	async context. The worker clears operation buffers and acknowledges transaction
  1678	settlement before the parent publishes committed removals and releases that
  1679	operation's writer admission. Later requests reuse the connection only for the
  1680	same physical database and shared-state owner; every request checks its live lease.
  1681	
  1682	During Doctor maintenance, session mutation and worker-close jobs borrow its
  1683	existing state-lifecycle coordinator through a live delegate bound to the actor,
  1684	shared database, and coordinator runtime. Delegation covers asynchronous work and
  1685	cleanup until the original result settles or native exit is joined. Revocation
  1686	still prevents later writes. Failed coordinator cleanup remains owned for drainage;
  1687	a confirmed mutation stays successful if only subsequent cleanup fails.
  1688	
  1689	Switching databases, deletion, quarantine, maintenance, root retirement, and shutdown
  1690	revoke reuse and join native worker exit before releasing the database owner. Pending
  1691	commit requests are rejected before synchronous close can wait on their writer lock.
  1692	Requests still waiting in the shared archive queue drop their callback before releasing
  1693	their retained claim, so retirement does not wait for unrelated queued work. Surviving
  1694	requests keep FIFO order. Once admitted, an operation retains custody through physical
  1695	settlement even if its request is revoked. Schemas, retention, and update behavior are unchanged.
  1696	Crash cleanup can release only the exact admitted lease receipt, after native exit;
  1697	uncertain cleanup remains an error and never causes mutation replay. The parent
  1698	adopts newly established integrity verification only after operation cleanup and
  1699	while its database claim remains current. These connection lifetimes are documented in the
  1700	[accepted reclamation design](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/140897#issuecomment-5647899202).
  1701	
  1702	Pressure sweeps and explicit deletion reuse one archive worker within their operation
  1703	scope. The shared archive queue admits each materialization or publication separately;
  1704	every request opens a fresh read-only database and closes its database and file handles
  1705	before acknowledging completion. No archive database connection or lease survives
  1706	between requests. Each victim still commits and publishes before the next victim is
  1707	deleted. A preparation or publication failure retires the worker and joins its native
  1708	exit before returning the existing error. Scope completion and database retirement
  1709	revoke queued requests, drain dispatched work, and join native exit. The process keeps
  1710	at most one reusable archive worker; competing scopes retire the previous idle worker.
  1711	Cold preparation and mutations retain their separate one-shot workers; cold mutations
  1712	join their existing page maintenance and native exit.
  1713	
  1714	Single-candidate reference checks narrow which node metadata reaches JavaScript.
  1715	Rows with optional historical references still use the canonical entry parser, and
  1716	ambiguous SQLite text or JSON retains the full read path. Each check reads current rows
  1717	in its existing planning phase or deletion transaction; no reference cache is introduced.
  1718	
  1719	Disk-budget cleanup rechecks protection after archive materialization. A candidate
  1720	already excluded by that fresh protection set is canceled before worker admission
  1721	and is not counted as reclaimed. After releasing its lifecycle holds, cleanup
  1722	remeasures physical usage before considering another candidate, so space freed by
  1723	a peer does not cause unnecessary eviction. Every admitted worker still performs
  1724	current-owner and schema checks; integrity reuse follows the Gateway-lifetime
  1725	policy described in [Integrity checks](/reference/database-schemas/integrity-and-recovery#integrity-checks).
  1726	
  1727	Archive publication and cascading deletion remain atomic. Before COMMIT, the
  1728	worker publishes its authorization request in shared memory and waits for the
  1729	parent's current owner check. Synchronous writers service that request at the shared
  1730	SQLite transaction boundary between short lock-admission attempts, in the reclamation
  1731	owner's captured async context. This includes session entries, delivery records, and
  1732	first-use board and Goal schema transactions. Registration uses the open connection's
  1733	native database location, so other connections and reopened handles share admission.
  1734	Only admission is retried; transaction callbacks and mutations are never replayed.
  1735	The original lock-admission deadline is retained. After granting approval,
  1736	the parent synchronously joins transaction settlement before allowing owner retirement;
  1737	that mandatory join cannot be abandoned at the append deadline.
  1738	
  1739	Periodic incremental vacuum uses the same write-admission boundary, so it can
  1740	service reclamation approval before taking the writer lock. Each connection starts
  1741	with eight-page units and adjusts toward a 25 ms hold target, growing at most twice
  1742	per unit up to 512 pages. The scheduling estimate expires with the connection.
  1743	Periodic maintenance retains its 512-page total budget per tick, reacquiring
  1744	admission between units; passive checkpoints remain outside the write transaction.
  1745	
  1746	The WAL owner supplies one checkpoint-before-vacuum operation for periodic,
  1747	reclamation, and archive maintenance. An incomplete checkpoint skips vacuum.
  1748	Each unit releases at most 512 pages and uses zero busy timeout for online lock
  1749	admission; checkpoint frame copying itself is not bounded by that page limit.
  1750	Archive pruning drains its initially observed free pages in these units and stops
  1751	before deleting archives when checkpointing is incomplete. Its outcome records
  1752	completion, checkpoint facts, and physical bytes before and after. Budget cleanup
  1753	remains deferred until the checkpoint owner reports completion, preserving retained
  1754	data instead of adding writes behind a pinned WAL.
  1755	
  1756	Checkpoint ordering uses a private monotonic observation shared by the host and
  1757	its workers; health timestamps remain wall-clock diagnostics. Post-commit page
  1758	maintenance also waits for the parent's commit-settlement probe to release its
  1759	writer lock. Child transaction settlement and parent probe release are distinct
  1760	facts in the existing commit gate; failed release cannot acknowledge success.
  1761	
  1762	Archive pruning retains its maintenance reader and execution lifetimes while each page-reclamation unit
  1763	acquires and releases the physical writer separately. Each archive removal acquires
  1764	archive admission before its physical writer and releases both after the item settles.
  1765	Foreground session writes and cold history restoration in unrelated stores can run
  1766	between reclamation units. Durable archive metadata reads
  1767	use the existing history worker; conditional deletions, legacy file removal, and
  1768	page reclamation use the agent database execution broker.
  1769	The host captures the physical file before
  1770	waiting and rechecks the original path alias and live authority before effects
  1771	and at worker admission. Each write joins native settlement without replaying a
  1772	dispatched mutation. Host connection eviction does not redirect work or require
  1773	a synchronous database reopen. Process-held incognito maintenance retains its
  1774	existing in-process owner and remains a separate worker migration. Explicit
  1775	Doctor/cleanup scopes also retain their native owner.
  1776	
  1777	Canonical archive removal holds archive admission and one writer section through
  1778	selection, derived-file removal, and conditional row deletion. It rechecks pressure,
  1779	authority, and the selected published row before deleting the canonical recovery copy. A failed
  1780	admission or changed row preserves that copy and stops the current
  1781	cleanup attempt. Legacy file removal checks exact canonical filename ownership,
  1782	stats the file, and unlinks it in one synchronous worker write transaction. It
  1783	preserves files owned by published or unpublished rows and holds the SQLite writer
  1784	lock through unlink. Since file removal cannot roll back, the host grants commit
  1785	immediately before unlink; no-effect outcomes also require current commit authority.
  1786	Native settlement finishes before the item writer is released. Filesystem inventory
  1787	and successive page drains run outside both archive admission and the item writer.
  1788	Aggregate pruning diagnostics report the whole operation separately from actual
  1789	writer waits.
  1790	Archive order, retention policy, schemas, and update behavior are unchanged.
  1791	
  1792	Worker retirement preserves the original operation failure without reporting it
  1793	again as a cleanup failure. A successfully retired execution owner is released for
  1794	later requests; genuine native-close and lease-cleanup failures retain their
  1795	existing retry custody. The next admitted agent operation retries that cleanup
  1796	before opening a replacement generation, so transient lifecycle contention does
  1797	not permanently disable history eviction. Cleanup rechecks the original database
  1798	identity and request authority; it never replays the failed operation. Explicit
  1799	resource revocation remains terminal. Schemas, retention, and update behavior are unchanged.
  1800	Successful pooled-agent close relays its recorded WAL checkpoint after native and
  1801	lease cleanup settle. The original generation and physical database identities
  1802	fence that observation, and the budget owner releases deferral only for a newer
  1803	completed checkpoint.
  1804	
  1805	Usage-cache rollup writes, pruning, and refresh-lock changes use the same async
  1806	agent-database admission. A cold mutation waits for the existing integrity worker;
  1807	its compare-and-set transaction remains synchronous on the admitted connection.
  1808	Refresh completion and cleanup await persistence. Operations capture their resolved
  1809	database path before admission, and refresh-lock release retains that path and its
  1810	original environment when the caller's directory or environment changes. Doctor reports rejected
  1811	pruning operations before continuing to the next agent.
  1812	
  1813	Usage-cache decoding, report folding, transcript inventory, and refresh scanning
  1814	run in the existing session-transcript worker. Foreground reports use a separate
  1815	bounded worker lane; background refreshes use shared compute admission. Reports
  1816	return compact results, and refreshes send prepared UTF-8 compare-and-set values
  1817	to the existing host writer. Selected reports read only their requested cache
  1818	keys, and refreshes decode only selected transcripts. Read-only operations do not
  1819	create or register missing databases and retain the empty-cache fallback for
  1820	transient SQLite failures. Refresh-lock status reads do not wait for the writer
  1821	queue.
  1822	
  1823	The host retains refresh locks, current write authority, pricing context, and
  1824	process-held incognito databases. Incognito transcript bytes stream to the worker
  1825	through bounded frames; the worker never reopens the in-memory database sentinel.
  1826	Cancellation and database closure join native worker work, accepted host effects,
  1827	and refresh-lock cleanup before releasing custody. Atomic pruning retains all
  1828	obsolete-row comparison bytes on the host until its transaction settles; bounded
  1829	SQL batches do not impose an aggregate memory limit. Cache formats, schemas,
  1830	retention, and update behavior are unchanged.
  1831	
  1832	Shared-state database drainage also joins resources registered while an earlier
  1833	resource is closing. Native retirement waits for those resources; failed cleanup
  1834	remains owned for a later explicit retry.
  1835	Maintenance cleanup joins work started by earlier cleanup phases before closing
  1836	the resources it uses. Clients adopted by actor retirement share its cleanup result.
  1837	
  1838	Memory managers admit writes on their exact borrowed agent connection. Provider
  1839	calls and source preparation run before admission; generated-cache and source
  1840	writes recheck their generation, revision, and source predicates after waiting.
  1841	Full reindex publication attaches, replaces, and detaches the completed shadow
  1842	inside one synchronous admitted operation. Manager close drains accepted syncs
  1843	through provider preparation and final writes before releasing the borrow.
  1844	
  1845	Workspace lease claims retain a comparison bound to the original physical state
  1846	database and a unique owner identity. After worker settlement, failed acquisition
  1847	replies and failed cleanup retain bounded in-memory receipts for conditional
  1848	release before another writer enters. Recovery never replays the task, deletes a
  1849	replacement lease, or redirects cleanup to another database. Live and unknown
  1850	owners retain the existing stale-lock checks. Receipt expiry only discards local
  1851	recovery metadata; stored lock fields, retention, and schema versions are unchanged.
  1852	
  1853	Native hook relay bridge persistence runs in the shared-state worker. Publication
  1854	and renewal request the live host's current-registration check inside their write
  1855	transaction. The bridge retains accepted operations through native settlement;
  1856	unregistering joins them before token-owned removal and listener closure. Pruning
  1857	keeps PID liveness checks on the host, then compares each complete candidate with
  1858	the authoritative row in the worker transaction before deletion. Reads retain
  1859	existing-only admission, and all stages of a prune use the captured database
  1860	context. The cold hook CLI retains its separate read-only locator worker.
  1861	
  1862	Browser board-change and deleted-session events discover retained dashboard tabs
  1863	and Stop intents through the shared-state worker. Discovery reads the existing
  1864	`browser.session-tabs` namespace without creating missing state. Browser service
  1865	shutdown joins accepted board-event discovery and reconciliation; replaced
  1866	runtimes discard late discovery results. Registration's alias bootstrap, tab
  1867	mutations, and the final synchronous ownership check before closing a browser
  1868	target retain their existing owners.
  1869	
  1870	Selected library resources read cold pin descriptions and eligible manifests
  1871	through the shared read-only worker. Resource preparation retains its captured
  1872	state root and admission through both reads and file preparation, preserving
  1873	snapshot scopes, selected revision bytes, hidden-pin omission, and the first
  1874	resource failure. Synchronous discovery and borrowed-database readers keep their
  1875	existing contracts. This changes no schema, migration, or persistent data.
  1876	
  1877	### Preserve the data and concurrency contracts
  1878	
  1879	Async device identity loads use the shared-state worker. A first creator runs
  1880	the existing identity owner before database bootstrap, so pending legacy identity
  1881	files still prevent creation. Read-only loads do not create a missing database
  1882	or change its artifacts. Existing Ed25519 keys, first-writer convergence,
  1883	permissions, and Doctor's migration and repair authority remain unchanged.
  1884	Process identity caches retain their existing database-path and identity-key
  1885	scope; warm cached values need no database operation. Schemas and update behavior
  1886	are unchanged; no migration or operator action is required.
  1887	
  1888	Skill Workshop proposal reads, publication, evaluation, rollback metadata, and
  1889	status transitions execute in the existing shared-state worker. Record and event
  1890	writes remain one synchronous transaction, including revision comparisons and
  1891	pending-proposal limits. The host retains filesystem work and the collection and
  1892	target leases through settlement; worker transactions verify every held lease
  1893	before effects and commit. A failed reply is reconciled before discarding a
  1894	staged generation or restoring live files.
  1895	
  1896	Collection history reads and experience-review outcomes use the same worker.
  1897	Doctor awaits legacy proposal imports before deleting their source sidecars.
  1898	Transaction-bound relocation kernels and read-only migration readers retain their
  1899	supplied connections. Proposal generations, schemas, limits, retention, and
  1900	rollback ordering are unchanged.
  1901	
  1902	Task, flow, and Cron receipt execution identity bindings run in the shared-state
  1903	worker. Their synchronous transactions reread the exact live owner rows and
  1904	recheck the caller's current execution authority before mutation and commit.
  1905	Callers capture one database context for each ordered binding sequence and await
  1906	its settlement before continuing or releasing their execution owner. Cron keeps
  1907	receipt, task, then flow order. Metadata remains provenance only; lifecycle,
  1908	collection settings, mismatch reporting, schemas, retention, and update behavior
  1909	are unchanged.
  1910	
  1911	Doctor's local device-token inventory executes in the shared-state worker. The
  1912	detector awaits its result and preserves role ordering, malformed-row omission,
  1913	and best-effort diagnostic behavior. Lint keeps this read in its private active
  1914	state view and joins worker cleanup before retiring that snapshot; source-path
  1915	legacy-file checks retain their separate environment. Device identity creation
  1916	retains its existing owner.
  1917	
  1918	Device pairing lists and lookups execute in the shared-state read-only workers.
  1919	The pairing snapshot cache checks SQLite `data_version` there, including commits
  1920	from a separate CLI connection. Workers project lists and node identity bindings;
  1921	the Gateway installs bindings against the pairing revision without reopening
  1922	SQLite. Historical inspection snapshots never publish live node authority.
  1923	
  1924	Pairing, approval, role-token, bootstrap, and node-surface mutations execute in
  1925	the shared-state writer. Each synchronous transaction reads the authoritative
  1926	rows and obtains current host policy or connection admission before mutation and
  1927	again before commit. Commit receipts publish the revision and changed node
  1928	bindings before callers continue; uncertain outcomes are not replayed. Node
  1929	prompt preparation refreshes the published facts, and Web Push retains pairing
  1930	and subscription admission through network start, releasing both before provider
  1931	completion. APNs registration checks pairing in its worker transaction and
  1932	revalidates the exact live node connection through the same broker admission.
  1933	
  1934	The cutover changes no schema, persisted record representation, config, retention,
  1935	or update behavior. Doctor/import transactions keep their synchronous maintenance
  1936	owner; regular CLI pairing uses the same worker operations as the Gateway.
  1937	
  1938	An adapter must make these contracts explicit and verify them against a real
  1939	database:
  1940	
  1941	| Contract           | Required behavior                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |
  1942	| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
  1943	| Store identity     | Keep global and per-agent ownership, incognito lifetime, quarantine, and disposal explicit. Filesystem paths currently participate in admission and registry identity; replacing a path with a connection string is not sufficient. |
  1944	| Read consistency   | Define whether each operation needs one snapshot or a fresh authoritative reread. Keep ordered, bounded queries and batch enrichment inside that consistency boundary.                                                              |
  1945	| Conditional writes | Preserve exact revision, session generation, writer claim, and lease-owner predicates. A stale or refused mutation must not publish a success result or alter live state.                                                           |
  1946	| Canonical payloads | Preserve serialized transcript and record text where byte identity, replay, or exact JSON comparison is part of the contract. Keep derived query projections separate.                                                              |
  1947	| Scalar decoding    | Decode driver values at the store boundary, including counts, integer ranges, nullable booleans, timestamps, JSON, and binary bytes. Match TypeScript declarations to observed driver values.                                       |
  1948	| Failure and retry  | Define which failures permit retry of the whole operation. Keep external effects outside a retried transaction, and revalidate authority after awaited work.                                                                        |
  1949	
  1950	Kysely's TypeScript types do not convert driver results; the driver determines
  1951	runtime values. See [Kysely data types](https://kysely.dev/docs/recipes/data-types).
  1952	PostgreSQL transactions must use one acquired client, and its default Read
  1953	Committed isolation can give successive statements different snapshots. An
  1954	adapter therefore needs operation-specific isolation and retry decisions, not
  1955	a mechanical replacement of `BEGIN IMMEDIATE`. See
  1956	[node-postgres transactions](https://node-postgres.com/features/transactions)
  1957	and [PostgreSQL isolation](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/transaction-iso.html).
  1958	
  1959	Do not automatically convert canonical JSON text to `jsonb`: PostgreSQL's
  1960	`jsonb` representation changes whitespace, object-key order, and duplicate-key
  1961	handling. A searchable `jsonb` projection would need an explicit design and
  1962	migration decision. See [PostgreSQL JSON types](https://www.postgresql.org/docs/current/datatype-json.html).
  1963	
  1964	Cross-store session handoffs preserve every retained generation, its lineage,
  1965	transcript bytes, physical sequence numbers, and recorded event identities,
  1966	including effective idempotency ownership. Accepted inputs and completion receipts
  1967	use their existing repair owners: queued input becomes interrupted, cancellation
  1968	and consumed-event references survive, and final destination receipts retain
  1969	precedence over retryable attempts. Boards, progress cards, heartbeat outcomes,
  1970	suggestions, participant history, and retained ACP provenance use the logical-node
  1971	repair owner and its existing revision and identity precedence. These copies
  1972	transfer no live execution or membership authority. Source cleanup verifies the physical source and a complete
  1973	destination receipt captured in the copy transaction before deleting each copied
  1974	generation. Unchanged SQLite data versions reuse that verified receipt; any commit
  1975	requires exact revalidation, including input and rewrite-watermark facts. Exact
  1976	node-artifact fingerprints protect source and destination state through entry
  1977	removal. Source deletion checks these node payloads only at entry boundaries;
  1978	historical-generation cleanup keeps its generation-only checks. In-place
  1979	key migrations preserve cold archives when the database and archive directory stay
  1980	the same; archive manifests remain part of their guarded source snapshot. Older exact
  1981	imports could retain a transcript without its identity rows. History readers
  1982	recognize that unindexed prefix and recover display navigation from the stored
  1983	events, including after later appends; they never reconstruct write authority or
  1984	idempotency ownership from JSON. The first new append uses the verified projection
  1985	cursor under the existing write authority, so continuation preserves the imported
  1986	conversation. A full transcript replacement retires that read path by creating a
