     8	
     9	## Preparing for another database backend
    10	
    11	SQLite remains the supported runtime store. Preparation for PostgreSQL should
    12	improve the existing store owners and their tests before adding a driver or
    13	configuration option. The initial target is remote persistence for one Gateway;
    14	multiple active Gateways would require a separate ownership and coordination
    15	design. A shared database alone does not make process-local writer queues,
    16	session lifecycles, or host-owned leases safe across Gateway instances.
    17	
    18	### Keep operations at the owning store
    19	
    20	Callers should request domain operations, such as claiming a cron run or
    21	appending a transcript report, from the store that owns the invariant. That
    22	owner selects and decodes rows, validates current authority, commits changes,
    23	and publishes the result. Avoid exposing a generic SQL callback to application
    24	code or adding an asynchronous wrapper around an existing asynchronous facade.
    25	The plugin KV API already has asynchronous methods over its SQLite owner.
    26	
    27	Worker inference admission, terminal completion, cancellation, and restart
    28	recovery execute in the shared-state worker. The inference owner registers
    29	pending starts before awaiting persistence, retains accepted provider and native
    30	work through cancellation, and keeps local settlement errors separate from the
    31	worker protocol's terminal outcome. Each write keeps its synchronous transaction
    32	and current-authority checks inside the worker admission boundary. The existing
    33	terminal replay keys, JSON payloads, retention limits, schema, and restart
    34	recovery policy remain unchanged.
    35	
    36	Ordinary operator approval lookups, pending replay, verdicts, expiry, and allow-once
    37	consumption execute in the shared-state worker. Lookups and pending scans retain
    38	their expiry and corrupt-row repair transactions; history pages use the read-only
    39	worker. The approval manager retains live authority and decision handoffs, checks
    40	authority at transaction and commit admission, and joins accepted mutations before
    41	retiring their local bindings. Startup orphan closure and pruning remain boot
    42	admission operations. Stored bytes, schemas, retention, and update behavior are
    43	unchanged.
    44	
    45	Captured request authority survives worker waits and is rechecked before lookup,
    46	verdict commit, reconciliation, and response publication. The store shares FIFO
    47	admission across worker operations and the retained v2026.9.4 opaque SDK commit
    48	guard. That explicitly selected compatibility family runs the same kernels in a
    49	native transaction so a guard that uses SQLite never waits on its own worker's
    50	writer lock. It is not a failure fallback. Removing this exception requires an SDK
    51	contract that can require worker-safe guards. History remains on the read-only
    52	worker, with full request checks outside its storage work. Legacy exec/plugin
    53	read and resolution handlers carry the same original request authority through
    54	expiry, prefix lookup, and verdict admission. An unknown verdict outcome gets one
    55	readback under the original physical database admission and captured maintenance,
    56	schema, and coordinator scopes without retrying the write. Replacing that owner
    57	leaves the verdict uncertain even if the replacement contains matching approval
    58	bytes; local settlement rechecks the original admission after readback. Settlement
    59	retains auto-review provenance only from that operation's postcommit receipt and
    60	preserves the unanswered no-route fallback contract. Runtime resolver IDs can be
    61	shared or null; a matching row without a receipt cannot establish auto-review
    62	provenance. Such a verdict stays locally uncertain, while a distinct device or
    63	channel winner settles as an operator decision. A confirmed pending readback
    64	clears an inconclusive attempt. Delayed reconciliation retains the original
    65	database admission until that uncertainty is resolved. Retries recheck that owner
    66	before work and at transaction/commit admission; a pending readback cannot clear
    67	a replaced owner's fence. Cancellation still closes local executable authority
    68	immediately when durable admission is refused.
    69	
    70	Autonomous expiry rearms its existing timer after a definite worker overload or
    71	unavailability refusal. The retry retains the original database, maintenance,
    72	schema, and coordinator owner and checks the unchanged deadline in the same CAS.
    73	Later verdicts and reconciliation retain that pending entry's owner until local
    74	settlement. They cannot adopt a replacement or redirect the write to another store.
    75	Closing or replacing that owner stops the retry. Cleanup failures and uncertain
    76	write outcomes do not authorize replay; ordinary corruption keeps its existing
    77	fail-closed settlement.
    78	
    79	Request config custody follows committed policy publications. Equivalent snapshots
    80	and settings unrelated to approval auth or session routing preserve the request.
    81	Changes to those retained policy facts permanently revoke it, including a change
    82	restored before the next worker check. Handler completion releases its publication
    83	listener; worker commit checks consume the retained revocation fact without loading
    84	config, profiles, or session rows. Native-compatible requests retain the same
    85	publication fence in addition to their synchronous SDK guard.
    86	
    87	Bundled in-process agent callers still compose opaque guards from their run,
    88	profile, and receipt authority owners. Those native-compatible carriers and custom
    89	guards that synchronously read placement, tool, or environment state require a
    90	separate owner migration before the complete approval flow is free of parent-thread SQL.
    91	That migration must prepare authority through those owners and retain live commit
    92	checks; it must not treat internal agent identity as WebSocket authority or drop
    93	the external SDK guard contract.
    94	
    95	Worker environment inventory is a committed, revisioned projection owned by its
    96	store. Startup hydrates through the read-only worker scope; runtime mutations use
    97	the shared-state SQLite worker broker and re-read environment, credential, and
    98	placement authority inside the committing transaction. The broker rechecks live
    99	caller authority before commit, and the store fences changed authority until
   100	committed facts are installed. Diagnostic writes preserve keyed reads only when
   101	the worker proves that every environment and credential field except the error
   102	text and update timestamp is unchanged. Transfer capabilities keep their separate
   103	authority and lifetime checks. List and keyed inventory reads use the projection.
   104	
   105	Bound worker execution identities and delegated approval checks prepare selected placement
   106	facts asynchronously through the existing placement reader. Retained checks
   107	consume the placement owner's live claim incarnation without issuing SQLite
   108	queries. Authority-changing placement kernels publish their existing postimages
   109	at the outer commit before observers; nested rollback discards those changes.
   110	Release and readmission cannot revive an earlier capability, even with identical
   111	claim fields. Compatible draining and retained local claims keep their authority,
   112	while database replacement, restart clearing, or an uncertain transaction owner
   113	revokes it. ACK, workspace metadata, and bundle updates do not independently
   114	revoke claims. Other placement reads, mutations, and their exact in-database checks retain
   115	their existing transaction boundary; moving those mutations and internal opaque
   116	approval carriers to workers remains separate work. External opaque SDK guards,
   117	stored rows, schemas, permissions, and update behavior are unchanged.
   118	
   119	Worker binding captures the complete original session target before preparing its
   120	claim authority. The bound identity and transcript capability retain that same
   121	store, lifecycle revision, and writer identity. Source validation still uses the
   122	session accessor's synchronous row check; moving that separate owner to prepared
   123	async custody remains follow-up work. The retained placement claim checks do not
   124	repeat the placement row read inside that source guard.
   125	
   126	Placement activation and prepared-environment consumption retain their existing
   127	synchronous atomic parent transactions. Node pairing uses its existing write
   128	worker and carries inventory changes in its committed receipt. All three publish
   129	fields already prepared by their transactions through the same inventory owner,
   130	before observers and without another SQLite read. Revisions reserved at commit
   131	admission preserve newer publications when a delayed worker reply supplies the
   132	rest of the committed row. Retention reads bounded pages
   133	in a read-only worker, applies the existing demand policy, and deletes only exact,
   134	still-unreferenced observations in the write worker. Shutdown joins accepted writes;
   135	stored rows, schemas, retention policy, configuration, and update behavior are unchanged.
   136	
   137	Task maintenance awaits global plugin-state expiry in the shared-state worker.
   138	The sweep samples expiry time inside its admitted write transaction and deletes
   139	at most 1,024 rows. Writer waits leave the Gateway event loop available, while
   140	the captured task owner and database lifecycle still authorize the operation.
   141	Maintenance joins the sweep before completing; expiry, storage formats, and
   142	update behavior are unchanged.
   143	
   144	Warm profile ensures read existing email, provider, and Gateway-owner identities
   145	without writer admission. Missing identities and display-name changes recheck
   146	their authoritative rows inside the existing write transaction. Exec authorization
   147	commits batch pending requests in order through the shared-state worker; unchanged
   148	policy snapshots require no write transaction. Changed batches reread policy and
   149	agent-deletion fences before committing, and execution rechecks current authority
   150	against the captured database owner. Coordinator contention stays on the worker
   151	for these commits. Remaining synchronous state writes report coordinator waits
   152	over 100 ms through the transaction diagnostics logger. Schema initialization,
   153	durability, migration, and update behavior are unchanged.
   154	
   155	Sandbox registry lists, point lookups, backend/scope runtime IDs, and browser
   156	registry reads execute in the shared-state read worker. CLI management and
   157	runtime provisioning await the same domain APIs. Reads retain inherited snapshot
   158	and disposable-source scopes, preserve read-only and missing-state behavior, and
   159	join native reader cleanup before returning. Doctor imports legacy registry rows
   160	through the shared-state writer, with host transaction and commit admission under
   161	the captured maintenance scope. Imports retain sequential per-row transactions,
   162	existing-row precedence, and sharded-before-monolithic ordering; source cleanup
   163	waits for durable acknowledgement. Container registry updates, completion, and
   164	removal execute through the same writer, preserving captured inputs, immutable
   165	fields, update/remove ordering, and removal-intent guards. Docker and Podman use
   166	the existing pending/ready state for one-time setup; interrupted setup retains
   167	its container and data and cannot be reused as ready. Legacy entries without a
   168	readiness marker keep their existing behavior. Browser writes, reservation/lock
   169	primitives, removal-intent admission, and currentness callbacks retain their
   170	existing synchronous owners. Existing records need no schema or data migration,
   171	and retention is unchanged.
   172	
   173	Shared-state operations that request host transaction or commit admission acquire
   174	fresh lifecycle coordinator custody on their executing SQLite worker. A live
   175	parent-owned maintenance or native lease still delegates its existing custody.
   176	The waiting job keeps its FIFO position and capacity reservation. Native attempts
   177	use zero busy timeout and asynchronous backoff within the original captured lock
   178	budget; only acquisition retries. The host rechecks current authority during
   179	preparation, before native execution, and at the existing transaction and commit
   180	grants. Cancellation before native execution joins coordinator cleanup without
   181	replaying the command.
   182	
   183	The broker admits up to 128 outstanding requests per worker. A busy worker does
   184	not consume another worker's request capacity. Count-only overflow waits in
   185	FIFO order for up to 10 seconds; queued and retained input across all workers
   186	shares a 256 MiB byte budget.
   187	Byte, message, and store limits continue to refuse immediately. Oversized streamed
   188	inputs still require immediately available admission instead of retaining the
   189	complete input in the waiting queue. Admission timeout
   190	or host drain rejects waiting requests before dispatch; caller cancellation
   191	releases a waiting request, while dispatched writes retain their native outcome.
   192	Maintenance scopes continue to drain accepted work. A rate-limited warning reports
   193	admission queue depth and wait time. Node uses two to eight worker threads based
   194	on available CPUs; Bun retains one worker per store actor.
   195	
   196	Legacy native host writers service the same job's preparation and authority ports
   197	between short coordinator-lock attempts, including path aliases. This lets the
   198	worker finish while the host is inside a synchronous native caller. Successful
   199	worker execution releases its physical coordinator after native settlement and
   200	before result framing; the broker retains operation admission and transport
   201	credits through the complete result. Unsettled native work retains custody until
   202	worker exit. If native coordinator cleanup fails after the command settles, the
   203	same per-job port services bounded result frames while a native host writer waits.
   204	The broker receives the complete outcome, joins worker exit, and reports cleanup
   205	separately without discarding that outcome or replaying the write. Incomplete
   206	result delivery retains its existing unknown-outcome handling. Final publication
   207	and follower dispatch run after synchronous native wait servicing returns.
   208	Preparation refusals retain that port through terminal cleanup replies too.
   209	The shared-state owner retires the exact unavailable actor after accepted callbacks
   210	finish across all clients sharing it, so the next call opens a usable actor without
   211	retrying the prior write.
   212	Nested callbacks return their completed outcomes while further commands on the
   213	failed actor refuse without waiting for the enclosing callback to close itself.
   214	Retirement cleanup failures retain canonical retry custody and report separately
   215	from the completed outcome.
   216	Native host writers, Gateway lifecycle ownership, and source-handle
   217	preparation retain their existing owners. Schemas, retention, and update behavior
   218	are unchanged.
   219	
   220	Outbound queue enqueue writes execute in the shared-state worker. The host captures
   221	canonical JSON custody and its state context before waiting, while the existing
   222	transaction owner preserves namespace conflicts, exact preparation comparisons,
   223	and atomic media-stage consumption. A complete enqueue result remains authoritative
   224	through later worker cleanup. Recorded transaction rollback preserves ordinary
   225	media cleanup and best-effort live sending. When execution may have occurred but no publication
   226	result is available, recovery retains queue custody and staged media; best-effort
   227	sending does not fall back to an independent live send. Native settlement alone
   228	is not evidence that a rejected command did not commit. Random insertion without
   229	a media stage keeps its single upsert inside the same tracked transaction, so an
   230	observed full rollback supplies authoritative nonpublication evidence. Other
   231	rejections without that evidence remain unconfirmed outcomes. Recovery
   232	owns terminal audit publication while custody is retained. Media preparation and
   233	callbacks stay on the host. Media stage creation, cancellation, pruning, stable
   234	preparation checkpoints, and other queue mutations retain their existing owners.
   235	Schemas, retention, and update behavior are unchanged.
   236	
   237	Managed outgoing image metadata lookups and cleanup inventories read through the
   238	shared-state worker, retaining their writable, creating database-open behavior.
   239	Typed columns, ordering, cleanup claims, and original-media references are unchanged.
   240	Downloads retain ticket or owner authorization and current transcript membership;
   241	verified descriptors and post-render thumbnail checks remain in place. Inserts, message-commit
   242	promotion, cleanup claim/deletion transactions, Doctor imports, and native session
   243	metadata reads keep their existing owners and remain separate worker migrations.
   244	
   245	Delivery queue maintenance expires tombstones and reads media custody in the
   246	shared-state worker. Stage expiry retains its existing transaction and unfinished
   247	delivery inventory, including retained migration media. Gateway shutdown joins an
   248	accepted queue sweep through filesystem cleanup, and replacement maintenance waits
   249	for earlier cleanup generations. Each sweep keeps its captured state directory.
   250	Queue and staging formats, retention limits, writable database preparation, and
   251	update behavior are unchanged; send admission and settlement retain their owners.
   252	
   253	Personal repository publication options scan receipts in the shared-state worker.
   254	The reader validates every matching pending receipt in the existing timestamp and
   255	request-ID order, retaining only the latest status. Title and body content remain
   256	in the worker; older corrupt receipts still fail the read. Options recheck current
