   304	summary: "Local command equivalents, shrink-only ratchets, and Crabbox remote proof"
   305	title: "Local checks and Testbox"
   306	read_when:
   307	  - You want to reproduce a CI lane on your own machine
   308	  - You are producing Testbox or Crabbox proof for a pull request
   309	---
   310	
   311	## Local equivalents
   312	
   313	The lint wrapper owns Go resource limits for current CI. It applies them on
   314	hosts with fewer than eight available CPUs or less than 24 GiB of memory,
   315	without applying lint defaults to declaration preparation. Explicit Go settings
   316	remain inherited. Frozen revisions retain the workflow limits because their
   317	wrappers can predate this policy.
   318	
   319	The runtime topology CI job also supplies `GOGC=30` and `GOMEMLIMIT=3GiB`
   320	defaults to `pnpm check:architecture`, preserving caller overrides. Both import
   321	cycle checks and the remaining architecture checks inherit these settings.
   322	The memory target is soft: a four-CPU, 15.42-GiB Testbox comparison on Node
   323	24.21.0 measured peak checker/compiler process-group RSS of 14.24 GiB without
   324	the defaults and 11.31 GiB with them; peak swap use fell from 4.74 GiB to zero.
   325	Two hosted runners shut down during the native Madge check, but their logs did
   326	not establish a kernel OOM. These measurements support reducing memory pressure,
   327	not a hard 3-GiB RSS cap or a confirmed cause for those shutdowns.
   328	
   329	On serial hosts with less than 24 GiB of memory, full lint runs core targets in
   330	five disjoint batches and plugins in smaller chunks. These runs retain the same
   331	type-aware rules and TypeScript configuration while bounding checker caches.
   332	Automatic Linux CI on at least four CPUs and 15 GiB of verified memory capacity
   333	uses sixteen-directory plugin chunks to amortize type-graph startup. Capacity
   334	includes physical RAM and ancestor cgroup limits. Smaller or unknown capacity,
   335	local runs, Windows, explicit plugin stripes, and explicit serial selections keep
   336	eight-directory chunks. Explicit split-core and parallel execution selections
   337	remain unchanged.
   338	
   339	Oxlint's type-aware backend discovers `src/tsconfig.json` and `ui/tsconfig.json`
   340	separately. Both inherit the root compiler options, include shared ambient
   341	declarations, and follow imported dependencies. Every existing lint target and
   342	rule still runs, including source CommonJS test preloads. The CLI `--tsconfig`
   343	option controls import resolution; it does not replace these discovery projects.
   344	
   345	Current CI core-test rows combine their paired stripes into one queue of fresh
   346	compiler processes, with the same two-child limit. Each independent graph runs
   347	in incremental project mode: solution-build mode can miss an added root whose
   348	timestamp predates restored build information. Frozen targets keep their
   349	original stripe invocations. Per-graph elapsed times appear in the job log.
   350	
   351	The test-type jobs restore their own `.artifacts/tsgo-cache` state across runs.
   352	Exact cache keys separate compiler/dependency/configuration versions and CI rows.
   353	Rows reuse incremental state across source revisions only when those inputs match.
   354	Compiler, dependency, or configuration changes start with an empty cache: updating
   355	older state can cost substantially more than a fresh check. The compiler still
   356	validates current roots, options, source, and dependency contents after restoration.
   357	The central changed-graph queue also
   358	restores the five core stripe caches that full runs publish. Every selected graph
   359	still runs after a hit. Pull requests only restore state, while the existing trusted cache writer policy controls
   360	publication after successful checks. Cache-off and frozen-target runs retain
   361	their original behavior. Lint programs do not share these compiler caches.
   362	
   363	Oxlint keeps `eslint/no-redeclare` enabled for JavaScript. For `.ts`, `.tsx`,
   364	`.mts`, and `.cts`, `tsgo` owns declaration validity, including intentional
   365	type/value pairs with the same public name. `eslint/no-var` remains enabled
   366	for all source formats; the compiler does not reject every `var` redeclaration.
   367	
   368	`eslint/no-eval` rejects direct and indirect evaluation by default. Only
   369	`extensions/qa-lab/src/web-runtime.ts` allows indirect evaluation, because QA
   370	scenario scripts need page-global declaration semantics that Playwright's
   371	expression evaluation does not preserve. Direct evaluation remains an error
   372	there. Tests that execute emitted browser scripts use isolated `node:vm`
   373	contexts instead of process-global evaluation.
   374	
   375	```bash
   376	pnpm changed:lanes                            # inspect the local changed-lane classifier for origin/main...HEAD
   377	pnpm check:changed                            # smart local check gate: changed formatting/typecheck/lint/guards by boundary lane
   378	pnpm check                                    # fast local gate: prod tsgo + sharded lint + parallel fast guards
   379	pnpm check:test-types
   380	pnpm check:timed                              # same gate with per-stage timings
   381	pnpm build:strict-smoke
   382	pnpm check:architecture
   383	pnpm test:gateway:watch-regression
   384	OPENCLAW_TUI_PTY_INCLUDE_LOCAL=1 node scripts/run-vitest.mjs run --config test/vitest/vitest.tui-pty.config.ts
   385	pnpm test                                     # vitest tests
   386	pnpm test:changed                             # cheap smart changed Vitest targets
   387	pnpm test:ui                                  # Control UI unit/browser suite
   388	pnpm ui:i18n:check                            # generated Control UI locale parity (release gate)
   389	pnpm native:i18n:baseline                     # update source-owned native extraction inventory
   390	pnpm native:i18n:verify                       # source inventory + Android/Apple localization safety
   391	pnpm native:i18n:check                        # strict local translated/platform-generated parity
   392	pnpm test:channels
   393	pnpm test:contracts:channels
   394	pnpm check:docs                               # docs format + lint + broken links
   395	pnpm build                                    # build dist when CI artifact/smoke checks matter
   396	pnpm ios:build                                # generate and build the iOS app project
   397	pnpm ci:timings                               # summarize the latest origin/main push CI run
   398	pnpm ci:timings:recent                        # compare recent successful main CI runs
   399	pnpm ci:timings:trend                         # 72h main baseline; latest 12h versus prior 12h
   400	node scripts/ci-run-timings.mjs <run-id>      # summarize wall time, queue time, and slowest jobs
   401	node scripts/ci-run-timings.mjs --latest-main # ignore issue/comment noise and choose origin/main push CI
   402	node scripts/ci-run-timings.mjs --recent 10   # compare recent successful main CI runs
   403	node scripts/ci-run-timings.mjs --trend-hours 72 --compare-hours 12 --detail-runs 100 --output .artifacts/ci-timings/trend.json
   404	pnpm test:perf:groups --full-suite --allow-failures --output .artifacts/test-perf/baseline-before.json
   405	pnpm test:perf:groups:compare .artifacts/test-perf/baseline-before.json .artifacts/test-perf/after-agent.json
   406	pnpm test:startup:memory
   407	pnpm test:extensions:memory -- --json .artifacts/openclaw-performance/source/mock-provider/extension-memory.json
   408	pnpm perf:kova:summary --report .artifacts/kova/reports/mock-provider/report.json --output .artifacts/kova/summary.md
   409	```
   410	
   411	Native locale checks remain strict locally. With `CI=true` or `CI=1`, the native
   412	check warns about obsolete translation IDs and Android generated rows awaiting
   413	the serialized locale refresh. Android warnings require canonical, unreferenced,
   414	noninterpolated obsolete rows whose removal leaves every other byte unchanged.
   415	Missing active translations or resources, invalid placeholders or artifact
   416	syntax, and other generated-output differences remain blocking. Generator sync
   417	and the standalone Android and Apple checks retain their strict behavior.
   418	
   419	The Gateway watch regression check starts its idle CPU window only after readiness
   420	and the settle period. Startup and early-exit failures still fail the check. Missing
   421	CPU samples from an otherwise valid window fail measurement; whole-run CPU is
   422	reported separately and never compared with the idle thresholds.
   423	
   424	The check joins the timed watch process and its output before taking the post-run
   425	snapshot or removing its private HOME. If cleanup cannot be confirmed, the check
   426	fails and retains that HOME for inspection; `watch.home.txt` in the output
   427	directory records its path.
   428	
   429	The native source gate covers catalog-owned macOS, iOS, and shared Apple source
   430	roots. Linux-runnable source extraction requires explicit typed localized formats
   431	(for example, `String(format: String(localized: "Expires in %lld minutes"), minutes)`
   432	for an `Int`) instead of arbitrary Swift interpolation. Constrained inflected
   433	count resources are supported on both platforms. Use explicit verbatim text for
   434	user, system, or already-localized data.
   435	
   436	For staged checks, `pnpm check:changed --staged` compares the index with `HEAD`.
   437	Use `pnpm check:changed --staged --base <commit>` to compare the index with an
   438	explicit commit, including during a pending merge. Path selection, package
   439	classification, and the staged ratchets use that same base. Without `--staged`,
   440	the default comparison base remains `origin/main`.
   441	
   442	Delegated staged checks carry the selected paths and comparison base to the
   443	remote checker. Crabbox synchronizes working-tree files, not the local Git
   444	index, so remote results describe those materialized files rather than an exact
   445	copy of the staged snapshot. Keep the intended proof files consistent before
   446	using that route.
   447	
   448	## Surface ratchets
   449	
   450	Size, length, count, and measured performance limits are errors locally and
   451	warnings in GitHub Actions. `scripts/lib/check-limits.mts` owns this decision
   452	using GitHub's `GITHUB_ACTIONS=true` signal. `CI=1` alone does not soften checks:
   453	local test runners and delegated local checks also set it. Each CI violation
   454	emits a file-associated GitHub warning at column zero and a job-summary entry.
   455	Docker proof wrappers carry the signal and relay their summaries to the runner.
   456	
   457	Oxlint keeps configured line caps and exclusions: 700 counted lines for ordinary
   458	TypeScript, 800 for JavaScript modules, and 1,000 for tests, with the existing
   459	explicit overrides. Local lint reports errors. CI uses a temporary configuration
   460	that changes only enabled size-rule severity to warning. SwiftLint likewise
   461	reports native length, nesting, complexity, and count limits as CI warnings;
   462	semantic lint errors remain blocking.
   463	
   464	`pnpm check`, `pnpm check:changed`, and `pnpm check:line-cap-ratchet --base <commit>`
   465	reject new over-cap files and growth above inherited over-cap debt locally. The
   466	PR `checks-fast-baseline-ratchets` job reports this growth as warnings. Renames
   467	compare against the old path; unchanged or shrinking over-cap files pass the
   468	ratchet. Measurement uses oxlint's actual caps and comment/blank-line exclusions
   469	and neutralizes suppression directives only in temporary measurement copies.
   470	Main-push CI does not run the PR growth comparison; ordinary lint still reports
   471	all unsuppressed over-cap files.
   472	
   473	The max-lines suppression inventory and environment-variable count budget use
   474	the same severity policy. After removing a suppression, remove its stale entry
   475	from `config/max-lines-baseline.txt`, or run
   476	`pnpm check:max-lines-ratchet --prune`. Keep the inventory shrinking; warning
   477	status does not authorize new suppressions or higher caps. The environment
   478	budget counts distinct `OPENCLAW_*` names in production `src/`, `packages/`, and
   479	`extensions/` source, excluding tests and QA Lab. Update
   480	`config/env-var-count-budget.txt` when cleanup reduces that count.
   481	
   482	The policy also covers numeric bundle, declaration, package, startup memory,
   483	CPU, timing, and test-root budgets. Measurements and thresholds are unchanged.
   484	When a file or artifact exceeds a cap, extract a coherent module or investigate
   485	the added cost. Do not trim coverage, disable rules, or raise thresholds just
   486	to silence a warning.
   487	
   488	Correctness checks stay blocking, including types, semantic lint, blanket lint
   489	disables, assertion safety, missing or malformed evidence, failed commands,
   490	forbidden eager imports, and exactly-once ownership. Public SDK inventories and
   491	generated configuration-schema baselines remain contract guards. Runner matrix
   492	caps protect shared runner-registration capacity and remain blocking. Explicit
   493	benchmark qualification verdicts retain their requested acceptance criteria.
   494	
   495	## Local check gates and changed routing
   496	
   497	### Config baseline count ratchet
   498	
   499	`pnpm config:docs:check` rejects undocumented config-surface growth and corrupt or stale count snapshots. When a reviewed product change intentionally adds schema paths, run `pnpm config:docs:gen`, inspect the core/channel/plugin count deltas and generated SHA-256 files, and commit the conscious baseline bump with the schema, help, labels, migration, and tests. Do not hand-edit the counts file to bypass the ratchet.
   500	
   501	Config authors must also tier new leaves for Settings. Add `advanced: false` or
   502	`advanced: true` at the leaf, or place the key beneath an ancestor whose tier
   503	all descendants should inherit. Unclassified roots fail the schema quality
   504	test with copy-paste stubs; paths without an ancestor are advanced by default.
   505	The curated common-leaf snapshot makes intentional tier changes visible in
   506	review.
   507	
   508	Local changed-lane logic lives in `scripts/changed-lanes.mjs` and is executed by `scripts/check-changed.mjs`. That local check gate is stricter about architecture boundaries than the broad CI platform scope:
   509	
   510	- core production changes run core prod and core test typecheck plus core lint/guards;
   511	- core test-only changes run only core test typecheck plus core lint;
   512	- root TypeScript tests and support files run root test typecheck plus targeted type-aware lint within `test/tsconfig/tsconfig.test.root.json`; the discoverable `test/tsconfig.json` inherits that source-only program. It includes `.ts`, `.tsx`, `.d.mts`, and `.d.cts`, not ordinary `.mts`/`.cts`; fixtures and built-artifact Docker clients stay outside targeted root lint;
   513	- extension production changes run extension prod and extension test typecheck plus extension lint;
   514	- extension test-only changes run extension test typecheck plus extension lint;
   515	- bundled channel manifests, package metadata, config schemas, UI hints, and generator owners also run the bundled channel config metadata drift check;
   516	- config schema/help, bundled plugin metadata, relative-import dependencies of source schema entries, generator/selector owners, and tracked config baseline changes run `pnpm config:docs:check`, including baseline files mixed with ordinary docs; all-lane and release metadata plans include it once;
   517	- public Plugin SDK or plugin-contract changes expand to extension typecheck because extensions depend on those core contracts (Vitest extension sweeps stay explicit test work);
   518	- release metadata-only version bumps run targeted version/config/root-dependency checks;
   519	- unknown root/config changes fail safe to all check lanes.
   520	
   521	JavaScript and TypeScript changes under `src/`, `extensions/`, `ui/`, `packages/`,
   522	`scripts/`, and `test/` also run the existing full unused-export audit.
   523	Import-only edits and deleted source or test files can orphan exports in
   524	unchanged files.
   525	
   526	Schema dependency selection reuses the local relative-import graph, including re-exports and deleted leaf paths still referenced by surviving source. Shared SDK channel UI-hint and secret-input schema owners, plus the workspace sensitive-URL hint owner, are explicit roots across alias boundaries. Edits to their SDK facades are also selected without traversing unrelated facade runtime dependencies. This is not universal alias or computed-import resolution.
   527	
   528	Local changed-test routing lives in `scripts/test-projects.test-support.mts` and is intentionally cheaper than `check:changed`: direct test edits run themselves, source edits prefer explicit mappings, then sibling tests and import-graph dependents. Shared group-room delivery config is one of the explicit mappings: changes to the group visible-reply config, source reply delivery mode, or the message-tool system prompt route through the core reply tests plus Discord and Slack delivery regressions so a shared default change fails before the first PR push. Use `OPENCLAW_TEST_CHANGED_BROAD=1 pnpm test:changed` only when the change is harness-wide enough that the cheap mapped set is not a trustworthy proxy.
   529	
   530	## Testbox validation
   531	
   532	Crabbox is the repo-owned remote-box wrapper for maintainer Linux proof. Agent
   533	sessions run trusted development tests, changed gates, typecheck/lint, and
   534	builds locally by default. They use Crabbox when the environment is part of the
   535	proof: clean-machine, install/package, Docker, E2E, live, desktop, cross-OS, or
   536	CI-parity work, or when the operator explicitly requests remote proof. Crabbox
   537	is not generic compute offload. `.crabbox.yaml` defaults remote proof to
   538	`blacksmith-testbox`. Its configured workflow hydrates provider and agent
   539	credentials, so untrusted contributor or fork code must use secretless fork CI
   540	or sanitized direct AWS Crabbox instead.
   541	The wrapper uses the bundled Crabbox plugin's binary manager. OpenClaw supports
   542	the current Crabbox CLI contract, starting at 0.56.0. If the selected binary is
   543	missing or older, the plugin installs a verified current release in its own
   544	managed directory before provider discovery or lease work. It leaves the original
   545	binary untouched. Provider readiness and broker authentication still determine
   546	which configured backend can run the proof.
   547	The check workflow hydrates its pinned dispatch commit with a depth-1 checkout;
   548	the changed gate later reconstructs the exact merge base and synced final tree.
   549	Its outer GitHub job defaults to 240 minutes, matching the native full-test
   550	gate's four-hour Testbox lease envelope. Manual dispatches can override
   551	`timeout_minutes`; the lease TTL and individual test deadlines remain separate
   552	limits.
   553	Sanitized AWS runs set `CRABBOX_ENV_ALLOW=CI`, pass
   554	`--no-hydrate`, and use a fresh temporary remote `HOME`; this prevents the repo
   555	`OPENCLAW_*` allowlist and existing auth profiles from reaching untrusted code.
   556	They use a newly warmed lease dedicated to that untrusted source, never a
   557	trusted or previously hydrated lease. Launch an installed trusted Crabbox
   558	binary from a clean trusted `main` checkout and fetch only the remote PR with
   559	`--fresh-pr`; never execute the untrusted checkout's wrapper or config locally.
   560	Unset `CRABBOX_AWS_INSTANCE_PROFILE` and fail closed unless resolved
   561	`aws.instanceProfile` is empty. Before any install/test, use trusted
   562	absolute-path tools to require an IMDSv2 token, prove the IAM credentials
   563	endpoint returns 404, and compare remote `git rev-parse HEAD` to the full
   564	reviewed PR head SHA. Bind the lease to that SHA and stop/rewarm on head change.
   565	Upload trusted `scripts/crabbox-untrusted-bootstrap.sh` from clean `main`
   566	alongside `--fresh-pr`; it installs pinned Node/pnpm, verifies the SHA and
   567	package-manager pin, isolates `HOME`, installs dependencies, then executes the
   568	requested test.
   569	When an image supplies `/opt/crabbox/toolchain-archives`, the bootstrap copies
   570	the matching Node, pnpm wrapper, and pnpm native archives into private temporary
   571	storage and verifies the copied bytes against digests in the trusted script.
   572	It still extracts fresh Node and Corepack installations on every invocation;
   573	existing executables, adjacent checksums, and completion markers are not trust
   574	anchors. Missing or invalid cached archives use the authenticated download path.
   575	A candidate cannot advance the package-manager pin; update the trusted bootstrap
   576	and its digest anchors when advancing the toolchain.
   577	
   578	Trusted Linux hydration uses the shared Node compatibility selector and can
   579	seed a job-private Corepack home from the same authenticated pnpm archives.
   580	The shared setup action also carries authenticated pnpm archives in its warmed
   581	store, so hosted and Blacksmith jobs can bootstrap before dependency installation
   582	without downloading pnpm again. These archives do not replace the frozen-lockfile
   583	dependency install.
   584	
   585	With `install-bun: "true"`, `setup-node-env` can also reuse the original pinned
   586	Bun 1.4.2 ZIPs from `/opt/crabbox/toolchain-archives` on Linux glibc x64.
   587	It authenticates a private copy before extracting a fresh job-private `bun`
   588	and `bunx`, then publishes their directory after the Node PATH entry.
   589	The baseline archive is the default; the optimized x64 archive requires AVX
   590	and AVX2 evidence for every visible guest CPU. Missing CPU evidence uses baseline.
   591	A missing matching archive or an unsupported platform keeps the pinned npm
   592	installation path. A present corrupt or malformed archive fails setup instead
   593	of falling back to an existing Bun. `install-bun: "false"` skips both paths;
   594	it does not remove Bun already supplied by the runner. This reuse does not
   595	cover ARM, musl, or the untrusted bootstrap.
   596	
   597	Unset all `CRABBOX_TAILSCALE*` overrides, force `--network public
   598	--tailscale=false`, clear exit-node/LAN flags, and require `crabbox inspect` to
   599	report public networking with no Tailscale state before uploading any script.
   600	Owned AWS/Hetzner capacity also remains the fallback for Blacksmith outages,
   601	quota issues, or explicit owned-capacity testing.
   602	
   603	For an explicitly authorized admin-only PR landing fallback, set
   604	`OPENCLAW_PR_GATES_REMOTE=crabbox-aws` before `scripts/pr prepare-gates`.
   605	The mode does not replace the default hosted aggregate gate. After the exact
   606	prep head is pushed, the wrapper synchronously dispatches the protected-main
   607	publisher. That trusted workflow checksum-installs Crabbox v0.46, resolves its
   608	service principal through `/v1/whoami`, then runs sanitized brokered AWS with
   609	`umask 022`, the canonical untrusted bootstrap, `pnpm build`, `pnpm check`, and
   610	a fail-closed PR-derived test plan. The existing changed-test owner evaluates
   611	every executable changed path independently and must resolve each one to
   612	concrete matched test files; broad fallback, skipped paths, config targets,
   613	deleted executable paths, and partial plans are refused. Explicit docs and
   614	`AGENTS.md`/`CLAUDE.md` instruction surfaces may produce a zero-test plan.
   615	The exact PR base SHA, head SHA, bootstrap hash, and deterministic plan digest
   616	are bound into the canonical command. The publisher streams a launcher through
   617	Crabbox's `--script-stdin`. Short broker arguments bind the head SHA and the
   618	bootstrap, canonical command, and launcher hashes. The AWS lease uses a 90-minute idle timeout
   619	and 240-minute TTL. The `pr-crabbox-gate-publisher.yml` workflow accepts an open draft
   620	because proof runs during prepare-push, then rereads the live same-repository
   621	PR and the exact active organization-admin membership object using the repo-native
   622	GitHub App token with `Members(read)` (the repository-scoped workflow token is
   623	not treated as org authority), validates its newly created authenticated broker
   624	run under the same service token, ordered complete events, independently rebuilt
   625	canonical command and launcher upload hash, and
   626	publishes the distinct `openclaw/crabbox-gate` only for the exact proven
   627	base/head/plan binding. The publisher also proves that the PR base is the merge
   628	base of its immutable protected-main workflow SHA and adds that workflow SHA to
   629	the strict check summary. Before and after the remote run, it proves that a
   630	candidate live `main` is identical to or descended from that workflow SHA, then
   631	rereads the ref and requires the candidate to remain unchanged. A descendant
   632	advance during the long remote run is allowed; movement inside either
   633	comparison-and-reread window fails closed.
   634	Retained broker logs are validated when non-empty but are optional because
   635	released Crabbox v0.46 can report zero retained log bytes after a successful
   636	run. Only after the publisher and exact-head check succeed does the local
   637	wrapper derive `.local/gates.env` provider/run/lease/URL recovery metadata from
   638	the trusted summary; those fields are not publication authority.
   639	
   640	The fallback never replaces or republishes `openclaw/ci-gate`. Native merge
   641	verification still rejects draft PRs and permits the server ruleset bypass only
   642	when the Crabbox check is
   643	completed successfully by GitHub Actions on the prepared SHA, its bound workflow
   644	SHA is an ancestor of a stable final live protected-main snapshot, the authenticated
   645	actor is still an active organization admin, and the sole unsatisfied required
   646	check is the normal CI gate with a recognized hosted-runner infrastructure
   647	failure represented by GitHub-owned job metadata with no executed workflow
   648	steps and no assigned `runner_name`. Job logs are never authority because PR
   649	code controls their text. Missing or mismatched checks, cancellation,
   650	action-required or stale conclusions, an assigned runner, any failed or executed
   651	workflow step, unknown runner backends, pending contexts, and additional
   652	required-check failures remain blocking. Only workflow `startup_failure` or an
   653	unacquired zero-step hosted job with `failure`/`timed_out` qualifies. The native
   654	flow repeats the full bypass verification immediately before the admin squash
   655	request and pins the prepared head with `--match-head-commit`. GitHub exposes
   656	no expected-base-OID merge precondition, so the final main read minimizes but
   657	cannot atomically eliminate a base movement race. Landing proof must compare
   658	the squash parent with that final main snapshot, not the older workflow SHA.
   659	The Crabbox merge path stores this comparison in
   660	`.local/merge-crabbox-parent-audit.json`, includes it in the completion comment,
   661	and reports any intervening main movement after the already-completed merge
   662	without claiming atomic prevention.
   663	
   664	Agents do not pre-warm for anticipated work. Acquire a Testbox lazily when the
   665	first environment-sensitive command is ready, reuse the returned `tbx_...` id
   666	for later remote commands, sync the current checkout on every run, and stop it
   667	before handoff.
   668	
   669	Crabbox-backed Blacksmith runs warm, claim, sync, run, report, and clean up
   670	one-shot Testboxes. Native Blacksmith owns synchronization; Crabbox's direct
   671	SSH sync controls and mass-deletion sanity checks do not run on this delegated
   672	path.
   673	
   674	Crabbox also terminates a local Blacksmith CLI invocation that stays in the
   675	sync phase for more than five minutes without post-sync output. Set
   676	`CRABBOX_BLACKSMITH_SYNC_TIMEOUT_MS=0` to disable that guard, or use a larger
   677	millisecond value for unusually large local diffs.
   678	
   679	Before a first run, check the wrapper from the repo root:
   680	
   681	```bash
   682	node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs run --help | sed -n '1,120p'
   683	```
   684	
   685	The repo wrapper validates the selected Crabbox binary and provider before running. In Codex worktrees or linked/sparse checkouts, avoid the local `pnpm crabbox:run` script because pnpm may reconcile dependencies before Crabbox starts; invoke the node wrapper directly instead:
   686	
   687	```bash
   688	node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs run --provider blacksmith-testbox --timing-json --shell -- "pnpm test <path-or-filter>"
   689	```
   690	
   691	A supported sibling or `PATH` binary can run directly. The wrapper automatically
   692	replaces an outdated selection with the plugin-managed binary; rebuilding the
   693	sibling checkout is no longer a prerequisite for proof.
   694	
   695	The `blacksmith:` block in `.crabbox.yaml` already pins the org, workflow, job, and ref defaults, so the explicit flags below are optional. Explicit clean-machine changed-gate parity:
   696	
   697	```bash
   698	pnpm crabbox:run -- --provider blacksmith-testbox \
   699	  --blacksmith-org openclaw \
   700	  --blacksmith-workflow .github/workflows/ci-check-testbox.yml \
   701	  --blacksmith-job check \
   702	  --blacksmith-ref main \
   703	  --idle-timeout 90m \
   704	  --ttl 240m \
   705	  --timing-json \
   706	  --shell -- \
   707	  "corepack pnpm check:changed"
   708	```
   709	
   710	Focused test rerun when clean-machine behavior is part of the proof:
   711	
   712	```bash
   713	pnpm crabbox:run -- --provider blacksmith-testbox \
   714	  --idle-timeout 90m \
   715	  --ttl 240m \
   716	  --timing-json \
   717	  --shell -- \
   718	  "corepack pnpm test <path-or-filter>"
   719	```
   720	
   721	Full suite on an explicitly requested clean machine:
   722	
   723	```bash
   724	pnpm crabbox:run -- --provider blacksmith-testbox \
   725	  --idle-timeout 90m \
   726	  --ttl 240m \
   727	  --timing-json \
   728	  --shell -- \
   729	  "corepack pnpm test"
   730	```
   731	
   732	Read the final JSON summary. The useful fields are `provider`, `leaseId`,
   733	`syncDelegated`, `exitCode`, `commandMs`, and `totalMs`. For delegated
   734	Blacksmith Testbox runs, the Crabbox wrapper exit code and JSON summary are the
   735	command result. The linked GitHub Actions run owns hydration and keepalive; it
   736	can finish as `cancelled` when the Testbox is stopped externally after the SSH
   737	command has already returned. Treat that as a cleanup/status artifact unless
   738	the wrapper `exitCode` is non-zero or the command output shows a failed test.
   739	One-shot Blacksmith-backed Crabbox runs should stop the Testbox automatically;
   740	if a run is interrupted or cleanup is unclear, inspect live boxes and stop only
   741	the boxes you created:
   742	
   743	```bash
   744	blacksmith testbox list --all
   745	blacksmith testbox status --id <tbx_id>
   746	blacksmith testbox stop --id <tbx_id>
   747	```
   748	
   749	Use reuse only when you intentionally need multiple commands on the same hydrated box:
   750	
   751	```bash
   752	node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs run --provider blacksmith-testbox --id <tbx_id> --timing-json --shell -- "corepack pnpm test <path-or-filter>"
   753	pnpm crabbox:stop -- <tbx_id>
   754	```
   755	
   756	Reuse the lease, not stale source. Blacksmith Testbox owns sync, including
   757	reused `--id` runs. Do not pass `--no-sync`: the wrapper rejects it before
   758	lease handling or delegation. A fingerprint cache hit is not a no-sync guarantee.
   759	
   760	Sync success is not proof of source identity. Verify the materialized Git tree
   761	before exact-candidate proof. Keep QA evidence outside the synced checkout and
   762	download it before another run. Do not bypass security exclusions, accept a
   763	mismatched tree, or silently switch providers.
   764	
   765	Untrusted contributor/fork code must use
   766	`CRABBOX_ENV_ALLOW=CI`, `--provider aws --no-hydrate`, and a fresh
   767	temporary remote `HOME` for every command; install dependencies inside that
   768	sanitized command before testing. Reuse only a newly warmed lease dedicated to
   769	the same untrusted source; never a trusted or previously hydrated lease. Never
   770	execute the untrusted checkout's wrapper or config locally: launch the installed
   771	trusted Crabbox binary from clean trusted `main` and pass `--fresh-pr` on every
   772	run. Keep `CRABBOX_AWS_INSTANCE_PROFILE` unset, reject a non-empty resolved
   773	instance profile, require a trusted remote IMDS no-role proof, and verify the
   774	reviewed head SHA before install/test. Bind the lease to that SHA; stop and
   775	rewarm after any head change. If no remote PR exists, use secretless fork CI.
   776	Never select `hydrate-github` or the credential-hydrated Blacksmith workflow
   777	for untrusted source.
   778	
   779	If Crabbox is the broken layer but Blacksmith itself works, use direct
   780	Blacksmith only for diagnostics such as `list`, `status`, and cleanup. Fix the
   781	Crabbox path before treating a direct Blacksmith run as maintainer proof.
   782	
   783	If `blacksmith testbox list --all` and `blacksmith testbox status` work but new
   784	warmups sit `queued` with no IP or Actions run URL after a couple of minutes,
   785	treat it as Blacksmith provider, queue, billing, or org-limit pressure. Stop the
   786	queued ids you created, avoid starting more Testboxes, and move the proof to the
   787	owned Crabbox capacity path below while someone checks the Blacksmith dashboard,
   788	billing, and org limits.
   789	
   790	Escalate to owned Crabbox capacity only when Blacksmith is down, quota-limited, missing the needed environment, or owned capacity is explicitly the goal:
   791	
   792	```bash
   793	CRABBOX_CAPACITY_REGIONS=eu-west-1,eu-west-2,eu-central-1,us-east-1,us-west-2 \
   794	  pnpm crabbox:warmup -- --provider aws --class standard --market on-demand --idle-timeout 90m
   795	pnpm crabbox:hydrate -- --provider aws --id <cbx_id-or-slug>
   796	pnpm crabbox:run -- --provider aws --id <cbx_id-or-slug> --timing-json --shell -- "pnpm check:changed"
   797	pnpm crabbox:stop -- --provider aws <cbx_id-or-slug>
   798	```
   799	
   800	Under AWS pressure, avoid `class=beast` unless the task really needs 48xlarge-class CPU. A `beast` request starts at 192 vCPUs and is the easiest way to trip regional EC2 Spot or On-Demand Standard quota. The repo-owned `.crabbox.yaml` defaults to `class: standard`, on-demand market, and `capacity.hints: true` so brokered AWS leases print selected region/market, quota pressure, Spot fallback, and high-pressure class warnings. Use `fast` for heavier broad checks, `large` only after standard/fast are not enough, and `beast` only for exceptional CPU-bound lanes such as full-suite or all-plugin Docker matrices, explicit release/blocker validation, or high-core performance profiling. Do not use `beast` for `pnpm check:changed`, focused tests, docs-only work, ordinary lint/typecheck, small E2E repros, or Blacksmith outage triage. Use `--market on-demand` for capacity diagnosis so Spot market churn is not mixed into the signal.
   801	
   802	`.crabbox.yaml` owns provider, sync, and GitHub Actions hydration defaults. Crabbox sync never transfers `.git`, so the hydrated Actions checkout keeps its own remote Git metadata instead of syncing maintainer-local remotes and object stores, and the repo config additionally excludes local runtime/build artifacts (such as `.artifacts` and test reports) that should never be transferred. `.github/workflows/crabbox-hydrate.yml` owns checkout, Node/pnpm setup, `origin/main` fetch, and the non-secret environment handoff for owned-cloud `crabbox run --id <cbx_id>` commands.
   803	
   804	Linux hydration keeps physical workspace `node_modules` directories and pnpm's
   805	default `.pnpm` virtual store. Only the package-content store uses the persistent
   806	`/var/cache/crabbox/pnpm/store` volume; its fallback lives at
   807	`.cache/openclaw-pnpm-store` beside the workspace dependencies. Ordinary POSIX
   808	sync preserves these ignored directories, so frozen reinstalls and later build
   809	commands use the same owned install. Hydration checks the tooling loader before
   810	marking the lease ready. When rehydrating an older lease, the workflow retires
   811	only its former root links to `/var/tmp/openclaw-pnpm/node_modules` or
   812	`${XDG_CACHE_HOME:-$RUNNER_TEMP/cache}/openclaw/pnpm/install/node_modules` before
   813	installing physical workspace dependencies, including links whose runner cache
   814	was already cleared. It preserves external package caches and unrelated
   815	dependency links.
   816	
   817	Native Windows daemon hydration retains its external dependency junction because
   818	released Crabbox native Windows delete-sync replaces workspace contents. Move
   819	that route to physical workspace dependencies only with a Crabbox sync version
   820	that preserves generated dependency directories.
