     1	---
     2	summary: "Gateway service, pairing, security, workspace status, auth, health, and supervisor checks (checks 8-17)"
     3	title: "Gateway, service, and security checks"
     4	read_when:
     5	  - Doctor reports a gateway service, supervisor, pairing, or auth problem
     6	  - You are changing gateway service migrations or runtime diagnostics
     7	---
     8	
     9	Checks 8-17 cover gateway service migrations, device pairing, security
    10	warnings, workspace status, gateway auth and health, and supervisors.
    11	
    12	## Checks 8-17
    13	
    14	<AccordionGroup>
    15	  <Accordion title="8. Gateway service migrations and cleanup hints">
    16	    Run `openclaw doctor` interactively to review legacy gateway services (launchd/systemd/schtasks) and confirm supported cleanup. Explicit repair maintenance skips this separate cleanup flow. Removal is reported separately from installation; use `openclaw gateway install` when the intended native service is missing. Doctor can also scan for extra gateway-like services and print cleanup hints. Profile-named OpenClaw gateway services are considered first-class and are not flagged as "extra."
    17	
    18	    Linux user-service cleanup preserves the unit file if stopping or disabling the service fails. An interrupted status probe does not permit file-only removal; that fallback is reported only when `systemctl` is unavailable.
    19	
    20	    On Linux, if the user-level gateway service is missing but a system-level OpenClaw gateway service exists, doctor does not install a second user-level service automatically. Inspect with `openclaw gateway status --deep` or `openclaw doctor --deep`, then remove the duplicate or set `OPENCLAW_SERVICE_REPAIR_POLICY=external` when a system supervisor owns the gateway lifecycle.
    21	
    22	  </Accordion>
    23	  <Accordion title="8b. Startup Matrix migration">
    24	    When a Matrix channel account has a pending or actionable legacy state migration, doctor (in `--fix` / `--repair` mode) creates a pre-migration snapshot and then runs the best-effort migration steps: legacy Matrix state migration and legacy encrypted-state preparation. Both steps are non-fatal; errors are logged and startup continues. Without explicit repair (`--fix`, `--repair`, or `--yes`), this check is skipped.
    25	  </Accordion>
    26	  <Accordion title="8c. Device pairing and auth drift">
    27	    Doctor inspects device-pairing state as part of the normal health pass, reporting:
    28	
    29	    - pending first-time pairing requests
    30	    - pending role or scope upgrades for already-paired devices
    31	    - public-key mismatch repairs where the device id still matches but the device identity no longer matches the approved record
    32	    - paired records missing an active token for an approved role
    33	    - paired tokens whose scopes drift outside the approved pairing baseline
    34	    - local cached device-token entries for the current machine that predate a gateway-side token rotation or carry stale scope metadata
    35	    - a retired `identity/device-auth.json` file that is still present and blocks inspection of locally cached tokens, including in remote Gateway mode; stop the Gateway and run `openclaw doctor --fix` to finish migration or cleanup
    36	    - retired `devices/*.json` and `nodes/*.json` stores on a local Gateway; stop the Gateway and run `openclaw doctor --fix` to import device approvals before node capabilities and archive the originals. Existing SQLite records take precedence; unreadable sources remain in place for repair.
    37	
    38	    Doctor does not auto-approve pair requests or auto-rotate device tokens. It prints the exact next steps:
    39	
    40	    - inspect pending requests with `openclaw devices list`
    41	    - approve the exact request with `openclaw devices approve <requestId>`
    42	    - rotate a fresh token with `openclaw devices rotate --device <deviceId> --role <role>`
    43	    - remove and re-approve a stale record with `openclaw devices remove <deviceId>`
    44	
    45	    This distinguishes first-time pairing from pending role/scope upgrades and from stale token/device-identity drift, closing the common "already paired but still getting pairing required" hole.
    46	
    47	  </Accordion>
    48	  <Accordion title="9. Security warnings">
    49	    Doctor emits a Security note only when it finds a warning, such as a provider open to DMs without an allowlist or a dangerously configured policy. Use `openclaw security audit` for the full security inventory.
    50	
    51	    Missing multi-agent DM routing ownership is reported as a finding. It does
    52	    not stop the remaining channel security checks or pending state migrations.
    53	    Configure the reported account binding before expecting that route to work.
    54	    Telegram account discovery preserves the legacy default-agent account choice
    55	    during upgrade previews without requiring an ambient agent.
    56	
    57	  </Accordion>
    58	  <Accordion title="10. systemd linger (Linux)">
    59	    If running as a systemd user service, doctor ensures lingering is enabled so the gateway stays alive after logout.
    60	  </Accordion>
    61	  <Accordion title="11. Workspace status (skills, plugins, and TaskFlows)">
    62	    Doctor prints problems and actions for the default agent, not healthy-state inventory:
    63	
    64	    - **Skills**: lists allowed but unusable skill names; use `openclaw skills check` for requirement details and full counts.
    65	    - **Plugins**: reports only errored plugin IDs; use `openclaw plugins list` for loaded, imported, disabled, and bundle-plugin inventory.
    66	    - **Plugin compatibility warnings**: flags plugins that have compatibility issues with the current runtime.
    67	    - **Plugin diagnostics**: surfaces any load-time warnings or errors emitted by the plugin registry.
    68	    - **TaskFlow recovery**: surfaces suspicious managed TaskFlows that need manual inspection or cancellation.
    69	    - **Claude CLI**: reports only binary, authentication, profile, workspace, or project-directory problems; healthy probe details are omitted.
    70	
    71	  </Accordion>
    72	  <Accordion title="11b. Bootstrap file size">
    73	    Doctor checks workspace bootstrap candidates (`AGENTS.md`, `SOUL.md`, `IDENTITY.md`, `USER.md`, `BOOTSTRAP.md`, and `MEMORY.md`) against the configured character budget after runtime filtering. Root `BOOTSTRAP.md` is excluded after workspace setup completes. It reports per-file raw vs. injected character counts, truncation percentage, truncation cause (`max/file` or `max/total`), and total injected characters as a fraction of the total budget. When files are truncated or near the limit, doctor prints tips for tuning `agents.defaults.bootstrapMaxChars` and `agents.defaults.bootstrapTotalMaxChars`. `USER.md` is the exception: it has a fixed 4,000-character cap that these settings cannot raise, so doctor names the cap and recommends compacting the file instead. See [User model](/concepts/user-model).
    74	
    75	    This includes files declared by the bundled `bootstrap-extra-files` hook when a fresh Gateway startup would select it, provided each matched basename is one of those six (for example, `packages/core/AGENTS.md`). Other basenames are ignored. Doctor uses each agent's workspace and limits without importing or running custom hook handlers. It predicts fresh-start selection, not the previous handler generation that a running Gateway can retain after a failed hook reload.
    76	
    77	  </Accordion>
    78	  <Accordion title="11c. Shell completion">
    79	    Doctor checks whether tab completion is installed for the current shell (zsh, bash, fish, or PowerShell):
    80	
    81	    - If the shell profile uses a slow dynamic completion pattern (`source <(openclaw completion ...)`), doctor upgrades it to the faster cached file variant.
    82	    - If completion is configured in the profile but the cache file is missing, doctor regenerates the cache automatically.
    83	    - If no completion is configured at all, doctor prompts to install it (interactive mode only; skipped with `--non-interactive`).
    84	
    85	    Run `openclaw completion --write-state` to regenerate the cache manually.
    86	
    87	  </Accordion>
    88	  <Accordion title="11d. Stale channel plugin cleanup">
    89	    When `openclaw doctor --fix` removes a missing channel plugin, it also removes the dangling channel-scoped config that referenced that plugin: `channels.<id>` entries, heartbeat targets that named the channel, and `agents.*.models["<channel>/*"]` overrides. This prevents Gateway boot loops where the channel runtime is gone but config still asks the gateway to bind to it.
    90	  </Accordion>
    91	  <Accordion title="11e. Project clone shape">
    92	    Doctor checks stored project clones registered with `source: "cloned"`. It
    93	    reports the project name and path, shallow state, every
    94	    `remote.*.partialclonefilter` and `remote.*.promisor` key (including URL-keyed
    95	    twins), and `extensions.partialclone` when present. Address-like names replace
    96	    userinfo with `***` and omit query strings and fragments, including remote-helper
    97	    and scp-like addresses that are not standard URLs. Full clones produce no
    98	    finding. Missing or unreadable clones are reported as skipped; other projects
    99	    are still checked. Agent workspaces and manually registered checkouts are
   100	    outside this check.
   101	
   102	    Each clone uses three local Git reads, each limited to five seconds and
   103	    64 KiB of captured output. Doctor does not fetch, repack, or change clone
   104	    configuration, even with `--fix`. Partial clones support managed-worktree
   105	    object prefetch, but full clones avoid missing-object and shallow-history
   106	    failures during checkout and snapshot restore.
   107	
   108	    For a focused read-only report, run:
   109	
   110	    ```bash
   111	    openclaw doctor --lint --only core/doctor/project-clone-shape --json
   112	    ```
   113	
   114	    The check also runs in ordinary Doctor and `--lint --all`; it is excluded
   115	    from the default lint profile. Lint inspects the registry through Doctor's
   116	    private state snapshot.
   117	
   118	    Follow Doctor's printed commands in a POSIX shell with access to `origin`.
   119	    Stop on any failed step. For a shallow partial clone with the usual origin
   120	    keys, the sequence is:
   121	
   122	    ```bash
   123	    cd /path/to/project-clone
   124	    git config --unset-all remote.origin.partialclonefilter
   125	    git fetch --refetch --unshallow origin
   126	    git rev-list --objects --missing=print --all | grep '^?' | cut -c2- | git fetch origin --no-tags --no-write-fetch-head --recurse-submodules=no --stdin
   127	    git config --unset-all remote.origin.promisor
   128	    git repack -a -d
   129	    ```
   130	
   131	    Unset **every** reported `partialclonefilter` key before refetching, including
   132	    keys such as `remote.https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw.git.partialclonefilter`.
   133	    Omit `--unshallow` if the repository is not shallow; Git rejects that option
   134	    for complete history. Keep promisor settings until missing objects have been
   135	    fetched by ID, then unset every reported `promisor` key and, if present,
   136	    `extensions.partialclone` before repacking. Doctor prints exact unset commands
   137	    for plain remote names without `://`, `::`, or `@`. For address-like entries, follow the local lookup and
   138	    unset instructions before continuing; the original key may contain credentials
   139	    and must not be copied into shared reports. The redacted name identifies the
   140	    entry but is not its literal Git config key.
   141	
   142	    Git can retain `objects/pack/*.promisor` sidecars after the remote keys are
   143	    unset and the repository is repacked. The sidecars are harmless: OpenClaw
   144	    determines partial-clone repair availability from Git configuration, not from
   145	    those files. To remove the stale on-disk label, first confirm that the missing
   146	    object command below prints nothing and that `git fsck` succeeds:
   147	
   148	    ```bash
   149	    git rev-list --objects --missing=print --all | sed -n 's/^?//p'
   150	    git fsck --full
   151	    ```
   152	
   153	    Then move only the sidecars out of the pack directory, keeping them as a
   154	    recoverable backup until the next successful Doctor and managed-worktree run:
   155	
   156	    ```bash
   157	    promisor_marker_backup="$(git rev-parse --git-dir)/retired-promisor-markers"
   158	    mkdir -p "$promisor_marker_backup"
   159	    find "$(git rev-parse --git-dir)/objects/pack" -maxdepth 1 -type f -name '*.promisor' -exec mv -n {} "$promisor_marker_backup"/ \;
   160	    git fsck --full
   161	    ```
   162	
   163	    Rerun Doctor afterward. If history or objects remain missing, recover them
   164	    from the original repository. Origin may not contain local-only snapshots;
   165	    see [snapshot restore](/concepts/managed-worktrees#snapshots-cleanup-and-restore).
   166	
   167	  </Accordion>
   168	  <Accordion title="12. Gateway auth checks (local token)">
   169	    Doctor checks local gateway token auth readiness.
   170	
   171	    - If token mode needs a token and no token source exists, doctor offers to generate one.
   172	    - If `gateway.auth.token` is SecretRef-managed but unavailable, doctor warns and does not overwrite it with plaintext.
   173	    - `openclaw doctor --generate-gateway-token` reports when a healthy SecretRef makes generation unnecessary.
   174	    - If a store-backed token resolves to a known redaction placeholder, `--fix` or `--generate-gateway-token` verifies a database backup and regenerates that entry while preserving its SecretRef. Doctor prints the backup path and restart/re-pair guidance. Other external secrets require replacement at their source.
   175	
   176	  </Accordion>
   177	  <Accordion title="12b. Read-only SecretRef-aware repairs">
   178	    Some repair flows need to inspect configured credentials without weakening runtime fail-fast behavior.
   179	
   180	    - `openclaw doctor --fix` uses the same read-only SecretRef summary model as status-family commands for targeted config repairs.
   181	    - Example: Telegram `allowFrom` / `groupAllowFrom` `@username` repair tries to use configured bot credentials when available.
   182	    - If the Telegram bot token is configured via SecretRef but unavailable in the current command path, doctor reports that the credential is configured-but-unavailable and skips auto-resolution instead of crashing or misreporting the token as missing.
   183	
   184	  </Accordion>
   185	  <Accordion title="13. Gateway health check + restart">
   186	    Guided Doctor runs a health check and can offer recovery for a local Gateway, subject to service ownership and confirmation. A failed remote health check does not trigger local service recovery, even when the remote URL is a loopback SSH tunnel. Check the remote connection and recover the Gateway on its host. Explicit repair maintenance only resumes the matching service it stopped. A loaded, enabled macOS job between respawns is not treated as an intentionally stopped service.
   187	  </Accordion>
   188	  <Accordion title="13b. Memory search readiness">
   189	    Doctor checks whether the configured memory search embedding provider is ready for the default agent. The behavior depends on the configured provider:
   190	
   191	    - **Explicit local provider**: checks for a local model file or a recognized remote/downloadable model URL. If missing, suggests switching to a remote provider.
   192	    - **Explicit remote provider** (`openai`, `voyage`, etc.): verifies an API key is present in the environment or auth store. Prints actionable fix hints if missing.
   193	    - **Legacy auto provider**: treats `memorySearch.provider: "auto"` as OpenAI, checks OpenAI readiness, and `doctor --fix` rewrites it to `provider: "openai"`.
   194	
   195	    When a cached gateway probe result is available (gateway was healthy at the time of the check), doctor cross-references its result with the CLI-visible config and notes any discrepancy. Doctor does not start a fresh embedding ping on the default path; use the deep memory status command when you want a live provider check.
   196	
   197	    Use `openclaw memory status --deep` to verify embedding readiness at runtime.
   198	
   199	    Embedding-provider readiness is a health check, not a state migration. Gateway startup does not initialize memory embedding providers during migration preflight, so auth-profile SecretRefs can activate afterward. If embeddings remain unavailable, memory sync preserves an existing semantic index rather than replacing it with FTS-only data. Migration warnings allow degraded startup; errors that leave required state unsafe to read still block Gateway readiness.
   200	
   201	  </Accordion>
   202	  <Accordion title="14. Channel status warnings">
   203	    If the gateway is healthy, doctor runs a channel status probe and reports warnings with suggested fixes.
   204	  </Accordion>
   205	  <Accordion title="15. Supervisor config audit + repair">
   206	    Plain Doctor inspection checks the installed supervisor config (launchd/systemd/schtasks) for missing or outdated defaults (for example systemd network-online dependencies and restart delay) and can offer an interactive repair. Explicit repair maintenance skips this separate service-rewrite phase, but reconciles [eligible installation drift in a previously running service](/cli/doctor/recovery#gateway-service-recovery) through the native installer. Stopped services keep their launcher and stop state. Run `openclaw gateway install --force` from the intended installation to replace the launcher and managed environment.
   207	
   208	    Before a Linux maintenance stop, policy refresh backs up outdated OpenClaw unit settings, confirms `daemon-reload`, and verifies that the effective `TimeoutStopSec` covers drain and cleanup (currently 330 seconds). This refresh preserves the launcher, environment, and operator drop-ins; an offline service can receive it without being started. A short or unknown resident shutdown budget uses bounded lifecycle drain before stopping. At the update deadline, reported write custody refuses the stop with its owner phase; remaining admitted turns or unknown custody produce a warning and the stop proceeds.
   209	
   210	    Notes:
   211	
   212	    - An older installation marker does not make custom native settings safe to replace. Automatic reconciliation repairs only missing or recognized released defaults; unrecognized base-unit, LaunchAgent, or Scheduled Task settings remain unchanged with a diagnostic. Linux operator drop-ins are never rewritten.
   213	    - If backup or migration subprocess cleanup cannot confirm that owned work stopped, its write-custody blocker remains for the Gateway process lifetime, even after the command reports an error. Automatic maintenance does not clear it on a timeout. The deployment owner must independently verify that the work stopped before replacing the Gateway process.
   214	    - `openclaw doctor` prompts before rewriting supervisor config. `openclaw doctor --force` alone remains guided: it allows aggressive repair choices but still requires interactive consent for an eligible service rewrite. It does not enter repair maintenance or bypass ownership and write-access checks.
   215	    - `openclaw doctor --yes` accepts default non-service repair prompts and enters maintenance under the policy-refresh and installation-drift rules above.
   216	    - `openclaw doctor --fix` applies recommended repairs without prompts (`--repair` is an alias; `--yes` also enters repair maintenance). It stops the matching managed Gateway before plugin or mutable-state inspection, verifies repairs, and restarts the same service once, even when no changes are needed. It reconciles eligible installation drift, preserves launchers and stop state for services confirmed offline before maintenance, and refuses to stop an ancestor Gateway. Plain inspection does not enter maintenance, and custom state directories do not adopt native services.
   217	    - Explicit repair refuses unavailable service inspection and unmatched services that may still run. After their owner stops them and the native manager confirms they are offline, Doctor repairs its selected state without changing or starting those services. A disabled systemd unit can still be restarting; Doctor checks runtime state as well as installation state.
   218	    - An updater's explicit Gateway activation policy leaves stop/restart ownership with the updater. Doctor still requires native proof that the service is offline; a live `update --no-restart` repair fails without stopping or restarting it. Stop the service through its owner before retrying the update. Older update parents without that policy retain ordinary Doctor maintenance.
   219	    - `openclaw doctor --fix --force` uses the same policy-refresh and installation-drift rules. Use `openclaw gateway install --force` to request a rewrite; operator-owned systemd drop-ins remain unchanged.
   220	    - `OPENCLAW_SERVICE_REPAIR_POLICY=external` keeps doctor read-only for gateway service lifecycle. Have the deployment owner stop the Gateway, run Doctor as the state-owning account, then restart through that owner. The policy skips native maintenance inspection and service mutations, including install/start/restart/bootstrap, supervisor config rewrites, and legacy service cleanup. It keeps Gateway/state coordinators and agent-database lease checks, reports service health, and runs non-service repairs. See [Existing system LaunchDaemons](/gateway#existing-system-launchdaemons).
   221	    - Doctor and `gateway status --deep` name unavailable launchd domains, missing systemd user-session buses, and native probe access denial separately. Linux guidance covers `XDG_RUNTIME_DIR`, `DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS`, and `dbus-user-session`; externally supervised deployments receive the existing policy above. See [Gateway and service recovery](/cli/doctor/recovery).
   222	    - Within a live Linux service inspection, OpenClaw can retain the authenticated user manager's private connection if its session bus stops. Typed command and runtime reads continue only for that original manager and unit. A missing initial manager identity or a replaced manager remains unavailable; OpenClaw does not start the bus or select another manager. This read-only recovery does not change service start/stop authority.
   223	
   224	    - On macOS, a same-label system LaunchDaemon blocks user LaunchAgent install, start, restart, and bootstrap repair. Doctor reports the system owner and stops service recovery; `--force` does not bypass this ownership boundary. See [Existing system LaunchDaemons](/gateway#existing-system-launchdaemons).
   225	    - On Linux, doctor does not rewrite command/entrypoint metadata while the matching systemd gateway unit is active. If a stopped unit's command or working directory is overridden by an operator-owned systemd drop-in, inspect it with `systemctl --user cat <unit>.service`, then update or remove the drop-in; rewriting the managed base cannot change the effective launcher. `Environment=` drop-ins remain supported. Doctor also ignores inactive non-legacy extra gateway-like units during the duplicate-service scan so companion service files do not create cleanup noise.
   226	    - On Linux, doctor checks authority over the installed and planned service files before persisting a recovered gateway token. If that check blocks service repair, the repair leaves config and token unchanged and reports how to restore inspection access or involve the deployment owner; `--force` cannot bypass it. Unrelated Doctor config repairs are unaffected.
   227	    - If token auth requires a token and `gateway.auth.token` is SecretRef-managed, doctor service install/repair validates the SecretRef but does not persist resolved plaintext token values into supervisor service environment metadata.
   228	    - Doctor detects managed `.env`/SecretRef-backed service environment values that older LaunchAgent, systemd, or Windows Scheduled Task installs embedded inline and rewrites the service metadata so those values load from the runtime source instead of the supervisor definition.
   229	    - Doctor detects when the service command still pins an old `--port` after `gateway.port` changes and rewrites the service metadata to the current port.
   230	    - If token auth requires a token and the configured token SecretRef is unresolved, doctor blocks the install/repair path with actionable guidance.
   231	    - If both `gateway.auth.token` and `gateway.auth.password` are configured and `gateway.auth.mode` is unset, doctor blocks install/repair until mode is set explicitly.
   232	    - For Linux user-systemd units, doctor token drift checks include both `Environment=` and `EnvironmentFile=` sources when comparing service auth metadata.
   233	    - Doctor service repairs refuse to rewrite, stop, or restart a gateway service from an older OpenClaw binary when the config was last written by a newer version. See [Gateway troubleshooting](/gateway/troubleshooting#split-brain-installs-and-newer-config-guard).
   234	    - `openclaw gateway install --force` rewrites the managed base unit, but never removes operator-owned systemd drop-ins; it warns if a command or working-directory override remains effective.
   235	
   236	  </Accordion>
   237	  <Accordion title="16. Gateway runtime + port diagnostics">
   238	    Doctor inspects the service runtime (PID, last exit status) and warns when the service is installed but not actually running. It also checks for port collisions on the gateway port (default `18789`) and reports likely causes (gateway already running, SSH tunnel).
   239	  </Accordion>
   240	  <Accordion title="17. Gateway runtime best practices">
   241	    Doctor accepts Bun 1.4+ runtimes that provide WAL-reset-safe `node:sqlite` and warns when the gateway service runs on an older or unsafe Bun or a version-managed Node path (`nvm`, `fnm`, `volta`, `asdf`, etc.). Repairs migrate unsupported Bun services to Node. Version-manager paths can break after upgrades because the service does not load your shell init. Doctor offers to migrate to a system Node install when available (Homebrew/apt/choco).
   242	
   243	    Explicit runtime-path pins are retained during service repair.
   244	    Doctor still checks their runtime capabilities, but does not migrate a valid
   245	    pin away from a version manager. Replace or remove an invalid pin with
   246	    `openclaw gateway install --runtime-path <path> --force` or
   247	    `openclaw gateway install --runtime node --force`.
   248	
   249	    Service installation and repair recognize current Node executables named `node`, `nodejs`, or versioned names such as `node24` and `node-24`, including Windows `.exe` variants. Each candidate still has to pass the Node and SQLite capability checks before selection.
   250	
   251	    Newly installed or repaired macOS LaunchAgents use a canonical system PATH (`/opt/homebrew/bin:/opt/homebrew/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin:/usr/sbin:/sbin`) instead of copying the interactive shell PATH, so Homebrew-managed system binaries stay available while Volta, asdf, fnm, pnpm, and other version-manager directories do not change which Node child processes resolve. Linux services still keep explicit environment roots (`NVM_DIR`, `FNM_DIR`, `VOLTA_HOME`, `ASDF_DATA_DIR`, `BUN_INSTALL`, `PNPM_HOME`) and stable user-bin directories, but guessed version-manager fallback directories are only written to the service PATH when those directories exist on disk.
   252	
   253	  </Accordion>
   254	</AccordionGroup>
   255	---
   256	summary: "Health check commands and gateway health monitoring"
   257	read_when:
   258	  - Diagnosing channel connectivity or gateway health
   259	  - Understanding health check CLI commands and options
   260	title: "Health checks"
   261	---
   262	
   263	Short guide to verify Gateway and channel health without guessing. It covers the
   264	CLI health checks, the HTTP probe endpoints, the dedicated `health` command, and
   265	uptime monitoring.
   266	
   267	## Quick checks
   268	
   269	- `openclaw status` - local summary: gateway reachability/mode, update hint, linked channel auth age, sessions + recent activity.
   270	- `openclaw status --all` - full local diagnosis (read-only, color, safe to paste for debugging).
   271	- `openclaw status --deep` - asks the running gateway for a live probe (`health` with `probe:true`), including per-account channel probes when supported.
   272	- `openclaw status --usage` - show model provider usage/quota snapshots.
   273	- `openclaw health` - asks the running gateway for its health snapshot (WS-only; no direct channel sockets from the CLI).
   274	- `openclaw health --verbose` (alias `--debug`) - forces a live health probe and prints gateway connection details.
   275	- `openclaw health --json` - machine-readable health snapshot output.
   276	- Send `/status` as a standalone chat command in any channel to get a status reply without invoking the agent.
   277	- Logs: run `openclaw logs --follow` (or `openclaw --profile <profile> logs --follow`) and filter for `web-heartbeat`, `web-reconnect`, `web-auto-reply`, `web-inbound`.
   278	
   279	For Discord and other chat providers, session rows are not socket liveness.
   280	`openclaw sessions`, Gateway `sessions.list`, and the agent `sessions_list` tool
   281	read stored conversation state. A provider can reconnect and show healthy channel
   282	status before any new session row is materialized. Use the channel status and
   283	health commands above for live connectivity checks.
   284	
   285	Per-agent session counts and recent activity include only that agent's sessions,
   286	even when agents share a SQLite session store. Status counts each physical store
   287	once in its aggregate. The top-level health session summary represents the
   288	default agent, or the first configured agent when there is no default; it is not
   289	a fleet total. A running Gateway serves clean health and status session summaries
   290	from its resident session-row projection. Store hydration and exact dirty-row
   291	refreshes retain the existing read-only SQLite fallback.
   292	
   293	## Deep diagnostics
   294	
   295	`openclaw health --json` reports `modelRuntime.degraded: true` when a large
   296	fleet's model preparation exceeds the startup budget. `pendingAgents` names the
   297	agents still preparing and `stage` identifies the current acquisition phase.
   298	The Gateway remains running and completed agents remain usable. Background
   299	preparation clears the degraded status when all runtimes are ready.
   300	
   301	Health and status collection groups fast session-store reads into short work
   302	slices, keeping busy background preparation from delaying every individual read.
   303	Slow reads finish their transaction before yielding to other Gateway work.
   304	
   305	- Creds on disk: `ls -l ~/.openclaw/credentials/whatsapp/<accountId>/creds.json` (mtime should be recent).
   306	- Session store: `ls -l ~/.openclaw/agents/<agentId>/agent/openclaw-agent.sqlite`. Count and recent recipients are surfaced via `status`.
   307	- Relink flow: `openclaw channels logout && openclaw channels login --verbose` when status codes 409-515 or `loggedOut` appear in logs. The QR login flow auto-restarts once for status 515 after pairing.
   308	- Diagnostics are enabled by default (`diagnostics.enabled: false` disables them). Memory events record RSS/heap byte counts and threshold/growth pressure. Liveness warnings record event-loop delay/utilization, CPU-core ratio, and active/waiting/queued session counts when the process is running but saturated. Oversized-payload events record what was rejected/truncated/chunked plus sizes and limits, never message text, attachment contents, webhook bodies, raw request/response bodies, tokens, cookies, or secret values.
   309	- The same heartbeat drives the bounded stability recorder: `openclaw gateway stability` (or the `diagnostics.stability` Gateway RPC). Fatal Gateway exits, shutdown timeouts, and restart startup failures persist the latest snapshot under `~/.openclaw/logs/stability/`. Inspect the newest bundle with `openclaw gateway stability --bundle latest`.
   310	- For bug reports, run `openclaw gateway diagnostics export` and attach the generated zip: a Markdown summary, the newest stability bundle, sanitized log metadata, sanitized Gateway status/health snapshots, and config shape. Chat text, webhook bodies, tool outputs, credentials, cookies, account/message identifiers, and secret values are omitted or redacted. See [Diagnostics Export](/gateway/diagnostics).
   311	
   312	## Health monitor config
   313	
   314	- `channels.<provider>.healthMonitor.enabled`: disable health-monitor restarts for a specific channel while leaving global monitoring enabled.
   315	- `channels.<provider>.accounts.<accountId>.healthMonitor.enabled`: multi-account override that wins over the channel-level setting.
   316	- These per-channel overrides apply to the channels that expose them today: Discord, Google Chat, iMessage, IRC, Microsoft Teams, Signal, Slack, Telegram, and WhatsApp.
   317	- A crashing channel is recovered by its own auto-restart backoff first (`auto-restart attempt N/10` in the logs). The health monitor stays out of the way until that ladder ends with `giving up after 10 restart attempts`, then takes over as the last restart owner.
   318	
   319	## Inbound ingress health
   320	
   321	Channel connectivity and inbound admission are separate failure domains. A channel can hold a healthy transport connection — sending replies normally — while its durable ingress queue is unavailable, so not a single inbound message is admitted.
   322	
   323	- When a channel cannot open its durable ingress queue, its start fails and the gateway records the account as unable to receive. `openclaw channels status` reports `Channel cannot admit inbound events; its durable ingress queue is unavailable. Outbound may still work.`
   324	- Such an account is **unhealthy** regardless of transport state, and readiness reports it as failing. Previously it reported `health: healthy` and the health monitor never touched it.
   325	- Recovery stays automatic. The ingress verdict describes the account's last start attempt and is cleared by the next one, so the ordinary restart path is also how a transient queue-open failure recovers. Those restarts log as `health-monitor: restarting (reason: ingress-unavailable)` instead of the generic `stuck`.
   326	- If the restarts keep repeating, the cause is not transient. Check the logged ingress failure: a plugin denied the `openChannelIngressQueue` capability, for example, needs operator action rather than another restart.
   327	- Channels that never report ingress state are unaffected: absence means "no signal", never "broken". There is no traffic-staleness heuristic, so a genuinely quiet channel is never marked unhealthy for having received nothing.
   328	
   329	## HTTP probes
   330	
   331	The Gateway exposes three unauthenticated `GET`/`HEAD` probe pairs:
   332	
   333	| Endpoints               | Meaning                                                                                                                                              | Use                                        |
   334	| ----------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------ |
   335	| `/health`, `/healthz`   | The HTTP server is live.                                                                                                                             | Process liveness and restart decisions.    |
   336	| `/startup`, `/startupz` | Startup sidecars have settled and the Gateway is not draining. Agent and channel health are not consulted.                                           | Startup-phase monitoring.                  |
   337	| `/ready`, `/readyz`     | Startup is complete, the Gateway is not draining, required agent databases are admitted, and configured channel accounts pass deep readiness checks. | Traffic admission and operator monitoring. |
   338	
   339	`/startupz` returns `503` with `status: "starting"` while startup sidecars are pending, `503` with `status: "draining"` during drain, and `200` with `status: "started"` otherwise. Use `/readyz` when traffic admission requires usable agents. A refused default or system agent database keeps readiness false after any migration outcome, with `failing: ["agent-database:<id>"]` and the exact admission reason and repair hint in `agentDatabases`. A refused optional agent can remain isolated while healthy agents serve requests. Gateway ready announcements use the same readiness decision.
   340	
   341	A broken Telegram or other channel account can also make `/readyz` return `503` while `/startupz` remains started. Neither probe replaces the other: startup completion alone does not certify agent or channel availability.
   342	
   343	Remote unauthenticated startup responses contain only `ok` and `status`. Local-direct and authenticated callers also receive `version`, `uptimeMs`, and `pendingReason` while startup is pending. Readiness details follow the same local-or-authenticated gate because they can name failing subsystems.
   344	
   345	### Shared-state integrity failure
   346	
   347	A terminal shared-state admission failure immediately makes `/ready` and `/readyz`
   348	return `503`, including failures discovered by a SQLite worker after startup.
   349	Detailed responses include `failing: ["state-database"]` and `stateDatabase.reason`
   350	with the recorded refusal. This bypasses cached channel health;
   351	probes read the admission owner's recorded result without querying SQLite.
   352	
   353	`/healthz` still reports HTTP liveness. Supervisors that need to detect a Gateway
   354	that is running but cannot admit work must monitor `/readyz`.
   355	
   356	### Plugin replacement recovery
   357	
   358	During plugin replacement or recovery, `/readyz` returns `503`. Detailed responses
   359	include `failing: ["plugin-reload"]` and a `pluginReload` object with the affected
   360	`pluginIds`, the current `phase` (`reloading`, `recovering`, or `failed`), and any
   361	recovery `deadlineAtMs` and actionable `reason`. These owner-reported facts bypass
   362	the channel readiness cache, so a failed replacement cannot appear as only a
   363	generic channel outage or stale healthy result.
   364	
   365	The health monitor does not spend channel restart attempts while replacement
   366	holds channel admission paused. After successful rollback, the previous plugin
   367	configuration restarts its channels and ordinary readiness checks resume. If
   368	automatic recovery reaches its deadline, `phase: "failed"` retains the failure
   369	reason and next action. Admission pauses are released, allowing the monitor to
   370	restart callable channels; a plugin whose admitted work or cleanup still owns
   371	resources requires the reported repair or retry before it can restart. See
   372	[Config hot reload](/gateway/configuration/hot-reload) for the recovery contract.
   373	
   374	### CPU pressure and event-loop delay
   375	
   376	Detailed readiness can include the latest completed `eventLoop` diagnostic
   377	snapshot. The sampler owns observation windows; health reads do not reset a
   378	pending measurement. No snapshot is available until the first window completes. Its
   379	`cpuCoreRatio` measures user and system CPU time across the whole Gateway process,
   380	including worker and native threads, divided by elapsed wall time. The unit is
   381	core equivalents: `1` means one CPU core fully occupied over the interval, and
   382	parallel work can produce values above `1`. It is not a percentage of the host's
   383	total CPU capacity.
   384	
   385	The `health` RPC also reads the latest completed sample when returning a cached
   386	summary or publishing a newly collected one. Slow channel checks do not freeze
   387	its CPU and delay readings. If the sampler resets, health responses omit
   388	`eventLoop` until a new window completes instead of reviving a cached sample.
   389	
   390	The optional `cpuBreakdown` separates independent native counters:
   391	
   392	- `hostUtilization` is the busy fraction of the host CPU time reported by
   393	  `os.cpus()`, from `0` to `1` across `hostCpuCount` logical CPUs. This includes
   394	  other processes and is not the Gateway's CPU quota or container allowance.
   395	- `mainThreadCoreRatio` measures the Gateway main thread with
   396	  `process.threadCpuUsage()`, not event-loop utilization.
   397	- `workerCoreRatio` sums `Worker.cpuUsage()` counters for workers owned by the
   398	  task pools and SQLite broker. It does not include subprocesses, remote workers,
   399	  or workers created outside those owners.
   400	- `otherThreadsCoreRatio` is an **estimated residual**: process CPU minus the
   401	  measured main and tracked worker CPU, clamped at zero. It includes untracked
   402	  workers and native threads, not a measured worker category.
   403	
   404	Thread values use the same core-equivalent unit as `cpuCoreRatio`. Worker reads
   405	are asynchronous and must complete within 100 ms of each sampling boundary;
   406	these are not atomic cross-thread measurements. The residual can vary with
   407	measurement skew. A timed-out request never delays the event-loop sample, and
   408	at most one native request is outstanding per tracked worker, even across
   409	monitor resets. Startup, worker creation/exit, host CPU topology changes, counter
   410	resets, and collection failures require fresh baselines before publishing the
   411	affected rates. Missing fields mean unavailable, not zero.
   412	
   413	The main-thread and host counters are collected independently on Node and Bun.
   414	Tracked worker CPU and the residual are omitted on Bun: its worker API can
   415	report zero when native counter collection fails. Unsupported or failed native
   416	APIs on any platform leave the corresponding field absent.
   417	
   418	The Control UI's **CPU** box reads the same sampler through `system.info.eventLoop`.
   419	Its detail overlay shows host usage separately from the process and thread
   420	breakdown. Process and thread percentages use `100%` for one fully occupied
   421	core; host usage uses `100%` for all reported logical CPUs. Values show a dash
   422	until their first complete measurement, or while unavailable.
   423	
   424	Event-loop delay and utilization describe the main thread separately. A `cpu`
   425	degradation reason reports process CPU pressure with delay co-evidence; it does
   426	not identify the thread consuming CPU or prove a main-thread hang. Inspect the
   427	delay measurements alongside CPU pressure. The `eventLoop` diagnostic does not
   428	change the readiness result by itself.
   429	
   430	## Uptime monitoring
   431	
   432	External uptime monitoring services should use the dedicated `/health` endpoint, not `/v1/chat/completions`.
   433	
   434	- **DO use:** `GET /health` - instant response, no session created, no LLM call, returns `{"ok":true,"status":"live"}`
   435	- **DON'T use:** `/v1/chat/completions` for health checks - each request creates a full agent session with skill snapshot, context assembly, and LLM calls
   436	
   437	When no `x-openclaw-session-key` header or `user` field is provided, `/v1/chat/completions` generates a new random session for each request. Monitoring services that ping every 15 minutes create ~96 sessions/day, each consuming 4-22KB. Over time this causes session store bloat and can lead to context window overflow.
   438	
   439	### Monitoring service setup examples
   440	
   441	- **BetterStack:** Set health check URL to `https://<your-gateway-host>:<port>/health`
   442	- **UptimeRobot:** Add a new HTTP monitor with URL `https://<your-gateway-host>:<port>/health`
   443	- **Generic:** Any HTTP GET to `/health` returns 200 with `{"ok":true,"status":"live"}` while the gateway's HTTP server is live
   444	
   445	## When something fails
   446	
   447	- `logged out` or status 409-515 -> relink with `openclaw channels logout` then `openclaw channels login`.
   448	- Gateway unreachable -> start it: `openclaw gateway --port 18789` (use `--force` if the port is busy).
   449	- No inbound messages -> confirm linked phone is online and the sender is allowed (`channels.whatsapp.allowFrom`); for group chats, ensure allowlist + mention rules match (`channels.whatsapp.groups`, `agents.entries.*.groupChat.mentionPatterns`).
   450	
   451	## Dedicated "health" command
   452	
   453	`openclaw health` asks the running gateway for its health snapshot (no direct channel
   454	sockets from the CLI). By default it returns a fresh cached gateway snapshot and the
   455	gateway refreshes that cache in the background; `--verbose` forces a live probe instead.
   456	Connections and cached health reads share a one-minute background refresh cadence, so
   457	repeated diagnostic connections do not each rebuild the health snapshot. Explicit live
   458	probes and refreshes for missing or stale health still run immediately.
   459	Snapshots describe loaded and configured channels. Stored credentials alone do not
   460	activate a channel or add it to Gateway health; use channel setup to enable it.
   461	The command reports linked creds/auth age when available, per-channel probe summaries,
   462	session-store summary, and probe duration. Live probes use bounded account concurrency
   463	and a Gateway-owned deadline, so one slow account returns a structured timeout while
   464	completed sibling results remain available. The command exits non-zero if the gateway is
   465	unreachable or the Gateway call itself times out.
   466	
   467	### Queue warnings
   468	
   469	A successful health RPC reports top-level `ok: true`. That value means the Gateway
   470	produced the snapshot; it does not mean every delivery queue is clear. Check
   471	`deliveryQueues.ingressPressure` for durable inbound lanes that may be blocking later
   472	events. The field is omitted when no pressured lanes are found.
   473	
   474	Ingress pressure uses conservative built-in diagnostic thresholds, not authoritative
   475	retry or claim policy for any plugin. A durable lane appears only when an active pending
   476	or claimed row has either reached at least eight attempts and has a recorded delivery
   477	error, or a claimed row has not refreshed its claim for 30 minutes. Ordinary retries
   478	1-7 are absent. Claim-recovery increments without a recorded error are also absent,
   479	while live claims stay absent because their claim timestamp is refreshed. Rows without
   480	a durable lane key are omitted because they cannot prove that later events are blocked;
   481	runtime persists a derived lane after a real derived-lane retry.
   482	
   483	Each result is grouped by channel account and reports pressured lane, pending, claimed,
   484	and blocked counts plus the oldest affected receive time. All active rows in a pressured
   485	lane contribute to those counts. The snapshot never includes lane IDs, event IDs,
   486	payloads, claim owners or tokens, recorded errors, or session and target identifiers.
   487	
   488	Options:
   489	
   490	- `--json`: machine-readable JSON output
   491	- `--timeout <ms>`: override the default 10s Gateway connection timeout; it does not widen the Gateway's internal live-probe deadline
   492	- `--verbose`: force a live probe and print gateway connection details
   493	- `--debug`: alias for `--verbose`
   494	
   495	The health snapshot includes: `ok` (boolean), `ts` (timestamp), `durationMs` (probe time), per-channel status, agent availability, session-store summary, and optional delivery-queue warnings.
   496	
   497	## Related
   498	
   499	- [Gateway runbook](/gateway)
   500	- [Diagnostics export](/gateway/diagnostics)
   501	- [Gateway troubleshooting](/gateway/troubleshooting)
   502	- [`openclaw health`](/cli/health) — request this snapshot over RPC from the CLI
   503	---
   504	summary: "Reference catalog of checkIds emitted by openclaw security audit"
   505	read_when:
   506	  - You saw a specific `checkId` in `openclaw security audit` output and want to know what it means
   507	  - You need the fix key/path for a given finding
   508	  - You are triaging severity across a security audit run
   509	title: "Security audit checks"
   510	---
   511	
   512	`openclaw security audit` emits structured findings keyed by `checkId`. This
   513	page is the reference catalog for those IDs. For the high-level threat model
   514	and hardening guidance, see [Security](/gateway/security).
   515	
   516	Some checks only run with `openclaw security audit --deep`: plugin/skill code
   517	scans (`plugins.code_safety*`, `skills.code_safety*`) and live Gateway probe
   518	checks (`gateway.probe_*`). Everything else in this table runs on a plain
   519	`openclaw security audit`.
   520	
   521	Deep code scans check up to 500 source files per plugin or skill, prioritizing
   522	declared plugin entrypoints, and skip files larger than 1 MiB. Traversal also
   523	stops after 100,000 directory entries, including excluded entries. A
   524	`*.code_safety.scan_truncated` warning means some files were not checked; it does
   525	not identify dangerous code. Workspace skill-path scans retain their separate
   526	limits of 2,000 skill files and 40,000 directory visits, with the same 100,000-entry
   527	traversal limit. Their `skills.workspace.scan_truncated` warning also covers
   528	unreadable portions of the tree.
   529	
   530	A severity like `warn/critical` means the same `checkId` can be emitted at
   531	either level depending on config (for example, whether the Gateway is remotely
   532	exposed). High-signal values you will most likely see in real deployments (not
   533	exhaustive):
   534	
   535	| `checkId`                                                       | Severity           | Why it matters                                                                                     | Primary fix key/path                                                                                    | Auto-fix |
   536	| --------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------ | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------- |
   537	| `fs.state_dir.perms_world_writable`                             | critical           | Other users/processes can modify full OpenClaw state                                               | filesystem perms on `~/.openclaw`                                                                       | yes      |
   538	| `fs.state_dir.perms_group_writable`                             | warn               | Group users can modify full OpenClaw state                                                         | filesystem perms on `~/.openclaw`                                                                       | yes      |
   539	| `fs.state_dir.perms_readable`                                   | warn               | State dir is readable by others                                                                    | filesystem perms on `~/.openclaw`                                                                       | yes      |
   540	| `fs.state_dir.symlink`                                          | warn               | State dir target becomes another trust boundary                                                    | state dir filesystem layout                                                                             | no       |
   541	| `fs.config.perms_writable`                                      | critical           | Others can change auth/tool policy/config                                                          | filesystem perms on `~/.openclaw/openclaw.json`                                                         | yes      |
   542	| `fs.config.symlink`                                             | warn               | Symlinked config files are unsupported for writes and add another trust boundary                   | replace with a regular config file or point `OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH` at the real file                     | no       |
   543	| `fs.config.perms_group_readable`                                | warn               | Group users can read config tokens/settings                                                        | filesystem perms on config file                                                                         | yes      |
   544	| `fs.config.perms_world_readable`                                | critical           | Config can expose tokens/settings                                                                  | filesystem perms on config file                                                                         | yes      |
   545	| `fs.config_include.perms_writable`                              | critical           | Config include file can be modified by others                                                      | include-file perms referenced from `openclaw.json`                                                      | yes      |
   546	| `fs.config_include.perms_group_readable`                        | warn               | Group users can read included secrets/settings                                                     | include-file perms referenced from `openclaw.json`                                                      | yes      |
   547	| `fs.config_include.perms_world_readable`                        | critical           | Included secrets/settings are world-readable                                                       | include-file perms referenced from `openclaw.json`                                                      | yes      |
   548	| `fs.auth_profiles.perms_writable`                               | critical           | Others can inject or replace stored model credentials                                              | Agent-local SQLite auth database/sidecar perms and legacy `auth-profiles.json` perms                    | yes      |
   549	| `fs.auth_profiles.perms_readable`                               | warn               | Others can read API keys and OAuth tokens                                                          | Agent-local SQLite auth database/sidecar perms and legacy `auth-profiles.json` perms                    | yes      |
   550	| `fs.credentials_dir.perms_writable`                             | critical           | Others can modify channel pairing/credential state                                                 | filesystem perms on `~/.openclaw/credentials`                                                           | yes      |
   551	| `fs.credentials_dir.perms_readable`                             | warn               | Others can read channel credential state                                                           | filesystem perms on `~/.openclaw/credentials`                                                           | yes      |
   552	| `fs.sessions_store.perms_readable`                              | warn               | Others can read session transcripts/metadata                                                       | session store perms                                                                                     | yes      |
   553	| `fs.log_file.perms_readable`                                    | warn               | Others can read redacted-but-still-sensitive logs                                                  | gateway log file perms                                                                                  | yes      |
   554	| `fs.synced_dir`                                                 | warn               | State/config in iCloud/Dropbox/Drive broadens token/transcript exposure                            | move config/state off synced folders                                                                    | no       |
   555	| `gateway.bind_no_auth`                                          | critical           | Remote bind without shared secret                                                                  | `gateway.bind`, `gateway.auth.*`                                                                        | no       |
   556	| `gateway.loopback_no_auth`                                      | critical           | Reverse-proxied loopback may become unauthenticated                                                | `gateway.auth.*`, proxy setup                                                                           | no       |
   557	| `gateway.trusted_proxies_missing`                               | warn               | Reverse-proxy headers are present but not trusted                                                  | `gateway.trustedProxies`                                                                                | no       |
   558	| `gateway.http.no_auth`                                          | warn/critical      | Gateway HTTP APIs reachable with `auth.mode="none"`                                                | `gateway.auth.mode`, `gateway.http.endpoints.*`, `plugins.entries.admin-http-rpc`                       | no       |
   559	| `gateway.http.session_key_override_enabled`                     | info               | HTTP API callers can override `sessionKey`                                                         | `gateway.http.allowSessionKeyOverride`                                                                  | no       |
   560	| `gateway.tools_invoke_http.dangerous_allow`                     | warn/critical      | Re-enables dangerous tools over HTTP API for owner/admin callers                                   | `gateway.tools.allow`                                                                                   | no       |
   561	| `gateway.nodes.allow_commands_dangerous`                        | warn/critical      | Enables high-impact node commands (desktop input/camera/screen/contacts/calendar/SMS)              | `gateway.nodes.commands.allow`                                                                          | no       |
   562	| `gateway.nodes.deny_commands_ineffective`                       | warn               | Pattern-like deny entries do not match shell text or groups                                        | `gateway.nodes.commands.deny`                                                                           | no       |
