     2	summary: "Recovering from a rejected config and reading gateway probe warnings"
     3	title: "Config validation and probes"
     4	sidebarTitle: "Config validation and probes"
     5	read_when:
     6	  - The Gateway rejected an invalid config and you need the recovery path
     7	  - You need to know what the `.rejected`, `.bak`, and last-known-good copies hold
     8	  - Gateway probe warnings appear in status or doctor output
     9	---
    10	
    11	## Gateway rejected invalid config
    12	
    13	Use when Gateway startup fails with `Invalid config` or hot reload logs say it skipped an invalid edit.
    14	
    15	Startup automatically migrates deterministic legacy keys in eligible single-file
    16	configs and continues only if the entire result validates, including plugins. It
    17	keeps the previous config in the `.bak` ring. Configs using `$include`, Nix-managed
    18	configs, configs written by a newer version, and configs that still fail validation
    19	require operator repair. See [Legacy config key migrations](/gateway/doctor#detailed-behavior-and-rationale).
    20	
    21	```bash
    22	openclaw logs --follow
    23	openclaw config file
    24	openclaw config validate
    25	openclaw doctor
    26	```
    27	
    28	Look for:
    29	
    30	- `Invalid config at ...`
    31	- `config reload skipped (invalid config): ...`
    32	- `Config write rejected: ...`
    33	- A timestamped `openclaw.json.rejected.*` file beside the active config.
    34	- A timestamped `openclaw.json.clobbered.*` file if `doctor --fix` repaired a broken direct edit.
    35	- OpenClaw keeps the latest 32 `.clobbered.*` files for each config path and rotates older ones.
    36	
    37	<AccordionGroup>
    38	  <Accordion title="What happened">
    39	    - The config did not validate during startup, hot reload, or an OpenClaw-owned write.
    40	    - Gateway startup leaves `openclaw.json` unchanged and fails closed when safe legacy-key migration cannot produce a fully valid config.
    41	    - Hot reload skips invalid external edits and keeps the current runtime config active.
    42	    - OpenClaw-owned writes reject invalid/destructive payloads before commit and save `.rejected.*`.
    43	    - `openclaw doctor --fix` owns repairs beyond automatic legacy-key migration. It can remove non-JSON prefixes or restore the last-known-good copy while preserving the rejected payload as `.clobbered.*`.
    44	    - When many repairs happen for one config path, OpenClaw rotates older `.clobbered.*` files so the newest repaired payload is still available.
    45	
    46	  </Accordion>
    47	  <Accordion title="Inspect and repair">
    48	    ```bash
    49	    CONFIG="$(openclaw config file)"
    50	    ls -lt "$CONFIG".clobbered.* "$CONFIG".rejected.* 2>/dev/null | head
    51	    diff -u "$CONFIG" "$(ls -t "$CONFIG".clobbered.* 2>/dev/null | head -n 1)"
    52	    openclaw config validate
    53	    openclaw doctor
    54	    ```
    55	  </Accordion>
    56	  <Accordion title="Common signatures">
    57	    - `.clobbered.*` exists → doctor preserved a broken external edit while repairing the active config.
    58	    - `.rejected.*` exists → an OpenClaw-owned config write failed schema or clobber checks before commit.
    59	    - `Config write rejected:` → the write tried to drop required shape, shrink the file sharply, or persist invalid config.
    60	    - `config reload skipped (invalid config):` → a direct edit failed validation and was ignored by the running Gateway.
    61	    - `Invalid config at ...` → startup failed before Gateway services booted.
    62	    - `missing-meta-vs-last-good`, `gateway-mode-missing-vs-last-good`, or `size-drop-vs-last-good:*` → an OpenClaw-owned write was rejected because it lost fields or size compared with the last-known-good backup.
    63	    - `Config last-known-good promotion skipped` → the candidate contained redacted secret placeholders such as `***`.
    64	
    65	  </Accordion>
    66	  <Accordion title="Fix options">
    67	    An interactive startup can offer to run `openclaw doctor --fix` and retry once when automatic legacy-key migration is not enough. Non-interactive startup prints the repair command instead.
    68	
    69	    1. Run `openclaw doctor --fix` to let doctor repair prefixed/clobbered config or restore last-known-good.
    70	    2. Copy only the intended keys from `.clobbered.*` or `.rejected.*`, then apply them with `openclaw config set` or `config.patch`.
    71	    3. Run `openclaw config validate` before restarting.
    72	    4. If you edit by hand, keep the full JSON5 config, not just the partial object you wanted to change.
    73	
    74	  </Accordion>
    75	</AccordionGroup>
    76	
    77	Related:
    78	
    79	- [Config](/cli/config)
    80	- [Configuration: hot reload](/gateway/configuration#config-hot-reload)
    81	- [Configuration: strict validation](/gateway/configuration#strict-validation)
    82	- [Doctor](/gateway/doctor)
    83	
    84	## Gateway probe warnings
    85	
    86	Use when `openclaw gateway probe` reaches something, but still prints a warning block.
    87	
    88	```bash
    89	openclaw gateway probe
    90	openclaw gateway probe --json
    91	openclaw gateway probe --ssh user@gateway-host
    92	```
    93	
    94	Look for:
    95	
    96	- `warnings[].code` and `primaryTargetId` in JSON output.
    97	- Whether the warning is about SSH fallback, multiple gateways, missing scopes, or unresolved auth refs.
    98	
    99	Common signatures:
   100	
   101	- `SSH tunnel failed to start; falling back to direct probes.` → SSH setup failed, but the command still tried direct configured/loopback targets.
   102	- `multiple reachable gateway identities detected` → distinct gateways answered, or OpenClaw could not prove reachable targets are the same gateway. An SSH tunnel, proxy URL, or configured remote URL to the same gateway is treated as one gateway with multiple transports, even when transport ports differ.
   103	- `Read-probe diagnostics are limited by gateway scopes (missing operator.read)` → connect worked, but detail RPC is scope-limited; pair device identity or use credentials with `operator.read`.
   104	- `Gateway accepted the WebSocket connection, but follow-up read diagnostics failed` → connect worked, but the full diagnostic RPC set timed out or failed. Treat this as a reachable Gateway with degraded diagnostics; compare `connect.ok` and `connect.rpcOk` in `--json` output.
   105	- `Capability: pairing-pending` or `gateway closed (1008): pairing required` → the gateway answered, but this client still needs pairing/approval before normal operator access.
   106	- Unresolved `gateway.auth.*` / `gateway.remote.*` SecretRef warning text → auth material was unavailable in this command path for the failed target.
   107	
   108	Related:
   109	
   110	- [Gateway](/cli/gateway)
   111	- [Multiple gateways on the same host](/gateway#multiple-gateways-same-host)
   112	- [Remote access](/gateway/remote)
   113	---
   114	summary: "Plugin and channel contract test commands, categories, and when to run them"
   115	title: "Contract tests"
   116	read_when:
   117	  - You changed a channel, provider, or plugin-sdk surface
   118	---
   119	
   120	## Contract tests (plugin and channel shape)
   121	
   122	Contract tests verify that every registered plugin and channel conforms to
   123	its interface contract. They iterate over all discovered plugins and run a
   124	suite of shape and behavior assertions. The default `pnpm test` unit lane
   125	intentionally skips these shared seam and smoke files; run the contract
   126	commands explicitly when you touch shared channel or provider surfaces.
   127	
   128	### Commands
   129	
   130	- All contracts: `pnpm test:contracts`
   131	- Channel contracts only: `pnpm test:contracts:channels`
   132	- Provider contracts only: `pnpm test:contracts:plugins`
   133	
   134	### Channel contracts
   135	
   136	Located in `src/channels/plugins/contracts/*.contract.test.ts`. Current
   137	top-level categories:
   138	
   139	- **channel-catalog** - bundled/registry channel catalog entry metadata
   140	- **plugin** (registry-backed, sharded) - basic plugin registration shape
   141	- **surfaces-only** (registry-backed, sharded) - per-surface shape checks for `actions`, `setup`, `status`, `outbound`, `messaging`, `threading`, `directory`, and `gateway`
   142	- **session-binding** (registry-backed) - session binding behavior
   143	- **outbound-payload** - message payload structure and normalization
   144	- **group-policy** (fallback) - default group policy enforcement per channel
   145	- **threading** (registry-backed, sharded) - thread id handling
   146	- **directory** (registry-backed, sharded) - directory/roster API
   147	- **registry** and **plugins-core.\*** - channel plugin registry, loader, and config-write authorization internals
   148	
   149	Inbound dispatch-capture and outbound-payload harness helpers used by these
   150	suites are exposed internally through `src/plugin-sdk/channel-contract-testing.ts`
   151	(npm-excluded, not a public SDK subpath); there is no standalone
   152	`inbound.contract.test.ts` file in this directory.
   153	
   154	### Provider contracts
   155	
   156	Located in `src/plugins/contracts/*.contract.test.ts`. Current categories
   157	include:
   158	
   159	- **shape** - plugin manifest, API, and runtime export shape
   160	- **plugin-registration** (+ parallel) - manifest registration cases
   161	- **package-manifest** - package manifest requirements
   162	- **loader** - plugin loader setup/teardown behavior
   163	- **registry** - plugin contract registry contents and lookup
   164	- **providers** - shared provider behavior across bundled providers, plus web-search providers
   165	- **auth-choice** - auth choice metadata and setup behavior
   166	- **provider-catalog-deprecation** - deprecated provider catalog metadata
   167	- **wizard.choice-resolution**, **wizard.model-picker**, **wizard.setup-options** - provider setup wizard contracts
   168	- **embedding-provider**, **memory-embedding-provider**, **web-fetch-provider**, **tts** - capability-specific provider contracts
   169	- **session-actions**, **session-attachments**, **session-entry-projection** - plugin-owned session state contracts
   170	- **scheduled-turns** - plugin scheduled turn metadata and timestamp bounds
   171	- **host-hooks**, **run-context-lifecycle**, **runtime-import-side-effects**, **runtime-seams** - plugin host/runtime lifecycle and import-boundary contracts
   172	- **extension-runtime-dependencies** - runtime dependency placement for extensions
   173	
   174	### When to run
   175	
   176	- After changing plugin-sdk exports or subpaths
   177	- After adding or modifying a channel or provider plugin
   178	- After refactoring plugin registration or discovery
   179	
   180	Contract tests run in CI and do not require real API keys.
   181	---
   182	summary: "The Docker works-in-Linux runners, their scheduler, lanes, and env vars"
   183	title: "Docker test runners"
   184	read_when:
   185	  - You are running the Docker test lanes
   186	  - You need a Docker lane name or env var
   187	---
   188	
   189	## Docker runners (optional "works in Linux" checks)
   190	
   191	These Docker runners split into two buckets:
   192	
   193	- Live-model runners: `test:docker:live-models` and `test:docker:live-gateway` run only their matching profile-key live file inside the repo Docker image (`src/agents/models.profiles.live.test.ts` and `src/gateway/gateway-models.profiles.live.test.ts`), mounting your local config dir, workspace, and optional profile env file. The matching local entrypoints are `test:live:models-profiles` and `test:live:gateway-profiles`.
   194	- Docker live runners keep their own practical caps where needed:
   195	  `test:docker:live-models` defaults to the curated supported high-signal set, and
   196	  `test:docker:live-gateway` defaults to `OPENCLAW_LIVE_GATEWAY_SMOKE=1`,
   197	  `OPENCLAW_LIVE_GATEWAY_MAX_MODELS=8`,
   198	  `OPENCLAW_LIVE_GATEWAY_STEP_TIMEOUT_MS=45000`, and
   199	  `OPENCLAW_LIVE_GATEWAY_MODEL_TIMEOUT_MS=90000`. Set `OPENCLAW_LIVE_MAX_MODELS`
   200	  or the gateway env vars when you explicitly want a smaller cap or larger scan.
   201	- `test:docker:all` builds the live Docker image once via `test:docker:live-build`, packs OpenClaw once as an npm tarball through `scripts/package-openclaw-for-docker.mjs`, then builds/reuses two `scripts/e2e/Dockerfile` images. The bare image is only the Node/Git runner for install/update/plugin-dependency lanes; those lanes mount the prebuilt tarball. The functional image installs the same tarball into `/app` for built-app functionality lanes. Docker lane definitions live in `scripts/lib/docker-e2e-scenarios.mts`; planner logic lives in `scripts/lib/docker-e2e-plan.mts`; `scripts/test-docker-all.mjs` executes the selected plan. The aggregate uses a weighted local scheduler: `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_PARALLELISM` controls process slots, while resource caps keep heavy live, npm-install, and multi-service lanes from all starting at once. If a single lane is heavier than the active caps, the scheduler can still start it when the pool is empty and then keeps it running alone until capacity is available again. Defaults are 10 slots, `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_LIVE_LIMIT=9`, `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_NPM_LIMIT=5`, and `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_SERVICE_LIMIT=7`; tune `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_WEIGHT_LIMIT` or `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_DOCKER_LIMIT` (and other `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_<RESOURCE>_LIMIT` overrides) only when the Docker host has more headroom. The runner performs a Docker preflight by default, removes stale OpenClaw E2E containers, prints status every 30 seconds, stores successful lane timings in `.artifacts/docker-tests/lane-timings.json`, and uses those timings to start longer lanes first on later runs. Use `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_ALL_DRY_RUN=1` to print the weighted lane manifest without building or running Docker, or `node scripts/test-docker-all.mjs --plan-json` to print the CI plan for selected lanes, package/image needs, and credentials.
   202	- `Package Acceptance` is the GitHub-native package gate for "does this installable tarball work as a product?" It resolves one candidate package from `source=npm`, `source=ref`, `source=url`, `source=trusted-url`, or `source=artifact`, uploads it as `package-under-test`, then runs the reusable Docker E2E lanes against that exact tarball instead of repacking the selected ref. Profiles are ordered by breadth: `smoke`, `package`, `product`, and `full` (plus `custom` for an explicit lane list). See [Testing updates and plugins](/help/testing-updates-plugins) for the package/update/plugin contract, published-upgrade survivor matrix, release defaults, and failure triage.
   203	- Build and release checks run `scripts/check-cli-bootstrap-imports.mts` after tsdown. The guard walks the static built graph from `dist/entry.js` and `dist/cli/run-main.js` and fails if that pre-dispatch bootstrap graph statically imports any external package (Commander, prompt UI, undici, logging, and similar startup-heavy deps all count) before command dispatch; it also caps the bundled gateway run chunk at 70 KB and rejects static imports of known cold gateway paths (`control-ui-assets`, `diagnostic-stability-bundle`, `onboard-helpers`, `process-respawn`, `restart-sentinel`, `server-close`, `server-reload-handlers`) from that chunk. Current builds locate the gateway chunk through hash-checked metadata emitted by tsdown, so discovery does not scan unrelated JavaScript under `dist`. Missing or stale metadata requires rebuilding; trusted release tooling retains source scanning only for frozen targets that predate the locator. Standalone workers still receive independent JavaScript dependency checks. `scripts/release-check.ts` separately smoke-tests the packed CLI with `--help`, `onboard --help`, `doctor --help`, `status --json --timeout 1`, `config schema`, and `models list --provider openai`.
   204	- Package Acceptance enforces current package metadata and persistence contracts. Pre-June 2026 warning and skip paths are retired; use matching historical tooling to reproduce acceptance results for those old candidates.
   205	- Container smoke runners: `test:docker:openwebui`, `test:docker:onboard`, `test:docker:npm-onboard-channel-agent`, `test:docker:release-user-journey`, `test:docker:release-typed-onboarding`, `test:docker:release-media-memory`, `test:docker:release-upgrade-user-journey`, `test:docker:release-plugin-marketplace`, `test:docker:skill-install`, `test:docker:update-channel-switch`, `test:docker:upgrade-survivor`, `test:docker:published-upgrade-survivor`, `test:docker:session-runtime-context`, `test:docker:agents-delete-shared-workspace`, `test:docker:gateway-network`, `test:docker:browser-cdp-snapshot`, `test:docker:mcp-channels`, `test:docker:agent-bundle-mcp-tools`, `test:docker:cron-mcp-cleanup`, `test:docker:plugins`, `test:docker:plugin-update`, `test:docker:plugin-lifecycle-matrix`, and `test:docker:config-reload` boot one or more real containers and verify higher-level integration paths.
   206	- Docker/Bash E2E lanes that install the packed OpenClaw tarball through `scripts/lib/openclaw-e2e-instance.sh` cap `npm install` at `OPENCLAW_E2E_NPM_INSTALL_TIMEOUT` (default `600s`; set `0` to disable the wrapper for debugging).
   207	
   208	The live-model Docker runners also bind-mount only the needed CLI auth homes
   209	(or all supported ones when the run is not narrowed), then copy them into the
   210	container home before the run so external-CLI OAuth can refresh tokens
   211	without mutating the host auth store:
   212	
   213	- Direct models: `pnpm test:docker:live-models` (script: `scripts/test-live-models-docker.sh`)
   214	- ACP bind smoke: `pnpm test:docker:live-acp-bind` (script: `scripts/test-live-acp-bind-docker.sh`; covers Claude, Codex, and Gemini by default, with strict Droid/OpenCode coverage via `pnpm test:docker:live-acp-bind:droid` and `pnpm test:docker:live-acp-bind:opencode`)
   215	- CLI backend smoke: `pnpm test:docker:live-cli-backend` (script: `scripts/test-live-cli-backend-docker.sh`)
   216	- Codex app-server harness smoke: `pnpm test:docker:live-codex-harness` (script: `scripts/test-live-codex-harness-docker.sh`)
   217	- Gateway + dev agent: `pnpm test:docker:live-gateway` (script: `scripts/test-live-gateway-models-docker.sh`)
   218	- Observability smokes: `pnpm qa:otel:smoke`, `pnpm qa:prometheus:smoke`, and `pnpm qa:observability:smoke` are private QA source-checkout lanes. They are intentionally not part of package Docker release lanes because the npm tarball omits QA Lab.
   219	- Open WebUI live smoke: `pnpm test:docker:openwebui` (script: `scripts/e2e/openwebui-docker.sh`)
   220	- Onboarding wizard (TTY, full scaffolding): `pnpm test:docker:onboard` (script: `scripts/e2e/onboard-docker.sh`)
   221	- Npm tarball onboarding/channel/agent smoke: `pnpm test:docker:npm-onboard-channel-agent` installs the packed OpenClaw tarball globally in Docker, configures OpenAI via env-ref onboarding plus Telegram by default, runs doctor, and runs one mocked OpenAI agent turn. Reuse a prebuilt tarball with `OPENCLAW_CURRENT_PACKAGE_TGZ=/path/to/openclaw-*.tgz`, skip the host rebuild with `OPENCLAW_NPM_ONBOARD_HOST_BUILD=0`, or switch channel with `OPENCLAW_NPM_ONBOARD_CHANNEL=discord` or `OPENCLAW_NPM_ONBOARD_CHANNEL=slack`.
   222	
   223	- Release user journey smoke: `pnpm test:docker:release-user-journey` installs the packed OpenClaw tarball globally in a clean Docker home, runs onboarding, configures a mocked OpenAI provider, runs an agent turn, installs/uninstalls external plugins, configures ClickClack against a local fixture, verifies outbound/inbound messaging, restarts Gateway, and runs doctor.
   224	- Release typed onboarding smoke: `pnpm test:docker:release-typed-onboarding` installs the packed tarball, drives `openclaw onboard` through a real TTY, configures OpenAI as an env-ref provider, verifies no raw key persistence, and runs a mocked agent turn.
   225	- Release media/memory smoke: `pnpm test:docker:release-media-memory` installs the packed tarball, verifies image understanding from a PNG attachment, OpenAI-compatible image generation output, memory search recall, and recall survival across Gateway restart.
   226	- Release upgrade user journey smoke: `pnpm test:docker:release-upgrade-user-journey` installs the newest published stable baseline older than the candidate tarball by default, onboards and installs a CLI plugin on the published package, then replaces the package and runs the documented Doctor migration step. It verifies the existing plugin still works and configures candidate-compatible mock provider/ClickClack settings for the agent/channel journey. If no older stable baseline exists, it reuses the candidate version only when that version is published and stable; otherwise it fails and requires an explicit baseline. Override the baseline with `OPENCLAW_RELEASE_UPGRADE_BASELINE_SPEC=openclaw@<version>`.
   227	- Release plugin marketplace smoke: `pnpm test:docker:release-plugin-marketplace` installs from a local fixture marketplace, updates the installed plugin, uninstalls it, and verifies the plugin CLI disappears with install metadata pruned.
   228	- Skill install smoke: `pnpm test:docker:skill-install` installs the packed OpenClaw tarball globally in Docker and disables uploaded archive installs in config. With no nonempty `OPENCLAW_SKILL_INSTALL_E2E_QUERY`, `OPENCLAW_SKILL_INSTALL_E2E_SLUG`, or `OPENCLAW_SKILL_INSTALL_E2E_PREFERRED_SLUG` override, it resolves the maintained `@steipete/gifgrep` fixture from live search, installs version `1.0.1`, and verifies its reviewed `SKILL.md` bytes plus exact owner/version in `.clawhub` origin/lock metadata. Security refusal is terminal; the smoke does not run the skill or install its optional tools. Any nonempty override retains custom selection and the existing security-checked fallback behavior without the maintained fixture pins.
   229	- Update channel switch smoke: `pnpm test:docker:update-channel-switch` installs the packed OpenClaw tarball globally in Docker, switches from package `stable` to git `dev`, verifies the persisted channel and plugin post-update work, then switches back to package `stable` and checks update status.
   230	- Upgrade survivor smoke: `pnpm test:docker:upgrade-survivor` installs the packed OpenClaw tarball over a dirty old-user fixture with agents, channel config, plugin allowlists, stale plugin dependency state, and existing workspace/session files. It runs package update plus non-interactive doctor without live provider or channel keys, then starts a loopback Gateway and checks config/state preservation plus startup/status budgets.
   231	- Published upgrade survivor smoke: `pnpm test:docker:published-upgrade-survivor` installs `openclaw@latest` by default, seeds realistic existing-user files, configures that baseline with a baked command recipe, validates the resulting config, updates that published install to the candidate tarball, runs non-interactive doctor, writes `.artifacts/upgrade-survivor/summary.json`, then starts a loopback Gateway and checks configured intents, state preservation, startup, `/healthz`, `/readyz`, and RPC status budgets. Override one baseline with `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_BASELINE_SPEC`, ask the aggregate scheduler to expand exact local baselines with `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_BASELINE_SPECS` such as `openclaw@2026.6.1 openclaw@2026.7.1 openclaw@2026.9.5`, and expand issue-shaped fixtures with `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_SCENARIOS` such as `reported-issues`; the reported-issues set includes `configured-plugin-installs` for automatic external OpenClaw plugin install repair. Package Acceptance exposes those as `published_upgrade_survivor_baseline`, `published_upgrade_survivor_baselines`, and `published_upgrade_survivor_scenarios`, resolves meta baseline tokens such as `last-stable-4` or `all-since-2026.6.1`, and Full Release Validation runs all `reported-issues` scenarios against the latest stable baseline, resolved once to an exact package before fanout. Historical matrices remain explicit manual overrides.
   232	
   233	  Successful published-upgrade runs also include a host-redacted `summary.json` in the Docker artifacts. The receipt keeps the initial update output and its post-core observation separate from repair and recovery output. Missing initial post-core evidence stays unknown; a passed scenario can include capability-consent recovery.
   234	
   235	- Session runtime context smoke: `pnpm test:docker:session-runtime-context` verifies hidden runtime context transcript persistence, Doctor repair of affected duplicated prompt-rewrite branches, and automatic transcript cold storage through a real Gateway running the installed package. Small, dated synthetic histories exercise the maintenance worker, changes to `session.maintenance.coldStorage.afterDays` without a Gateway restart, exact transcript restoration, explicit errors for missing archive files, Activity listing without rehydration, and history preservation across restart and portable backup recovery.
   236	- Bun global install and runtime smoke: `bash scripts/e2e/bun-global-install-smoke.sh` packs the current tree, installs it with `bun install -g --trust` in an isolated home, verifies OpenClaw's lifecycle scripts ran, and executes the installed package with Bun 1.4 or newer. It checks representative CLI state, bundled image providers, a mocked local agent turn, Gateway readiness and health, and a mocked agent turn through the Bun-hosted Gateway. Reuse a prebuilt tarball with `OPENCLAW_BUN_GLOBAL_SMOKE_PACKAGE_TGZ=/path/to/openclaw-*.tgz`, skip the host build with `OPENCLAW_BUN_GLOBAL_SMOKE_HOST_BUILD=0`, or copy `dist/` from a built Docker image with `OPENCLAW_BUN_GLOBAL_SMOKE_DIST_IMAGE=openclaw-dockerfile-smoke:local`.
   237	- Installer Docker smoke: `bash scripts/test-install-sh-docker.sh` shares one npm cache across its root, update, and direct-npm containers. Update smoke defaults to npm `latest` as the stable baseline before upgrading to the candidate tarball. Override with `OPENCLAW_INSTALL_SMOKE_UPDATE_BASELINE=2026.9.5` locally, or with the Install Smoke workflow's `update_baseline_version` input on GitHub. Non-root installer checks keep an isolated npm cache so root-owned cache entries do not mask user-local install behavior. Set `OPENCLAW_INSTALL_SMOKE_NPM_CACHE_DIR=/path/to/cache` to reuse the root/update/direct-npm cache across local reruns.
   238	- Install Smoke CI skips the duplicate direct-npm global update with `OPENCLAW_INSTALL_SMOKE_SKIP_NPM_GLOBAL=1`; run the script locally without that env when direct `npm install -g` coverage is needed.
   239	- Agents delete shared workspace CLI smoke: `pnpm test:docker:agents-delete-shared-workspace` (script: `scripts/e2e/agents-delete-shared-workspace-docker.sh`) builds the root Dockerfile image by default, seeds two agents with one workspace in an isolated container home, runs `agents delete --json`, and verifies valid JSON plus retained workspace behavior. Reuse the install-smoke image with `OPENCLAW_AGENTS_DELETE_SHARED_WORKSPACE_E2E_IMAGE=openclaw-dockerfile-smoke:local OPENCLAW_AGENTS_DELETE_SHARED_WORKSPACE_E2E_SKIP_BUILD=1`.
   240	- Gateway networking and host lifecycle: `pnpm test:docker:gateway-network` (script: `scripts/e2e/gateway-network-docker.sh`) preserves the two-container LAN WebSocket auth/health smoke, then uses loopback Admin HTTP to prove prepare fencing, retained-control access, resume recovery, and a prepared same-container stop/start. The restart check must finish before the original lease expires, verifies that suspension state is process-local while persisted Gateway config and container identity survive, and emits machine-readable phase timing JSON.
   241	- Browser CDP snapshot smoke: `pnpm test:docker:browser-cdp-snapshot` (script: `scripts/e2e/browser-cdp-snapshot-docker.sh`) builds the source E2E image plus a Chromium layer, starts Chromium with raw CDP, runs `browser doctor --deep`, and verifies CDP role snapshots cover link URLs, cursor-promoted clickables, iframe refs, and frame metadata.
   242	- OpenAI Responses web_search minimal reasoning regression: `pnpm test:docker:openai-web-search-minimal` (script: `scripts/e2e/openai-web-search-minimal-docker.sh`) runs a mocked OpenAI server through Gateway, verifies `web_search` raises `reasoning.effort` from `minimal` to `low`, then forces the provider schema reject and checks the raw detail appears in Gateway logs.
   243	- OpenAI chat tools and archived-history recall: `pnpm test:docker:openai-chat-tools` verifies an external tool call through the chat-completions endpoint, then runs one live agent turn whose answer requires a nonce from an archived transcript. The recall prompt omits the nonce, so the response proves that restored history reaches the provider. This lane requires an OpenAI API key.
   244	- MCP channel bridge (seeded Gateway + stdio bridge + raw Claude notification-frame smoke): `pnpm test:docker:mcp-channels` (script: `scripts/e2e/mcp-channels-docker.sh`)
   245	- OpenClaw bundle MCP tools (real stdio MCP server + embedded OpenClaw profile allow/deny smoke): `pnpm test:docker:agent-bundle-mcp-tools` (script: `scripts/e2e/agent-bundle-mcp-tools-docker.sh`)
   246	- Cron/subagent MCP cleanup (real Gateway + stdio MCP child teardown after isolated cron and one-shot subagent runs): `pnpm test:docker:cron-mcp-cleanup` (script: `scripts/e2e/cron-mcp-cleanup-docker.sh`)
   247	- Plugins (install/update smoke for local path, `file:`, npm registry with hoisted dependencies, malformed npm package metadata, git moving refs, ClawHub kitchen-sink, marketplace updates, and Claude-bundle enable/inspect): `pnpm test:docker:plugins` (script: `scripts/e2e/plugins-docker.sh`)
   248	  Set `OPENCLAW_PLUGINS_E2E_CLAWHUB=0` to skip the ClawHub block, or override the default kitchen-sink package/runtime pair with `OPENCLAW_PLUGINS_E2E_CLAWHUB_SPEC` and `OPENCLAW_PLUGINS_E2E_CLAWHUB_ID`. Without `OPENCLAW_CLAWHUB_URL`/`CLAWHUB_URL`, the test uses a hermetic local ClawHub fixture server.
   249	  Set `OPENCLAW_PLUGINS_E2E_LIVE_CLAWHUB=1` to use live ClawHub with the published `@openclaw/kitchen-sink` package; otherwise the hermetic fixture remains the Docker/CI default, even when a ClawHub URL is set.
   250	- Plugin update unchanged smoke: `pnpm test:docker:plugin-update` (script: `scripts/e2e/plugin-update-unchanged-docker.sh`)
   251	- Plugin lifecycle matrix smoke: `pnpm test:docker:plugin-lifecycle-matrix` installs the packed OpenClaw tarball in a bare container, installs an npm plugin, toggles enable/disable, upgrades and downgrades it through a local npm registry, deletes the installed code, then verifies uninstall still removes stale state while logging RSS/CPU metrics for each lifecycle phase.
   252	- Config reload metadata smoke: `pnpm test:docker:config-reload` (script: `scripts/e2e/config-reload-source-docker.sh`)
   253	
   254	Full Release Validation includes both cold-storage cases in its stable and full
   255	Docker release-path profiles: `session-runtime-context` belongs to `core`, and
   256	`openai-chat-tools` belongs to `package-update-openai`. Both use the candidate
   257	package installed in the shared functional image. These bounded cases complement
   258	the storage unit tests by checking packaged workers, Gateway configuration and
   259	history requests, filesystem recovery, and a real provider turn; they do not
   260	repeat a large database stress campaign on every release.
   261	
   262	When newer tooling validates an explicitly authorized older frozen target that
   263	predates cold storage, compatibility preflight records the cold subcases as
   264	unsupported and the lanes report **NOT RUN** for them. The original checks still
   265	run, and current targets always require cold-storage coverage.
   266	
   267	To prebuild and reuse the shared functional image manually:
   268	
   269	```bash
   270	OPENCLAW_DOCKER_E2E_IMAGE=openclaw-docker-e2e-functional:local pnpm test:docker:e2e-build
   271	OPENCLAW_DOCKER_E2E_IMAGE=openclaw-docker-e2e-functional:local OPENCLAW_SKIP_DOCKER_BUILD=1 pnpm test:docker:mcp-channels
   272	```
   273	
   274	Suite-specific image overrides such as `OPENCLAW_GATEWAY_NETWORK_E2E_IMAGE` still win when set. When `OPENCLAW_SKIP_DOCKER_BUILD=1` points at a remote shared image, the scripts pull it if it is not already local. The QR and installer Docker tests keep their own Dockerfiles because they validate package/install behavior rather than the shared built-app runtime.
   275	
   276	The live-model Docker runners also bind-mount the current checkout read-only
   277	and stage it into a temporary workdir inside the container. This keeps the
   278	runtime image slim while still running Vitest against your exact local
   279	source/config. The staging step skips large local-only caches and app build
   280	outputs such as `.pnpm-store`, `.worktrees`, `__openclaw_vitest__`, and
   281	app-local `.build` or Gradle output directories so Docker live runs do not
   282	spend minutes copying machine-specific artifacts. They also set
   283	`OPENCLAW_SKIP_CHANNELS=1` so gateway live probes do not start real
   284	Telegram/Discord/etc. channel workers inside the container.
   285	`test:docker:live-models` still runs `pnpm test:live`, so pass through
   286	`OPENCLAW_LIVE_GATEWAY_*` as well when you need to narrow or exclude gateway
   287	live coverage from that Docker lane.
   288	
   289	`test:docker:openwebui` is a higher-level compatibility smoke: it starts an
   290	OpenClaw gateway container with the OpenAI-compatible HTTP endpoints enabled,
   291	starts a pinned Open WebUI container against that gateway, signs in through
   292	Open WebUI, verifies `/api/models` exposes `openclaw/default`, then sends a
   293	real chat request through Open WebUI's `/api/chat/completions` proxy. Set
   294	`OPENWEBUI_SMOKE_MODE=models` for release-path CI checks that should stop
   295	after Open WebUI sign-in and model discovery, without waiting on a live model
   296	completion. The first run can be noticeably slower because Docker may need to
   297	pull the Open WebUI image and Open WebUI may need to finish its own
   298	cold-start setup. This lane expects a usable live model key, provided through
   299	the process environment, staged auth profiles, or an explicit
   300	`OPENCLAW_PROFILE_FILE`. Successful runs print a small JSON payload like
   301	`{ "ok": true, "model": "openclaw/default", ... }`.
   302	
   303	`test:docker:mcp-channels` is intentionally deterministic and does not need a
   304	real Telegram, Discord, or iMessage account. It boots a seeded Gateway
   305	container, starts a second container that spawns `openclaw mcp serve`, then
   306	verifies routed conversation discovery, transcript reads, attachment
   307	metadata, live event queue behavior, outbound send routing, and Claude-style
   308	channel + permission notifications over the real stdio MCP bridge. The
   309	notification check inspects the raw stdio MCP frames directly so the smoke
   310	validates what the bridge actually emits, not just what a specific client SDK
   311	happens to surface.
   312	
   313	`test:docker:agent-bundle-mcp-tools` is deterministic and does not need a
   314	live model key. It builds the repo Docker image, starts a real stdio MCP
   315	probe server inside the container, materializes that server through the
   316	embedded OpenClaw bundle MCP runtime, executes the tool, then verifies
   317	`coding` and `messaging` keep `bundle-mcp` tools while `minimal` and
   318	`tools.deny: ["bundle-mcp"]` filter them.
   319	
   320	`test:docker:cron-mcp-cleanup` is deterministic and does not need a live
   321	model key. It starts a seeded Gateway with a real stdio MCP probe server,
   322	runs an isolated cron turn and a `sessions_spawn` one-shot child turn, then
   323	verifies the MCP child process exits after each run.
   324	
   325	Manual ACP plain-language thread smoke (not CI):
   326	
   327	- `bun scripts/dev/discord-acp-plain-language-smoke.ts --channel <discord-channel-id> ...`
   328	- Keep this script for regression/debug workflows. It may be needed again for ACP thread routing validation, so do not delete it.
   329	
   330	Useful env vars:
   331	
   332	- `OPENCLAW_CONFIG_DIR=...` (default: `~/.openclaw`) mounted to `/home/node/.openclaw`
   333	- `OPENCLAW_WORKSPACE_DIR=...` (default: `~/.openclaw/workspace`) mounted to `/home/node/.openclaw/workspace`
   334	- `OPENCLAW_PROFILE_FILE=...` mounted and sourced before running tests
   335	- `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_PROFILE_ENV_ONLY=1` to verify only env vars sourced from `OPENCLAW_PROFILE_FILE`, using temporary config/workspace dirs and no external CLI auth mounts
   336	- `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_CLI_TOOLS_DIR=...` (default: `~/.cache/openclaw/docker-cli-tools`, unless the run already uses a CI/managed bind dir) mounted to `/home/node/.npm-global` for cached CLI installs inside Docker
   337	- External CLI auth dirs/files under `$HOME` are mounted read-only under `/host-auth...`, then copied into `/home/node/...` before tests start
   338	  - Default dirs (used when the run is not narrowed to specific providers): `.factory`, `.gemini`, `.minimax`
   339	  - Default files: `~/.codex/auth.json`, `~/.codex/config.toml`, `.claude.json`, `~/.claude/.credentials.json`, `~/.claude/settings.json`, `~/.claude/settings.local.json`
   340	  - Narrowed provider runs mount only the needed dirs/files inferred from `OPENCLAW_LIVE_PROVIDERS` / `OPENCLAW_LIVE_GATEWAY_PROVIDERS`
   341	  - Override manually with `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_AUTH_DIRS=all`, `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_AUTH_DIRS=none`, or a comma list like `OPENCLAW_DOCKER_AUTH_DIRS=.claude,.codex`
   342	- `OPENCLAW_LIVE_GATEWAY_MODELS=...` / `OPENCLAW_LIVE_MODELS=...` to narrow the run
   343	- `OPENCLAW_LIVE_GATEWAY_PROVIDERS=...` / `OPENCLAW_LIVE_PROVIDERS=...` to filter providers in-container
   344	- `OPENCLAW_SKIP_DOCKER_BUILD=1` to reuse an existing `openclaw:local-live` image for reruns that do not need a rebuild
   345	- `OPENCLAW_LIVE_REQUIRE_PROFILE_KEYS=1` to ensure creds come from the profile store (not env)
   346	- `OPENCLAW_OPENWEBUI_MODEL=...` to choose the model exposed by the gateway for the Open WebUI smoke
   347	- `OPENCLAW_OPENWEBUI_PROMPT=...` to override the nonce-check prompt used by the Open WebUI smoke
   348	- `OPENWEBUI_IMAGE=...` to override the pinned Open WebUI image tag
   349	---
   350	summary: "Live provider debugging lanes plus the Docker and Parallels smokes that support them"
   351	title: "Live and Docker/Parallels workflows"
   352	read_when:
   353	  - You are debugging a real provider or model
   354	  - You need a live Docker or Parallels lane
   355	---
   356	
   357	## Live and Docker/Parallels workflows
   358	
   359	When debugging real providers/models (requires real creds):
   360	
   361	- Live suite (models + gateway tool/image probes): `pnpm test:live`
   362	- Target one live file quietly: `pnpm test:live -- src/agents/models.profiles.live.test.ts`
   363	- Progress-card refresh: `OPENCLAW_LIVE_TEST=1 pnpm test:live -- src/gateway/gateway-progress-refresh.live.test.ts`
   364	  - Requires `OPENAI_API_KEY` and uses `openai/gpt-5.6-luna` with isolated Gateway state.
   365	  - Completes an earlier turn, then refreshes during a second turn while a command remains held. The original parent must update the card and retain its final reply. A later idle refresh must update the card without adding chat messages or resuming pending work.
   366	- Live subagent handoff stress:
   367	  `OPENCLAW_LIVE_TEST=1 OPENCLAW_LIVE_SUBAGENT_STRESS=1 pnpm test:live -- src/agents/subagents/announce/subagent-yield-resume.live.test.ts`
   368	  - Requires `OPENAI_API_KEY` and defaults to `openai/gpt-5.6-luna`; select another
   369	    OpenAI model with `OPENCLAW_LIVE_SUBAGENT_E2E_MODEL`.
   370	  - Pins the OpenClaw agent harness and uses isolated Gateway state, synthetic
   371	    files, and externally held HTTP responses. It checks concurrent hidden-result
   372	    fanout, status-only interrogation of a waiting child tree, operator resume
   373	    with preserved task identity and idempotent replay, child timeout, a live HTTP
   374	    503 retrieval failure, and cancellation racing an in-flight result after its
   375	    owner claims the run. Parent reports are checked against held requests,
   376	    child execution, task delivery state, and hidden results. The 503 case checks
   377	    that a completed agent turn does not imply a successful retrieval.
   378	  - Each case incrementally preserves bounded runtime facts, plus final assistant replies, in
   379	    `.artifacts/qa-e2e/subagent-challenges-*/evidence.json`, including failed runs.
   380	    Set `OPENCLAW_LIVE_SUBAGENT_EVIDENCE_DIR` to change the output directory.
   381	    This live lane does not simulate cold process loss; restart and lost-acceptance
   382	    ownership are covered by `subagent-orphan-recovery.restart-integration.test.ts`.
   383	  - Defaults to two batches of three children. Set
   384	    `OPENCLAW_LIVE_SUBAGENT_STRESS_BATCHES` (1–5) and
   385	    `OPENCLAW_LIVE_SUBAGENT_STRESS_CHILDREN` (1–6) to change the bounded workload.
   386	- Live Gateway concurrency: dispatch `OpenClaw Performance` on `main` with
   387	  `mode=gateway-concurrency` and `live_openai_candidate=true`. It runs 96 real
   388	  turns across 32 agents with 1,000 seeded sessions, concurrent session activity,
   389	  and load-phase CPU profiles. Dreaming is disabled while ordinary indexing and
   390	  recaps remain enabled. Results stay in Actions artifacts. See
   391	  [Gateway concurrency benchmark](/ci/scheduled-workflows#gateway-concurrency-benchmark).
   392	- Runtime performance reports: dispatch `OpenClaw Performance` with
   393	  `live_openai_candidate=true` for a real `openai/gpt-5.6-luna` agent turn or
   394	  `deep_profile=true` for Kova CPU/heap/trace artifacts. Daily scheduled runs
   395	  publish mock-provider, deep-profile, and GPT-5.6 Luna lane reports to
   396	  `openclaw/clawgrit-reports` from a separate artifact-consuming publisher job;
   397	  missing or invalid publisher authentication fails scheduled and
   398	  `profile=release` runs. Manual non-release dispatches keep the GitHub artifacts
   399	  and treat report publication as advisory. The mock-provider report also
   400	  includes source-level gateway boot, memory, plugin-pressure, repeated
   401	  fake-model hello-loop, and CLI startup numbers.
   402	- Docker live model sweep: `pnpm test:docker:live-models`
   403	  - Each selected model runs a text turn plus a small file-read-style probe.
   404	    Models whose metadata advertises `image` input also run a tiny image turn.
   405	    Disable the extra probes with `OPENCLAW_LIVE_MODEL_FILE_PROBE=0` or
   406	    `OPENCLAW_LIVE_MODEL_IMAGE_PROBE=0` when isolating provider failures.
   407	  - CI coverage: daily `OpenClaw Scheduled Live And E2E Checks` and manual
   408	    `OpenClaw Release Checks` both call the reusable live/E2E workflow with
   409	    `include_live_suites: true`, which includes Docker live model matrix jobs
   410	    sharded by provider.
   411	  - For focused CI reruns, dispatch `OpenClaw Live And E2E Checks (Reusable)`
   412	    with `include_live_suites: true` and `live_models_only: true`.
   413	  - Add new high-signal provider secrets to `scripts/ci-hydrate-live-auth.sh`
   414	    plus `.github/workflows/openclaw-live-and-e2e-checks-reusable.yml` and its
   415	    scheduled/release callers.
   416	- Native Codex bound-chat smoke: `pnpm test:docker:live-codex-bind`
   417	  - Runs a Docker live lane against the Codex app-server path, binds a
   418	    synthetic Slack DM with `/codex bind`, exercises `/codex fast` and
   419	    `/codex permissions`, then verifies a plain reply and an image attachment
   420	    route through the native plugin binding instead of ACP.
   421	- Codex app-server harness smoke: `pnpm test:docker:live-codex-harness`
   422	  - Runs gateway agent turns through the plugin-owned Codex app-server
   423	    harness, verifies `/codex status` and `/codex models`, and by default
   424	    exercises image, cron MCP, sub-agent, and Guardian probes. Disable the
   425	    sub-agent probe with `OPENCLAW_LIVE_CODEX_HARNESS_SUBAGENT_PROBE=0` when
   426	    isolating other failures. For a focused sub-agent check, disable the
   427	    other probes:
   428	    `OPENCLAW_LIVE_CODEX_HARNESS_IMAGE_PROBE=0 OPENCLAW_LIVE_CODEX_HARNESS_MCP_PROBE=0 OPENCLAW_LIVE_CODEX_HARNESS_GUARDIAN_PROBE=0 OPENCLAW_LIVE_CODEX_HARNESS_SUBAGENT_PROBE=1 pnpm test:docker:live-codex-harness`.
   429	    This exits after the sub-agent probe unless
   430	    `OPENCLAW_LIVE_CODEX_HARNESS_SUBAGENT_ONLY=0` is set.
   431	- Codex on-demand install smoke: `pnpm test:docker:codex-on-demand`
   432	  - Installs the packaged OpenClaw tarball in Docker, runs OpenAI API-key
   433	    onboarding, and verifies the Codex plugin plus `@openai/codex` dependency
   434	    were downloaded into the managed npm project root on demand.
   435	- Codex npm-plugin live package smoke: `pnpm test:docker:live-codex-npm-plugin`
   436	  - Installs the candidate OpenClaw package and exact Codex plugin into Docker,
   437	    then uses a real OpenAI key for CLI preflight and same-session turns.
   438	  - Its zero-retry medium-thinking follow-through turn must send progress, keep
   439	    working through randomized workspace reads and an exact artifact write,
   440	    then send completion. A progress-only terminal turn fails the lane.
   441	- Live plugin tool dependency smoke: `pnpm test:docker:live-plugin-tool`
   442	  - Packs a fixture plugin with a real `slugify` dependency, installs it
   443	    through `npm-pack:`, verifies the dependency under the managed npm
   444	    project root, then asks a live OpenAI model to call the plugin tool and
   445	    return the hidden slug.
   446	- OpenClaw rescue command smoke: `pnpm test:live:system-agent-rescue-channel`
   447	  - Opt-in belt-and-suspenders check for the message-channel rescue command
   448	    surface. Exercises `/openclaw status`, queues a persistent model
   449	    change, replies `/openclaw yes`, and verifies the audit/config write
   450	    path.
   451	- OpenClaw first-run Docker smoke: `pnpm test:docker:system-agent-first-run`
   452	  - Starts from an empty OpenClaw state dir and first proves the packaged
   453	    `openclaw setup` CLI fails closed without inference. It then
   454	    tests and activates fake Claude through the packaged activation module.
   455	    Only afterward does a fuzzy packaged CLI request reach the planner and
   456	    resolve to typed setup, followed by one-shot model, agent, Discord config,
   457	    and SecretRef operations. It validates config and audit entries. This is
   458	    supporting gate/operation evidence, not an interactive onboarding or
   459	    OpenClaw agent/tool/approval proof. The same lane is exposed in QA Lab by
   460	    `pnpm openclaw qa suite --scenario system-agent-ring-zero-setup`.
   461	- Moonshot/Kimi cost smoke: with `MOONSHOT_API_KEY` set, run
   462	  `openclaw models list --provider moonshot --json`, then run an isolated
   463	  `openclaw agent --local --session-id live-kimi-cost --message 'Reply exactly: KIMI_LIVE_OK' --thinking off --json`
   464	  against `moonshot/kimi-k2.6`. Verify the JSON reports Moonshot/K2.6 and the
   465	  assistant transcript stores normalized `usage.cost`.
   466	
   467	<Tip>
   468	When you only need one failing case, prefer narrowing live tests via the [allowlist env vars](/help/testing/docker).
   469	</Tip>
   470	---
   471	summary: "The qa-lab command surface, shared Convex credentials, and adding a channel to QA"
   472	title: "QA-specific runners"
   473	read_when:
   474	  - You are running a QA Lab or live transport lane
   475	  - You need the shared QA credential contract
   476	---
   477	
   478	## QA-specific runners
   479	
   480	These commands sit beside the main test suites when you need QA-lab realism.
   481	
   482	CI runs QA Lab in dedicated workflows. Agentic parity is nested under
   483	`QA-Lab - All Lanes` and release validation, not a standalone PR workflow.
   484	Broad validation should use `Full Release Validation` with
   485	`rerun_group=qa-parity` for parity or `rerun_group=qa-live` for live QA.
   486	The direct `OpenClaw Release Checks` child alone may use `rerun_group=qa` as a
   487	manual aggregate of both groups. Stable/full, soak-enabled, and explicit
   488	`qa-live` release checks include the QA-live Matrix and Telegram lanes. Bounded
   489	beta-publish `all` without soak runs parity but defers those live lanes to
   490	postpublish-confidence. `QA-Lab - All Lanes` runs
   491	nightly on `main` and from manual dispatch with the mock parity lane, live
   492	Matrix lane, Convex-managed live Telegram lane, and Convex-managed live Discord
   493	lane as parallel jobs. Scheduled QA and selected release checks run the
   494	catalog-derived Matrix selection through the shared live adapter. Release
   495	transport checks use `mock-openai/gpt-5.6-luna` so they stay deterministic and
   496	avoid normal provider-plugin startup. These live transport gateways disable
   497	memory search; memory behavior stays covered by the QA parity suites.
   498	
   499	Full release live media shards use
   500	`ghcr.io/openclaw/openclaw-live-media-runner:ubuntu-24.04`, which already has
   501	`ffmpeg` and `ffprobe`. Docker live model/backend shards use the shared
   502	`ghcr.io/openclaw/openclaw-live-test:<sha>` image built once per selected
   503	commit, then pull it with `OPENCLAW_SKIP_DOCKER_BUILD=1` instead of rebuilding
   504	inside every shard.
   505	
   506	- `pnpm openclaw qa suite`
   507	  - Runs repo-backed QA scenarios directly on the host.
   508	  - Writes top-level `qa-evidence.json`, `qa-suite-summary.json`, and
   509	    `qa-suite-report.md` artifacts for the selected scenario set, including
   510	    mixed flow, Vitest, and Playwright scenario selections.
   511	  - When dispatched by `pnpm openclaw qa run --qa-profile <profile>`, embeds
   512	    the selected taxonomy profile scorecard in the same `qa-evidence.json`.
   513	    `smoke-ci` writes slim evidence (`evidenceMode: "slim"`, no per-entry
   514	    `execution`). `release` covers the curated release-readiness slice; `all`
   515	    selects every active maturity category and targets explicit QA Profile
   516	    Evidence workflow dispatches when a full scorecard artifact is needed.
   517	  - Runs multiple selected scenarios in parallel by default with isolated
   518	    gateway workers. `qa-channel` defaults to concurrency 4 (bounded by the
   519	    selected scenario count). Use `--concurrency <count>` to tune the worker
   520	    count, or `--concurrency 1` for the older serial lane.
   521	    Each worker owns a stable named profile as well as separate home, state,
   522	    and config paths, so its CLI bootstrap cannot select the operator's installed
   523	    Gateway service. Parent profiles and runtime environment patches do not
   524	    override that worker identity.
   525	  - Exits non-zero when any scenario fails. Use `--allow-failures` for
   526	    artifacts without a failing exit code.
   527	  - Supports provider modes `live-frontier`, `mock-openai`, and `aimock`.
   528	    `aimock` starts a local AIMock-backed provider server for experimental
   529	    fixture and protocol-mock coverage without replacing the scenario-aware
   530	    `mock-openai` lane.
   531	- `pnpm openclaw qa coverage --match <query>`
   532	  - Searches scenario IDs, titles, surfaces, coverage IDs, docs refs, code
   533	    refs, plugins, and provider requirements, then prints matching suite
   534	    targets.
   535	  - Use this before a QA Lab run when you know the touched behavior or file
   536	    path but not the smallest scenario. Advisory only - still choose mock,
   537	    live, Multipass, Matrix, or transport proof from the behavior being
   538	    changed.
   539	- `pnpm test:plugins:kitchen-sink-live`
   540	  - Runs the live OpenAI Kitchen Sink plugin gauntlet through QA Lab.
   541	    Installs the external Kitchen Sink package, verifies the plugin SDK
   542	    surface inventory, probes `/healthz` and `/readyz`, records gateway
   543	    CPU/RSS evidence, runs a live OpenAI turn, and checks adversarial
   544	    diagnostics. Requires live OpenAI auth such as `OPENAI_API_KEY`. In
   545	    hydrated Testbox sessions it automatically sources the Testbox live-auth
   546	    profile when the `openclaw-testbox-env` helper is present.
   547	- `pnpm test:gateway:cpu-scenarios`
   548	  - Runs the gateway startup bench plus a small mock QA Lab scenario pack
   549	    (`channel-chat-baseline`, `memory-failure-fallback`,
   550	    `gateway-restart-inflight-run`) and writes a combined CPU observation
   551	    summary under `.artifacts/gateway-cpu-scenarios/`.
   552	  - Flags only sustained hot CPU observations by default (`--cpu-core-warn`,
   553	    default `0.9`; `--hot-wall-warn-ms`, default `30000`), so short startup
   554	    bursts are recorded as metrics without looking like the minutes-long
   555	    gateway peg regression.
   556	  - Runs against built `dist` artifacts; run a build first when the checkout
   557	    does not already have fresh runtime output.
   558	- `pnpm openclaw qa suite --runner multipass`
   559	  - Runs the same QA suite inside a disposable Multipass Linux VM, keeping
   560	    the same scenario-selection and provider/model flags as `qa suite`.
   561	  - Live runs forward the QA auth inputs practical for the guest:
   562	    env-based provider keys, the QA live provider config path, and
   563	    `CODEX_HOME` when present.
   564	  - Output dirs must stay under the repo root so the guest can write back
   565	    through the mounted workspace.
   566	  - Writes the normal QA report + summary plus Multipass logs under
   567	    `.artifacts/qa-e2e/...`.
   568	- `pnpm qa:lab:up`
   569	  - Starts the Docker-backed QA site for operator-style QA work.
   570	- `pnpm test:docker:npm-onboard-channel-agent`
   571	  - Builds an npm tarball from the current checkout, installs it globally in
   572	    Docker, runs non-interactive OpenAI API-key onboarding, configures
   573	    Telegram by default, verifies the packaged plugin runtime loads without
   574	    startup dependency repair, runs doctor, and runs one local agent turn
   575	    against a mocked OpenAI endpoint.
   576	  - Use `OPENCLAW_NPM_ONBOARD_CHANNEL=discord` to run the same packaged-install
   577	    lane with Discord.
   578	- `pnpm test:docker:session-runtime-context`
   579	  - Runs a deterministic built-app Docker smoke for embedded runtime context
   580	    transcripts. Verifies hidden OpenClaw runtime context persists as a
   581	    non-display custom message instead of leaking into the visible user
   582	    turn, then seeds an affected broken session JSONL and verifies
   583	    `openclaw doctor --fix` rewrites it to the active branch with a backup.
   584	- `pnpm test:docker:npm-telegram-live`
   585	  - Installs an OpenClaw package candidate in Docker, runs installed-package
   586	    onboarding, configures Telegram through the installed CLI, then reuses
   587	    the live Telegram QA lane with that installed package as the SUT
   588	    Gateway.
   589	  - The trusted checkout owns the QA harness source, taxonomy, scenarios,
   590	    dependencies, and private SDK build. The installed package remains the
   591	    absolute CLI, Gateway, and bundled-plugin runtime under test, and its CLI
   592	    writes the package candidate's persisted auth state.
   593	  - Defaults to `OPENCLAW_NPM_TELEGRAM_PACKAGE_SPEC=openclaw@beta`; set
   594	    `OPENCLAW_NPM_TELEGRAM_PACKAGE_TGZ=/path/to/openclaw-current.tgz` or
   595	    `OPENCLAW_CURRENT_PACKAGE_TGZ` to test a resolved local tarball instead
   596	    of installing from the registry.
   597	  - Emits repeated RTT timing in `qa-evidence.json` by default with
   598	    `OPENCLAW_NPM_TELEGRAM_RTT_SAMPLES=20`. Override
   599	    `OPENCLAW_NPM_TELEGRAM_RTT_SAMPLES`,
   600	    `OPENCLAW_NPM_TELEGRAM_RTT_TIMEOUT_MS`, or
   601	    `OPENCLAW_NPM_TELEGRAM_RTT_MAX_FAILURES` to tune the run.
   602	    `OPENCLAW_NPM_TELEGRAM_RTT_CHECKS` accepts zero or exactly one canonical
   603	    Telegram QA scenario id. When omitted, the normal lane samples
   604	    `channel-canary`; focused non-RTT scenario runs stay probe-free. An explicit
   605	    RTT scenario is included in scenario selection automatically, so callers do
   606	    not need to repeat it in `OPENCLAW_NPM_TELEGRAM_SCENARIOS`. Multiple ids
   607	    fail immediately, while unknown or inapplicable ids fail canonical scenario
   608	    validation. The package runner promotes the selected RTT scenario once to
   609	    the first position before the remaining taxonomy-backed fail-fast release
   610	    scenarios.
   611	  - Uses the same Convex-leased Test Server userbot credentials as
   612	    `pnpm openclaw qa telegram`. Set `OPENCLAW_QA_CONVEX_SITE_URL` and the
   613	    secret for the selected role. The Docker wrapper selects Convex by default.
   614	  - The wrapper validates Convex credential env on the host before Docker
   615	    build/install work. Set
   616	    `OPENCLAW_NPM_TELEGRAM_SKIP_CREDENTIAL_PREFLIGHT=1` only when
   617	    deliberately debugging pre-credential setup.
   618	  - `OPENCLAW_NPM_TELEGRAM_CREDENTIAL_ROLE=ci|maintainer` overrides the
   619	    shared `OPENCLAW_QA_CREDENTIAL_ROLE` for this lane only. With no role, the
   620	    wrapper uses `ci` in CI and `maintainer` outside CI.
   621	  - GitHub Actions exposes this lane as the manual maintainer workflow
   622	    `NPM Telegram Beta E2E`. It does not run on merge. The workflow uses the
   623	    `qa-live-shared` environment and Convex CI credential leases. Set its
   624	    optional `rtt_scenario` input to select the repeated RTT scenario, or leave
   625	    it empty for the default behavior above. Enable
   626	    `allow_older_binary_destructive_actions` only for intentional historical
   627	    downgrade or recovery proof; it remains false by default.
   628	- GitHub Actions also exposes `Package Acceptance` for side-run product proof
   629	  against one candidate package. It accepts a Git ref, published npm spec,
   630	  HTTPS tarball URL plus SHA-256, trusted-URL policy, or tarball artifact
   631	  from another run (`source=ref|npm|url|trusted-url|artifact`), uploads the
   632	  normalized `openclaw-current.tgz` as `package-under-test`, then runs the
   633	  existing Docker E2E scheduler with `smoke`, `package`, `product`, `full`,
   634	  or `custom` lane profiles. Set `telegram_mode=mock-openai` or
   635	  `live-frontier` to run the Telegram QA workflow against the same
   636	  `package-under-test` artifact.
   637	  - Latest beta product proof:
   638	
   639	```bash
   640	gh workflow run package-acceptance.yml --ref main \
   641	  -f source=npm \
   642	  -f package_spec=openclaw@beta \
   643	  -f suite_profile=product \
   644	  -f telegram_mode=mock-openai
   645	```
   646	
   647	- Exact tarball URL proof requires a digest and uses the public URL safety policy:
   648	
   649	```bash
   650	gh workflow run package-acceptance.yml --ref main \
   651	  -f source=url \
   652	  -f package_url=https://registry.npmjs.org/openclaw/-/openclaw-VERSION.tgz \
   653	  -f package_sha256=<sha256> \
   654	  -f suite_profile=package
   655	```
   656	
   657	- Enterprise/private tarball mirrors use an explicit trusted-source policy:
   658	
   659	```bash
   660	gh workflow run package-acceptance.yml --ref main \
   661	  -f source=trusted-url \
   662	  -f trusted_source_id=enterprise-artifactory \
   663	  -f package_url=https://packages.example.internal:8443/artifactory/openclaw/openclaw-VERSION.tgz \
   664	  -f package_sha256=<sha256> \
   665	  -f suite_profile=package
   666	```
   667	
   668	`source=trusted-url` reads `.github/package-trusted-sources.json` from the trusted workflow ref and does not accept URL credentials or a workflow-input private-network bypass. If the named policy declares bearer auth, configure the fixed `OPENCLAW_TRUSTED_PACKAGE_TOKEN` secret.
   669	
   670	- Artifact proof downloads a tarball artifact from another Actions run:
   671	
   672	```bash
   673	gh workflow run package-acceptance.yml --ref main \
   674	  -f source=artifact \
   675	  -f artifact_run_id=<run-id> \
   676	  -f artifact_name=<artifact-name> \
   677	  -f suite_profile=smoke
   678	```
   679	
   680	- `pnpm test:docker:plugins`
   681	  - Packs and installs the current OpenClaw build in Docker, starts the
   682	    Gateway with OpenAI configured, then enables bundled channel/plugins via
   683	    config edits.
   684	  - Verifies setup discovery leaves unconfigured downloadable plugins
   685	    absent, the first configured doctor repair installs each missing
   686	    downloadable plugin explicitly, and a second restart does not run
   687	    hidden dependency repair.
   688	  - Also installs a known older npm baseline, enables Telegram before
   689	    running `openclaw update --tag <candidate>`, and verifies the
   690	    candidate's post-update doctor cleans legacy plugin dependency debris
   691	    without a harness-side postinstall repair.
   692	- `pnpm test:parallels:npm-update`
   693	  - Runs the native packaged-install update smoke across Parallels guests.
   694	    Each selected platform first installs the requested baseline package,
   695	    then runs the installed `openclaw update` command in the same guest and
   696	    verifies the installed version, update status, gateway readiness, and
   697	    one local agent turn.
   698	  - Use `--platform macos`, `--platform windows`, or `--platform linux`
   699	    while iterating on one guest. Use `--json` for the summary artifact
   700	    path and per-lane status.
   701	  - The OpenAI lane uses `openai/gpt-5.6-luna` for the live agent-turn proof by
   702	    default. Pass `--model <provider/model>` or set
   703	    `OPENCLAW_PARALLELS_OPENAI_MODEL` to validate another OpenAI model.
   704	  - Wrap long local runs in a host timeout so Parallels transport stalls
   705	    cannot consume the rest of the testing window:
   706	
   707	    ```bash
   708	    timeout --foreground 150m pnpm test:parallels:npm-update -- --json
   709	    timeout --foreground 90m pnpm test:parallels:npm-update -- --platform windows --json
   710	    ```
   711	
   712	  - The script writes nested lane logs under
   713	    `/tmp/openclaw-parallels-npm-update.*`. Inspect `windows-update.log`,
   714	    `macos-update.log`, or `linux-update.log` before assuming the outer
   715	    wrapper is hung.
   716	  - Windows update can spend 10 to 15 minutes in post-update doctor and
   717	    package update work on a cold guest; that is still healthy when the
   718	    nested npm debug log is advancing.
   719	  - Do not run this aggregate wrapper in parallel with individual Parallels
   720	    macOS, Windows, or Linux smoke lanes. They share VM state and can
   721	    collide on snapshot restore, package serving, or guest gateway state.
   722	  - The post-update proof runs the normal bundled plugin surface because
   723	    capability facades such as speech, image generation, and media
   724	    understanding load through bundled runtime APIs even when the agent
   725	    turn itself only checks a simple text response.
   726	
   727	- `pnpm openclaw qa aimock`
   728	  - Starts only the local AIMock provider server for direct protocol smoke
   729	    testing.
   730	- `pnpm openclaw qa buzz`
   731	  - Runs the Buzz live QA lane against a real relay room using dedicated driver
   732	    and SUT identities.
   733	  - Local runs use `--credential-file <path>` with `relayUrl`, `roomId`,
   734	    `driverPrivateKey`, and `sutPrivateKey`. Closed relays may also need
   735	    `driverAuthTag` and `sutAuthTag`. Hosted relays require `wss://`; `ws://` is
   736	    accepted only for loopback development relays.
   737	  - Defaults to `mock-openai` and runs canary and mention-gating scenarios
   738	    through the real Buzz plugin path.
   739	  - Supports `--credential-source convex` with a pooled `kind: "buzz"` row.
   740	    Both public keys must be relay/room members, and the SUT must have the
   741	    **Bot** room role. Never use a human owner or admin private key.
   742	- `pnpm openclaw qa matrix`
   743	  - Runs the Matrix live QA lane against a disposable Docker-backed Tuwunel
   744	    homeserver. Source-checkout only - packaged installs do not ship
   745	    `qa-lab`.
   746	  - Full CLI, profile/scenario catalog, env vars, and artifact layout:
   747	    [Matrix smoke lanes](/concepts/qa-e2e-automation#matrix-live-lane).
   748	- `pnpm openclaw qa telegram`
   749	  - Runs the Telegram live QA lane on Telegram's Test Server with one
   750	    Convex-leased SUT bot and one independent TDLib user session.
   751	  - Uses `--credential-source convex` by default and rejects `env`. Provide
   752	    `OPENCLAW_QA_CONVEX_SITE_URL` and the secret for the selected
   753	    `--credential-role`.
   754	  - Defaults cover canary, mention gating, command addressing, `/status`,
   755	    bot-to-bot mentioned replies, and core native command replies.
   756	    `mock-openai` defaults also cover deterministic reply-chain and
   757	    Telegram final-message streaming regressions. Use `--list-scenarios`
   758	    for optional probes such as `session_status`.
   759	  - Exits non-zero when any scenario fails. Use `--allow-failures` for
   760	    artifacts without a failing exit code.
   761	  - The leased user drives and observes the shared Test Server group. No
   762	    production Telegram account or bot-to-bot observer is used.
   763	  - Writes a Telegram QA report, summary, and `qa-evidence.json` under
   764	    `.artifacts/qa-e2e/...`. Replying scenarios include RTT from driver send
   765	    request to observed SUT reply.
   766	
   767	Live transport lanes share one standard contract so new transports do not
   768	drift; the per-lane coverage matrix lives in
   769	[QA overview - Live transport coverage](/concepts/qa-e2e-automation#buzz%2C-discord%2C-slack%2C-telegram%2C-and-whatsapp-qa-reference).
   770	`qa-channel` is the broad synthetic suite and is not part of that matrix.
   771	
   772	### Shared Telegram credentials via Convex (v1)
   773	
   774	When `--credential-source convex` (or `OPENCLAW_QA_CREDENTIAL_SOURCE=convex`)
   775	is enabled for live transport QA, QA lab acquires an exclusive lease from a
   776	Convex-backed pool, heartbeats that lease while the lane is running, and
   777	releases the lease on shutdown. Telegram always uses this source. The section
   778	name predates Buzz, Discord, Slack, and WhatsApp support; the lease contract is
   779	shared across kinds.
   780	
   781	Reference Convex project scaffold: `qa/convex-credential-broker/`
   782	
   783	Required env vars:
   784	
   785	- `OPENCLAW_QA_CONVEX_SITE_URL` (for example `https://your-deployment.convex.site`)
   786	- One secret for the selected role:
   787	  - `OPENCLAW_QA_CONVEX_SECRET_MAINTAINER` for `maintainer`
   788	  - `OPENCLAW_QA_CONVEX_SECRET_CI` for `ci`
   789	- Credential role selection:
   790	  - CLI: `--credential-role maintainer|ci`
   791	  - Env default: `OPENCLAW_QA_CREDENTIAL_ROLE` (defaults to `ci` in CI, `maintainer` otherwise)
   792	
   793	Optional env vars:
   794	
   795	- `OPENCLAW_QA_CREDENTIAL_LEASE_TTL_MS` (default `1200000`)
   796	- `OPENCLAW_QA_CREDENTIAL_HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS` (default `30000`)
   797	- `OPENCLAW_QA_CREDENTIAL_ACQUIRE_TIMEOUT_MS` (default `90000`)
   798	- `OPENCLAW_QA_CREDENTIAL_HTTP_TIMEOUT_MS` (default `15000`)
   799	- `OPENCLAW_QA_CONVEX_ENDPOINT_PREFIX` (default `/qa-credentials/v1`)
   800	- `OPENCLAW_QA_CREDENTIAL_OWNER_ID` (optional trace id)
   801	- `OPENCLAW_QA_ALLOW_INSECURE_HTTP=1` allows loopback `http://` Convex URLs for local-only development.
   802	
   803	`OPENCLAW_QA_CONVEX_SITE_URL` should use `https://` in normal operation.
   804	
   805	Maintainer admin commands (pool add/remove/list) require
   806	`OPENCLAW_QA_CONVEX_SECRET_MAINTAINER` specifically.
   807	
   808	CLI helpers for maintainers:
   809	
   810	```bash
   811	pnpm openclaw qa credentials doctor
   812	pnpm openclaw qa credentials add --kind telegram --payload-file qa/telegram-credential.json
   813	pnpm openclaw qa credentials list --kind telegram
   814	pnpm openclaw qa credentials remove --credential-id <credential-id>
   815	```
   816	
   817	Use `doctor` before live runs to check the Convex site URL, broker secrets,
   818	endpoint prefix, HTTP timeout, and admin/list reachability without printing
   819	secret values. Use `--json` for machine-readable output in scripts and CI
   820	utilities.
   821	
   822	Default endpoint contract (`OPENCLAW_QA_CONVEX_SITE_URL` + `/qa-credentials/v1`).
   823	Requests authenticate with an `Authorization: Bearer <role secret>` header;
   824	bodies below omit that header:
   825	
   826	- `POST /acquire`
   827	  - Request: `{ kind, ownerId, actorRole, leaseTtlMs, heartbeatIntervalMs }`
   828	  - Success: `{ status: "ok", credentialId, leaseToken, payload, leaseTtlMs?, heartbeatIntervalMs? }`
   829	  - Exhausted/retryable: `{ status: "error", code: "POOL_EXHAUSTED" | "NO_CREDENTIAL_AVAILABLE", ... }`
   830	- `POST /payload-chunk`
   831	  - Request: `{ kind, ownerId, actorRole, credentialId, leaseToken, index }`
   832	  - Success: `{ status: "ok", index, data }`
   833	- `POST /heartbeat`
   834	  - Request: `{ kind, ownerId, actorRole, credentialId, leaseToken, leaseTtlMs }`
   835	  - Success: `{ status: "ok" }` (or empty `2xx`)
   836	- `POST /release`
   837	  - Request: `{ kind, ownerId, actorRole, credentialId, leaseToken }`
   838	  - Success: `{ status: "ok" }` (or empty `2xx`)
   839	- `POST /admin/add` (maintainer secret only)
   840	  - Request: `{ kind, actorId, payload, note?, status? }`
   841	  - Success: `{ status: "ok", credential }`
   842	- `POST /admin/remove` (maintainer secret only)
   843	  - Request: `{ credentialId, actorId }`
   844	  - Success: `{ status: "ok", changed, credential }`
   845	  - Active lease guard: `{ status: "error", code: "LEASE_ACTIVE", ... }`
   846	- `POST /admin/list` (maintainer secret only)
   847	  - Request: `{ kind?, status?, includePayload?, limit? }`
   848	  - Success: `{ status: "ok", credentials, count }`
   849	
   850	Payload shape for Telegram kind:
   851	
   852	- `{ groupId: string, driverToken: string, sutToken: string }`
   853	- `groupId` must be a numeric Telegram chat id string.
   854	- `admin/add` validates this shape for `kind: "telegram"` and rejects malformed payloads.
   855	
   856	Broker-validated multi-channel payloads:
   857	
   858	- Buzz: `{ relayUrl: string, roomId: string, driverPrivateKey: string, sutPrivateKey: string, driverAuthTag?: string, sutAuthTag?: string }`
   859	- Discord: `{ guildId: string, channelId: string, driverBotToken: string, sutBotToken: string, sutApplicationId: string, voiceChannelId?: string }`
   860	- WhatsApp: `{ driverPhoneE164: string, sutPhoneE164: string, driverAuthArchiveBase64: string, sutAuthArchiveBase64: string, groupJid?: string }`
   861	
   862	Slack lanes can also lease from the pool, but Slack payload validation
   863	currently lives in the Slack QA runner rather than the broker. Use
   864	`{ channelId: string, driverBotToken: string, sutBotToken: string, sutAppToken: string }`
   865	for Slack rows.
   866	
   867	### Adding a channel to QA
   868	
   869	The architecture and scenario-helper names for new channel adapters live in
   870	[QA overview - Adding a channel](/concepts/qa-e2e-automation#adding-a-channel).
   871	The minimum bar: implement the transport runner on the shared `qa-lab` host
   872	seam, add an `adapterFactory` for shared scenarios, declare `qaRunners` in the
   873	plugin manifest, mount as `openclaw qa <runner>`, and author scenarios under
   874	`qa/scenarios/`.
   875	---
   876	summary: "The unit, e2e, and live suites, which one to run, and the offline regression checks"
   877	title: "Test suites and commands"
   878	read_when:
   879	  - You need to pick a test suite or command
   880	  - You want to know what each suite covers
   881	---
   882	
   883	## Quick start
   884	
   885	Most days:
   886	
   887	- Full gate (expected before push): `pnpm build && pnpm check && pnpm check:test-types && pnpm test`
   888	- Faster local full-suite run on a roomy machine: `pnpm test:max`
   889	- Direct Vitest watch loop: `pnpm test:watch`
   890	- Direct file targeting routes plugin/channel paths too: `pnpm test extensions/discord/src/monitor/message-handler.preflight.test.ts`
   891	- Prefer targeted runs first when iterating on a single failure.
   892	- Docker-backed QA site: `pnpm qa:lab:up`
   893	- Linux VM-backed QA lane: `pnpm openclaw qa suite --runner multipass --scenario channel-chat-baseline`
   894	
   895	When you touch tests or want extra confidence:
   896	
   897	- Informational V8 coverage report: `pnpm test:coverage`
   898	- E2E suite: `pnpm test:e2e`
   899	
   900	Oxlint's `max-lines` rule warns when files exceed the per-scope limits in
   901	`.oxlintrc.json`; these warnings remain visible in lint logs and do not fail CI.
   902	PR CI and `pnpm check:changed` separately reject new line-cap violations and
   903	growth in already over-cap files. The suppression baseline ratchet remains a
   904	required check. See [surface ratchets](/ci/local-proof#surface-ratchets) for
   905	comparison bases and shrink-only maintenance.
   906	
   907	## Test suites (what runs where)
   908	
   909	Think of the suites as "increasing realism" (and increasing flakiness/cost).
   910	
   911	### Unit / integration (default)
   912	
   913	- Command: `pnpm test`
   914	- Config: untargeted runs use the `vitest.full-*.config.ts` shard set and may
   915	  expand multi-project shards into per-project configs for parallel
   916	  scheduling
   917	- Files: core/unit inventories under `src/**/*.test.ts`,
   918	  `packages/**/*.test.ts`, and `test/**/*.test.ts`; UI unit tests run in the
   919	  dedicated `unit-ui` shard
   920	- Scope:
   921	  - Pure unit tests
   922	  - In-process integration tests (gateway auth, routing, tooling, parsing, config)
   923	  - Deterministic regressions for known bugs
   924	- Expectations:
   925	  - Runs in CI
   926	  - No real keys required
   927	  - Should be fast and stable
   928	  - Resolver and public-surface loader tests must prove broad `api.js` and
   929	    `runtime-api.js` fallback behavior with generated tiny plugin fixtures,
   930	    not real bundled plugin source APIs. Real plugin API loads belong in
   931	    plugin-owned contract/integration suites.
   932	
   933	Native dependency policy:
   934	
   935	- Default test installs skip optional native Discord opus builds. Discord
   936	  voice uses bundled `libopus-wasm`, and `@discordjs/opus` stays disabled in
   937	  `allowBuilds` so local tests and Testbox lanes do not compile the native
   938	  addon.
   939	- Compare native opus performance in the `libopus-wasm` benchmark repo, not
   940	  in default OpenClaw install/test loops. Do not set `@discordjs/opus` to
   941	  `true` in the default `allowBuilds`; that makes unrelated install/test
   942	  loops compile native code.
   943	
   944	<AccordionGroup>
   945	  <Accordion title="Projects, shards, and scoped lanes">
   946	
   947	    - Untargeted `pnpm test` runs thirteen smaller shard configs (`core-unit-fast`, `core-unit-src`, `core-unit-security`, `core-unit-ui`, `core-unit-support`, `core-support-boundary`, `core-tooling`, `core-contracts`, `core-bundled`, `core-runtime`, `agentic`, `auto-reply`, `extensions`) instead of one giant native root-project process. This cuts peak RSS on loaded machines and avoids auto-reply/plugin work starving unrelated suites.
   948	    - `pnpm test --watch` still uses the native root `vitest.config.ts` project graph, because a multi-shard watch loop is not practical.
   949	    - `pnpm test`, `pnpm test:watch`, and `pnpm test:perf:imports` route explicit file/directory targets through scoped lanes first, so `pnpm test extensions/discord/src/monitor/message-handler.preflight.test.ts` avoids paying the full root project startup tax.
   950	    - `pnpm test:changed` expands changed git paths into cheap scoped lanes by default: direct test edits, sibling `*.test.ts` files, explicit source mappings, and local import-graph dependents. Config/setup/package edits do not broad-run tests unless you explicitly use `OPENCLAW_TEST_CHANGED_BROAD=1 pnpm test:changed`.
   951	    - `pnpm check:changed` is the normal smart local check gate for narrow work. It classifies the diff into core, core tests, extensions, extension tests, apps, docs, release metadata, live Docker tooling, and tooling, then runs the matching typecheck, lint, and guard commands. Selected paths also schedule targeted Vitest owner tests via `pnpm test:serial`; use `pnpm test:changed` or explicit `pnpm test <target>` for additional test proof matching the touched contract. Release metadata-only version bumps run targeted version/config/root-dependency checks, with a guard that rejects package changes outside the top-level version field.
   952	    - Live Docker ACP harness edits run focused checks: shell syntax for the live Docker auth scripts and a live Docker scheduler dry-run. `package.json` changes are included only when the diff is limited to `scripts["test:docker:live-*"]`; dependency, export, version, and other package-surface edits still use the broader guards.
   953	    - Import-light unit tests from agents, commands, plugins, auto-reply helpers, `plugin-sdk`, and similar pure utility areas route through the `unit-fast` lane, which skips `test/setup-openclaw-runtime.ts`; stateful/runtime-heavy files stay on the existing lanes.
   954	    - Selected `plugin-sdk` and `commands` helper source files also map changed-mode runs to explicit sibling tests in those light lanes, so helper edits avoid rerunning the full heavy suite for that directory.
   955	    - `auto-reply` has dedicated buckets for top-level core helpers, top-level `reply.*` integration tests, and the `src/auto-reply/reply/**` subtree. CI further splits the reply subtree into agent-runner, dispatch, and commands/state-routing shards so one import-heavy bucket does not own the full Node tail.
   956	    - Normal PR/main CI intentionally skips the bundled plugin batch sweep and release-only `agentic-plugins` shard. Full Release Validation dispatches the separate `Plugin Prerelease` child workflow for those plugin-heavy suites on release candidates.
   957	
   958	  </Accordion>
   959	
   960	  <Accordion title="Embedded runner coverage">
   961	
   962	    - When you change message-tool discovery inputs or compaction runtime
   963	      context, keep both levels of coverage.
   964	    - Add focused helper regressions for pure routing and normalization
   965	      boundaries.
   966	    - Keep the embedded runner integration suites healthy:
   967	      `src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/compact.hooks.test.ts`,
   968	      `src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/run.overflow-compaction.test.ts`, and
   969	      `src/agents/embedded-agent-runner/run.overflow-compaction.loop.test.ts`.
   970	    - Those suites verify that scoped ids and compaction behavior still flow
   971	      through the real `run.ts` / `compact.ts` paths; helper-only tests are
   972	      not a sufficient substitute for those integration paths.
   973	
   974	  </Accordion>
   975	
   976	  <Accordion title="Vitest pool and isolation defaults">
   977	
   978	    - Base Vitest config defaults to `forks` on Windows and `threads` elsewhere.
   979	      Windows workers need separate native handle tables: concurrent thread
   980	      spawns can inherit another worker's temporary output pipe handles and
   981	      prevent that worker's child cleanup from observing EOF. Worker counts
   982	      and file parallelism remain unchanged.
   983	    - The shared Vitest config fixes `isolate: false` and uses the
   984	      non-isolated runner across the root projects, e2e, and live configs.
   985	    - The root UI lane keeps its `jsdom` setup and optimizer, but runs on the
   986	      shared non-isolated runner too.
   987	    - Provider plugin shards reuse workers with the shared cleanup runner.
   988	      Track global replacements with `vi.stubGlobal` so cleanup can restore them
   989	      before the next file.
   990	    - Each `pnpm test` shard inherits the platform pool and `isolate: false`
   991	      defaults from the shared Vitest config unless its owner selects otherwise.
   992	    - `scripts/run-vitest.mjs` adds `--no-maglev` for Vitest child Node
   993	      processes by default to reduce V8 compile churn during big local runs.
   994	      Set `OPENCLAW_VITEST_ENABLE_MAGLEV=1` to compare against stock V8
   995	      behavior.
   996	    - `scripts/run-vitest.mjs` terminates explicit non-watch Vitest runs
   997	      when their configured no-output deadline expires. Expiry fails the run
   998	      without retrying the shard, even when the child shuts down with exit code zero. Set
   999	      `OPENCLAW_VITEST_NO_OUTPUT_TIMEOUT_MS=0` to disable the watchdog for
  1000	      an intentionally silent investigation.
  1001	    - `scripts/run-tsgo.mjs` leaves tsgo unbounded by default, preserving the
  1002	      behavior of existing local workflows. Set `OPENCLAW_TSGO_TIMEOUT_MS` to
  1003	      a positive millisecond value to make a wedged compiler fail loudly
  1004	      instead of blocking its caller forever. On expiry the whole tsgo process
  1005	      tree is killed and the run fails. Values above Node's timer
  1006	      ceiling saturate at it instead of collapsing to a 1ms deadline; `0`, a
  1007	      negative, a fraction, or anything above `Number.MAX_SAFE_INTEGER` is
  1008	      rejected and fails the run. Surrounding whitespace is trimmed first;
  1009	      the remaining value must use plain decimal digits without leading zeros,
  1010	      so values such as `1e5` or `007` are rejected. Unset the variable to
  1011	      disable the watchdog.
  1012	
  1013	  </Accordion>
  1014	
  1015	  <Accordion title="Fast local iteration">
  1016	
  1017	    - `pnpm changed:lanes` shows which architectural lanes a diff triggers.
  1018	    - The pre-commit hook formats and restages files. When private rules are
  1019	      configured, it also scans staged content before and after formatting.
  1020	      See [Local commit hook setup](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/main/CONTRIBUTING.md#local-commit-hook).
  1021	      It does not run lint, typecheck, or tests.
  1022	    - Run `pnpm check:changed` explicitly before handoff or push when you
  1023	      need the smart local check gate.
  1024	    - `pnpm test:changed` routes through cheap scoped lanes by default. Use
  1025	      `OPENCLAW_TEST_CHANGED_BROAD=1 pnpm test:changed` only when the agent
  1026	      decides a harness, config, package, or contract edit really needs
  1027	      broader Vitest coverage.
  1028	    - `pnpm test:max` and `pnpm test:changed:max` keep the same routing
  1029	      behavior, just with a higher worker cap.
  1030	    - Local worker auto-scaling is intentionally conservative and backs off
  1031	      when the host load average is already high, so multiple concurrent
  1032	      Vitest runs do less damage by default.
  1033	      CI hosts with at least eight available CPUs can use six workers at
  1034	      24 to below 28 GiB and eight workers at 28–128 GiB. The measured memory allowance
  1035	      reserves 25% of RAM even for two heavy test processes. Interactive local
  1036	      sizing is unchanged; explicit worker overrides, load backoff, process
  1037	      memory constraints, free-memory pressure limits, and the 16-worker cap
  1038	      still apply. See [CI worker sizing](/ci/capacity#vitest-worker-sizing)
  1039	      for the measurements and workflow limits.
  1040	    - The base Vitest config marks the projects/config files as
  1041	      `forceRerunTriggers` so changed-mode reruns stay correct when test
  1042	      wiring changes.
  1043	    - The config keeps `OPENCLAW_VITEST_FS_MODULE_CACHE` enabled on
  1044	      supported hosts; set `OPENCLAW_VITEST_FS_MODULE_CACHE_PATH=/abs/path`
  1045	      for one explicit cache location for direct profiling.
  1046	    - Local Node runs also reuse compiled runtime-worker outputs from
  1047	      `.artifacts/vitest-worker-cache`. The runner reserves an exclusive
  1048	      checkout-local output slot and transfers verified artifacts into that slot.
  1049	      After its borrowers and resources join, it transfers completed artifacts
  1050	      back to the cache and removes the invocation directory. Occupied or
  1051	      uncertain generations are never reclaimed automatically.
  1052	      Content, compiler, configuration, or resolution changes invalidate the
  1053	      seed; timestamp-only touches do not. Build provenance and resource receipts
  1054	      are refreshed for each invocation, and source/output verification still
  1055	      runs before lending and after completion. Cold cache-enabled preparation overlaps
  1056	      the independent worker and finalizer builds when at least 8 GiB of memory
  1057	      is available; smaller hosts keep sequential compilation. CI keeps fresh compilation
  1058	      unless its workflow enables `OPENCLAW_VITEST_WORKER_CACHE=1` after restoring
  1059	      a protected cache. Only the protected warmer publishes shared generations;
  1060	      ordinary CI jobs remain remote-cache readers. Reuse requires the same
  1061	      absolute checkout and reserved output paths, Node version, compiler options,
  1062	      and verified inputs. Incompatible or missing generations compile normally.
  1063	      Bun and custom Node loader runs keep fresh compilation.
  1064	      This cache does not share Vitest's writable
  1065	      filesystem module cache with another checkout.
  1066	
  1067	  </Accordion>
  1068	
  1069	  <Accordion title="Perf debugging">
  1070	
  1071	    - `pnpm test:perf:imports` enables Vitest import-duration reporting plus
  1072	      import-breakdown output.
  1073	    - `pnpm test:perf:imports:changed` scopes the same profiling view to
  1074	      files changed since `origin/main`.
  1075	    - Shard timing data is written to `.artifacts/vitest-shard-timings.json`.
  1076	      Whole-config runs use the config path as the key; include-pattern CI
  1077	      shards append the shard name so filtered shards can be tracked
  1078	      separately.
  1079	    - When one hot test still spends most of its time in startup imports,
  1080	      keep heavy dependencies behind a narrow local `*.runtime.ts` seam and
  1081	      mock that seam directly instead of deep-importing runtime helpers
  1082	      just to pass them through `vi.mock(...)`.
  1083	    - `pnpm test:perf:changed:bench -- --ref <git-ref>` compares routed
  1084	      `test:changed` against the native root-project path for that
  1085	      committed diff and prints wall time plus macOS max RSS.
  1086	    - `pnpm test:perf:changed:bench -- --worktree` benchmarks the current
  1087	      dirty tree by routing the changed file list through
  1088	      `scripts/test-projects.mts` and the root Vitest config.
  1089	    - `pnpm test:perf:profile:main` writes a main-thread CPU profile for
  1090	      Vitest/Vite startup and transform overhead.
  1091	    - `pnpm test:perf:profile:runner` writes runner CPU+heap profiles for
  1092	      the unit suite with file parallelism disabled. Profiles span each worker's
  1093	      files and finish before teardown acknowledgement, including failed runs.
  1094	      Both commands print their output directory; see [Test performance tooling](/reference/test/performance#test-performance-tooling)
  1095	      for output selection, capture boundaries, and supported runners.
  1096	
  1097	  </Accordion>
  1098	</AccordionGroup>
  1099	
  1100	### Stability (gateway)
  1101	
  1102	- Command: `pnpm test:stability:gateway`
  1103	- Config: `test/vitest/vitest.gateway.config.ts`, `test/vitest/vitest.logging.config.ts`, and `test/vitest/vitest.infra.config.ts`, each forced to one worker
  1104	- Scope:
  1105	  - Starts a real loopback Gateway with diagnostics enabled by default
  1106	  - Drives synthetic gateway message, memory, and large-payload churn through the diagnostic event path
  1107	  - Queries `diagnostics.stability` over the Gateway WS RPC
  1108	  - Covers diagnostic stability bundle persistence helpers
  1109	  - Asserts the recorder remains bounded, synthetic RSS samples stay under the pressure budget, and per-session queue depths drain back to zero
  1110	- Expectations:
  1111	  - CI-safe and keyless
  1112	  - Narrow lane for stability-regression follow-up, not a substitute for the full Gateway suite
  1113	
  1114	### E2E (repo aggregate)
  1115	
  1116	- Command: `pnpm test:e2e`
  1117	- Scope:
  1118	  - Runs the gateway smoke E2E lane
  1119	  - Runs the mocked Control UI browser E2E lane
  1120	- Expectations:
  1121	  - CI-safe and keyless
  1122	  - Requires Playwright Chromium to be installed
  1123	
  1124	### E2E (gateway smoke)
  1125	
  1126	- Command: `pnpm test:e2e:gateway`
  1127	- Config: `test/vitest/vitest.e2e.config.ts`
  1128	- Files: `src/**/*.e2e.test.ts`, `test/**/*.e2e.test.ts`, and bundled-plugin E2E tests under `extensions/`
  1129	- Runtime defaults:
  1130	  - Uses Vitest `threads` with `isolate: false`, matching the rest of the repo.
  1131	  - Uses one worker by default to keep non-isolated gateway state deterministic.
  1132	  - Runs in silent mode by default to reduce console I/O overhead.
  1133	- Useful overrides:
  1134	  - `OPENCLAW_E2E_WORKERS=<n>` to opt into parallel workers (capped at 16).
  1135	  - `OPENCLAW_E2E_VERBOSE=1` to re-enable verbose console output.
  1136	- Scope:
  1137	  - Multi-instance gateway end-to-end behavior
  1138	  - WebSocket/HTTP surfaces, node pairing, and heavier networking
  1139	- Expectations:
  1140	  - Runs in CI (when enabled in the pipeline)
  1141	  - No real keys required
  1142	  - More moving parts than unit tests (can be slower)
  1143	
  1144	### E2E (Control UI mocked browser)
  1145	
  1146	- Command: `pnpm test:ui:e2e`
  1147	- Config: `test/vitest/vitest.ui-e2e.config.ts`
  1148	- Files: `ui/src/**/*.e2e.test.ts` and the QA Lab media-transcript real-Gateway suite
  1149	- Scope:
  1150	  - Uses four resource groups in two execution phases: `ui-e2e-bundled` and `ui-e2e-standalone` share the parallel phase (at most two workers total); `ui-e2e-serial` and `ui-e2e-serial-standalone` share the later single-worker phase
  1151	  - The two bundle-consuming projects lazily acquire one temporary UI bundle/preview per invocation; standalone projects own their fixture, source, or custom-build servers
  1152	  - Selecting only standalone suites skips the shared bundle build; new E2E files default to bundled ownership
  1153	  - Every selected project discovers Chromium and drives real pages through Playwright; the root config retains the complete discovery inventory
  1154	  - Most suites replace the Gateway WebSocket with deterministic in-browser mocks; some start isolated real Gateways
  1155	- Expectations:
  1156	  - Runs in CI as part of `pnpm test:e2e`; the resource groups add no CI jobs
  1157	  - No provider keys required; `OPENCLAW_UI_E2E_SKIP_REAL_GATEWAY=1` excludes real-Gateway suites
  1158	  - Browser dependency must be present (`pnpm --dir ui exec playwright install chromium`)
  1159	
  1160	The dedicated real-Gateway CI lane uses `test/vitest/vitest.ui-e2e-prebuilt.config.ts` after `OPENCLAW_BUILD_PRIVATE_QA=1 OPENCLAW_RUN_NODE_SKIP_DTS_BUILD=1 pnpm build` completes in each clean checkout. The separate artifact job retains SDK declaration validation. The planner balances existing serial files and selected standalone companions in one row, with the remaining audited parallel files in another. The companions share the existing two-worker phase after serial execution without an extra bundled preview. The real node/SSH desktop resize tour is release-only: when its file is selected by full manual/release validation or a direct spec edit, the first row runs `node --import tsx scripts/test-desktop-resize-real.mts` once for both carriers. The bootstrap remains required; invoking the desktop spec without its real fixture is not equivalent proof. Frequent resize, revocation, view-only filtering, takeover, and UI sizing tests remain in ordinary CI. Placement consumes the parallel-eligibility allowlist from `vitest.ui-paths.mjs` without changing Vitest scheduling. Each selected file has one CI owner; the desktop bootstrap executes each carrier once. Full manual and release selection retain the complete inventory. Prebuilt previews borrow the validated canonical Control UI assets without rebuilding or deleting them; default mocked Gateway hellos use the same artifact identity, and explicit mock identity overrides still apply. Ordinary local runs retain their private builds. Keep source and built outputs unchanged until all workers and children finish. Fixtures retain their private HOME, state, ports, cleanup, and existing worker limits. Readiness failures stop without rebuilding or falling back. The ordinary local config keeps real-Gateway files serial; frozen targets and older planners retain one complete CI row and their own config/command. See [CI](/ci) for the resource policy and timing evidence.
  1161	
  1162	### Network-isolated local E2E
  1163	
  1164	When Chromium is available, UI E2E setup proves loopback HTTP through the parent
  1165	Node process's normal `fetch` before acquiring Gateway fixtures or the shared UI
  1166	build. A proxy refusal or malformed response fails this environment preflight
  1167	with a sanitized error instead of consuming the Gateway startup budget. Existing
  1168	optional missing-browser skips remain unchanged. If later Gateway readiness
  1169	expires, its error also retains the last completed HTTP failure so a final
  1170	deadline-edge timeout does not hide useful status and error-category evidence.
  1171	
  1172	Gateway-hosted exec can inherit the [secret egress proxy](/gateway/secrets/secret-store-and-egress#secret-egress-proxy). Its plain-HTTP refusal also applies to a test process calling its own loopback fixture. A Gateway can therefore log that it is ready while its parent receives a proxy error from `/readyz`. Building the UI again or increasing the readiness timeout does not repair that transport mismatch.
  1173	
  1174	For trusted, keyless local tests, run the complete invocation in an isolated Linux container instead. Select an already prepared, trusted image by its full image ID or digest; the runner never pulls an image or falls back to the host:
  1175	
  1176	```bash
  1177	node scripts/run-vitest.mjs --isolated-image "$IMAGE_ID" run \
  1178	  --config test/vitest/vitest.ui-e2e.config.ts --configLoader runner \
  1179	  ui/src/e2e/command-palette-catalog.real-gateway.e2e.test.ts
  1180	```
  1181	
  1182	The adapter requires Linux, an existing rootless Podman installation, checkout-local `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile` dependencies, and native Node and pnpm executables compatible with the image. Put the native pnpm executable matching `package.json` on `PATH`, or select it through the existing `npm_execpath` environment variable; a Corepack/download shim is not an offline executable. The image must already contain the Chromium revision required by the installed Playwright package under `/ms-playwright`, including its headless shell and system libraries. The runner checks versions and launches Chromium before starting the test command. SELinux-labeled hosts are not supported by this initial adapter: it refuses before creating a container rather than relabeling shared host files or disabling enforcement. Use an already supported isolated runner instead of changing host security policy.
  1183	
  1184	It runs Vitest, Chromium, the provider fixture, and the test Gateway in the same network-none namespace. Host proxy settings stay unchanged; host credentials, Gateway state, Git metadata, and private scratch are not exposed to the container. There are no published ports or external network access. Missing prerequisites fail with setup guidance instead of installing packages or weakening isolation.
  1185	
  1186	Use the ordinary local config, not the CI-only prebuilt config. For the canonical Control UI E2E config, the adapter runs the existing private-QA `ciArtifacts` build inside the container before admitting tests; backend readiness alone does not mean the dashboard assets are ready. The isolated source snapshot uses tracked working-tree files, including staged new files; stage a new test before selecting it. Keep source and dependencies unchanged during the invocation, and keep dependency installation separate. The initial interface supports exact tracked test files, a tracked config, and console reporters; it does not export files from the disposable snapshot. This route is not suitable for live-provider tests or tests that must contact services outside their own container.
  1187	
  1188	### E2E: OpenShell backend smoke
  1189	
  1190	- Command: `pnpm test:e2e:openshell`
  1191	- File: `extensions/openshell/src/backend.e2e.test.ts`
  1192	- Scope:
  1193	  - Reuses an active local OpenShell gateway
  1194	  - Creates a sandbox from a temporary local Dockerfile
  1195	  - Exercises remote and default mirrored OpenShell backends over real SSH
  1196	  - Creates an isolated non-default OpenShell workspace and custom workspace roots
  1197	  - Verifies nested mirrored file writes and excludes host Git metadata and hooks
  1198	  - Verifies remote-canonical filesystem behavior through the sandbox fs bridge
  1199	- Expectations:
  1200	  - Opt-in only; not part of the default `pnpm test:e2e` run
  1201	  - Requires a local `openshell` CLI plus a working Docker daemon
  1202	  - Requires an active local OpenShell gateway and its config source
  1203	  - Uses isolated `HOME` / `XDG_CONFIG_HOME`, then waits for durable sandbox absence before deleting the test workspace
  1204	  - Reports cleanup failures, including failed inventory queries; it does not retry database errors
  1205	- Useful overrides:
  1206	  - `OPENCLAW_E2E_OPENSHELL=1` to enable the test when running the broader e2e suite manually
  1207	  - `OPENCLAW_E2E_OPENSHELL_COMMAND=/path/to/openshell` to point at a non-default CLI binary or wrapper script
  1208	  - `OPENCLAW_E2E_OPENSHELL_CONFIG_HOME=/path/to/config` to expose the registered gateway config to the isolated test
  1209	  - `OPENCLAW_E2E_OPENSHELL_HOST_IP=172.18.0.1` to replace the host policy fixture's default ranges with one explicit Docker gateway address and its existing `/32` suffix
  1210	
  1211	### Live (real providers + real models)
  1212	
  1213	- Command: `pnpm test:live`
  1214	- Config: `test/vitest/vitest.live.config.ts`
  1215	- Files: `src/**/*.live.test.ts`, `test/**/*.live.test.ts`, and bundled-plugin live tests under `extensions/`
  1216	- Default: **enabled** by `pnpm test:live` (sets `OPENCLAW_LIVE_TEST=1`)
  1217	- Scope:
  1218	  - "Does this provider/model actually work _today_ with real creds?"
  1219	  - Catch provider format changes, tool-calling quirks, auth issues, and rate limit behavior
  1220	- Expectations:
  1221	  - Not CI-stable by design (real networks, real provider policies, quotas, outages)
  1222	  - Costs money / uses rate limits
  1223	  - Prefer running narrowed subsets instead of "everything"
  1224	- Live runs use already-exported API keys and staged auth profiles.
  1225	- By default, live runs still isolate `HOME` and copy config/auth material into a temp test home so unit fixtures cannot mutate your real `~/.openclaw`.
  1226	- Set `OPENCLAW_LIVE_USE_REAL_HOME=1` only when you intentionally need live tests to use your real home directory.
  1227	- `pnpm test:live` defaults to a quieter mode: it keeps `[live] ...` progress output and mutes gateway bootstrap logs/Bonjour chatter. Set `OPENCLAW_LIVE_TEST_QUIET=0` if you want the full startup logs back.
  1228	- API key rotation (provider-specific): set `*_API_KEYS` with comma/semicolon format or `*_API_KEY_1`, `*_API_KEY_2` (for example `OPENAI_API_KEYS`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEYS`, `GEMINI_API_KEYS`) or per-live override via `OPENCLAW_LIVE_*_KEY`; tests retry on rate limit responses.
  1229	- Progress/heartbeat output:
  1230	  - Live suites emit progress lines to stderr so long provider calls are visibly active even when Vitest console capture is quiet.
  1231	  - `test/vitest/vitest.live.config.ts` disables Vitest console interception so provider/gateway progress lines stream immediately during live runs.
  1232	  - Tune direct-model heartbeats with `OPENCLAW_LIVE_HEARTBEAT_MS`.
  1233	  - Tune gateway/probe heartbeats with `OPENCLAW_LIVE_GATEWAY_HEARTBEAT_MS`.
  1234	
  1235	## Which suite should I run?
  1236	
  1237	Use this decision table:
  1238	
  1239	- Editing logic/tests: run `pnpm test` (and `pnpm test:coverage` if you changed a lot)
  1240	- Touching gateway networking / WS protocol / pairing: add `pnpm test:e2e`
  1241	- Debugging "my bot is down" / provider-specific failures / tool calling: run a narrowed `pnpm test:live`
  1242	
  1243	## Live (network-touching) tests
  1244	
  1245	For the live model matrix, CLI backend smokes, ACP smokes, Codex app-server
  1246	harness, and all media-provider live tests (Deepgram, BytePlus, ComfyUI,
  1247	image, music, video, media harness) - plus credential handling for live runs
  1248	
  1249	- see [Testing live suites](/help/testing-live). For the dedicated update and
  1250	  plugin validation checklist, see
  1251	  [Testing updates and plugins](/help/testing-updates-plugins).
  1252	
  1253	## Docs sanity
  1254	
  1255	Run docs checks after doc edits: `pnpm check:docs`.
  1256	Run the shared publishing parser's anchor audit when you need in-page heading checks too: `pnpm docs:check-links:anchors`.
  1257	Diagnostics show `unknown` when a reliable source line is unavailable.
  1258	
  1259	## Offline regression (CI-safe)
  1260	
  1261	These are "real pipeline" regressions without real providers:
  1262	
  1263	- Gateway agent admission (real Gateway with a mock OpenAI provider): `src/gateway/gateway.test.ts` (case: "accepts a gateway agent request over ws and returns a run id"; checks acceptance, a run ID, and an abort response).
  1264	- Gateway wizard (WS `wizard.start`/`wizard.next`, writes config + auth enforced): `src/gateway/gateway.test.ts` (case: "runs wizard over ws and writes auth token config")
