  1073	---
  1074	summary: "How OpenClaw validates update paths, package migrations, and plugin install/update behavior"
  1075	read_when:
  1076	  - Changing OpenClaw update, doctor, package acceptance, or plugin install behavior
  1077	  - Preparing or approving a release candidate
  1078	  - Debugging package update, plugin dependency cleanup, or plugin install regressions
  1079	title: "Testing: updates and plugins"
  1080	sidebarTitle: "Update and plugin tests"
  1081	---
  1082	
  1083	Checklist for update and plugin validation: prove the installable package can
  1084	update real user state, repair stale legacy state through `doctor`, and still
  1085	install, load, update, and uninstall plugins from every supported source.
  1086	
  1087	For the broader test runner map, see [Testing](/help/testing). For live provider
  1088	keys and network-touching suites, see [Testing live](/help/testing-live).
  1089	
  1090	## On this page
  1091	
  1092	- [What we protect](#what-we-protect) - the guarantees these lanes exist to defend.
  1093	- [Local proof during development](#local-proof-during-development) - the commands to run while you iterate.
  1094	- [Headless node auto-update proof](#headless-node-auto-update-proof) - installed-node activation and shared-Gateway safeguards.
  1095	- [Docker lanes](#docker-lanes) - lane reference: what each lane runs and when.
  1096	- [Package Acceptance](#package-acceptance) - lane reference: the acceptance matrix and its gates.
  1097	- [Release default](#release-default) - which lanes a release candidate must clear.
  1098	- [Legacy compatibility](#legacy-compatibility) - older package and plugin states still covered.
  1099	- [Adding coverage](#adding-coverage) - where a new regression belongs.
  1100	- [Failure triage](#failure-triage) - what to do when a lane goes red.
  1101	
  1102	## What we protect
  1103	
  1104	- A package tarball is complete, has a valid `dist/postinstall-inventory.json`,
  1105	  and does not depend on unpacked repo files.
  1106	- A user can move from an older published package to the candidate package
  1107	  without losing config, agents, sessions, workspaces, plugin allowlists, or
  1108	  channel config.
  1109	- `openclaw doctor --fix --non-interactive` owns legacy migrations and repairs,
  1110	  including genuinely dangling plugin-runtime aliases. Package postinstall owns
  1111	  package-local dependency debris; both preserve valid shared runtime roots that
  1112	  another installation or profile may use. Startup should not grow hidden
  1113	  compatibility migrations for stale plugin state.
  1114	- Plugin installs work from local directories, git repos, npm packages, and the
  1115	  ClawHub registry path.
  1116	- Plugin npm dependencies install in one managed npm project per plugin,
  1117	  get scanned before trust, and get removed through `npm uninstall` during
  1118	  plugin uninstall so hoisted dependencies do not linger.
  1119	- Plugin update is a no-op when nothing changed: install records, resolved
  1120	  source, installed dependency layout, and enabled state stay intact.
  1121	
  1122	## Local proof during development
  1123	
  1124	Start narrow:
  1125	
  1126	```bash
  1127	pnpm changed:lanes --json
  1128	pnpm check:changed
  1129	pnpm test:changed
  1130	```
  1131	
  1132	For plugin install, uninstall, dependency, or package-inventory changes, also
  1133	run the focused tests that cover the edited seam:
  1134	
  1135	```bash
  1136	pnpm test src/plugins/uninstall.test.ts src/infra/package-dist-inventory.test.ts test/scripts/package-acceptance-workflow.test.ts
  1137	```
  1138	
  1139	Before any package Docker lane consumes a tarball, prove the package artifact:
  1140	
  1141	```bash
  1142	pnpm release:check
  1143	```
  1144	
  1145	`release:check` runs generated config/docs and plugin checks (config schema,
  1146	config docs baseline, plugin SDK exports and surface budget, plugin
  1147	versions/inventory), writes the package dist inventory, runs
  1148	`npm pack --dry-run`, rejects forbidden packed files, installs the tarball into
  1149	a temp prefix, runs postinstall, and smokes bundled channel entrypoints.
  1150	
  1151	For a Plugin SDK change, compare the exact commits separately:
  1152	
  1153	```bash
  1154	base_sha=$(git merge-base origin/main HEAD)
  1155	head_sha=$(git rev-parse HEAD)
  1156	pnpm plugin-sdk:api:diff -- --base "$base_sha" --head "$head_sha"
  1157	```
  1158	
  1159	Release npm preflight uses the same readable diff against the prior published
  1160	dist-tag and prints the 8-character acknowledgement digest required when that
  1161	release changes the Plugin SDK API.
  1162	
  1163	## Headless node auto-update proof
  1164	
  1165	`pnpm test:e2e:node-auto-update <built-openclaw.tgz> [new-artifact-directory]`
  1166	runs a real installed-package scenario on Linux. Run it in a task-owned Testbox
  1167	or Crabbox with Node, npm, and registry access. It needs no provider credentials
  1168	and is opt-in; the default `pnpm test:e2e` aggregate does not run it.
  1169	
  1170	Build and pack the candidate on the test host, then pass that exact tarball:
  1171	
  1172	```bash
  1173	pnpm build
  1174	node scripts/package-openclaw-for-docker.mjs --skip-build \
  1175	  --output-dir /tmp/openclaw-node-update-package \
  1176	  --output-name openclaw-node-update.tgz
  1177	pnpm test:e2e:node-auto-update \
  1178	  /tmp/openclaw-node-update-package/openclaw-node-update.tgz \
  1179	  /tmp/openclaw-node-update-proof
  1180	```
  1181	
  1182	The proof starts isolated Gateway, paired-node, supervisor, and fixture-registry
  1183	processes. It verifies busy-work deferral, idle activation, preserved pairing
  1184	and launch options, activation cooldown, all three public opt-outs, and retention
  1185	of the working node when a candidate is malformed. A same-state Gateway/node
  1186	case confirms that the Gateway process, configuration, and global installation
  1187	stay unchanged while the node activates its private runtime. A separate cell
  1188	runs the published `openclaw@2026.9.4` updater against the candidate.
  1189	
  1190	A legacy-plugin case returns from its command while a child keeps running,
  1191	then proves that a missing idle-work callback blocks activation both during
  1192	that work and after the child finishes.
  1193	
  1194	Use a new artifact directory outside the source checkout for every run, or omit
  1195	it to create a fresh temporary directory. The scenario retains `observations.json`
  1196	and per-process logs there and stops its child processes on completion or failure.
  1197	Collect the proof before stopping the remote lease. This scenario proves Linux
  1198	behavior; it does not establish Windows or macOS activation coverage. See
  1199	[Node auto-updates](/cli/node#automatic-updates) for the operator contract.
  1200	
  1201	## Docker lanes
  1202	
  1203	The Docker lanes are the product-level proof. They install or update a real
  1204	package inside Linux containers and assert behavior through CLI commands,
  1205	Gateway startup, HTTP probes, RPC status, and filesystem state.
  1206	
  1207	Use focused lanes while iterating:
  1208	
  1209	```bash
  1210	pnpm test:docker:plugins
  1211	pnpm test:docker:plugin-lifecycle-matrix
  1212	pnpm test:docker:plugin-update
  1213	pnpm test:docker:upgrade-survivor
  1214	pnpm test:docker:published-upgrade-survivor
  1215	pnpm test:docker:update-restart-auth
  1216	pnpm test:docker:update-migration
  1217	```
  1218	
  1219	Important lanes:
  1220	
  1221	- `test:docker:plugins` covers plugin install smoke, local folder installs,
  1222	  local folder update skip behavior, local folders with preinstalled
  1223	  dependencies, `file:` package installs, git installs with CLI execution, git
  1224	  moving-ref updates, npm registry installs with hoisted transitive
  1225	  dependencies, npm update no-ops, malformed npm package metadata rejection,
  1226	  local ClawHub fixture installs and update no-ops, marketplace update behavior,
  1227	  and Claude-bundle enable/inspect. Set `OPENCLAW_PLUGINS_E2E_CLAWHUB=0` to
  1228	  keep the ClawHub block hermetic/offline.
  1229	- `test:docker:plugin-lifecycle-matrix` installs the candidate package in a bare
  1230	  container, runs an npm plugin through install, inspect, disable, enable,
  1231	  explicit upgrade, explicit downgrade, and uninstall after deleting the plugin
  1232	  code. It logs RSS and CPU metrics per phase.
  1233	- `test:docker:plugin-update` validates that an unchanged installed plugin does
  1234	  not reinstall or lose install metadata during `openclaw plugins update`.
  1235	- `test:docker:upgrade-survivor` installs the candidate tarball over a dirty
  1236	  old-user fixture, runs package update plus non-interactive doctor, then starts
  1237	  a loopback Gateway and checks state preservation.
  1238	- `test:docker:published-upgrade-survivor` first installs the latest stable release,
  1239	  configures it through a baked `openclaw config set` recipe, updates it to the
  1240	  candidate tarball, runs doctor, checks legacy cleanup, starts the Gateway, and
  1241	  probes `/healthz`, `/readyz`, and RPC status. The baseline recipe configures
  1242	  Anthropic, Google Gemini, and OpenAI through env-referenced API keys, keeping
  1243	  OpenAI as the agents' primary model.
  1244	- `test:docker:update-restart-auth` installs the candidate package, starts a
  1245	  managed token-auth Gateway, unsets caller gateway auth env for
  1246	  `openclaw update --yes --json`, and requires the candidate update command to
  1247	  restart the Gateway before the normal probes.
  1248	- `test:docker:update-migration` is the cleanup-heavy published-update lane. It
  1249	  installs the latest stable release by default, starts from a configured
  1250	  Discord/Telegram-style user state, seeds package-local plugin dependency debris
  1251	  and shared runtime sentinels, and updates to the candidate tarball. Package
  1252	  postinstall must remove package-local debris while update and Doctor preserve
  1253	  the shared runtime roots.
  1254	
  1255	Set `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_LIVE_MODELS` to a whitespace-separated list of
  1256	model refs to run one `openclaw agent --local` marker turn per model after the
  1257	update. Anthropic uses `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`, Google uses `GEMINI_API_KEY`, and
  1258	OpenAI uses `OPENAI_API_KEY`; missing selected keys fail the lane. Docker forwards
  1259	only selected provider keys. Each turn has its own session and
  1260	`live-<provider>.json` / `.err` artifacts (additional models from the same provider
  1261	use `-2`, `-3`, etc.). `summary.json` records `liveModels.models` entries with
  1262	`model`, `ok`, and `latencyMs`.
  1263	
  1264	The legacy `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_LIVE_OPENAI=1` form still selects
  1265	`openai/gpt-5.5`, or `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_LIVE_OPENAI_MODEL` when supplied.
  1266	An explicit model list takes precedence over that flag; the summary records
  1267	`liveModels.source` and `overridesLiveOpenai`. The existing
  1268	`OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_LIVE_OPENAI_TIMEOUT_SECONDS` budget (default 180 seconds)
  1269	applies separately to each turn. Live turns use the recipe's configured thinking
  1270	default so each model can apply its supported reasoning levels. Scenarios that
  1271	prohibit live providers retain that restriction.
  1272	
  1273	Frozen extended-stable candidate targets use their historical runner and reject
  1274	both live-selection variables before Docker starts, with exit code 2.
  1275	
  1276	```bash
  1277	# Export the three provider keys before invoking the lane.
  1278	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_BASELINE_SPEC=openclaw@2026.9.5 \
  1279	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_LIVE_MODELS="openai/gpt-5.5 anthropic/claude-opus-5 google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview" \
  1280	pnpm test:docker:published-upgrade-survivor
  1281	```
  1282	
  1283	Source-pinned tarball runs of `base` and `sqlite-volume` verify the candidate
  1284	commit before the update and compare the installed application payload with the
  1285	frozen tarball afterward, before candidate probes. This distinguishes different
  1286	builds with the same version string. npm still owns dependency reification;
  1287	manual tarball runs without a selected source SHA retain their existing contract.
  1288	
  1289	Useful published-upgrade survivor variants:
  1290	
  1291	```bash
  1292	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_BASELINE_SPEC=openclaw@2026.6.1 \
  1293	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_SCENARIO=versioned-runtime-deps \
  1294	pnpm test:docker:published-upgrade-survivor
  1295	
  1296	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_BASELINE_SPEC=openclaw@latest \
  1297	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_SCENARIO=bootstrap-persona \
  1298	pnpm test:docker:published-upgrade-survivor
  1299	
  1300	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_BASELINE_SPEC=openclaw@2026.7.1-2 \
  1301	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_SCENARIO=sqlite-volume \
  1302	pnpm test:docker:published-upgrade-survivor
  1303	
  1304	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_BASELINE_SPEC=openclaw@2026.6.34 \
  1305	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_SCENARIO=legacy-operator-state \
  1306	pnpm test:docker:published-upgrade-survivor
  1307	
  1308	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_BASELINE_SPECS=openclaw@2026.9.4 \
  1309	OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_SCENARIOS=custom-plugin-siblings \
  1310	pnpm test:docker:published-upgrade-survivor
  1311	```
  1312	
  1313	Available scenarios: `base`, `acpx-openclaw-tools-bridge`, `feishu-channel`,
  1314	`bootstrap-persona`, `channel-post-core-restore`, `plugin-deps-cleanup`,
  1315	`configured-plugin-installs`, `custom-plugin-siblings`, `stale-source-plugin-shadow`, `tilde-log-path`,
  1316	`meeting-transcripts-sqlite`, `versioned-runtime-deps`, `cron-scheduled-authority`,
  1317	`legacy-operator-state`, and `sqlite-volume`. In aggregate runs,
  1318	`OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_SCENARIOS=reported-issues` expands the release-soak
  1319	fixtures but excludes the expensive `sqlite-volume` scenario. Use
  1320	`OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_SCENARIOS=far-reaching` to include it.
  1321	
  1322	The `custom-plugin-siblings` scenario starts from published 2026.9.4 or later
  1323	with an enabled custom memory plugin importing `../shared/value.mjs` from both
  1324	its runtime entry and Doctor config-repair contract. It runs the published
  1325	updater against the selected candidate tarball and requires both that contract
  1326	and Gateway plugin registration to execute with the expected sibling value from
  1327	private canary state. Readiness alone is insufficient. It also checks actual plugin loading
  1328	before and after the update, preserved enablement, and unchanged original source
  1329	files. Current-source Full Release Validation includes this scenario in its
  1330	normal Package Acceptance coverage and in release soak.
  1331	Those default release runs pin this scenario to the published 2026.9.4 driver,
  1332	including when the source candidate still reports version 2026.9.4; other
  1333	scenarios retain their existing baseline selection.
  1334	
  1335	The opt-in `projects-doctor`, `projects-startup-migration`, and `taskflow-restoration` scenarios require the exact
  1336	published `openclaw@2026.9.4` baseline and a frozen candidate tarball. They use
  1337	isolated state, manual restart, and no live providers or registry companion fixtures;
  1338	none runs through `reported-issues` or `far-reaching`. They verify the original
  1339	published driver and installed candidate payload bytes, including when their version
  1340	strings are equal. Select one with `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_SCENARIO` and set
  1341	`OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_BASELINE_SPEC=openclaw@2026.9.4`.
  1342	
  1343	`projects-doctor` preserves one registered project and one configured workspace,
  1344	then runs the real `doctor --lint --only core/doctor/project-clone-shape --json`
  1345	twice. It checks stored rows, schema, sentinels, and read-only snapshot cleanup.
  1346	`projects-startup-migration` prepares two independent project/worktree specimens
  1347	through the published owners using local Git. Each has a verified backup and
  1348	synthetic legacy session JSON/JSONL imported through published Doctor. The update
  1349	must repair the first specimen's canonical workspace through candidate Doctor.
  1350	The second state stays outside that update's discovery. Before startup, the
  1351	candidate's Doctor schema owner runs under its maintenance lock to upgrade that
  1352	database while preserving the legacy workspace fields and exact session/transcript
  1353	bytes. Its first normal Gateway startup must preserve that state. After
  1354	clean shutdown, an explicit `doctor --fix --non-interactive` repairs its canonical
  1355	workspace; a second startup must leave the repaired state unchanged. These are
  1356	supported legacy-format imports, not historical runtime-generated sessions.
  1357	Both Gateway runs must become ready and report clean shutdown before persisted
  1358	readback. Set
  1359	`OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_STARTUP_BINDINGS` to a reviewed JSON file containing the
  1360	candidate `commit`, `agentSchema`, and `operations.prepare`/`operations.open`
  1361	triples of compiled basename, exact export symbol, and SHA-256. The snapshot
  1362	preparer and SQLite opener must match the installed candidate payload; the file
  1363	is mounted read-only. This scenario uses no remote repository or model turn.
  1364	`taskflow-restoration` preserves three terminal tasks and two flows, starts a fresh
  1365	candidate Gateway, exercises awaited task SDK reads through a synthetic local plugin,
  1366	and reads two task pages on the same Gateway connection. Complete task, delivery,
  1367	and flow records are checked again after Gateway shutdown. The taskflow cell covers
  1368	terminal persisted state; it does not exercise active task recovery or provider work.
  1369	
  1370	The `legacy-operator-state` scenario uses the published baseline's own CLI to
  1371	create a second agent, allowlist exec approvals, and two command cron jobs: one
  1372	without an explicit agent and one owned by `ops`. It leaves `systemAgent`
  1373	unset, installs one version-matched npm plugin through the local registry, and
  1374	preserves a workspace skill. It also configures DuckDuckGo without an install
  1375	record: bundled baselines use their own CLI web-search settings; newer baselines
  1376	receive the retained configuration of an already-broken upgrade. The local
  1377	registry supplies the candidate's official external DuckDuckGo package. Before
  1378	standalone Doctor or consent repair, assertions require the npm install record,
  1379	candidate package version and integrity, `plugins list` entry, and clean
  1380	`config validate --json`. A separate isolated missing-plugin state exercises
  1381	`doctor --fix --non-interactive` without an update or capability acceptance.
  1382	A mock OpenAI server verifies a real agent turn
  1383	before and after the update without provider credentials. After the update,
  1384	the lane checks approvals and legacy-file retirement, effective cron owners,
  1385	the candidate plugin artifact, the candidate state schema, an idempotent update,
  1386	and Gateway health. Assertions run before a standalone Doctor can conceal an
  1387	incomplete update migration.
  1388	
  1389	Published companion package versions retain identical archive bytes throughout
  1390	the fixture. If a source candidate still uses the published companion's version,
  1391	the registry and installation assertions preserve that published archive. A new
  1392	companion version instead uses the prepared candidate artifact. The npm integrity
  1393	guard remains enabled in both cases; the fixture never replaces a published
  1394	version's bytes to make an update pass.
  1395	
  1396	The ownerless cron job is created before adding the second agent because newer
  1397	baselines reject ambiguous new jobs. Approval snapshots are written back through
  1398	the baseline CLI before comparison: JSON-era reads can assign IDs without
  1399	persisting them. The final seeded state still has both agents, both jobs, and no
  1400	explicit `systemAgent`.
  1401	
  1402	The PR/main gate uses `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_UPDATE_RESTART_MODE=auto-auth`.
  1403	For this scenario, the baseline updater must replace its running managed Gateway;
  1404	the harness checks process replacement and configured authentication. Cron owners
  1405	are queried immediately after that first update, before any consent repair can
  1406	conceal an incomplete migration. The default local `manual` mode passes
  1407	`--no-restart` and starts the candidate for probes, so it does not prove an
  1408	updater-owned restart. Both modes require a clean `doctor --lint --json` report.
  1409	
  1410	Schema snapshots record both published `userVersion` and applied `contentVersion`
  1411	in `schema-before.json` and `schema-after.json`. The lane compares applied content
  1412	with the candidate package's schema constants. Since #141109, shared-state
  1413	publication can lag completed migrations by the legacy updater's five-minute
  1414	terminal grace period; requiring the published number immediately would reject a
  1415	healthy upgrade. Agent schemas still use their published version. The observer
  1416	opens databases read-only and never triggers migrations or publication. See
  1417	[Schema bumps and older updaters](/reference/database-schemas#schema-bumps-and-older-updaters).
  1418	
  1419	The before snapshot also records the baseline's configured agent roster and
  1420	agent-scoped legacy specimens, including session rows, transcript and trajectory
  1421	files, trajectory pointers, and skill-prompt blobs. Model catalogs and unrelated
  1422	per-agent artifacts are outside this observer's session-migration scope. Before candidate probes or
  1423	agent turns, each agent with existing SQLite or legacy session history must have
  1424	a store at the candidate agent schema. The observer verifies imported session
  1425	identities and transcript events, completed archive receipts and retained source
  1426	bytes, and unchanged prompt blobs. An unused agent with no legacy history may
  1427	still create its store lazily. These checks follow the [Doctor session SQLite
  1428	migration contract](/cli/doctor#session-sqlite-migration); the observer never
  1429	imports files or opens a writable database.
  1430	
  1431	Every supported baseline, including `2026.9.2`, must complete the update and
  1432	migrate its databases to the candidate schemas. A typed
  1433	`update-schema-bump-unfenced` refusal, a rollback, an unmigrated database, or an
  1434	unusable Gateway fails the lane. Required plugin capability consent can use the
  1435	existing explicit recovery step only after the automatic-migration assertions
  1436	pass; it never permits a failed schema repair.
  1437	
  1438	Other scenarios keep their existing success assertions. Their deliberately
  1439	injected legacy files can prevent the baseline from starting before an update;
  1440	the updater must migrate those fixtures before the candidate probes. The lane
  1441	does not pre-repair or skip those older migration specimens.
  1442	
  1443	`auth-profile-v2026-7-2-beta-5` is explicitly selectable outside those aggregate
  1444	aliases. It imports the historical JSON credential fixture, verifies credentials
  1445	and auth ordering in the current shared store, and checks archived source bytes.
  1446	It does not test retention of credentials created in a published SQLite store.
  1447	
  1448	The `sqlite-volume` scenario combines configured Matrix, Discord, and Telegram
  1449	plugin/channel state with 4,800 sessions, 23,890 transcript events, and 2,200
  1450	cron crawl jobs by default. For baselines that expose the plugin-state SDK, it
  1451	uses that installed SDK to create the released shared database and write 512
  1452	permanent records across two namespaces, then checks that every stored value
  1453	and timestamp survives. Older baselines without that API explicitly report
  1454	this part as not applicable. It also seeds account-scoped pairing requests and
  1455	allowlists, plus workspace identity, instructions, and memory files. It verifies
  1456	exact JSONL-to-SQLite and cron migration, legacy archival, database integrity,
  1457	account isolation, and workspace contents immediately after the update, before
  1458	any standalone Doctor repair can hide an incomplete migration. It then reads
  1459	sampled conversations through Gateway RPC, runs an idempotent Doctor pass, and
  1460	repeats the history and preservation checks after a Gateway restart.
  1461	
  1462	This is a package-update test inside Docker. It does not prove container image
  1463	replacement or background update campaigns; see [Updating](/install/updating)
  1464	for those separate entry points. A required plugin capability consent remains
  1465	an explicit recovery step and is recorded in the survivor summary.
  1466	
  1467	The `2026.9.2` to `2026.9.3` survivor transition exercises the installed updater.
  1468	Shared-state migration content can be current while the published schema version
  1469	remains at 15 until the old updater clears its publication grace period. Schema
  1470	proof records both values and requires current content; it does not wait for or
  1471	force publication. See [older updater schema handling](/reference/database-schemas#schema-bumps-and-older-updaters).
  1472	
  1473	`test:docker:release-upgrade-user-journey` separately covers the explicit external
  1474	package-manager and fresh Doctor procedure, with an owner-stopped Gateway,
  1475	verified backup, and retained baseline and new conversations through Gateway
  1476	history. Its receipt records `selfUpdatePassed: false` and a `not-run` self-update
  1477	status; external installation is not evidence of an internal updater outcome.
  1478	Agent-schema and unsupported shared-state migration refusals remain covered by
  1479	the Doctor owner tests.
  1480	
  1481	Scale the fixture with `OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_VOLUME_SESSIONS`,
  1482	`OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_VOLUME_EVENTS_PER_SESSION`, and
  1483	`OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_VOLUME_CRON_JOBS`. The default budget for the
  1484	idempotent Doctor pass is 60 seconds; override it with
  1485	`OPENCLAW_UPGRADE_SURVIVOR_VOLUME_IDEMPOTENCE_BUDGET_SECONDS` on slower hosts.
  1486	
  1487	The `Update Migration` workflow runs weekly and supports manual dispatch. Its
  1488	default `supported-lines` baseline set resolves npm dist-tags and published
  1489	versions at run time: `latest`, the previous stable release, `extended-stable`
  1490	when that tag exists, and the supported floor `2026.6.34`. Duplicate versions
  1491	run once. It updates each baseline to the selected `package_ref` artifact
  1492	(`main` by default), exercising plugin cleanup and legacy operator state.
  1493	Leave `baselines` blank to use that default. For an explicit historical replay
  1494	from every published stable release since 2026.6.1, pass
  1495	`baselines=all-since-2026.6.1`:
  1496	
  1497	```bash
  1498	gh workflow run update-migration.yml \
  1499	  --ref main \
  1500	  -f workflow_ref=main \
  1501	  -f package_ref=main \
  1502	  -f baselines=all-since-2026.6.1 \
  1503	  -f scenarios=plugin-deps-cleanup
  1504	```
  1505	
  1506	## Package Acceptance
  1507	
  1508	Package Acceptance is the GitHub-native package gate. It resolves one candidate
  1509	package into a `package-under-test` tarball, records version and SHA-256, then
  1510	runs reusable Docker E2E lanes against that exact tarball. The workflow harness
  1511	ref is separate from the package source ref, so current test logic can validate
  1512	older trusted releases.
  1513	
  1514	Candidate sources:
  1515	
  1516	- `source=npm`: validate `openclaw@extended-stable`, `openclaw@beta`,
  1517	  `openclaw@latest`, or an exact published version.
  1518	- `source=ref`: pack a trusted branch, tag, or commit with the selected current
  1519	  harness.
  1520	- `source=url`: validate a public HTTPS tarball with required `package_sha256`.
  1521	  This path rejects URL credentials, non-default HTTPS ports, private/internal
  1522	  hostnames or DNS/IP results, special-use IP space, and unsafe redirects.
  1523	- `source=trusted-url`: validate an HTTPS tarball with required
  1524	  `package_sha256` and `trusted_source_id` against the maintainer-owned policy
  1525	  in `.github/package-trusted-sources.json`. Use this for enterprise/private
  1526	  mirrors instead of weakening `source=url` with an input-level allow-private
  1527	  switch. Bearer auth, when configured by policy, uses the fixed
  1528	  `OPENCLAW_TRUSTED_PACKAGE_TOKEN` secret.
  1529	- `source=artifact`: reuse a tarball uploaded by another Actions run.
  1530	
  1531	Full Release Validation uses `source=artifact` by default, built from the
  1532	resolved release SHA. For post-publish proof, pass
  1533	`package_acceptance_package_spec=openclaw@YYYY.M.PATCH` so the same upgrade matrix
  1534	targets the shipped npm package instead.
  1535	
  1536	Release checks call Package Acceptance with the package/update/restart/plugin set:
  1537	
  1538	```text
  1539	doctor-switch update-channel-switch skill-install update-corrupt-plugin upgrade-survivor published-upgrade-survivor root-managed-vps-upgrade update-restart-auth plugins-offline plugin-update plugin-binding-command-escape
  1540	```
  1541	
  1542	When release soak is enabled (forced on for `release_profile=stable` and
  1543	`full`), they also pass:
  1544	
  1545	```text
  1546	published_upgrade_survivor_scenarios=reported-issues
  1547	telegram_mode=mock-openai
  1548	```
  1549	
  1550	This keeps package migration, update channel switching, corrupt managed-plugin
  1551	tolerance, stale plugin dependency cleanup, offline plugin coverage, plugin
  1552	update behavior, and Telegram package QA on the same resolved artifact without
  1553	making the default release package gate walk every published release.
  1554	
  1555	Current source release checks use the same `supported-lines` baseline expansion,
  1556	resolved once to exact packages before Docker fanout. Candidate source metadata
  1557	must expose the new harness, and its `YYYY.M.PATCH` base version must be at least
  1558	the trusted workflow package's base version; prerelease suffixes are ignored
  1559	for this comparison. The child prepares or reuses the prerelease plugin registry.
  1560	The default scenario set includes `base` and `legacy-operator-state`; release
  1561	soak runs `reported-issues`.
  1562	
  1563	The standalone `supported-lines` selector expands only `legacy-operator-state`.
  1564	Every preexisting synthetic scenario remains on the separately resolved
  1565	candidate-relative predecessor, including weekly `plugin-deps-cleanup` proof.
  1566	Comma and whitespace delimiters and repeated selectors are accepted. Explicit version lists and mixed
  1567	selector/version lists preserve the full Cartesian matrix for manual proof.
  1568	
  1569	Older source targets, extended-stable qualification, published packages, and
  1570	separate npm overrides retain the candidate-relative predecessor and previous
  1571	scenario set. Published qualification does not prepare the registry required
  1572	by the new operator-state scenario. Historical soak keeps every preexisting reported-issue
  1573	fixture; it does not automatically enable frozen-target scenario omissions.
  1574	See [release qualification](/ci/release-validation#suite-profiles) for the exact
  1575	boundary. The candidate remains the selected package-under-test tarball. The per-PR
  1576	`docker-seed-e2e` tripwire stays limited to `latest` and `legacy-operator-state`
  1577	and also runs on every canonical `main` push that runs CI. Docs-only pushes
  1578	matching `**/*.md` and `docs/**` skip CI; mixed docs and code pushes still run it.
  1579	
  1580	For manual historical coverage, `last-stable-4` selects four recent stable
  1581	npm-published releases. Exact versions, `all-since-2026.6.1`, and
  1582	`release-history` remain available through `published_upgrade_survivor_baselines`.
  1583	Current tooling executes baselines from `2026.6.1` onward. `release-history`
  1584	selects the six most recent supported stable releases without adding older
  1585	March or April anchors. To replay pre-June upgrades, select matching historical
  1586	tooling; for an old installation, [upgrade through `2026.9.5`](/install/updating#upgrading-very-old-versions)
  1587	before installing the latest release.
  1588	Use those overrides when replaying migrations outside the bounded supported
  1589	baseline set.
  1590	
  1591	When multiple published-upgrade survivor baselines are selected, the reusable
  1592	Docker workflow shards each baseline into its own targeted runner job. Each
  1593	baseline shard still runs the selected scenario set, but logs and artifacts stay
  1594	per-baseline and wall time is bounded by the slowest shard instead of one large
  1595	serial job.
  1596	
  1597	Run a package profile manually when validating a candidate before release:
  1598	
  1599	```bash
  1600	gh workflow run package-acceptance.yml \
  1601	  --ref main \
  1602	  -f workflow_ref=main \
  1603	  -f source=npm \
  1604	  -f package_spec=openclaw@beta \
  1605	  -f suite_profile=package \
  1606	  -f published_upgrade_survivor_scenarios=reported-issues \
  1607	  -f telegram_mode=mock-openai
  1608	```
  1609	
  1610	For a published extended-stable canary, set
  1611	`package_spec=openclaw@extended-stable`. Package Acceptance resolves that
  1612	selector into an exact tarball before the Docker lanes run.
  1613	
  1614	Use `suite_profile=product` when the release question includes MCP channels,
  1615	cron/subagent cleanup, OpenAI web search, or OpenWebUI. Use `suite_profile=full`
  1616	only when you need full Docker release-path coverage.
  1617	
  1618	## Release default
  1619	
  1620	For release candidates, the default proof stack is:
  1621	
  1622	1. `pnpm check:changed` and `pnpm test:changed` for source-level regressions.
  1623	2. `pnpm release:check` for package artifact integrity.
  1624	3. Package Acceptance `package` profile or the release-check custom package
  1625	   lanes for install/update/restart/plugin contracts.
  1626	4. Cross-OS release checks for OS-specific installer, onboarding, and platform
  1627	   behavior.
  1628	5. Live suites only when the changed surface touches provider or hosted-service
  1629	   behavior.
  1630	
  1631	On maintainer machines, broad gates and Docker/package product proof should run
  1632	in Testbox unless explicitly doing local proof.
  1633	
  1634	## Legacy compatibility
  1635	
  1636	Package Acceptance applies current metadata and persistence contracts without
  1637	the retired pre-June 2026 warning or skip paths. Reproducing acceptance of those
  1638	historical candidates requires their historical `workflow_ref` tooling.
  1639	
  1640	For retained upgrade contracts, keep migrations in Doctor and prove changes with
  1641	`upgrade-survivor`, `published-upgrade-survivor`, or `update-restart-auth` when the
  1642	update command owns the restart. Pre-June task and flow sidecar imports are
  1643	retired; use the [intermediate upgrade procedure](/install/updating) to preserve
  1644	those records.
  1645	
  1646	## Adding coverage
  1647	
  1648	When changing update or plugin behavior, add coverage at the lowest layer that
  1649	can fail for the right reason:
  1650	
  1651	- Pure path or metadata logic: unit test beside the source.
  1652	- Package inventory or packed-file behavior: `package-dist-inventory` or tarball
  1653	  checker test.
  1654	- CLI install/update behavior: Docker lane assertion or fixture.
  1655	- Published-release migration behavior: `published-upgrade-survivor` scenario.
  1656	- Update-owned restart behavior: `update-restart-auth`.
  1657	- Registry/package source behavior: `test:docker:plugins` fixture or ClawHub
  1658	  fixture server.
  1659	- Dependency layout or cleanup behavior: assert both runtime execution and the
  1660	  filesystem boundary. npm dependencies may be hoisted inside the plugin's
  1661	  managed npm project, so tests should prove that project is scanned/cleaned
  1662	  instead of assuming only the plugin package-local `node_modules` tree.
  1663	
  1664	Keep new Docker fixtures hermetic by default. Use local fixture registries and
  1665	fake packages unless the point of the test is live registry behavior.
  1666	
  1667	## Failure triage
  1668	
  1669	Start with the artifact identity:
  1670	
  1671	- Package Acceptance `resolve_package` summary: source, version, SHA-256, and
  1672	  artifact name.
  1673	- Docker artifacts: `.artifacts/docker-tests/**/summary.json`,
  1674	  `failures.json`, lane logs, and rerun commands.
  1675	- Upgrade survivor summary: `.artifacts/upgrade-survivor/summary.json`,
  1676	  including baseline version, candidate version, scenario, phase timings, and
  1677	  config recipe coverage.
  1678	
  1679	The `legacy-operator-state` survivor installs a matching published companion
  1680	through a moving tag (`latest`, `beta`, or `alpha`). If npm confirms that the
  1681	exact companion version was never published, the row records that companion as
  1682	unavailable in `baselineCompanion` and continues the remaining operator-state
  1683	and external-plugin migration checks. Registry errors still fail fixture setup.
  1684	An update that has not started reports an `unknown` outcome; a failed update
  1685	attempt reports `failed`, independently of later scenario assertions.
  1686	
  1687	Failure capture retains the latest session SQLite migration manifest and Doctor
  1688	issue report in the private diagnostics snapshot, subject to bounded size limits.
  1689	The published diagnostics contain issue histograms and capture omissions rather
  1690	than raw session details. Collect private artifacts before removing the test host.
  1691	Container-owned observation directories can require `sudo tar` on that isolated
  1692	host; keep their permissions intact. When collecting through a remote wrapper,
  1693	use distinct success/failure download destinations. A successful download does
  1694	not change the survivor exit code or `summary.status`.
  1695	
  1696	Prefer rerunning the failed exact lane with the same package artifact over
  1697	rerunning the whole release umbrella.
  1698	
