    82	summary: "How the macOS app reports gateway/channel health states"
    83	read_when:
    84	  - Debugging mac app health indicators
    85	title: "Health checks (macOS)"
    86	---
    87	
    88	# Health checks on macOS
    89	
    90	The macOS app reads channel health from the Gateway. Configured channels do
    91	not need a linked-session field to report a healthy state. Explicitly disabled
    92	accounts stay inactive and cannot provide a healthy fallback.
    93	
    94	## Menu bar
    95	
    96	The menu shows actionable health problems: an orange failure reason or a red
    97	login requirement. Healthy and pending states stay quiet. Opening the menu
    98	refreshes health on demand.
    99	
   100	## Settings
   101	
   102	- In the native **Connection… → Connection** tab, **Local Gateway** shows a health row: status
   103	  dot, channel summary, and an optional failure detail line, with **Retry now** and
   104	  **Open logs** buttons.
   105	- **Dashboard → Settings → Channels** surfaces per-channel status and controls (login QR,
   106	  logout, probe, last disconnect/error).
   107	
   108	The health row uses these states:
   109	
   110	- Green: the selected linked or configured channel has no reported failure.
   111	- Orange: a channel or health request reports a failure. An unlinked channel
   112	  also stays orange when another configured channel is healthy.
   113	- Red: linking is required and no healthy configured channel is available.
   114	- Gray: health is pending or the selected channel is disabled or not configured.
   115	
   116	The summary can read "Telegram ready", "Telegram running", or
   117	"linked · auth 12m". The app honors the Gateway's startup and reconnect grace
   118	windows. Transient transport flags alone do not mark a channel degraded.
   119	Reported failures such as a stale socket or unavailable inbound processing
   120	still take precedence over a ready-looking connection.
   121	
   122	A missing HTTP status does not by itself mean a timeout. The app preserves
   123	the Gateway's reported probe error.
   124	
   125	## How health refresh works
   126	
   127	The app calls the Gateway's `health` RPC over its existing WebSocket
   128	connection (not a CLI shell-out) every ~60s and on demand. This reads the
   129	Gateway's health snapshot. It does not request an active channel probe or
   130	send messages. The app caches the last
   131	good snapshot and the last error separately so the UI loads instantly and
   132	does not flicker while offline.
   133	
   134	The native health row follows the Primary Gateway.
   135	Switching Primary immediately clears the previous Gateway's cached status.
   136	Delayed replies from that Gateway cannot replace the new results. Reconnecting
   137	to the same Gateway retains its last good health snapshot while a fresh check
   138	runs. Connection errors stay with the Gateway that reported them.
   139	
   140	## When in doubt
   141	
   142	Use the CLI flow in [Gateway health](/gateway/health) (`openclaw status`,
   143	`openclaw status --deep`, `openclaw health --json`) and run
   144	`openclaw logs --follow`, filtering for `web-heartbeat` / `web-reconnect`.
   145	
   146	## Related
   147	
   148	- [Gateway health](/gateway/health)
   149	- [macOS app](/platforms/macos)
   150	---
   151	summary: "Testing utilities and patterns for OpenClaw plugins"
   152	title: "Plugin testing"
   153	sidebarTitle: "Testing"
   154	read_when:
   155	  - You are writing tests for a plugin
   156	  - You need test utilities from the plugin SDK
   157	  - You want to understand contract tests for bundled plugins
   158	---
   159	
   160	Reference for test utilities, patterns, and lint enforcement for OpenClaw
   161	plugins.
   162	
   163	<Tip>
   164	  **Looking for test examples?** The how-to guides include worked test examples:
   165	  [Channel plugin tests](/plugins/sdk-channel-plugins#step-6-test) and
   166	  [Provider plugin tests](/plugins/sdk-provider-plugins#step-6-test).
   167	</Tip>
   168	
   169	## Test utilities
   170	
   171	These subpaths are repo-local source entrypoints for OpenClaw's own bundled
   172	plugin tests. They are not published `package.json` exports for third-party
   173	plugins, and they may import Vitest or other repo-only test dependencies.
   174	
   175	```typescript
   176	import {
   177	  shouldAckReaction,
   178	  removeAckReactionAfterReply,
   179	} from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-feedback";
   180	import { installCommonResolveTargetErrorCases } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-target-testing";
   181	import { AUTH_PROFILE_RUNTIME_CONTRACT } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/agent-runtime-test-contracts";
   182	import { createTestPluginApi } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/plugin-test-api";
   183	import { expectChannelInboundContextContract } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-contract-testing";
   184	import { createStartAccountContext } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-test-helpers";
   185	import { describePluginRegistrationContract } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/plugin-test-contracts";
   186	import { registerSingleProviderPlugin } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/plugin-test-runtime";
   187	import { describeOpenAIProviderRuntimeContract } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/provider-test-contracts";
   188	import { getProviderHttpMocks } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/provider-http-test-mocks";
   189	import { createOpenClawTestState } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/test-state";
   190	import { withEnv, withFetchPreconnect, withServer } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/test-env";
   191	import { isLiveTestEnabled } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/test-live";
   192	import { createRequestCaptureJsonFetch } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/test-media-understanding";
   193	import {
   194	  bundledPluginRoot,
   195	  createCliRuntimeCapture,
   196	  runDirectImportSmoke,
   197	  typedCases,
   198	} from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/test-fixtures";
   199	import { mockNodeBuiltinModule } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/test-node-mocks";
   200	```
   201	
   202	Use these focused subpaths for bundled plugin tests. The former
   203	`openclaw/plugin-sdk/testing` barrel was repo-local, excluded from shipped
   204	packages, and has been removed. The former `openclaw/plugin-sdk/test-utils`
   205	alias was removed with it. `pnpm run lint:plugins:no-extension-test-core-imports`
   206	(`scripts/check-no-extension-test-core-imports.ts`) keeps extension tests on
   207	the focused test subpaths above.
   208	
   209	Bundled channel integration tests can use `agent-runtime-test-contracts` for
   210	real session and subscriber fixtures, `reply-payload-testing` for payload
   211	construction and delivery settlement, and `plugin-test-runtime` for hook
   212	runners and registries. These helpers reuse their core owners; register the
   213	session fixture lifecycle explicitly. Use published runtime subpaths when
   214	they already expose the needed operation.
   215	
   216	### Available exports
   217	
   218	| Export                                                                    | Purpose                                                                                                                                     |
   219	| ------------------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   220	| `createTestPluginApi`                                                     | Build a minimal plugin API mock for direct registration unit tests. Import from `plugin-sdk/plugin-test-api`                                |
