   287	| `sanitizeTerminalText`                                                    | Sanitize terminal output for assertions. Import from `plugin-sdk/test-fixtures`                                                             |
   288	| `countLines` / `hasBalancedFences`                                        | Assert chunking output shape. Import from `plugin-sdk/test-fixtures`                                                                        |
   289	| `typedCases`                                                              | Preserve literal types for table-driven tests. Import from `plugin-sdk/test-fixtures`                                                       |
   290	
   291	Bundled-plugin contract suites also use these SDK testing subpaths for
   292	test-only registry, manifest, public-artifact, and runtime fixture helpers.
   293	Core-only suites that depend on bundled OpenClaw inventory stay under
   294	`src/plugins/contracts` instead.
   295	
   296	For channel account-policy tests, `createAccountPolicyInheritanceCases()` from
   297	`openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-test-helpers` returns four literal inheritance rows
   298	with fresh objects and arrays on each call, preserving omitted policy fields.
   299	Use it alongside `validateTestChannelConfig(channelId, channelConfig)`, which
   300	validates schema-parsed channel data through the host config boundary. Each
   301	plugin test still owns its schema parsing, account resolver, and assertions,
   302	including checks that omitted account policies remain absent.
   303	
   304	For complete zero-usage inputs, `createZeroUsageFixture()` from
   305	`openclaw/plugin-sdk/test-fixtures` returns fresh usage and nested cost objects
   306	without optional telemetry fields. Keep expected usage values explicit.
   307	
   308	### Types
   309	
   310	Focused testing subpaths also re-export types useful in test files:
   311	
   312	```typescript
   313	import type {
   314	  ChannelAccountSnapshot,
   315	  ChannelGatewayContext,
   316	} from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-contract";
   317	import type { OpenClawConfig } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/config-contracts";
   318	import type { MockFn, PluginRuntime, RuntimeEnv } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/plugin-test-runtime";
   319	```
   320	
   321	## Testing target resolution
   322	
   323	Use `installCommonResolveTargetErrorCases` to add standard error cases for
   324	channel target resolution:
   325	
   326	```typescript
   327	import { describe } from "vitest";
   328	import { installCommonResolveTargetErrorCases } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/channel-target-testing";
   329	
   330	describe("my-channel target resolution", () => {
   331	  installCommonResolveTargetErrorCases({
   332	    resolveTarget: ({ to, mode, allowFrom }) => {
   333	      // Your channel's target resolution logic
   334	      return myChannelResolveTarget({ to, mode, allowFrom });
   335	    },
   336	    implicitAllowFrom: ["user1", "user2"],
   337	  });
   338	
   339	  // Add channel-specific test cases
   340	  it("should resolve @username targets", () => {
   341	    // ...
   342	  });
   343	});
   344	```
   345	
   346	## Testing patterns
   347	
   348	### Testing registration contracts
   349	
   350	Unit tests that pass a hand-written `api` mock to `register(api)` do not
   351	exercise OpenClaw's loader acceptance gates. Add at least one loader-backed
   352	smoke test for each registration surface your plugin depends on, especially
   353	hooks and exclusive capabilities such as memory.
   354	
   355	The real loader fails plugin registration when required metadata is missing or
   356	a plugin calls a capability API it does not own. For example,
   357	`api.registerHook(...)` requires a hook name, and
   358	`api.registerMemoryCapability(...)` requires the plugin manifest or exported
   359	entry to declare `kind: "memory"`.
   360	
   361	### Testing runtime config access
   362	
   363	Prefer the shared plugin runtime mock from
   364	`openclaw/plugin-sdk/plugin-test-runtime`. Its runtime config helpers model the
   365	current snapshot and mutation APIs.
   366	
   367	### Unit testing a channel plugin
   368	
   369	```typescript
   370	import { describe, it, expect, vi } from "vitest";
   371	
   372	describe("my-channel plugin", () => {
   373	  it("should resolve account from config", () => {
   374	    const cfg = {
   375	      channels: {
   376	        "my-channel": {
   377	          token: "test-token",
   378	          allowFrom: ["user1"],
   379	        },
   380	      },
   381	    };
   382	
   383	    const account = myPlugin.setup.resolveAccount(cfg, undefined);
   384	    expect(account.token).toBe("test-token");
   385	  });
   386	
   387	  it("should inspect account without materializing secrets", () => {
   388	    const cfg = {
   389	      channels: {
   390	        "my-channel": { token: "test-token" },
   391	      },
   392	    };
   393	
   394	    const inspection = myPlugin.setup.inspectAccount(cfg, undefined);
   395	    expect(inspection.configured).toBe(true);
   396	    expect(inspection.tokenStatus).toBe("available");
   397	    // No token value exposed
   398	    expect(inspection).not.toHaveProperty("token");
   399	  });
   400	});
   401	```
   402	
   403	### Unit testing a provider plugin
   404	
   405	For bundled catalog tests that resolve provider endpoint capabilities, call
   406	`useProviderCatalogMetadata(new URL(".", import.meta.url))` from
   407	`openclaw/plugin-sdk/plugin-test-runtime` at file or suite scope. It prepares
   408	the plugin's manifest metadata once, installs and clears that snapshot around
   409	each test, and rejects Jiti loading during assertions. This keeps cold runtime
   410	discovery out of catalog test deadlines without changing provider behavior.
   411	
   412	Pass additional manifest roots when a case exercises another provider's endpoints,
   413	for example `useProviderCatalogMetadata(new URL(".", import.meta.url), new URL("../google/", import.meta.url))`.
   414	Assert the endpoint class in route-specific cases so missing metadata cannot turn
   415	a provider route into an unintended custom-endpoint case.
   416	
   417	```typescript
   418	import { describe, it, expect } from "vitest";
   419	
   420	describe("my-provider plugin", () => {
   421	  it("should resolve dynamic models", () => {
   422	    const model = myProvider.resolveDynamicModel({
   423	      modelId: "custom-model-v2",
   424	      // ... context
   425	    });
   426	
   427	    expect(model.id).toBe("custom-model-v2");
   428	    expect(model.provider).toBe("my-provider");
   429	    expect(model.api).toBe("openai-completions");
   430	  });
   431	
   432	  it("should return catalog when API key is available", async () => {
   433	    const result = await myProvider.catalog.run({
   434	      resolveProviderApiKey: () => ({ apiKey: "test-key" }),
   435	      // ... context
   436	    });
   437	
   438	    expect(result?.provider?.models).toHaveLength(2);
   439	  });
   440	});
   441	```
   442	
   443	### Mocking the plugin runtime
   444	
   445	For code that uses `createPluginRuntimeStore`, mock the runtime in tests:
   446	
   447	```typescript
   448	import { createPluginRuntimeStore } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/runtime-store";
   449	import type { PluginRuntime } from "openclaw/plugin-sdk/runtime-store";
   450	
   451	const store = createPluginRuntimeStore<PluginRuntime>({
   452	  pluginId: "test-plugin",
   453	  errorMessage: "test runtime not set",
   454	});
   455	
   456	// In test setup
   457	const mockRuntime = {
   458	  agent: {
   459	    resolveAgentDir: vi.fn().mockReturnValue("/tmp/agent"),
   460	    // ... other mocks
   461	  },
   462	  config: {
   463	    current: vi.fn(() => ({}) as const),
   464	    mutateConfigFile: vi.fn(),
   465	    replaceConfigFile: vi.fn(),
   466	  },
   467	  // ... other namespaces
   468	} as unknown as PluginRuntime;
   469	
   470	store.setRuntime(mockRuntime);
   471	
   472	// After tests
   473	store.clearRuntime();
   474	```
   475	
   476	### Testing with per-instance stubs
   477	
   478	Prefer per-instance stubs over prototype mutation:
   479	
   480	```typescript
   481	// Preferred: per-instance stub
   482	const client = new MyChannelClient();
   483	client.sendMessage = vi.fn().mockResolvedValue({ id: "msg-1" });
   484	
   485	// Avoid: prototype mutation
   486	// MyChannelClient.prototype.sendMessage = vi.fn();
   487	```
   488	
   489	## Contract tests (in-repo plugins)
   490	
   491	Bundled plugins have contract tests that verify registration ownership:
   492	
   493	```bash
   494	pnpm test src/plugins/contracts/
   495	```
   496	
   497	These tests assert:
   498	
   499	- Which plugins register which providers
   500	- Which plugins register which speech providers
   501	- Registration shape correctness
   502	- Runtime contract compliance
   503	
   504	### Running scoped tests
   505	
   506	For a specific plugin:
   507	
   508	```bash
   509	pnpm test <bundled-plugin-root>/my-channel/
   510	```
   511	
   512	For contract tests only:
   513	
   514	```bash
   515	pnpm test src/plugins/contracts/shape.contract.test.ts
   516	pnpm test src/plugins/contracts/auth-choice.contract.test.ts
   517	pnpm test src/plugins/contracts/runtime-seams.contract.test.ts
   518	```
   519	
   520	## Lint enforcement (in-repo plugins)
   521	
   522	`scripts/run-additional-boundary-checks.mts` runs a set of `lint:plugins:*`
   523	import-boundary checks in CI; each can also be run standalone locally:
   524	
   525	| Command                                                        | Enforces                                                                                     |
   526	| -------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   527	| `pnpm run lint:plugins:no-monolithic-plugin-sdk-entry-imports` | Bundled plugins cannot import the monolithic `openclaw/plugin-sdk` root barrel.              |
   528	| `pnpm run lint:plugins:no-extension-src-imports`               | Production extension files cannot import the repo `src/**` tree directly (`../../src/...`).  |
   529	| `pnpm run lint:plugins:no-extension-test-core-imports`         | Extension test files cannot import removed SDK test aliases or other core-only test helpers. |
   530	
   531	External plugins are not subject to these lint rules, but following the same
   532	patterns is recommended.
   533	
   534	## Test configuration
   535	
   536	OpenClaw uses Vitest 5 with informational V8 coverage reporting. For plugin tests:
   537	
   538	```bash
   539	# Run all tests
   540	pnpm test
   541	
   542	# Run specific plugin tests
   543	pnpm test <bundled-plugin-root>/my-channel/src/channel.test.ts
   544	
   545	# Run with a specific test name filter
   546	pnpm test <bundled-plugin-root>/my-channel/ -t "resolves account"
   547	
   548	# Run with coverage
   549	pnpm test:coverage
   550	```
   551	
   552	If local runs cause memory pressure:
   553	
   554	```bash
   555	OPENCLAW_VITEST_MAX_WORKERS=1 pnpm test
   556	```
