     1	---
     2	summary: "Routine local test order, the core test commands, and the local PR gate"
     3	title: "Run tests locally"
     4	read_when:
     5	  - You are running or fixing tests on your own machine
     6	  - You need the local land and gate command list
     7	---
     8	
     9	## Routine local order
    10	
    11	1. `pnpm test:changed` for changed-scope Vitest proof.
    12	2. `pnpm test <path-or-filter>` for one file, directory, or explicit target.
    13	3. `pnpm test` only when you intentionally need the full local Vitest suite.
    14	
    15	The repository wrappers partition ordinary unfiltered Gateway-server run-mode
    16	selections and expanded full-suite plans into at most 50 test files per process.
    17	This bounds the non-isolated module graph as the inventory grows without changing
    18	worker concurrency, heap limits, or individual file boundaries. Raw Vitest and
    19	existing single-invocation selections, such as explicit targets, coverage, report
    20	output, bail, and watch mode, retain their existing behavior.
    21	
    22	Tests that create real managed worktrees must satisfy the
    23	[capacity and disk-space requirements](/concepts/managed-worktrees#capacity-and-disk-space),
    24	including the additional allowance for executable setup scripts. Keep that space
    25	available throughout the run.
    26	
    27	The project runner prints wrapper usage for a sole `--help` or `-h` request.
    28	Compound requests, including `--help --no-help`, follow native Vitest option semantics.
    29	
    30	An existing UI directory target stays scoped to that directory, including when
    31	combined with explicit E2E test files. Tests retain their owning shared, isolated,
    32	or browser lane. UI source/support-file targets that need whole-lane coverage
    33	(such as shared styles or setup files) still use that broader fallback; use a
    34	directory or explicit test files when you want a bounded run.
    35	
    36	When a one-shot routed run targets only explicit test-file paths, each selected
    37	Vitest invocation must discover at least one test file. Excluding every selected
    38	file fails even when the lane normally permits empty runs. To allow that outcome
    39	intentionally, use `pnpm test <test-file-path> -- --passWithNoTests`. Use
    40	`--passWithNoTests=false` to require nonempty discovery explicitly. Broader
    41	selectors and source-derived selections retain their lane defaults.
    42	
    43	An explicit `--config` run through `scripts/run-vitest.mjs` keeps its stricter
    44	named-file policy and does not permit empty named-file runs. Plugin
    45	`--allow-no-tests` and `--allow-empty-after-exclude` controls are unchanged.
    46	
    47	Codex and other linked/sparse worktrees can run local tests and checks. Tooling
    48	requires prepared dependencies; it does not create an implicit link to the
    49	primary checkout. Keep any existing borrowed install unchanged while another
    50	task uses it. A source install's `pnpm:devPreinstall` check refuses borrowed links
    51	at the checkout-root module directories and their `.pnpm` directories before
    52	normal dependency reconciliation. It does not inspect every workspace package's
    53	dependencies or lock paths against concurrent replacement; `--ignore-scripts`
    54	skips it, and alternate pnpm directory settings are not exhaustively validated.
    55	When dependencies are ready, use the normal commands above. To avoid pnpm's
    56	package-manager preflight against an existing shared install, use these direct
    57	Node harnesses:
    58	
    59	- Bounded focused proof with ready dependencies:
    60	  `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs <path-or-filter>`.
    61	- Changed typecheck/lint/guard proof: `node scripts/check-changed.mjs`.
    62	
    63	Fresh source installs clone package files from the pnpm store, falling back to
    64	copies when the filesystem cannot clone. Separate checkouts therefore keep
    65	independent file metadata: installing dependencies elsewhere cannot change an
    66	active compiler input's modification history through a shared hardlink. Existing
    67	hardlinked installs are not converted by an up-to-date `pnpm install`; use a fresh
    68	task-owned checkout and install for isolated proof. Do not reinstall borrowed
    69	dependencies or replace an installation while another task uses it.
    70	
    71	For Control UI route tests, run `node scripts/run-tsgo-core-test-shards.mjs ui`
    72	to check fixture types; `node scripts/run-tsgo.mjs -p tsconfig.ui.json` checks
    73	production UI code and excludes tests. Type route fixtures against the loader's
    74	required capabilities instead of asserting a partial fixture as the full
    75	application context. Keep real selection capabilities in lifecycle tests so
    76	agent scope changes and subscription cleanup follow the application behavior.
    77	
    78	For remote-environment proof, invoke `node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs`
    79	directly. Avoid local `pnpm crabbox:run` in linked worktrees because pnpm may
    80	reconcile dependencies before the remote wrapper starts.
    81	
    82	## Core commands
    83	
    84	Run the test toolchain on Node 24.16+ or Node 26.1+, matching the packaged
    85	runtime floor. Older Node bindings can truncate SQLite TEXT values at embedded NUL characters.
    86	
    87	To compare the same Vitest selection on Node and Bun, use the existing wrapper:
    88	
    89	```sh
    90	OPENCLAW_VITEST_RUNTIME=node pnpm test <path-or-filter>
    91	OPENCLAW_VITEST_RUNTIME=bun pnpm test <path-or-filter>
    92	```
    93	
    94	Install the exact Bun fork build pinned by `.github/actions/setup-test-bun/action.yml`
    95	for comparable results. This selects the
    96	actual Vitest process and workers while retaining Node for orchestration and
    97	compiler preparation. It does not use Bun's native test runner. `bun run` alone
    98	does not select Bun for tests. Node remains the local default.
    99	
   100	For the CI Control UI comparison, run the full Node selection followed by its
   101	compatible Bun partition:
   102	
   103	```sh
   104	OPENCLAW_NODE_TEST_CONFIGS_JSON='["ui/vitest.config.ts"]' \
   105	OPENCLAW_NODE_TEST_VITEST_ARGS_JSON='["--maxWorkers", "3"]' \
   106	OPENCLAW_CI_TEST_RUNTIME_POLICY=dual \
   107	node --import tsx scripts/ci-run-node-test-shard.mts
   108	```
   109	
   110	The Bun partition deliberately excludes two whole GC-sensitive files, which
   111	remain covered by Node. Running the complete UI config directly with
   112	`OPENCLAW_VITEST_RUNTIME=bun` also runs those currently incompatible assertions.
   113	
   114	Test processes and their CLI fixtures keep Sparkplug baseline compilation enabled
   115	but run it synchronously. This avoids a Node 24 shutdown deadlock where a
   116	background compiler waits for main-thread garbage collection while `process.exit`
   117	joins that compiler. The shared Node argument policy owns this test-only
   118	mitigation; production CLI exit behavior, assertions, and deadlines are unchanged.
   119	
   120	The script erasability gate uses Node's strip-only parser, including when package
   121	checks run under Bun. It selects an installed Node runtime and skips Bun's `node` shim.
   122	
   123	The test toolchain pins stable Vitest `5.0.1`, including its browser and coverage
   124	packages. Use `describe(name, { concurrent: false }, callback)` for ordered
   125	suites. Await asynchronous assertions, keep `vi.mock`/`vi.hoisted` at module
   126	scope, and perform actions whose mock calls you assert inside the test.
   127	OpenClaw sets `clearMocks: false`, so setup and `beforeAll` calls are preserved.
   128	Clear or reset each assertion's owned mock actions explicitly as needed.
   129	Name patterns spanning suites use `suite > test`; native JSON retains its
   130	space-joined `fullName`, so evidence readers match `ancestorTitles` plus `title`.
   131	
   132	Filesystem transform caching uses `test.fsModuleCache` and
   133	`test.fsModuleCachePath`; the existing `OPENCLAW_VITEST_FS_MODULE_CACHE` and
   134	`OPENCLAW_VITEST_FS_MODULE_CACHE_PATH` controls retain their ownership and
   135	disable behavior. Cache-key plugins use `defineCacheKeyGenerator`.
   136	The jsdom lanes optimize Lit and its exported subpaths together through
   137	`deps.optimizer.client`. CodeMirror and Lezer stay in Vite's module graph so
   138	editor classes and parser properties retain one dependency identity.
   139	When `NODE_COMPILE_CACHE` is configured, test launchers preserve it for Vitest
   140	and its workers. Vitest disables bytecode caching in workers and their child
   141	processes for V8 and custom coverage providers; explicit
   142	`NODE_DISABLE_COMPILE_CACHE=1` still disables caching for the entire invocation.
   143	Inline projects inherit root configuration in Vitest 5, including concatenated
   144	setup and include arrays. The four UI E2E resource projects declare
   145	`extends: false` because each supplies its complete inventory and setup.
   146	
   147	Maintained JavaScript tooling wrappers and root package commands load TypeScript
   148	through `scripts/tsx.mjs`, using tsx's ESM entry. This preserves native loading of
   149	compiled ESM plugins and their import-only dependencies, including when loaded
   150	through `require()`. Source TypeScript imports and tsconfig path aliases remain
   151	available.
   152	
   153	These launchers retain tsx's in-process transform cache and Node's module cache.
   154	They skip tsx's shared disk cache before the loader starts, and child tooling
   155	inherits that policy. This cache policy does not clean
   156	existing temporary directories, Node or Vitest caches, or other global caches. Standalone
   157	`pnpm ui:build` starts natively and runs its post-build validators directly with Node.
   158	Those validators do not load tsx or require `TSX_DISABLE_CACHE` in the invoking shell.
   159	Raw external `tsx` and `node --import tsx` invocations outside these launchers are unchanged.
   160	
   161	Node Vitest workers also preload `scripts/tsx.mjs` once per worker. Vitest still
   162	owns test module mocks, while native plugin SDK imports use Node's source module
   163	graph with TypeScript syntax and `.js`-to-`.ts` resolution. Bun uses its native
   164	TypeScript loader. This keeps source-host tests from relying on Jiti to evaluate
   165	another copy of the host SDK.
   166	
   167	Scheduler-owned project runs on macOS and Linux reuse filesystem transforms within
   168	exclusive slots, including serial runs that mix configurations. Each Vitest
   169	configuration keeps separate directories. A slot stays owned through preflight,
   170	retries, and verified child/group completion; uncertain cleanup retires it.
   171	Same-config serial runs without scheduler assignment, explicit isolated cache paths,
   172	watch runs, and Windows retain their existing cache ownership. Concurrent invocations
   173	still need separate cache roots.
   174	
   175	Control UI builds report size budgets without enforcing them. Run
   176	`pnpm ui:check-performance` after a build to enforce absolute budgets, or
   177	`pnpm ui:check-performance:base <base-commit-sha>` to build and compare both
   178	revisions with the same toolchain. See [Control UI size budgets](/ci/pipeline#control-ui-size-budgets).
   179	
   180	### Source tests and subprocess builds
   181	
   182	Non-watch runs through `pnpm test` or `scripts/run-vitest.mjs` keep Vitest tests
   183	and runtime parents on TypeScript. Importing a declared subprocess entrypoint
   184	compiles the fixed test entry set and its workspace dependencies into one fresh
   185	invocation directory under `.artifacts/vitest-workers/`.
   186	
   187	The declared application entries run as plain Node JavaScript without a
   188	TypeScript loader: SQLite read-only snapshots, database verification, Tailscale
   189	route ownership, the service relay, its POSIX and Windows anchors, the memory
   190	plugin's KNN child, session transcript archive and reconciliation workers, and
   191	managed GitHub credential resolution. The same generation also compiles the fake-backend TUI
   192	fixture's four runtime roots together: the real TUI, embedded reply producer,
   193	reply metadata reader, and outbound normalizer. Shared chunks preserve their
   194	module and WeakMap identity. Generated TUI fixtures remain `.mts` files: Node
   195	launches them with `--import tsx` for their own syntax, while Bun handles that
   196	syntax natively without the Node loader. Only their runtime imports change.
   197	Existing package build entry paths and Vitest source parents stay unchanged. The
   198	CLI fork-recovery regression also compiles the real CLI entry and its concurrent
   199	rebind's session accessor and binding helper together. Both processes use the same
   200	runtime graph while retaining the durable-write race and process-exit assertions.
   201	Doctor process output tests with bundled plugins disabled reuse that compiled CLI
   202	inside one lazily created package fixture per test run, keeping real UI checks on
   203	fixture-owned assets and each scenario’s state separate. Standalone and watch runs
   204	use live source inside the same fixture.
   205	
   206	Isolated Doctor config scripts also share the prepared config-flow, health-writer,
   207	and install-index modules. Each case still starts a fresh process with separate
   208	state; standalone and watch runs resolve the original TypeScript entrypoints.
   209	
   210	The model-catalog, Codex catalog-page, and session model-context workers also use this compiled generation.
   211	Model-catalog workers still belong to their prepared model generations; context reads
   212	retain their serial worker pool. Plugin source/built selection remains independent
   213	of worker compilation.
   214	Other worker-thread entries and arbitrary source CLI fixtures remain outside
   215	this declared set.
   216	
   217	The agent database module-identity test shares the compiled host and SQLite SDK
   218	entries while forcing a separate plugin transform of the SDK. Its standalone and
   219	watch runs retain a disposable build from current source because this regression
   220	specifically checks a packaged graph. Both modes use the same assertions and
   221	subprocess deadline.
   222	
   223	The session-title and child-link retention tests declare their title-reader,
   224	session-utils, and listing roots in this same generation. Each fresh
   225	heap-measurement child runs their JavaScript without spending its execution
   226	deadline on TypeScript imports.
   227	
   228	Native Bash output-lifecycle fixtures also prepare the real tool and executor
   229	roots in this generation. Each scenario still uses a fresh process and real
   230	shell, pipe, and spill file; its unchanged child deadline covers prepared
   231	JavaScript startup and output handling instead of repeated TypeScript compilation.
   232	
   233	Automatic-triage process fixtures share this generation for admission, failure handling, execution, process identity, and respawn checks. Compilation finishes before readiness deadlines begin, so children load prepared JavaScript. The detached helper uses the same sealed lease runtime as the installed package.
   234	
   235	Known core database-worker consumers prepare the invocation's compiled generation
   236	before test processes start, including when a case imports its worker declaration
   237	dynamically. Selection uses the existing database-worker inventory and honors CLI
   238	filters, exclusions, and include files. Other tests retain lazy preparation across
   239	projects and shards. Config imports, listing tests, watch runs, custom selections,
   240	and tiny tests that do not import these declarations do not eagerly compile workers.
   241	A shard that imports a declaration requests the outer runner's single build through
   242	its existing Node IPC channel; eager consumers reuse the completed generation.
   243	Static imports acquire it during module collection, before fixture hooks and readiness
   244	deadlines. Every finite invocation
   245	that needs a declaration pays for this fixed entry set; preparation timing is
   246	reported separately from child execution. The runner starts one short-lived native
   247	Node or Bun compiler child and joins it before returning the verified manifest to
   248	borrowers. The compiler module graph lives in that child, not the long-lived runner
   249	or Vitest worker. No shard can select a different build graph or adopt another invocation's output. The outer
   250	runner retains the generation until child close and process-group cleanup
   251	finish, then verifies it before reporting success. Verification reads every recorded
   252	input and output with bounded asynchronous I/O, keeping the runner responsive during
   253	large shutdown scans. The invocation owner verifies each borrower's preparation
   254	before replying and verifies again after all borrowers close; Vitest does not repeat
   255	these scans inside each shard or during its concurrent pool shutdown. Standalone
   256	Vitest and watch runs retain source execution: compilation, verification, and artifact
   257	deletion require the repository runner's ownership.
   258	A lost owner or failed build fails the run.
   259	Disposal cancels pending compilation and joins it, every borrower, and outstanding
   260	preparation requests before asynchronously removing the directory. Signal handlers
   261	remain active through removal, even when large generations take time to delete.
   262	Borrower completion does not wait for compilation, so an early child exit can reach that cancellation path. An uncertain compiler or borrower join retains the
   263	generation and fails the run. Abnormal termination can also leave an unused
   264	directory; later runs never adopt it.
   265	
   266	Every preparation compiles current source; checkout `dist/` is neither an input
   267	nor a fallback. Build errors, missing artifacts, and changes to recorded build
   268	inputs fail the run. Compilation includes the native subprocess fixtures before
   269	they impose resource limits. Third-party dependencies remain external except for
   270	the always-bundled OpenClaw packages. fs-safe remains external so its native loader
   271	resolves the optional platform package from fs-safe's own dependency scope, including
   272	nested pnpm installs. Compiled workers use that same installed package; they do not
   273	copy native binaries. Native mode defaults to `auto` on macOS, Linux, and Windows.
   274	No-clobber Root moves require native support; Windows secure credential reads
   275	require the matching helper for descriptor-bound ACL checks. Explicit
   276	`off`/`auto`/`require` settings and programmatic configuration retain their
   277	precedence. Sealed portable worker bundles use guarded JavaScript only and
   278	explicitly disable native loading.
   279	
   280	Watch mode deliberately keeps the existing live-source path, including tsx for
   281	Node subprocesses and native TypeScript handling for Bun. It creates no prepared generation, so a new child launch
   282	reads current source rather than reusing a compiled snapshot. Existing Vitest
   283	watch dependency tracking still determines when tests rerun.
   284	
   285	Test wrapper runs end with a short `[test] passed|failed|skipped ... in ...` summary; Vitest's own duration line stays the per-shard detail.
   286	
   287	A failed invocation ends with one `[test] FAILED (exit N)` line after child
   288	processes, cleanup, and report publication settle. Direct `run-vitest.mts` calls
   289	use `[vitest]` instead. Nested runners retain their diagnostics and exit status;
   290	the top-level CLI owns the final failure line. Successful runs emit no failure
   291	trailer.
   292	
   293	| Command                                           | What it does                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |
   294	| ------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   295	| `pnpm test`                                       | Explicit file/directory targets route through scoped Vitest lanes. Untargeted runs are full-suite proof: fixed shard groups expand to leaf configs for local parallel execution, with the expected shard fanout printed before starting. The extension group always expands to per-extension shard configs instead of one giant root-project process. |
   296	| `pnpm test:changed`                               | Cheap smart changed-test run: precise targets from direct test edits, sibling `*.test.ts` files, explicit source mappings, and the local import graph. Broad/config/package changes are skipped unless they map to precise tests.                                                                                                                     |
   297	| `OPENCLAW_TEST_CHANGED_BROAD=1 pnpm test:changed` | Explicit broad changed-test run; use when a test harness/config/package edit should fall back to Vitest's broader changed-test behavior.                                                                                                                                                                                                              |
   298	| `pnpm test:force`                                 | Frees the configured OpenClaw gateway port (default `18789`), then runs the full suite with an isolated gateway port so server tests do not collide with a running instance.                                                                                                                                                                          |
   299	| `pnpm test:coverage`                              | Emits an informational V8 coverage report for the default unit lane (`vitest.unit.config.ts`); no coverage thresholds are enforced.                                                                                                                                                                                                                   |
   300	| `pnpm test:coverage:changed`                      | Unit coverage only for files changed since `origin/main`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |
   301	| `pnpm changed:lanes`                              | Shows the architectural lanes triggered by the diff against `origin/main`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                            |
   302	| `pnpm check:changed`                              | Runs the local changed formatting/typecheck/lint/guard plan, including targeted Vitest owner tests for selected paths. Use `pnpm test:changed` or `pnpm test <target>` for additional test proof matching the touched contract.                                                                                                                       |
   303	
   304	`pnpm check:changed` also runs the mobile protocol-event coverage guard when
   305	changes affect the gateway event catalog or constants, scanned mobile sources,
   306	coverage declarations, or the guard, its execution helpers, and its routing.
   307	All-lane checks include it too. Every gateway event must have a handler or an
   308	explicitly approved non-consumption declaration for each mobile client. To run
   309	only this guard, use `pnpm check:protocol-coverage`.
   310	
   311	For native app changes, `pnpm check:changed` uses platform scope to select lint:
   312	Android selects `pnpm android:lint` (the Gradle ktlint checks), while Apple app
   313	changes retain Swift lint. Android-only changes do not select Swift lint or its
   314	missing-tool notice. Android framework/resource lint and runtime tests remain
   315	separate checks; Kotlin lint does not replace them.
   316	
   317	Remote filesystem fixtures that execute GNU `stat` and `readlink` run locally
   318	only on Linux. The shared leading-`@` file-tool scenario
   319	also runs against a portable remote-only bridge on every platform. Native
   320	Python helper coverage remains separate, including macOS; these fixture gates
   321	do not restrict the [SSH backend's Gateway host](/gateway/sandboxing#ssh-backend).
   322	
   323	Tests that discover real bundled provider runtimes declare that prerequisite in
   324	`scripts/lib/vitest-build-prerequisites.mts`, including Telegram sticker-model
   325	selection. Local runners and CI prepare those artifacts before admitting workers.
   326	
   327	## Local PR gate
   328	
   329	For local PR land/gate checks, run:
   330	
   331	- `pnpm check:changed`
   332	- `pnpm check`
   333	- `pnpm check:test-types`
   334	- `pnpm build`
   335	- `pnpm test`
   336	- `pnpm check:docs`
   337	
   338	If `pnpm test` flakes on a loaded host, rerun once before treating it as a regression, then isolate with `pnpm test <path/to/test>`. For memory-constrained hosts:
   339	
   340	- `OPENCLAW_VITEST_MAX_WORKERS=1 pnpm test`
   341	- `OPENCLAW_VITEST_FS_MODULE_CACHE_PATH=/tmp/openclaw-vitest-cache pnpm test:changed`
   342	---
   343	summary: "Import profiling, CPU and heap profiles, shard timings, and benchmark scripts"
   344	title: "Test performance and benchmarks"
   345	read_when:
   346	  - You are profiling a slow test run
   347	  - You need a startup, gateway, or model latency benchmark
   348	---
   349	
   350	## Test performance tooling
   351	
   352	- `pnpm test:perf:imports`: enables Vitest import-duration + import-breakdown reporting, while still using scoped lane routing for explicit file/directory targets. `pnpm test:perf:imports:changed` scopes the same profiling to files changed since `origin/main`.
   353	- `pnpm test:perf:changed:bench -- --ref <git-ref>` benchmarks the routed changed-mode path against the native root-project run for the same committed git diff; `pnpm test:perf:changed:bench -- --worktree` benchmarks the current worktree change set without committing first.
   354	- `pnpm test:perf:profile:main` writes a CPU profile for the Vitest main thread; `pnpm test:perf:profile:runner` writes CPU + heap profiles for each unit worker. Both print their output directory (a temporary directory by default). Use `-- --output-dir <dir>` or `OPENCLAW_VITEST_PROFILE_DIR` to retain profiles at a chosen location.
   355	- `pnpm test:perf:groups --full-suite --allow-failures --output .artifacts/test-perf/baseline-before.json`: runs every full-suite Vitest leaf config serially and writes grouped duration data plus per-config JSON/log artifacts. Full-suite reports isolate files by default so retained module graphs and GC pauses from earlier files are not charged to later assertions; pass `-- --no-isolate` only when intentionally profiling shared-worker accumulation. `pnpm test:perf:groups:compare .artifacts/test-perf/baseline-before.json .artifacts/test-perf/after-agent.json` compares grouped reports after a performance-focused change.
   356	- Full, extension, and include-pattern shard runs update local timing data in `.artifacts/vitest-shard-timings.json`; later whole-config runs use those timings to balance slow and fast shards. Include-pattern CI shards append the shard name to the timing key, which keeps filtered shard timings visible without replacing whole-config timing data. Set `OPENCLAW_TEST_PROJECTS_TIMINGS=0` to ignore the local timing artifact.
   357	- `pnpm ci:timings:refit`: regenerate committed `config/ci-test-timings.json` from the last five successful main CI runs; add `--dry-run` to preview the changed-entry table. This file owns per-file UI E2E and per-profile compact-group weights, unlike the gitignored `.artifacts/vitest-shard-timings.json` whole-config timing cache. Independent CI shards use only the committed weights, never that cache. See [CI timing refits](/ci/capacity#measured-shard-weights) for the daily refresh and sampling rules.
   358	
   359	Runner profiling preserves the selected `forks` or `threads` pool, isolation, environment, and custom runners extending Vitest's `TestRunner`. Capture starts in a Node preload before Vitest worker imports, spans all files assigned to that worker, and finishes both profile files in awaited worker cleanup before teardown is acknowledged. It does not depend on exit-time profile flushing. Root global setup configures every selected project without replacing its reporters or setup. Main capture spans Vitest/Vite startup through run completion and close. Process termination before cleanup, bootstrap failures before runner construction, and teardown timeouts can still prevent output. Browser/VM pools, custom runners without `onCleanupWorkerContext`, and additional native `--cpu-prof`/`--heap-prof` flags are rejected for runner profiling.
   360	
   361	Forward Vitest options after the profiler separator. Forwarded options use Vitest's native CLI validation before loading config. Config-only settings, such as `runner` and `globalSetup`, belong in the Vitest config file, not CLI flags. For example:
   362	
   363	```bash
   364	pnpm test:perf:profile:runner -- --output-dir .artifacts/profiles -- --config test/vitest/vitest.unit.config.ts --pool threads
   365	```
   366	
   367	`pnpm test:extensions:memory` profiles built plugin index entries from `dist/extensions` (including nested `dist` output) and package-local `extensions/<id>/dist` output; TypeScript source entries are excluded. Root artifacts take precedence when both builds exist. Selecting an already-built plugin with `--extension <id>` reuses its output without requiring unrelated plugin builds; build the plugin package first if its output is not supplied by `pnpm build`.
   368	
   369	Native imports also need the plugin's declared dependencies and a resolvable `openclaw` host package. The profiler does not install or link dependencies: missing dependencies remain import failures in the JSON report and cause a nonzero exit.
   370	
   371	The report labels this **cold-import** screening, not plugin activation, Gateway startup, or workload coverage. Each fresh child reports process user/system CPU in microseconds through its exit hook and process peak RSS in MiB (the existing `maxRssMb` field). CPU and peak RSS come from one observation bound to the importing child PID; inherited descendant output cannot replace it. CPU totals reconcile with user plus system counters. Deltas subtract the same empty-child baseline, retain negative values, and must not be summed across plugins. The combined-import case measures a separate process with shared imports. Explicit process exit is not proof of plugin lifecycle cleanup or memory retention.
   372	
   373	Source commit/tree and tracked cleanliness, build metadata and selected entry hashes are observed before and after the run. Child Node/V8/ABI/platform/architecture identify the measured runtime. These snapshots do not attest the transitive dependency closure or prove that build metadata describes every generated byte. Package-local builds without their own provenance remain unqualified. Schema 2 preserves the existing `repoRoot` and absolute `results[].file` fields for saved-report readers and adds `results[].relativeFile` for portable screening. Entry provenance paths are repository-relative. Raw reports still contain local paths and captured diagnostics; use the screening consumer’s public projection or redact them before sharing.
   374	
   375	`qualification.qualified` admits only complete cold-import snapshots with matching source/build declarations, stable selected inputs, resource counters, a child-recorded completion after every awaited import, and verified process-group/output and temporary-home cleanup. Missing identity or cleanup proof stays explicit in `qualification.gaps`; screening consumers must reject unqualified reports even when the observation command exits successfully. Windows currently verifies child/output closure without descendant-group proof, so those observations remain unqualified. Import failures (including exit zero before the sequence completes), missing RSS, timeouts, and cleanup failures produce their nonzero command outcome.
   376	
   377	### Kitchen Sink Gateway resource comparison
   378	
   379	The existing Kitchen Sink RPC walk can compare a fresh Gateway with plugins
   380	disabled against a fresh Gateway with only Kitchen Sink `conformance` active:
   381	
   382	```bash
   383	OPENCLAW_KITCHEN_SINK_NPM_SPEC=npm-pack:/fixtures/kitchen-sink.tgz \
   384	  pnpm test:plugins:kitchen-sink-rpc -- --resource-profile /out/resources.json
   385	```
   386	
   387	This mode requires Linux Node with `process.threadCpuUsage`, a built OpenClaw
   388	entry in the current package root, `dist/build-info.json` with a full source
   389	commit, and a local npm-pack fixture. It does not download a floating fixture.
   390	The normal RPC walk, including its Bun command, is unchanged.
   391	
   392	Run only in a prepared secretless container or remote runner. For example, bake
   393	the frozen built host, its dependencies and the pinned fixture into a reviewed
   394	image, then execute with explicit limits and networking disabled:
   395	
   396	```bash
   397	docker run --rm --network none --memory 4g --cpus 2 --pids-limit 512 \
   398	  -v "$PWD/proof-output:/out" -w /app \
   399	  -e OPENCLAW_KITCHEN_SINK_NPM_SPEC=npm-pack:/fixtures/kitchen-sink.tgz \
   400	  <prepared-image> \
   401	  node --import ./scripts/tsx.mjs scripts/e2e/kitchen-sink-rpc-walk.mts \
   402	  --resource-profile /out/resources.json
   403	```
   404	
   405	Prepare any managed npm installation prerequisites in the image; an offline
   406	install failure is blocked proof, not permission to copy credentials or enable
   407	network access. The harness supplies a minimal child environment, but does not
   408	enforce container isolation itself. Preserve the image digest and runner limits
   409	alongside the report.
   410	
   411	Each case records startup from the initialized measurement preload to HTTP
   412	readiness, one unmeasured health warmup, a 250 ms idle window, 20 completed
   413	`health` RPCs, and a 250 ms post-work window. The conformance case additionally
   414	creates a session and runs 20 asserted `kitchen_sink_text` calls with unique
   415	idempotency keys, followed by another observation window. Setup and package
   416	installation are outside measured phases; failed measured calls are not retried.
   417	
   418	After those matched phases, the conformance case uses `kitchen.resources` to
   419	verify ten million CPU iterations and a fixed checksum, hold a 16 MiB Buffer,
   420	and start a referenced 10 ms timer. Timer progress is observed with bounded
   421	status probes. Each calibration operation counts one asserted control step,
   422	including its probes; it is not an RPC throughput count. The fixture must include
   423	the calibration controls from Kitchen Sink commit `051db418820c2f0a73f8d349c88eb002b3c2d6e2`
   424	or later. Older fixtures fail visibly instead of skipping calibration.
   425	
   426	The harness resets the controls, asserts empty owner state, then reacquires the
   427	Buffer and timer before `plugins.setEnabled` disables the fixture. It requires
   428	an applied runtime receipt, no restart or cleanup warnings, an inactive catalog,
   429	and before/after samples from the same Gateway PID. This gives an in-process
   430	retirement observation before normal Gateway shutdown. Forced termination or a
   431	nonzero Gateway exit fails the report even when the process group is gone.
   432	
   433	The report preserves raw phase-boundary snapshots, completed/failed operation
   434	counts, provenance hashes and signed conformance-minus-empty deltas. CPU counters
   435	cover the Gateway process and main thread, excluding separate child processes.
   436	RSS is process-wide; other memory fields describe the main isolate. ArrayBuffers
   437	overlap external memory. Boundary samples are not peaks, and no forced GC occurs.
   438	Active-resource histograms count the types keeping the event loop alive, not
   439	plugin ownership or all live objects. The report labels CPU, held-memory and timer
   440	signals as observed or inconclusive; unrelated collection or host timers can
   441	obscure them. Reset proves owner-state release, not immediate RSS reclamation.
   442	The fixed post-work window is not a plugin drain receipt. Host shutdown is checked
   443	separately; post-process-exit sampling and guaranteed reclamation remain unsupported.
   444	These observations establish neither a leak nor a budget violation. Repeat comparable pairs through the campaign owner before drawing
   445	performance conclusions; do not sum individual plugin costs.
   446	
   447	### Plugin coverage inventory
   448	
   449	Export the source plugin inventory before a profiling campaign:
   450	
   451	```bash
   452	pnpm --silent plugins:inventory:json > plugin-inventory.json
   453	pnpm --silent plugins:inventory:json --commit <full-commit-sha> > plugin-inventory.json
   454	```
   455	
   456	The command reads committed Git objects, defaults to `HEAD`, and needs Git 2.45
   457	or newer. It works without installed dependencies or a complete working tree.
   458	Local edits do not affect the export. Missing objects fail the command; it does
   459	not fetch them or load plugin code.
   460	
   461	The versioned JSON includes the commit and tree, plugin IDs, relative paths,
   462	package metadata, distribution classes, and declared surfaces. It shares the
   463	documentation inventory's collector and distribution rules but excludes
   464	docs-only external seeds. `sha256` hashes the payload without that field using
   465	the shared stable JSON serializer (object keys sorted, array order preserved).
   466	Declaration in this inventory is not evidence that a workload was exercised.
   467	Keep import, registration, workload, and cleanup coverage separate when joining
   468	profiling results to this inventory.
   469	
   470	### Reusing the resource host
   471	
   472	Source-checkout campaign tools can import `resolveResourceGatewayRuntime` and
   473	`runResourceGatewayCase` from `scripts/e2e/kitchen-sink-rpc-walk.mts`. Kitchen Sink
   474	uses this same host lifecycle. Run from one frozen, built OpenClaw package root
   475	per process; this is a testing seam, not a published plugin SDK API.
   476	
   477	The preparation callback receives an isolated config path, loopback port, test
   478	token and a local-archive installer that verifies the supplied SHA-256. Enable
   479	only the selected plugins there. The workload callback receives authenticated
   480	CLI-mode RPC calls, resource snapshots and counted `measure(name, count, run)`
   481	phases. Assert the active plugin inventory and operation results in the workload;
   482	registration or a successful transport response alone does not establish coverage.
   483	
   484	The host records startup, preserves failed phases and joins Gateway shutdown
   485	before checking service-stop logs. A failed workload, nonzero exit, attempted
   486	forced cleanup or shutdown error retains temporary state and fails the case.
   487	The campaign owns bounded callback deadlines, mock-service cleanup, runner
   488	isolation, repetitions and report publication. Keep mock-service measurements
   489	separate from Gateway observations and preserve host, archive and harness hashes.
   490	On an outer timeout, the runner must terminate and join the whole container or
   491	cgroup: the Gateway has its own process group, so killing the campaign process
   492	alone does not clean it up.
   493	
   494	### Zod schema compilation
   495	
   496	Compile individual schemas only after measuring a repeated validation path.
   497	Use the pinned Zod package's `z.compile(schema)` API and retain the compiled
   498	schema at its existing owner. The `zod/compile` side-effect import enables a
   499	process-wide hook and is unsuitable for a selective optimization.
   500	
   501	Nested tool activity validation compiles its schema on the first matching read
   502	or creation and reuses it across Gateway turns. Ordinary transcript rows bypass
   503	that compilation. Config, transcript entry, and browser relay schemas retain
   504	their existing parsers because their measured caller costs did not justify
   505	compilation.
   506	
   507	Measure compilation and the first operation separately from warmed operations.
   508	Compare valid, invalid, and mixed inputs through the actual caller, including
   509	any JSON decoding, copying, or context construction it performs. For predicates
   510	that discard parsed output, compare ordinary `safeParse(...).success`,
   511	`z.validate(schema, input)`, and validation with a compiled schema; avoiding
   512	error allocation can help independently of compilation.
   513	
   514	Keep schemas shared with the strict-CSP Control UI uncompiled: explicit
   515	compilation attempts code generation even when `jitless` is set. Reusing a
   516	stable schema across calls is a separate optimization that needs no compiler.
   517	
   518	Keep refinement and transform callbacks pure: an invalid compiled parse can
   519	fall back to the runtime parser and execute those callbacks twice. Default
   520	compilation preserves runtime fallback for unsupported schemas; async parsing
   521	and encoding keep their existing runtime behavior.
   522	
   523	## Benchmarks
   524	
   525	<Accordion title="Session history (scripts/bench-session-history.ts)">
   526	
   527	Measure SQLite history pages and the Gateway's bounded history reader with
   528	synthetic conversations, including sparse markers, dense markers, and resets:
   529	
   530	```bash
   531	pnpm test:sessions:history:bench --samples 30 --output history.json
   532	pnpm test:sessions:history:bench --profile sparse,trailing,reset --operation recent --analyze --samples 15 --output history-analyzed.json
   533	```
   534	
   535	The second command includes 5,000 trailing compaction markers and refreshes
   536	SQLite planner statistics before reading. Compare both statistics states when
   537	changing a query. `--operation` selects `recent`, `page`, or `gateway-tail`;
   538	omitting it measures all three.
   539	
   540	Each reader runs in a fresh process. Reports separate imports, the first read
   541	(including database open), and warm p50/p95 wall and CPU time. OS caches are not
   542	flushed. SQL plans, statement counts, rows delivered to JavaScript, and JSON
   543	parsing counts come from a separate instrumented read. Heap deltas are
   544	uncollected observations, not total allocations. Fixtures are removed afterward.
   545	
   546	</Accordion>
   547	
   548	<Accordion title="Model latency (scripts/bench-model.ts)">
   549	
   550	```bash
   551	pnpm tsx scripts/bench-model.ts --runs 10
   552	```
   553	
   554	Optional env: `MINIMAX_API_KEY`, `MINIMAX_BASE_URL`, `MINIMAX_MODEL`, `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY`. Default prompt: "Reply with a single word: ok. No punctuation or extra text."
   555	
   556	</Accordion>
   557	
   558	<Accordion title="CLI startup (scripts/bench-cli-startup.ts)">
   559	
   560	```bash
   561	pnpm test:startup:bench
   562	pnpm test:startup:bench:smoke
   563	pnpm test:startup:bench:save
   564	pnpm test:startup:bench:update
   565	pnpm test:startup:bench:check
   566	pnpm tsx scripts/bench-cli-startup.ts --runs 12
   567	pnpm tsx scripts/bench-cli-startup.ts --preset real --case status --case gatewayStatus --runs 3
   568	pnpm tsx scripts/bench-cli-startup.ts --entry openclaw.mjs --entry-secondary dist/entry.js --preset all
   569	pnpm tsx scripts/bench-cli-startup.ts --runtime-rss --case status --runs 3
   570	```
   571	
   572	Presets:
   573	
   574	- `startup`: `--version`, `--help`, `health`, `health --json`, `status --json`, `status`
   575	- `real`: `health`, `status`, `status --json`, `sessions`, `sessions --json`, `tasks --json`, `tasks list --json`, `tasks audit --json`, `agents list --json`, `gateway status`, `gateway status --json`, `gateway health --json`, `config get gateway.port`
   576	- `all`: both presets combined
   577	
   578	Output includes `sampleCount`, avg, p50, p95, min/max, exit-code/signal distribution, and RSS per command. The `maxRssMb` fields use MiB. By default, RSS uses the last preload marker received on stderr, preserving the historical fixture's attribution. A respawning launcher can supply that last marker. Default reports omit `memoryMetric` and sample `memory`; no runtime identity or temporary observation files are required. For a silent command, the exit marker can count as first output.
   579	
   580	Pass `--runtime-rss` to opt into runtime-process attribution. `primary.memoryMetric` identifies `cli-runtime-max-rss-v1`, and each sample's `memory` records PID, parent PID, role, and high-water RSS in bytes. The runtime is the terminal process in a unique matching CLI invocation chain; launcher and auxiliary observations are not added to it. This is not simultaneous process-tree memory.
   581	
   582	High-water RSS is observed when the preload's `exit` listener runs. Allocations in later application exit handlers are outside this observation; this is not a full-lifetime OS measurement.
   583	
   584	With `--runtime-rss`, the preload records observations in temporary files, separate from stdout/stderr and first-output timing. Runtime identity does not depend on command output; a silent entry has `firstOutputMs: null`. Missing or ambiguous runtime identity fails the sample only in this opt-in mode. Both modes are instrumented launches, separate from the no-preload, no-respawn `scripts/check-cli-startup-memory.mjs` diagnostic. `--cpu-prof-dir` / `--heap-prof-dir` write V8 profiles per run.
   585	
   586	Saved-report comparison uses report metadata, not `--runtime-rss`. Comparison, enforced fixture budgets, and source-summary memory trends reject mixed legacy/runtime metrics rather than silently comparing different processes. Historical fixtures cannot be relabeled; any replacement baseline needs separate validation and approval.
   587	
   588	Saved output: `pnpm test:startup:bench:smoke` writes `.artifacts/cli-startup-bench-smoke.json`; `pnpm test:startup:bench:save` writes `.artifacts/cli-startup-bench-all.json` (`runs=5 warmup=1`). Checked-in fixture: `test/fixtures/cli-startup-bench.json`, refreshed by `pnpm test:startup:bench:update`, compared by `pnpm test:startup:bench:check`.
   589	
   590	</Accordion>
   591	
   592	<Accordion title="Gateway startup (scripts/bench-gateway-startup.ts)">
   593	
   594	Gateway startup, restart, and agent concurrency benchmark fixtures use temporary home and state directories, loopback binding, and `discovery.mdns.mode: "off"` so synthetic Gateways do not advertise on the LAN, including on macOS.
   595	
   596	Defaults to the built CLI entry at `dist/entry.js`; run `pnpm build` first. Pass `--entry scripts/run-node.mjs` to measure the source runner instead, and keep those results separate from built-entry baselines.
   597	
   598	```bash
   599	pnpm test:startup:gateway -- --runs 5 --warmup 1
   600	pnpm test:startup:gateway -- --case skipChannels --case fiftyPlugins --runs 5
   601	node --import tsx scripts/bench-gateway-startup.ts --case default --runs 5 --output .artifacts/gateway-startup.json
   602	node --import tsx scripts/bench-gateway-startup.ts --case incidentCombined --runs 5 --warmup 1 --timeout-ms 60000 --output .artifacts/gateway-startup-incident.json
   603	```
   604	
   605	Case ids: `default`, `skipChannels` (channel startup skipped), `oneInternalHook`, `allInternalHooks`, `fiftyPlugins` (50 manifest plugins), `fiftyStartupLazyPlugins` (50 startup-lazy manifest plugins), `incidentDatabase`, `incidentNullMetadata`, `incidentWorkspace`, `incidentPackagedPlugins`, and `incidentCombined`.
   606	
   607	The incident cases are opt-in because each sample builds an isolated, non-sensitive load fixture: current global and agent databases, 100,000 retained audit rows with freelist fragmentation, eight agent workspaces containing 80,000 files (about 800 MB), and the packaged plugin inventory. Run the combined case only on a clean machine with enough free disk space; the fixture directory is removed after each sample. `incidentCombined` fails when `/healthz` p95 reaches 30 seconds or `/readyz` p95 reaches 60 seconds.
   608	
   609	Output includes first process output, `/healthz`, `/readyz`, HTTP listen log time, Gateway ready log time, CPU time, CPU core ratio, max RSS, heap, startup trace metrics, event-loop delay, and plugin lookup-table detail metrics. The script sets `OPENCLAW_GATEWAY_STARTUP_TRACE=1` in the child Gateway environment.
   610	
   611	`/healthz` is liveness (HTTP server can answer). `/readyz` is usable readiness (startup plugin sidecars, channels, and ready-critical post-attach work have settled). Startup hooks dispatch asynchronously and are not part of the readiness guarantee. Ready log time is the Gateway's internal timestamp, useful for process-side attribution but not a substitute for the external `/readyz` probe.
   612	
   613	Use JSON output or `--output` when comparing changes. Use `--cpu-prof-dir` only after trace output points at import, compile, or CPU-bound work that phase timings alone cannot explain.
   614	
   615	</Accordion>
   616	
   617	<Accordion title="Workspace computation (scripts/bench-workspace-computation.ts)">
   618	
   619	Compare workspace inventory, manifest capture, and result preparation against a
   620	frozen source checkout with its own installed dependencies:
   621	
   622	```bash
   623	node --import ./scripts/tsx.mjs scripts/bench-workspace-computation.ts \
   624	  --baseline /path/to/baseline-checkout \
   625	  --scenarios inventory,manifest,delta,unchanged \
   626	  --sizes 1000,32000,100000 --concurrency 1,4 \
   627	  --runs 3 --warmup 1 --output .artifacts/workspace-computation.json
   628	```
   629	
   630	Use `--changed-files 2000 --scenarios delta --sizes 32000` to include a larger
   631	changed payload, and `--file-bytes` to vary the content hashed during capture.
   632	The default workload is smaller: 1,000 entries and one concurrent operation.
   633	
   634	The benchmark checks identical inventory bytes, manifest references, and changed
   635	payloads. Separate processes measure first invocation and warm throughput, CPU,
   636	event-loop delay, memory, and HTTP latency from an external probe. Worker task
   637	diagnostics distinguish queueing, input preparation, transfer, and execution.
   638	The HTTP probe measures responsiveness of the computation's owning process;
   639	paired-node wire tests provide the full Gateway dispatch and reconciliation proof.
   640	
   641	</Accordion>
   642	
   643	<Accordion title="Gateway concurrency (scripts/bench-gateway-concurrency.ts)">
   644	
   645	Runs synthetic streaming agent turns in parallel sessions on one isolated
   646	Gateway. Add tool calls, session history, observers, and control-plane probes to
   647	reproduce allocation pressure from a busy Gateway. Build with `pnpm build`
   648	first. The default mock provider needs no key. Dreaming is disabled in this
   649	isolated benchmark; ordinary indexing, recaps, and database idle retention keep
   650	their normal settings.
   651	
   652	If a sample fails, `--output` and `--json` retain completed measured runs,
   653	completed `warmupRuns`, and a `failedAttempt` with its phase, one-based index,
   654	available observations, and workload, diagnostics, or cleanup errors. Failed
   655	attempts and warmups do not enter aggregate summaries. Null or absent partial
   656	measurements mean unavailable, not zero. The command still exits with status 1;
   657	live and activity-summary diagnostic failure sidecars remain available.
   658	
   659	```bash
   660	pnpm test:gateway:concurrency -- --concurrency 16 --tool-events --workspace-fanout --session-count 100 --history-messages 20 --history-clients 4 --subscribers 4 --visible-observer --control-plane --heap-prof-dir .artifacts/gateway-heap --output .artifacts/gateway-concurrency.json
   661	pnpm test:gateway:concurrency -- --concurrency 64 --turns-per-session 8 --tool-events --timeout-ms 600000 --heap-prof-dir .artifacts/gateway-sustained-heap --output .artifacts/gateway-sustained.json
   662	```
   663	
   664	Use `--provider openai` with `OPENAI_API_KEY` supplied in the environment for
   665	real OpenAI turns:
   666	
   667	```bash
   668	pnpm test:gateway:concurrency -- --provider openai --runs 1 --warmup 0 \
   669	  --agent-warmup-turns 0 --agent-count 32 --concurrency 32 --turns-per-session 3 \
   670	  --session-count 1000 --history-messages 20 --history-message-chars 1024 \
   671	  --probe-rounds 64 --cadence-ms 100 --session-updates 100 \
   672	  --session-update-clients 2 --history-clients 2 --history-burst 2 \
   673	  --subscribers 4 --control-plane --timeout-ms 120000 \
   674	  --load-cpu-prof-dir .artifacts/gateway-live-cpu \
   675	  --output .artifacts/gateway-live.json
   676	```
   677	
   678	Live mode uses a fixed OpenAI model, denies tools, and limits output to 128
   679	tokens. It permits one run with no warmups and at most 96 turns, and checks
   680	streamed replies, terminal receipts, history, and persisted replies after
   681	shutdown. It does not report synthetic provider request counts. CPU profiles
   682	are instrumented observations; keep them separate from unprofiled latency
   683	measurements. The [manual workflow](/ci/scheduled-workflows#gateway-concurrency-benchmark)
   684	runs this workload with repository-managed credentials.
   685	
   686	`--concurrency` controls parallel sessions; `--turns-per-session` controls serial
   687	turns in each session (default 1, maximum 100). The second example completes 512
   688	turns across 64 sessions. Each session starts its next turn as soon as its
   689	previous turn completes, retaining its conversation history and workspace;
   690	there is no barrier between rounds. The fresh-connection probe runs once after
   691	every session has started its first turn. `--tool-events` requires a matching
   692	successful `exec` result and the expected visible final reply on every turn,
   693	including follow-ups. Missing or duplicate tool evidence fails the run. The load
   694	timeout bounds turns and probes; startup, setup, and probe warmup have separate
   695	budgets. Health/control sampling is capped at 2,048 samples, while heap
   696	sampling continues until the full workload finishes.
   697	
   698	The fixture gives the utility model its own structured mock response, preserving
   699	the agent model's automatic tool loop. `turnEvidence.observerModelDigestTurns`
   700	counts turns with a published model-derived observer digest. A short run can
   701	legitimately report zero; observer correctness proof requires a positive count.
   702	
   703	`mockRequests` retains six mock-server counter checkpoints and their parent
   704	monotonic request bounds. Ingress deltas cover `startupAndWarmup` (readiness,
   705	connect, visibility, and probe warmup), `setup`, `agentWarmup` (optional agent
   706	turns on the same Gateway), `loadBracket`, and `postLoad` (through Gateway
   707	shutdown). The `agentWarmup` bracket remains present when `--agent-warmup-turns`
   708	is zero (the default); warmup turns are excluded from measured load. These
   709	deltas distinguish Responses, Chat Completions, embeddings, and other routes,
   710	including rejected request bodies; health and
   711	model-catalog reads are excluded. These HTTP brackets are not exact CPU capture
   712	windows or causal attribution. `selections` through the final checkpoint separately
   713	count model/global controlled responses and automatic tool/text branches, not completed
   714	responses. Auxiliary model requests remain included; neither total is a count
   715	of agent turns. Missing, regressing, or replaced-server checkpoints fail instead
   716	of becoming zero. Reports retain counters, not raw prompts or request bodies.
   717	
   718	`--agent-count N` distributes the same session inventory round-robin across
   719	1–128 configured agents. It defaults to one agent and cannot exceed the larger
   720	of `--session-count` and `--concurrency`. Increasing it does not add sessions or
   721	turns. Multi-agent runs use `main`, `bench-agent-2`, and subsequent IDs with
   722	separate workspaces. `--workspace-fanout` still assigns a distinct workspace per
   723	session. Separate browser click targets remain on `main`.
   724	
   725	For example, `--agent-count 32 --session-count 1000 --concurrency 32
   726	--turns-per-session 3` seeds 1,000 sessions and completes 96 turns, three per
   727	agent. Before the load window and its CPU/allocation profiling, multi-agent runs
   728	require each agent's current published configured model through `models.list`,
   729	then verify its complete seeded inventory and reported per-agent SQLite path
   730	through all pages of scoped `sessions.list` reads. This checks the Gateway's
   731	reported storage route; it does not independently inspect database files.
   732	`agentCoverage.beforeLoad` retains the model response and every session page.
   733	`activeTurnAgentIds` and
   734	`completedTurns` distinguish the agents handling turns from the configured
   735	roster: 128 configured agents with 16 parallel sessions does not mean 128 agents
   736	handled turns. Multi-agent history probe rows retain `sessionKey`, which maps
   737	successful requests to the independently verified store inventory. These extra
   738	setup reads do not run in the default one-agent case.
   739	
   740	Use `--probe-rounds N` for allocation comparisons with equal probe work. It
   741	attempts exactly N sampler rounds and N history bursts per configured history
   742	client, regardless of which finishes first. Each sampler round requests
   743	`/readyz`, the Control UI, and `sessions.list`; `--control-plane` adds one each
   744	of `tasks.list`, `cron.list`, and `cron.status`. Enabling `--subscribers` adds
   745	one subscribe attempt per round and an unsubscribe after each successful
   746	subscription. History attempts total `N × historyClients × historyBurst`, capped
   747	at 2048 per run. Slow clients receive the same history budget as fast clients.
   748	Failed probes remain recorded failures; counts describe attempts, not successes.
   749	Omitting the flag retains adaptive probing until agent turns and mutations end.
   750	
   751	Fixed probes can finish before or after agent turns. Every configured workload
   752	joins before final memory and allocation capture; an exhausted load deadline
   753	fails the run instead of reporting a partial fixed workload as complete. Output
   754	records the mode and requested counts in `probeWorkload`; actual sampler and
   755	history counts remain in `summary.sampleCount` and `summary.historySampleCount`.
   756	Peak RSS is sampled during sampler rounds plus the final memory observation. If
   757	those rounds finish early, a later transient RSS peak can be missed; this is not
   758	continuous peak-RSS coverage of the entire agent workload.
   759	Equal request counts do not equalize their overlap with agent turns or the
   760	Gateway's time-dependent background work.
   761	
   762	Each run's `cpuUsage` records user, system, and total CPU milliseconds for the
   763	Gateway process and its main thread. Two private IPC snapshots bound the load
   764	after setup and profiler activation through completion of all configured work,
   765	before the final memory probe, profile export, and teardown. Their child-side
   766	monotonic timestamps define `wallMs`; CPU time can exceed wall time when threads
   767	run in parallel. Ordinary runs do not connect an inspector or start a profiler.
   768	
   769	The process counters include Workers and native threads, including Workers that
   770	exit during the load, but exclude separate child processes, the mock provider,
   771	and the browser. Main-thread CPU is part of process CPU; do not add them. Their
   772	difference estimates work on other threads, with small skew from reading the
   773	counters sequentially. The summary reports `gatewayProcessCpuMs`,
   774	`gatewayProcessCpuMsPerTurn`, `gatewayMainThreadCpuMs`, and
   775	`gatewayProcessCpuCoreRatio`. CPU per turn includes the configured concurrent
   776	probes and mutations. Compare fixed workloads and identical profiling settings;
   777	moving work to Workers can improve responsiveness without reducing process CPU.
   778	The existing `cpuCoreRatio` summary remains sampled readiness-window data.
   779	
   780	To measure clicking an existing session in the Control UI sidebar during load,
   781	build the UI and install Playwright Chromium, then enable the browser probe:
   782	
   783	```bash
   784	pnpm ui:build
   785	pnpm --dir ui exec playwright install chromium
   786	pnpm test:gateway:concurrency -- --session-count 1000 --concurrency 16 --turns-per-session 2 --browser-session-clicks 3 --browser-history-messages 80 --timeout-ms 240000 --no-diagnostics-timeline --output .artifacts/gateway-session-clicks.json
   787	```
   788	
   789	`--browser-session-clicks` defaults to 0 and accepts up to 20 first visits,
   790	followed by one revisit to a recent pane. The probe seeds separate idle click
   791	targets after the inventory. `--browser-history-messages` defaults to 80 per
   792	target (maximum 500), independent of `--history-messages`, so a large inventory
   793	does not require history in every session. `--history-message-chars` also sizes
   794	the browser targets' synthetic Markdown. The browser uses the built assets in
   795	`dist/control-ui`; `PLAYWRIGHT_CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE_PATH` can select an installed
   796	Chromium executable.
   797	
   798	`browser.inventory` records requested load and click target counts alongside
   799	the authoritative unarchived and retained session counts before load, including
   800	the click targets. Retained counts include archived sessions; normal inventory
   801	maintenance can reduce the unarchived count during seeding.
   802	
   803	Each run's `browser.clicks` records time from the actual browser click event to
   804	the target pane's visible history, enabled composer, and successful transcript load
   805	followed by a paint opportunity (`readyMs`). These timings exclude Playwright
   806	actionability waits. RPC `windowStartMs` offsets begin before that wait;
   807	`latencyMs` measures the observed request/response round trip. Request records
   808	include socket identity, method, session key, success, error, and response bytes.
   809	An `inherited` request began before the click window and has a negative
   810	`windowStartMs`; its latency includes the earlier wait. Completed click records
   811	remain unchanged when a later window observes the response. The probe includes
   812	message subscribe/unsubscribe requests to expose subscription recovery waits.
   813	`connections` records observed socket, hello, and outer event sequence gaps;
   814	negative offsets include recent setup events. Close events report observed inbound
   815	silence, without inferring a close reason. `paneStates` records changes to the selected pane's loading,
   816	readiness, connection epoch, and rendered history error. A displayed history
   817	failure retains visible-history/composer timings but fails the click instead of
   818	counting as ready. Browser `longTasks`
   819	contains the milliseconds each observed long task overlaps the click-to-ready
   820	interval, separate from the Gateway's Node CPU, event-loop, and memory
   821	samples. Pre-load `/new` and initial-session timings stay outside the click
   822	summary. Compare first visits and cached revisits separately, and check
   823	`activeLoadAtStart`, `activeLoadAtFinish`, and `samplesOutsideActiveLoad` before
   824	attributing latency to concurrent work. A recorded click failure makes the benchmark
   825	exit unsuccessfully after writing its report.
   826	
   827	`--heap-prof-dir` samples allocations in the Gateway's main V8 isolate, starting
   828	after startup, session seeding, and probe warmup. Sampling ends after the load
   829	and its final memory probe, before profile serialization and teardown. It uses
   830	a 32 KiB sampling interval and includes objects collected by both minor and
   831	major GC, so `sampledAllocatedBytes` estimates gross allocations rather than
   832	retained heap. Each run records its `.heapprofile` path and the twenty largest
   833	allocation stacks with `scope: "main-isolate"`; open the raw file in the Chrome
   834	DevTools Memory panel. Worker isolates have separate profiles, described below.
   835	Native allocations are outside these V8 profiles.
   836	
   837	The summary includes sampled allocation bytes per run and per completed turn.
   838	The per-turn figure also includes concurrent probes and session mutations;
   839	compare identical workload settings and Node versions across multiple runs.
   840	Initial and follow-up turns overlap across sessions, so the allocation profile
   841	covers their combined workload rather than attributing separate cold and warm
   842	allocations. Compare matched one-turn and sustained runs to study reuse.
   843	Sampling is statistical and adds overhead. Use unprofiled runs for latency
   844	comparisons. Existing heap/RSS measurements are taken before exporting the
   845	profile. `--cpu-prof-dir` remains available separately and includes startup;
   846	the recorded `loadWindow` identifies the measured interval in that CPU profile.
   847	
   848	For CPU attribution during concurrent work, including on Windows, add
   849	`--load-cpu-prof-dir .artifacts/gateway-load-cpu`. This captures the Gateway's
   850	main V8 isolate at a 1 ms sampling interval after setup and through the final
   851	memory probe. The private benchmark IPC channel stops the profiler and writes
   852	the `.cpuprofile` before process teardown, without depending on signal-driven
   853	profile flushing. Each run's `loadCpuProfile` records its path, duration, and
   854	sample count with `scope: "main-isolate"`; open the raw profile in Chrome
   855	DevTools. Profiled runs add overhead, so keep them separate from latency
   856	comparisons. `--cpu-prof-dir` retains its startup-inclusive native profiling
   857	behavior. `--load-cpu-prof-dir` and `--heap-prof-dir` require separate runs so
   858	exporting one profile cannot contaminate the other capture.
   859	
   860	Both load-profile flags also capture observed Worker isolates over the existing
   861	private inspector connection. The main profile summary links
   862	`workersManifestPath`, a `.workers.json` manifest beside the main profile. Its
   863	rows distinguish native `threadId` from `inspectorWorkerId` and link each
   864	Worker's profile. `completed: true` means that profile was written; inspect
   865	row-level errors for missing identity, retired Workers, or other incomplete
   866	captures. A successful profiling command does not mean every Worker produced a
   867	usable profile. Workers are not paused at birth, so profiling can miss their
   868	earliest work. These files exclude separate child processes.
   869	
   870	The Worker manifest's samples use `performance.now` in the Gateway process, with
   871	timestamps taken before asynchronous Worker reads. The 100 ms cadence is
   872	nominal; overlapping reads are coalesced. CPU counters are cumulative
   873	microseconds: difference observations for the same thread identity instead of
   874	summing samples. Those deltas cover each Worker's observed interval and can
   875	miss work before its first or after its last successful sample. The sample's
   876	`memory.rss` covers the process; other `memory` fields describe the main isolate,
   877	while each Worker's `heap` contains its own V8 heap statistics.
   878	
   879	Capture windows differ: load CPU counters end before the final memory probe;
   880	the main V8 profile includes that probe; Worker profiles and samples also extend
   881	through main-profile serialization before their own stop. Use the raw CPU
   882	profiles' timestamps and manifest observations for attribution, and keep these
   883	windows separate from `cpuUsage`. Main-thread CPU plus observed Worker CPU does
   884	not account for every native thread or unobserved Worker interval. Profiling and
   885	periodic Worker inspection add overhead; compare equally instrumented runs.
   886	
   887	Both load-phase profile modes also attach `diagnostics` and a `diagnosticsPath`
   888	to the run report. The private benchmark preload subscribes only during capture
   889	and aggregates the main isolate's redaction, session writer, session list, and
   890	worker-task completion records, including fast work below slow-log thresholds.
   891	It records counts, totals, and maxima in at most 256 groups; `droppedEvents`
   892	and `collectionErrors` report incomplete collection. No per-call record history,
   893	session or agent IDs, paths, message text, regex patterns, or exception text is
   894	retained in these aggregates. Worker artifact basenames identify task groups.
   895	
   896	Redaction reports synchronous thread CPU separately from elapsed time and input
   897	UTF-16 character counts. These are inclusive measurements: nested redaction
   898	operations overlap, so do not add their times. Session writer timing separates
   899	queue wait, writer-held elapsed time, and completion delay. List records
   900	distinguish `sessions.list` from the initial `sessions.subscribe` snapshot and
   901	separate projection owners, in-flight followers, and completed cache hits.
   902	Worker-task records separate queue, preparation, run, and transfer measurements.
   903	Elapsed intervals can overlap across concurrent work and do not measure CPU.
   904	
   905	The capture also aggregates main-isolate GC pause entries. Allocation profiles
   906	identify allocation sites, including collected objects; they do not establish
   907	which objects remain reachable or prove a leak. Raw profiles contain function
   908	names and source locations and need inspection before sharing.
   909	
   910	These diagnostics channels have no collector in an ordinary Gateway. The
   911	benchmark uses isolated synthetic state, private process IPC, and no inspector
   912	listener. It does not attach to or modify an existing operator Gateway.
   913	
   914	</Accordion>
   915	
   916	<Accordion title="Gateway restart (scripts/bench-gateway-restart.ts)">
   917	
   918	macOS and Linux only (uses SIGUSR2 for in-process restarts; fails immediately on Windows). Same built-entry default and `--entry scripts/run-node.mjs` override as gateway startup above.
   919	
   920	```bash
   921	pnpm test:restart:gateway -- --case skipChannels --runs 1 --restarts 5
   922	pnpm test:restart:gateway -- --case default --runs 3 --restarts 3 --warmup 1
   923	```
   924	
   925	Case ids: `skipChannels`, `skipChannelsAcpxProbe` (ACPX startup probe on), `skipChannelsNoAcpxProbe` (probe off), `default`, `fiftyPlugins`.
   926	
   927	Output includes next `/healthz`, next `/readyz`, downtime, restart ready timing, CPU, RSS, startup trace metrics for the replacement process, and restart trace metrics for signal handling, active-work drain, close phases, next start, ready timing, and memory snapshots. The script sets `OPENCLAW_GATEWAY_STARTUP_TRACE=1` and `OPENCLAW_GATEWAY_RESTART_TRACE=1`.
   928	
   929	Use this benchmark when a change touches restart signaling, close handlers, startup-after-restart, sidecar shutdown, service handoff, or readiness after restart. Start with `skipChannels` to isolate Gateway mechanics from channel startup; use `default` or plugin-heavy cases only after the narrow case explains the restart path. Trace metrics are attribution hints, not verdicts — judge a restart change from multiple samples, the matching owner span, `/healthz`/`/readyz` behavior, and the user-visible restart contract.
   930	
   931	</Accordion>
   932	---
   933	summary: "When agents use Crabbox or Testbox, and the wrapper, lease, and trust rules"
   934	title: "Remote test proof"
   935	read_when:
   936	  - You are deciding between local and remote proof
   937	  - You are running OpenClaw tests on Crabbox or Testbox
   938	---
   939	
   940	<a id="agent-default" />
   941	
   942	## Remote proof policy for agents
   943	
   944	Agent sessions run trusted development tests, changed gates, typecheck/lint,
   945	and builds locally by default, broadening only when the touched contract
   946	requires it. Never execute untrusted repository tooling locally. Use Crabbox
   947	when the environment is part of the proof: clean-machine, install/package,
   948	Docker, E2E, live, desktop, or cross-platform work, or when the operator
   949	explicitly requests remote proof. Do not use Crabbox merely as generic compute
   950	offload. The configured Testbox workflow hydrates credentials, so untrusted
   951	contributor or fork code must use secretless fork CI or sanitized direct AWS
   952	Crabbox instead.
   953	
   954	Do not pre-warm for anticipated work. Acquire the backend lazily when the
   955	first environment-sensitive command is ready, reuse the returned `tbx_...` id
   956	for later remote commands, sync the current checkout on every run, and stop it
   957	before handoff.
   958	
   959	After the first successful reuse, the wrapper records the lease's base,
   960	dependency, and Testbox workflow fingerprint under `.crabbox/testbox-leases/`.
   961	Source-only edits keep reusing the warmed box. A changed merge base, lockfile,
   962	package-manager input, wrapper, or Testbox workflow fails closed and requires a
   963	fresh lease. Every run still syncs the current checkout.
   964	`OPENCLAW_TESTBOX_ALLOW_STALE=1` is only for intentional diagnostics, not
   965	release proof.
   966	
   967	The Testbox workflow registers a separate disposable checkout for native sync.
   968	The hydrated execution workspace stays at its original absolute path, so native
   969	Git cleanup and rsync cannot delete dependencies, build output, or ignored runtime
   970	there. The wrapper applies and verifies the source bundle in that execution
   971	workspace, runs its frozen dependency install, and checks the source and Git
   972	identity again before the payload. Install output goes to stderr; an install or
   973	verification failure stops the payload. It never restores runtime from the caller
   974	or changes the selected rsync binary.
   975	
   976	Workspace preparation changes require a fresh lease. A missing or overlapping
   977	execution-workspace binding stops the payload; stop that lease and warm a new one.
   978	Use the OpenClaw wrapper for proof: direct native Blacksmith commands target the
   979	transport checkout, which deliberately has no hydrated runtime.
   980	
   981	Testbox requests with `--artifact-glob` or `--require-artifact` collect from the
   982	prepared execution workspace. Collection stays anchored there across payload
   983	directory changes and normal failure exits; existing cancellation and
   984	signal-related artifact withholding remains unchanged.
   985	
   986	The wrapper uses the bundled Crabbox plugin to update missing or outdated CLI
   987	binaries before provider discovery, source sync, or lease work. Every command uses
   988	the current supported CLI contract; there are no separate version requirements
   989	for artifacts, providers, or `sync-plan --json`.
   990	
   991	Testbox runs and POSIX remote changed gates freeze source into a Git bundle
   992	against the pinned base.
   993	Selection uses Crabbox's sync policy and Git's repository, info, and effective global
   994	exclusions, including repo-local overrides, for untracked files. Tracked ignored
   995	files and staged ignored additions remain source; an explicit privacy exclusion
   996	conflicting with required tracked source stops the run before upload.
   997	
   998	The command binds the bundle digest and raw source tree. Before running the payload,
   999	the receiver applies deletions and restores file bytes, symlink target bytes, and
  1000	Git executable modes, then verifies the filesystem directly. Git text filters do not
  1001	normalize this snapshot. Missing, stale, or mismatched bundles fail closed.
  1002	Producer-declared deletions must also be absent, even when the remote index has lost
  1003	them. Deletions use the same privacy policy as source selection; unknown ignored
  1004	runtime data is preserved. Unexpected nonignored receiver files stop the run instead
  1005	of being deleted. The verification receipt reports the original source revision
  1006	separately from the synthetic transport commit; remote `HEAD` identifies that
  1007	verified transport tree, and changed gates compare it with the pinned base.
  1008	Raw-byte differences can conservatively select additional changed paths. Git path
  1009	names must be UTF-8; symlink targets remain raw bytes. Symlinked repository Crabbox
  1010	configuration or ignore files, and privacy-excluded runtime configuration, are
  1011	rejected before upload rather than changing their trust or privacy treatment.
  1012	
  1013	The [local test commands](/reference/test/local) are the normal trusted development path. Keep proof
  1014	proportional to the touched contract.
  1015	
  1016	For untrusted proof, lazily warm with `--provider aws`. Every run must set
  1017	`CRABBOX_ENV_ALLOW=CI`, pass `--provider aws --no-hydrate`, and use
  1018	a fresh temporary remote `HOME` before installing dependencies or running
  1019	tests. Use a newly warmed lease dedicated to that untrusted source; never reuse
  1020	a trusted or previously hydrated lease. Launch an installed trusted Crabbox
  1021	binary from a clean trusted `main` checkout and fetch only the remote PR with
  1022	`--fresh-pr`; never execute the untrusted checkout's wrapper or config locally.
  1023	Unset `CRABBOX_AWS_INSTANCE_PROFILE` and fail closed unless resolved
  1024	`aws.instanceProfile` is empty. Before any install/test, use trusted
  1025	absolute-path tools to require an IMDSv2 token, prove the IAM credentials
  1026	endpoint returns 404, and verify remote `git rev-parse HEAD` equals the full
  1027	reviewed PR head SHA. Bind the lease to that SHA and stop/rewarm when the head
  1028	changes. Upload trusted `scripts/crabbox-untrusted-bootstrap.sh` from clean
  1029	`main` alongside `--fresh-pr`; it installs pinned Node/pnpm, verifies the SHA
  1030	and package-manager pin, isolates `HOME`, installs dependencies, then executes
  1031	the requested test. If the broker cannot prove no role or no remote PR exists,
  1032	use secretless fork CI. Do not use `hydrate-github`, `--no-sync`, or a
  1033	credential-hydrated Testbox workflow.
  1034	Unset all `CRABBOX_TAILSCALE*` overrides, force `--network public
  1035	--tailscale=false`, clear exit-node/LAN flags, and require `crabbox inspect` to
  1036	report public networking with no Tailscale state before uploading any script.
  1037	
  1038	## Crabbox repository setup
  1039	
  1040	The shared [Crabbox skill](https://github.com/openclaw/agent-skills/tree/main/skills/crabbox)
  1041	owns portable lease, trust, sync, and cleanup procedures. This section owns the
  1042	OpenClaw wrapper and workflow inputs. Routine task-needed Crabbox/Testbox use
  1043	and task-owned worktrees do not require another confirmation; preserve unrelated
  1044	work and existing credential, production, budget, and publication boundaries.
  1045	
  1046	Run trusted OpenClaw remote proof through the wrapper from the repository root:
  1047	
  1048	```bash
  1049	node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs run --help
  1050	```
  1051	
  1052	Read `.crabbox.yaml` and the resolved provider before running. The repository
  1053	default is `blacksmith-testbox`, with `.github/workflows/ci-check-testbox.yml`
  1054	owning its prepared environment. Direct providers use
  1055	`.github/workflows/crabbox-hydrate.yml`. Keep the resolved provider unless the
  1056	requested proof requires another environment; capacity or hydration failure
  1057	does not make a different provider equivalent.
  1058	
  1059	The direct `.github/workflows/windows-blacksmith-testbox.yml` workflow runs
  1060	native Windows. The wrapper's Blacksmith adapter supports Linux only; explicit
  1061	`--provider blacksmith-testbox` prevents automatic Azure routing but does not
  1062	enable Windows support. As checked on 2026-09-01, Blacksmith CLI 0.4.57 targets `runner`
  1063	and has no native username override, so supported CLI sync/run on this Windows
  1064	image remains blocked. Recheck against a newer CLI before relying on this. Native SSH inspection with the per-Testbox key is not CLI end-to-end
  1065	proof.
  1066	
  1067	The wrapper checks an executable sibling `../crabbox/bin/crabbox`, then `PATH`,
  1068	then the sibling of the Git common checkout. Verify the selected binary and
  1069	its source rather than trusting a directory name. The plugin installs a verified
  1070	managed copy when the selected executable is missing or outdated. For an explicit
  1071	[Crabbox](https://github.com/openclaw/crabbox) source repair, build `./cmd/crabbox`
  1072	in a clean task-owned checkout and leave the operator's installation untouched. The existing
  1073	`OPENCLAW_CRABBOX_WRAPPER_IGNORE_REPO_BINARY=1` setting skips the first sibling
  1074	candidate; a task binary on `PATH` then takes precedence over the common-checkout
  1075	candidate. A dirty or occupied sibling is not a reason to stop and ask.
  1076	
  1077	Mantis uses the same plugin-owned discovery and managed installation. Relative
  1078	executable overrides and `PATH` entries resolve from its requested `--repo-root`,
  1079	and version probes run there with the same environment as lease commands. Its workflows
  1080	prepare the executable with `node scripts/crabbox-setup.mjs`; the command prints
  1081	the selected binary and verified version as JSON and, in GitHub Actions, adds its
  1082	directory to `GITHUB_PATH`. Later QA and media commands reuse that executable.
  1083	
  1084	For a selected trusted Testbox lane:
  1085	
  1086	```bash
  1087	node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs run --timing-json -- \
  1088	  CI=1 NODE_OPTIONS=--max-old-space-size=4096 \
  1089	  OPENCLAW_TEST_PROJECTS_PARALLEL=6 OPENCLAW_VITEST_MAX_WORKERS=1 \
  1090	  OPENCLAW_TESTBOX=1 OPENCLAW_TESTBOX_REMOTE_RUN=1 \
  1091	  pnpm test <path-or-filter>
  1092	```
  1093	
  1094	For several commands, warm once with
  1095	`node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs warmup --keep --timing-json`, save the returned
  1096	lease ID, and reuse it with `run --id <tbx_id>`. Stop the owned lease with
  1097	`node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs stop --id <tbx_id>`; stop has no `--timing-json`.
  1098	
  1099	- Warm from the task checkout. Claims belong to checkout paths; `--reclaim`
  1100	  deliberately transfers that ownership and never changes repository identity.
  1101	  Sparse staging uses the wrapper's ownership path. Do not sync or reclaim
  1102	  while another command owns the lease.
  1103	- Wrapper reuse requires the local SSH key created by Crabbox. A missing key
  1104	  requires a fresh warmup. Leases created directly by Blacksmith remain usable
  1105	  through `blacksmith testbox run --id <tbx_id>`, not Crabbox wrapper reuse.
  1106	- Every native Testbox run syncs again, including reused leases. `--no-sync`
  1107	  cannot preserve a remote baseline. Compare revisions in separate remote
  1108	  worktrees within one synced command; never switch refs in the synced root.
  1109	- Compound remote shell commands use `bash -lc`, not `sh -lc`; hydration can
  1110	  depend on Bash declarations. Testbox's workflow owns Chromium, so do not pass
  1111	  Crabbox `--browser` to that provider.
  1112	- Keep the lease fingerprint checks described above. No stale-lease override
  1113	  for release proof. Direct-provider flags such as `--fresh-pr`, `--full-resync`,
  1114	  `--script*`, `--env-helper`, capture/download flags, and `--stop-after` are not
  1115	  a substitute for the delegated Testbox workflow.
  1116	- For Testbox scripts, run a synced file as trailing command arguments or use
  1117	  `--shell`. Active `--script` and `--script-stdin` uploads are rejected before
  1118	  source preparation or lease work.
  1119	
  1120	When remote sync uses a temporary checkout, the wrapper preserves native
  1121	`.crabbox/runs` and `.crabbox/captures` outputs together beneath a fresh
  1122	`.crabbox/wrapper-artifacts/run-*` directory before removing that checkout.
  1123	Repeated runs retain separate evidence even when native filenames match. The
  1124	wrapper prints the old-to-new root mapping; native logs and generated proof may
  1125	still reference the old paths. A preservation error fails the wrapper and retains
  1126	the temporary checkout at the reported path for manual recovery, preserving the
  1127	child's nonzero exit code. The wrapper rejects symlinks in artifact trees
  1128	and destination parents, and copies only regular files and real directories.
  1129	Retained files use mode `0600` and new directories use `0700` on POSIX systems.
  1130	If preservation fails, recover the outputs from the reported checkout before
  1131	removing it. Recognized unchanged partial copies are removed; a partial destination
  1132	that cannot be verified is retained and reported with the original outputs.
  1133	
  1134	On normal completion or a supported POSIX interrupt, the wrapper settles its
  1135	child process tree and output streams before restoring retained lease ownership, preserving
  1136	artifacts, and removing disposable source. Allow the wrapper to finish after
  1137	Ctrl-C; additional signals reuse that shutdown and its bounded escalation.
  1138	A package-manager proxy can return its interruption status before the wrapper
  1139	finishes. That status or remote lease completion alone does not establish that
  1140	local cleanup finished.
  1141	
  1142	Cleanup errors are reported and make an otherwise successful invocation fail;
  1143	an existing nonzero command or cancellation status is preserved. If child
  1144	termination cannot be verified, the wrapper retains its local inputs and reports
  1145	that recovery is needed. A failed retained-lease ownership restore also retains
  1146	the checkout; restore that lease to the original repository or stop it before
  1147	removing the checkout. Stop any remaining owned processes before recovering
  1148	artifacts or removing their temporary inputs.
  1149	
  1150	The wrapper records future temporary checkouts only when staging is provably
  1151	outside the source repository, using private recovery metadata beside the payload.
  1152	The default `~/.cache/openclaw/crabbox-sync` location normally meets this condition.
  1153	Configured repo-local roots, including ignored directories such as `.artifacts/`,
  1154	continue to work through ordinary unmarked staging and cleanup. They receive no
  1155	recovery receipts or manifests and remain protected after abrupt loss. Uncertain
  1156	placement also uses that compatible unmarked path.
  1157	
  1158	Changing the configured root or ignore rules does not create new recovery metadata
  1159	inside the repository. Existing copies at another root are not moved or adopted;
  1160	select that root to inspect them. This feature does not filter existing user data,
  1161	broader source scopes, or explicit raw workspace mounts. Inspect recorded copies with:
  1162	
  1163	```bash
  1164	node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs staging inspect
  1165	node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs staging recover <id>
  1166	```
  1167	
  1168	These local commands use the existing Crabbox binary without installing it,
  1169	starting a provider, changing claims, or stopping a lease. Recovery checks the
  1170	original native claims namespace, verifies a recorded snapshot against an
  1171	independently retained Git ref, and verifies saved diagnostics before deleting
  1172	unchanged staging. A claim that still names staging requires an operator to stop
  1173	the exact lease or explicitly reclaim it from the real repository, then retry.
  1174	Changing `HOME` or `XDG_STATE_HOME` cannot establish absence in the old namespace.
  1175	
  1176	A prepared capsule with no admitted consumer can be recovered after its owner
  1177	disappears. An admitted consumer requires a persisted writer-settlement receipt.
  1178	Git configurations that may launch preparation helpers, or additional local Git
  1179	seed preparation, remain held because parent-command completion does not certify
  1180	those helpers. Their ordinary execution is unchanged. Earlier experimental
  1181	receipt versions also remain protected.
  1182	Explicit recovery can retry pending diagnostic preservation after a destination
  1183	problem is repaired. Automatic recovery leaves artifact or claim failures held.
  1184	Missing or changed saved outputs prevent removal; recovery never recreates a
  1185	vanished original repository to invent a preservation destination.
  1186	
  1187	Dirty source requires another complete retained copy. After deliberately retaining
  1188	that snapshot in an independent Git repository and named ref, select it explicitly:
  1189	
  1190	```bash
  1191	node scripts/crabbox-wrapper.mjs staging recover <id> \
  1192	  --witness-repo /path/to/retained-repository --witness-ref refs/heads/saved-source
  1193	```
  1194	
  1195	Recovery does not create backup repositories, archives, or permanent refs. A stage's
  1196	own Git objects or bundle do not count as another copy. Live or uncertain owners,
  1197	unrecorded writer settlement, interrupted recovery ownership, substituted metadata,
  1198	other boot/process namespaces, and historical unmarked directories remain protected.
  1199	Recovery is limited to the same boot and a known PID namespace; even a reboot of
  1200	the same computer leaves earlier copies protected. Full worktrees also remain
  1201	protected because hooks, filters, and raw source require separate proof. Their
  1202	ordinary cleanup retains the remaining staging if exact Git registration removal
  1203	fails, including its receipt when registration was eligible. Repo-local copies
  1204	remain unmarked. There is no global worktree prune or force-recovery option.
  1205	
  1206	Source-transfer commands inspect at most 64 bounded headers with a 250-ms soft
  1207	discovery budget. This scan does not hash payloads, search Git history, or query a
  1208	provider. A temporary cursor advances subsequent scans past protected entries;
  1209	`staging inspect --after <nextCursor>` also pages the local report. After successful
  1210	normal completion the wrapper attempts at most one discovered candidate. Help,
  1211	listing, version output, and cancellation do not start old-stage recovery, and a
  1212	recovery failure does not change the completed command's result. Inspection reports
  1213	incomplete scans and elapsed time; bulk verification is separately bounded and can
  1214	take longer. Unsupported filesystem durability, including native Windows directory
  1215	flushes, prevents orphan recovery while preserving ordinary operation. Normal
  1216	cleanup still removes its own successful or dirty-source staging without requiring
  1217	an independent recovery witness.
  1218	
  1219	These are local artifacts, not published or fully sanitized proof. Blacksmith's
  1220	native failure bundle contains captured stdout/stderr and diagnostic metadata;
  1221	it does not automatically include remote UI screenshots or reports. Retrieve
  1222	those separately before stopping the owned Testbox, and inspect all artifacts
  1223	for secrets and private data before sharing.
  1224	
  1225	The native Windows Testbox idle monitor uses the running `sshd` service's local
  1226	listener ports, not Blacksmith's externally forwarded SSH port. Established SSH
  1227	connections keep the job alive; the `~/.testbox-last-activity` modification time
  1228	covers short commands between the 30-second polls. Once neither indicates recent
  1229	activity, the configured idle timeout still ends the job.
  1230	
  1231	The shared skill's command placeholders map to the focused commands in this
  1232	guide. Its trusted bootstrap is `scripts/crabbox-untrusted-bootstrap.sh`; the
  1233	untrusted path above invokes the installed trusted CLI, never the PR's wrapper.
  1234	For an explicitly selected local-container lane, the existing example image is
  1235	`node:24-bookworm` and the install command is
  1236	`corepack pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --store-dir .pnpm-store`, followed by
  1237	the chosen test. Keep `--no-hydrate` and a repository-local dependency store
  1238	when host caches cannot cross filesystems. The OpenClaw broker login endpoint
  1239	is `https://crabbox.openclaw.ai`; normal brokered validation does not require
  1240	asking for AWS keys.
  1241	
  1242	Live Gateway, channel, and agent-turn proof uses an isolated
  1243	`OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR`, a free port, and the real user path. Test-only plugin
  1244	artifacts may use `OPENCLAW_ALLOW_PLUGIN_INSTALL_OVERRIDES=1`; that does not make
  1245	them official installs. Before sharing WebVNC, inspect a screenshot of the
  1246	working app. Keep proof media out of the product repository and compare source
  1247	hashes before and after generator runs. If a final timing result is written but
