     7	---
     8	
     9	## Shared test state and process helpers
    10	
    11	TypeScript tooling uses the native `typescript` package. The `pnpm tsgo` lanes
    12	resolve its executable directly; syntax and semantic tools use its unstable API
    13	with explicit process lifetimes. The package root exports version metadata,
    14	while AST, filesystem, and compiler APIs live under `typescript/unstable/*`.
    15	Packaged declaration builds run the same native compiler with semantic checking
    16	and validate its complete source and package-manifest receipts before bundling.
    17	The tsdown wrapper defaults declaration builds to one configuration at a time,
    18	bounding native compiler processes alongside the existing Node heap budget.
    19	Runtime-only builds keep their parallelism, and an explicit `--concurrency` value
    20	retains tsdown's own behavior.
    21	Code Mode executes JavaScript directly and does not use this compiler;
    22	its TypeScript-style tool declarations are model-facing documentation.
    23	
    24	`build-all`, standalone tsdown builds, tsgo, SDK declaration preparation,
    25	package-boundary checks, and dependent lint use checkout-local ownership at
    26	`.artifacts/dist-artifacts.lock`. Ownership spans
    27	cleanup, generation, cache restoration, and the checks consuming those outputs;
    28	independent checkouts remain independent. Competing commands print a waiting
    29	message and wait for a live owner without an acquisition deadline. Compiler and
    30	build execution timeouts are unchanged. Standalone tsgo runs serialize, including
    31	source-only checks; the core test shard runner retains its explicit concurrency
    32	inside one owner. Do not delete `dist` manually while these commands are running.
    33	An abrupt owner or nested wrapper exit, or unverified child cleanup, retains the
    34	lock. A missing or unverifiable owner PID, or a recorded child-cleanup failure,
    35	fails acquisition promptly without reclaiming anything. PID death does not prove
    36	detached descendants stopped. Before manually removing an abandoned lock directory,
    37	inspect its `owner.json` and verify all associated build, compiler, and lint
    38	processes, including detached descendants, have stopped; then retry the command.
    39	
    40	Lint reports its final failure on stderr after child joins and artifact ownership
    41	have settled, including retained ownership when cleanup is uncertain. Standalone
    42	Oxlint and its shard CLI end with `[oxlint] FAILED (exit N)`; `pnpm lint` owns the
    43	whole pipeline and ends with one `[lint] FAILED (exit N)` instead. Shard progress
    44	distinguishes `passed` from `failed (exit N)`, and stdout remains available for
    45	machine-readable tool output. Successful runs have no failure trailer. Signals
    46	forwarded during child execution and shard timeouts fail the command; whole-host
    47	loss or `SIGKILL` of the reporting process can prevent a final line.
    48	
    49	Local plugin lint consumes native SDK declarations in `packages/plugin-sdk/dist`.
    50	The dedicated package-boundary compiler also consumes seven plugin API trees in
    51	`.artifacts/extension-package-boundary/plugins`. Each declaration and compile
    52	owner validates its consumed source content, inherited config, selected compiler,
    53	and complete output inventory. Unrelated existing source or test edits retain
    54	cache hits. Resolution-topology changes invalidate conservatively, including new
    55	module candidates outside declared roots. Stale declarations get a full native
    56	emit after clearing only their build-info file; the successful emitted inventory
    57	then drives obsolete declaration pruning. Missing or tampered outputs invalidate
    58	the owner. The content records live under
    59	`.artifacts/extension-package-boundary`, outside packaged build cleanup. A warm run validates the records without emitting declarations.
    60	
    61	Packaged declaration builds and package-boundary records accept only checkout-owned input
    62	realpaths, including compiler libraries, inherited config, dependency links, and
    63	package manifests. Local pnpm links remain supported when their targets stay
    64	inside the checkout. The tsgo wrapper does not create or reuse a shared external
    65	install; invocations from subdirectories still use the containing checkout as
    66	the ownership boundary. Declared checkout junctions and platform path aliases map
    67	to the same native root for validation and actual snapshot reads. Local declaration
    68	preparation also aligns the compiler's `PWD` with its working directory so shell
    69	aliases do not change emitted inventory paths. Native resolution itself is not
    70	sandboxed: an ancestor install can still enter a successful compiler
    71	receipt. Resolution can read an ancestor's candidate `package.json` while searching
    72	for declarations, then resolve the import to checkout-local JavaScript. This can
    73	happen with a complete local frozen install and no external source files in the
    74	compiler Program; it does not by itself prove an undeclared dependency. Those
    75	manifests still affect resolution and must remain in the receipt. The owner fails
    76	with `Declaration input escapes checkout`, without publishing a success record or
    77	pruning obsolete declarations. Warm records use the same input check.
    78	
    79	Repair this at checkout provisioning: use a separate physical checkout whose
    80	ancestor directories do not contain `node_modules`, with the same candidate source
    81	(including any uncommitted changes) and its own `pnpm install --frozen-lockfile`.
    82	Run declaration preparation and dependent lint or package-boundary checks in that
    83	checkout, so the checks consume its freshly sealed receipts. A symlink to the
    84	nested checkout, a repeated install there, or `nodeLinker: isolated` does not bound
    85	native ancestor lookup. Do not alter the ancestor installation, add incidental
    86	dependencies, filter compiler receipts, or transplant declarations to bypass the
    87	checks. The pinned native compiler's filesystem callback API supports analysis,
    88	not declaration and build-info emission; native validation does not automatically
    89	create an isolated checkout.
    90	
    91	Packaged SDK declarations belong to one staged owner shared by full, package, and
    92	`ciArtifacts` builds. It serializes the two canonical tsdown SDK groups on a miss
    93	and caches their complete staged generation. Each successful compiler supplies its
    94	source and package-manifest membership through a private staged receipt; missing receipts or inputs
    95	changed during compilation prevent publication. The shared input snapshot policy
    96	validates consumed bytes, inherited configuration, generator and manifest inputs,
    97	and resolution topology without starting a compiler on hits. Cache hits restore
    98	into fresh staging and pass the same entry and relative declaration closure checks
    99	before publication.
   100	All tsdown declaration builds (the eight SDK/unified groups, workspace packages,
   101	and the AI package) use the same admission policy: native resolution may read an
   102	ancestor install, but outside input paths prevent accepting compiler output,
   103	publishing declarations, or sealing a success record. Successful builds need
   104	checkout-owned compiler libraries and declaration dependencies. In-checkout pnpm
   105	links remain supported; shared external installs are not. Compiler receipts retain
   106	complete source membership. The emitting process also
   107	records lexical resolution paths before symlink canonicalization, so an ancestor
   108	package alias pointing back inside the checkout still fails admission. Malformed
   109	resolution traces fail closed.
   110	
   111	Default type roots stay within the checkout, and a valid root `package.json` stops
   112	source-package scope lookup. Explicit type roots must be local. For metadata
   113	lookups that native emission does not trace, each emitted source package must
   114	resolve its runtime dependencies to local package directories before ancestor
   115	search. Native emission also considers JSON program inputs during this lookup,
   116	so their package dependencies receive the same check. A missing local dependency with an ancestor installation, or an actual
   117	failed type lookup there, requires local declarations or a separate physical
   118	checkout. Complete nested checkouts can still build when their local inputs
   119	satisfy every lookup. Ancestor-install appearance and removal invalidate cached
   120	records; source and namespace changes during compilation prevent acceptance.
   121	Missing-file probes outside both the checkout and its absent ancestor install
   122	roots also fail admission, because their later appearance is not owned by the
   123	checkout cache.
   124	Each emitted declaration must have one source-map owner in the successful compiler
   125	membership. The bundler consumes those declarations under their original source
   126	paths; private compiler stages are removed only after their child settles.
   127	Compiler configuration, native executable and API files, input bytes, and
   128	resolution topology participate in cache invalidation. Runtime module resolution
   129	is unchanged.
   130	
   131	Local preparation never overwrites packaged declarations or writes workspace
   132	forwarding bridges.
   133	
   134	Plugin SDK declaration preparation and `scripts/run-tsgo.mjs` require child work
   135	to finish before reporting success. On POSIX, each verifies its own managed
   136	process group: leftover children are terminated and the command fails instead of
   137	allowing artifact stamps or downstream checks to proceed. POSIX process groups do
   138	not detect descendants that deliberately leave the group.
   139	
   140	On Windows, managed commands and the Gateway test instance use a retained kernel
   141	Job. Platform code loads only when a Windows command is requested; planner imports
   142	stay independent of installed application packages. Loading finishes before spawn,
   143	so listener registration remains synchronous with child creation. Tooling resolves
   144	its worker URL with Node built-ins and reuses the native Job bindings from core.
   145	The launcher joins that Job before it can start the command. Cleanup waits
   146	for an empty Job, leader exit, and output closure; leader exit alone never proves
   147	descendant completion. Failed termination reports the observed surviving PIDs and
   148	retains resource claims while the Job remains unresolved. Normal leader exit also
   149	terminates remaining Job members. Finalization records its outcome before closing
   150	the Job handle, including on failure; closing the handle alone does not verify
   151	termination or release resource claims. Existing callers with
   152	their own IPC channel keep the direct-launch contract; failed taskkill without an
   153	owned Job stays indeterminate even if the leader and its pipes have closed.
   154	
   155	`run-vitest` (including project shards), plugin batches, `test-live`
   156	(including live shards), `run-vitest-profile`, and the TUI PTY watcher give each
   157	Vitest invocation an owned temporary namespace through `TMPDIR`, `TMP`, and `TEMP`.
   158	Before Vitest starts, isolated invocations also receive native `HOME` and
   159	`USERPROFILE` inside that namespace. This protects home fallbacks used by worker
   160	threads, named builtin imports, and import-time captures; changing only a worker's
   161	JavaScript `process.env` does not change native thread home lookup. Per-worker and
   162	per-test fixture homes remain separate. Installed Corepack and Playwright browser
   163	caches retain their caller-selected locations.
   164	
   165	Gateway port claims remain in the common temporary directory outside all enclosing
   166	Vitest namespaces, found through their explicit resource owners. Parallel invocations
   167	therefore share port ownership while a fixture hands its reserved socket to a child;
   168	removing one invocation's files cannot remove another fixture's port claim.
   169	
   170	Live-aware setup still loads the original profile and stages live state when
   171	requested. A bounded invocation artifact carries the original home to that setup;
   172	it does not grant live access, and hermetic setup never consults it. Known
   173	hermetic selections ignore ambient live and real-home flags. Known wholly
   174	live-aware selections retain explicit `OPENCLAW_LIVE_USE_REAL_HOME` behavior.
   175	An explicitly real-home live invocation is refused before config loading if its
   176	selection mixes home policies or cannot be classified, including custom configs
   177	and ambiguous project selectors. Run hermetic tests without `LIVE`,
   178	`OPENCLAW_LIVE_TEST`, `OPENCLAW_LIVE_GATEWAY`, and `OPENCLAW_LIVE_USE_REAL_HOME`
   179	using `node scripts/run-vitest.mjs <test-path>`, then run the intended live
   180	selection separately using `node scripts/test-live.mts -- <live-test-path>`.
   181	The launcher does not split runs or change watch, filter, or report semantics.
   182	
   183	The namespace contains isolated homes, their JIT caches, SDK/shared-home allocation
   184	roots, and fallback SQLite state; its lifetime spans shared-worker files and module
   185	resets. On POSIX detached launches, the parent removes
   186	only that namespace after its child process group has stopped, output pipes
   187	have closed, and nested resource owners have released their pending claims,
   188	including passing and failing runs, child crashes, caught `SIGINT`/`SIGTERM`
   189	signals, and watchdog termination where supported. Explicit state, profile output,
   190	and mirror artifacts outside the namespace remain untouched. Failed or unverified
   191	group joins or unresolved nested claims retain the namespace and report the exact
   192	path for manual recovery. Nested namespaces, fixture lifetimes, and managed commands
   193	register ephemeral filesystem ownership before admitting work. Release requires
   194	positive completion evidence; caught cleanup failures, module resets, worker exit,
   195	or an intermediate runner crash cannot release a pending claim or its ancestors.
   196	Managed commands keep their existing output-drain contract; Windows Job commands
   197	also join kernel Job completion. Failed finalization never releases ownership.
   198	Stop all remaining writers before manually removing the reported exact directory.
   199	Windows and non-detached launches allocate the same isolated native home, but retain
   200	their namespace and enclosing claims with a diagnostic after child exit and pipe
   201	closure because descendant completion cannot be verified. Raw external invocations do not gain
   202	this boundary. Forced parent or supervisor death (such as `SIGKILL`) can prevent
   203	cleanup; unregistered descendants that intentionally escape the owned group remain
   204	outside this contract. The wrappers do
   205	not sweep old directories or infer ownership from names, ages, or PIDs.
   206	This is home isolation, not a filesystem sandbox: explicit absolute paths,
   207	`os.userInfo()` account lookup, children with stripped or replaced home variables,
   208	and intentionally real-home live execution remain outside its protection.
   209	
   210	Codex app-server fixtures await agent and shared-state SQLite drainage between
   211	cases. Their file teardown drains the shared disk-budget scan worker, preserving
   212	reuse during the file and releasing it before isolated fork shutdown.
   213	
   214	- `src/test-utils/openclaw-test-state.ts`: use from Vitest when a test needs an isolated `HOME`, `OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR`, `OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH`, config fixture, workspace, agent dir, or auth-profile store.
   215	- `pnpm test:env-mutations:report`: non-blocking report of tests/harnesses that mutate `HOME`, `OPENCLAW_STATE_DIR`, `OPENCLAW_CONFIG_PATH`, `OPENCLAW_WORKSPACE_DIR`, or related env keys directly. Use it to find migration candidates for the shared test-state helper.
   216	- `test/helpers/openclaw-test-instance.ts`: process-level E2E tests needing a running Gateway, CLI env, log capture, and cleanup in one place.
   217	- Docker/Bash E2E lanes that source `scripts/lib/docker-e2e-image.sh` can pass `docker_e2e_test_state_shell_b64 <label> <scenario>` into the container and decode it with `scripts/lib/openclaw-e2e-instance.sh`; multi-home scripts can pass `docker_e2e_test_state_function_b64` and call `openclaw_test_state_create <label> <scenario>` in each flow. `node --import tsx scripts/lib/openclaw-test-state.mts -- create --label <name> --scenario <name> --env-file <path> --json` writes a sourceable host env file (the `--` before `create` keeps newer Node runtimes from treating `--env-file` as a Node flag). Lanes that launch a Gateway can source `scripts/lib/openclaw-e2e-instance.sh` for entrypoint resolution, mock OpenAI startup, foreground/background launch, readiness probes, state env export, log dumps, and process cleanup.
   218	
   219	`createOpenClawTestState` selects and owns temporary paths and process environment
   220	selectors. It is not filesystem sandboxing and does not stop external producers.
   221	Await its asynchronous `restoreEnv()`; stop and join required producers before
   222	restoring selectors or removing state. Runtime reproductions of state-selection
   223	leaks require enforced storage isolation, such as a VM or container without access
   224	to operator stores, not merely temporary `HOME` or state-directory overrides.
   225	
   226	## Public test diagnostics
   227	
   228	The shared Vitest reporter factory redacts credential-shaped fields in assertion
   229	messages, diffs, expected/received values, stacks, source excerpts, and annotations
   230	before forwarding them to the selected reporters. Keys remain visible and values
   231	become `<redacted len=N>`. This also applies to explicit `--reporter` selections,
   232	UI/browser configurations, and JSON/JUnit reports. Hosted logs are public, and
   233	runner-issued tokens may not be registered for GitHub masking.
   234	
   235	Unquoted environment records use one assignment per line: spaces and punctuation
   236	on the right-hand side belong to that value. Multiline strings split into quoted
   237	fragments by Node's inspector are redacted as one value.
   238	
   239	Redaction is unconditional and affects diagnostic output, not assertion behavior.
   240	Test console capture is outside this boundary; tests must still avoid logging
   241	credentials directly.
   242	
   243	Configured extension fork projects use the `openclaw-forks` diagnostic adapter
   244	around Vitest's native fork transport. If the existing stop deadline fails while
   245	the child remains alive, the adapter spends at most two additional seconds
   246	collecting a Node report before native termination and pipe cleanup. The timeout
   247	remains a test failure. The report distinguishes a missing stop acknowledgement
   248	from a stall after acknowledgement and includes native stacks, libuv handles, and
   249	worker-thread reports. Environment variables, command arguments, and socket
   250	endpoints are omitted. A blocked event loop can prevent signal reporting; that
   251	case explicitly reports that no complete report was captured.
   252	
   253	Successful shutdown remains quiet. An explicit `--pool=forks` selects Vitest's
   254	built-in pool and bypasses this adapter.
   255	
   256	## JSON reports across native processes
   257	
   258	For a multi-project or chunked run, explicitly request native JSON with an output
   259	file, for example:
   260	
   261	```bash
   262	pnpm test test/vitest/vitest.unit-fast-isolated.config.ts test/vitest/vitest.agents-embedded-agent.config.ts --reporter=verbose --reporter=json --outputFile=.artifacts/test-results.json
   263	```
   264	
   265	The project runner and plugin batch runner give each attempt separate native JSON
   266	and blob files, then publish the requested JSON from Vitest's native report merge.
   267	They print a companion `<output>.reports-<unique>` directory. Keep that directory:
   268	it contains original reports, per-attempt coverage files when coverage is enabled,
   269	and an `index.json` with child exit codes, signals, timeouts and unstarted work.
   270	Each invocation runs once. A no-output timeout fails the command and leaves the
   271	report set incomplete; it never starts a replacement attempt.
   272	Blob reports are exact-version artifacts. Rerun child reports with the current
   273	Vitest version before merging artifacts produced by another version.
   274	
   275	The aggregate preserves the accepted case inventory, but is not a lossless
   276	replacement for the originals. Native merging does not restore snapshot summaries
   277	or JSON `coverageMap`, and its `startTime` is the merge time. Passing snapshot tests
   278	still succeed. Read native originals for those details and the index for process
   279	outcomes: JSON `success` does not encode every wrapper or unhandled-error failure.
   280	Separate built-in coverage reports remain per attempt in the companion directory.
   281	Custom coverage providers/reporters and coverage reporter tuple options require
   282	separate invocations with unique destinations.
   283	
   284	A complete failed-test aggregate is retained with a failing command exit. Missing
   285	or invalid evidence, cancellation, unstarted required work, or publication failure
   286	does not publish a complete aggregate; an existing output file is not proof of the
   287	new run. The diagnostic prints the retained report-set location. Report sets are
   288	not automatically swept.
   289	
   290	Overlapping selections can share native task IDs, so merging them can replace
   291	independent failure details even when case counts match. Such report sets retain
   292	their originals and fail publication. Select each configuration once, or run
   293	overlapping selections separately with distinct output files.
   294	
   295	This ownership applies to explicit CLI JSON file requests with named, file-based
   296	Node projects and native console reporters. Scalar `--outputFile` and
   297	`--outputFile.json` both work. Config-owned reporter options, other file formats,
   298	custom reporters and inline/browser project composition require separate native
   299	invocations with unique output destinations. Do not assume those outputs are
   300	aggregated. Single-process and console-only runs keep their existing native behavior.
   301	Native help and other non-test controls stay with the child CLI and do not allocate
   302	report sets. `run --version` still runs tests, as it does in native Vitest.
   303	Config-only reporters are not intercepted: multiple children can still overwrite
   304	the same configured file. Run those configurations separately with distinct paths;
   305	adding `--reporter=json` alone does not override a reporter tuple's own `outputFile`.
   306	---
