     1	---
     2	summary: "CLI reference for activity records, execution identity, and decision receipts"
     3	read_when:
     4	  - You need to answer who ran an agent or tool, when it ran, and how it ended
     5	  - You need content-free inbound or outbound message lifecycle metadata
     6	  - You need a bounded, redaction-safe activity export
     7	title: "Audit records"
     8	---
     9	
    10	# `openclaw audit`
    11	
    12	Query the Gateway's metadata-only activity ledger, discover executions that
    13	share a run correlation, or inspect immutable identity context for one exact
    14	agent execution.
    15	
    16	Run and tool activity records are on by default. Execution identity is
    17	separately off by default on fresh installs and upgrades. Enable it explicitly:
    18	
    19	```bash
    20	openclaw config set logging.audit.executionIdentity true
    21	openclaw gateway restart
    22	```
    23	
    24	Identity collection requires `logging.audit.enabled` to remain enabled.
    25	Message records are also separately disabled by default; set
    26	`logging.audit.messages` to `direct` or `all` and restart the Gateway to
    27	record them. Existing records stay queryable until they expire (30 days).
    28	
    29	Direct local commands use the same bounded writer lifecycle as the Gateway.
    30	`openclaw agent exec` deletes its temporary state directory by default, so its
    31	audit evidence is intentionally discarded with the rest of that isolated run.
    32	Use `agent exec --state-dir <dir>` when the run state must remain available,
    33	and inspect it through a Gateway using that same state directory.
    34	
    35	The ledger is separate from conversation transcripts: it records identity,
    36	ordering, provenance, action, status, and normalized outcome codes, but never
    37	stores content, and message identifiers appear only as installation-local
    38	keyed pseudonyms. [Audit history](/gateway/audit) owns the full data model,
    39	privacy semantics, storage/retention bounds, and coverage limits; this page
    40	covers the command surface.
    41	
    42	```bash
    43	openclaw audit
    44	openclaw audit --agent main --status failed
    45	openclaw audit --session "agent:main:main" --after 2026-07-01T00:00:00Z
    46	openclaw audit --run 8c69f72e-8b11-4c54-98d5-1a3dd67450c3
    47	openclaw audit --run 8c69f72e-8b11-4c54-98d5-1a3dd67450c3 --explain
    48	openclaw audit --execution 5da4c4c3-e1c9-4c95-a17d-6e5c10fd45cf --explain
    49	openclaw audit --execution 5da4c4c3-e1c9-4c95-a17d-6e5c10fd45cf --explain --json
    50	openclaw audit --run 8c69f72e-8b11-4c54-98d5-1a3dd67450c3 --explain --json
    51	openclaw audit --kind tool_action --limit 50 --json
    52	openclaw audit --kind message --direction outbound --channel telegram --json
    53	```
    54	
    55	## Filters
    56	
    57	- `--agent <id>`: exact agent id
    58	- `--session <key>`: exact session key
    59	- `--run <id>`: exact run id; filters activity unless `--explain` is also set
    60	- `--execution <id>`: exact execution id; requires `--explain`
    61	- `--kind <kind>`: `agent_run`, `tool_action`, or `message`
    62	- `--status <status>`: `started`, `succeeded`, `failed`, `cancelled`,
    63	  `timed_out`, `blocked`, or `unknown`
    64	- `--direction <direction>`: message direction, `inbound` or `outbound`
    65	- `--channel <channel>`: exact message channel
    66	- `--after <timestamp>` / `--before <timestamp>`: inclusive ISO timestamp or
    67	  Unix milliseconds
    68	- `--limit <count>`: activity page size from 1 to 500 (default `100`), decision
    69	  page size from 1 to 100, or ambiguous execution-candidate page size from 1
    70	  to 50 with `--explain` (default `50`)
    71	- `--cursor <sequence>`: continue an activity, decision, or ambiguous
    72	  execution-candidate page
    73	- `--explain`: inspect immutable execution identity and run-admission reasoning;
    74	  requires exactly one of `--run` or `--execution` and accepts only `--limit`,
    75	  `--cursor`, and `--json`
    76	- `--json`: print the bounded page as JSON
    77	
    78	The CLI queries the versioned activity RPC so one command shows the complete
    79	configured ledger. Text output shows time, kind, direction, channel, status,
    80	agent, run, and action. Missing message provenance renders as `-`; OpenClaw
    81	does not invent agent or run ids. Tool actions also show the tool name. JSON
    82	output includes `nextCursor` when another page exists. Pass that value to
    83	`--cursor` to continue without reordering records that arrive during paging.
    84	
    85	These exports remain sensitive operational metadata even though message bodies
    86	and raw message identity fields are absent. Agent, session, and run ids, timing,
    87	channels, outcomes, and stable HMAC references can correlate activity. Protect
    88	them with the same access controls and retention practices as other operator
    89	records.
    90	
    91	The Gateway intentionally exposes retained execution-identity diagnostics to
    92	every client with `operator.read` in its operator domain. That scope is a
    93	trusted read-only boundary, not hostile multi-tenant isolation. Use separate
    94	Gateway trust domains when operators must not share audit identity data.
    95	
    96	## Discover and explain executions
    97	
    98	Every admitted outer turn receives an opaque `executionId`. `contextId`
    99	identifies its immutable evidence record; the existing `runId` stays a
   100	possibly shared session, routing, or recovery correlation. Use `--run <id>
   101	--explain` to discover retained executions rather than query the best-effort
   102	activity list. One match resolves directly. Multiple matches return
   103	`ambiguous`, list at most 50 candidates, and tell you to select one explicitly:
   104	
   105	```bash
   106	openclaw audit --execution <execution-id> --explain
   107	```
   108	
   109	OpenClaw never silently selects the first or latest execution. The exact text
   110	view renders these sections:
   111	
   112	1. **Identity**: trust domain, invoker, ingress, agent principal, agent
   113	   definition, runtime instance, represented subject, and sponsor.
   114	2. **Authority**: applicable grants and assurance evidence.
   115	3. **Lineage**: parent context or an explicit absent, unknown, or unsupported
   116	   state.
   117	4. **Decisions**: bounded run-admission and authoritative action-decision
   118	   receipts, including terminal operator approvals and exact-bound cron, task,
   119	   and task-flow lifecycle rows.
   120	5. **Missing evidence** and **Next steps**.
   121	
   122	Every field includes `present`, `absent`, `unknown`, or `unsupported`; the CLI
   123	does not infer a user from a session key, device id, display name, or shared
   124	credential. A direct local run shows authoritative `local-cli`
   125	ingress, an absent invoker, and
   126	`unattributed` coverage. Its admission receipt says `not-applicable` because no
   127	identity-aware policy or grant evaluation was proven.
   128	
   129	Lifecycle rows from `cron_run_receipts`, `task_runs`, and `flow_runs` appear as
   130	owner-native, attribution-only receipts when their keyed lifecycle metadata
   131	carries the exact inspected context and execution ids. They contain status and
   132	bounded record references, not prompts, task goals, hook payloads, paths, or raw
   133	errors. Their decision is `not-applicable` because lifecycle attribution does
   134	not prove authorization.
   135	Treat every decision cursor as opaque: numeric and `a:`, `m:`, and `g:` values
   136	remain compatible, while cron/task/flow pages may return `c:`, `t:`, or `f:`.
   137	
   138	For Gateway runs, a resolved authenticated profile can make the invoker
   139	`present` and coverage `attribution-only`. Paired devices and shared credentials
   140	do not establish a person: without a durable profile the invoker stays absent,
   141	or `unknown` when authenticated user evidence promised a profile that could not
   142	be resolved. Session creation retains the live canonical durable profile id so
   143	profile linking does not orphan ownership, while run inspection consumes the
   144	immutable connection-time audit fact. Ordinary session provenance stores no
   145	display label. An optional bounded, secret-redacted label can be retained only
   146	in execution identity after that audit storage is explicitly enabled.
   147	
   148	An admitted channel run can also show a pseudonymized person invoker. The
   149	trusted active registered native plugin produces the remote participant fact;
   150	core verifies its exact record, registry epoch, scope, and one-shot handoff.
   151	The resulting `boundary-verified` assurance describes that in-process boundary,
   152	not an independent core query to the remote service. Identity never comes from
   153	the conversation, room, route, account, thread, message, transport, session
   154	key, or display name. A collected run shows the person only when all queued
   155	inputs carry valid evidence for the same participant; mixed or missing evidence
   156	shows an unknown invoker. Its `channel/admission` receipt is enforced only when
   157	the participant affected every contributing access decision; otherwise it is
   158	attribution-only.
   159	
   160	For channel ingress, `unknown` means a supported integration could not supply
   161	valid host-bound evidence; it never means allowed. `unsupported` is reserved
   162	for a named path with no authoritative ingress integration. A plugin-provided
   163	sender or structurally copied resolver result cannot upgrade either state.
   164	
   165	A terminal approval display shows `allowed` or `denied`, its stable reason
   166	code, enforcement state, verified producer class, policy and grant counts,
   167	context fields used, and remediation. Expired and cancelled
   168	approvals are denied non-actions with distinct reason codes. `no-route` is an
   169	enforced denial only when the approval owner recorded that terminal state. A
   170	corrupt approval is `unknown`. The text view labels a verified
   171	operator-approval producer as an authoritative owner-native SQLite record
   172	retained for 30 days. Neither text nor JSON exposes the raw source owner, record
   173	reference, policy reference, or grant reference.
   174	`enforced` requires the approval's immutable owner-local binding to match the
   175	selected context, execution, and run exactly. A missing, malformed, or
   176	mismatched binding reports `operator_approval_execution_link_missing`,
   177	`operator_approval_execution_link_malformed`, or
   178	`operator_approval_execution_link_mismatch` with unknown coverage and no grant
   179	references. The inspector never reconstructs that binding from `runId`, session
   180	metadata, timestamps, or the number of retained executions.
   181	
   182	Outbound message receipts distinguish the durable lifecycle without treating
   183	transport progress as authorization:
   184	
   185	- `message_queued`: the shared delivery queue accepted custody.
   186	- `message_platform_started`: the channel adapter began the platform send.
   187	- `message_delivered`: the adapter returned recipient-visible delivery identity.
   188	- `message_delivery_failed_<stage>` or `message_delivery_unknown_<stage>`:
   189	  delivery did not produce a proven success; the suffix identifies `queue`,
   190	  `platform_send`, or an `unknown` stage before retrying.
   191	- `message_suppressed_<reason>`: the owning hook or payload normalizer
   192	  intentionally produced no visible message.
   193	
   194	These owner-native records are always `attribution-only`. Queue and
   195	platform-start progress comes from the lazy `outbound_message_progress`
   196	companion; terminal outcomes remain in `audit_events`. Both retain only a
   197	host-validated context/execution/run binding when the admitted turn supplied
   198	one. Inspection requires that exact binding and never assigns run-only delivery
   199	evidence to an execution from `runId`. The binding is diagnostic provenance,
   200	not proof that identity or a grant authorized delivery. Target validation,
   201	message policy, and active-turn capability denials are `enforced` only when
   202	their exact tuple was recorded and the gate changed the outcome.
   203	Portable actions and early suppressions that have no durable delivery record
   204	use the generic decision-fact owner instead of duplicating delivery state.
   205	
   206	Plugin, node, and worker receipts use the same coverage vocabulary:
   207	
   208	- A registered plugin `before_tool_call` hook allow/block, node pairing or
   209	  capability decision, and exact worker credential/build/owner-epoch admission
   210	  are `enforced` gates.
   211	- A successful node result or completed plugin-owned run is
   212	  `attribution-only`; success never upgrades the earlier gate into proof of
   213	  authorization.
   214	- A plugin node policy that returns without its supplied node callback is
   215	  `unknown` with `node.action_callback` missing.
   216	- An action performed wholly inside an ACP or other external native runtime
   217	  without an OpenClaw pre-action callback produces an ACP-owner `unsupported`
   218	  receipt after admitted prompt submission, with `native.action_callback`
   219	  missing. It does not claim a side effect. Add an authoritative native-action
   220	  callback to the adapter to provide stronger evidence; transcript or task text
   221	  cannot repair this evidence gap.
   222	
   223	These generic receipts retain no plugin id, node id, worker environment or
   224	session id, credential or build hash, token, command, parameters, or raw error
   225	text. Owner-native approval, pairing, placement, and worker-operation rows are
   226	not duplicated.
   227	
   228	JSON output is the Gateway's safe-only result without lossy reformatting. An
   229	exact result contains one bounded V1 context (maximum 16 KiB), up to 100
   230	`decisionDisplays`, coverage and
   231	missing-evidence codes, and an optional `nextDecisionCursor`. An ambiguous run
   232	result instead contains at most 50 execution candidates and an optional
   233	`nextExecutionCursor`. Sensitive domain,
   234	runtime, invoker, assurance, ingress-source, and grant references are
   235	installation-local HMAC projections. Configured agent ids and exact run ids
   236	remain visible, as do context and execution ids, so redirected output is still
   237	private operator data.
   238	
   239	An older Gateway produces an explicit `unsupported` result with
   240	`gateway_upgrade_required` and an upgrade-and-rerun next step. The CLI never
   241	reconstructs identity from legacy audit rows. A current Gateway distinguishes
   242	an unknown run, an unavailable pre-feature, disabled, or failed context write,
   243	an expired context, and a corrupt context without claiming that missing
   244	best-effort activity proves no execution. A newly admitted run can also be
   245	temporarily unavailable while its bounded identity envelope waits in the audit
   246	writer queue; retry inspection after the run or normal process shutdown.
   247	Admission never waits for writer readiness, schema or HMAC-key initialization,
   248	SQLite, or persistence.
   249	
   250	Once a context is older than 30 days, the CLI returns no fields or linked
   251	decisions from it. While bounded cleanup is pending, the result is `unsupported`
   252	with an expiry-and-rerun next step. After cleanup it can become `unknown` if no
   253	separately retained activity remains; this absence does not prove that the run
   254	did not occur. Startup and hourly maintenance prune at most 1,024 identity
   255	contexts per tick and continue when collection is disabled. Queue saturation,
   256	storage failure, cleanup failure, shutdown timeout, or abrupt process termination can lose
   257	best-effort evidence but never block or abort the agent run. Normal Gateway and
   258	direct-local CLI shutdown flushes accepted work when its writer lifecycle
   259	permits.
   260	
   261	## Recorded events
   262	
   263	The Gateway collects trusted lifecycle streams for eight actions:
   264	
   265	- `agent.run.started`
   266	- `agent.run.finished`
   267	- `tool.action.started`
   268	- `tool.action.finished`
   269	- `message.inbound.processed`
   270	- `message.outbound.queued`
   271	- `message.outbound.platform-started`
   272	- `message.outbound.finished`
   273	
   274	The activity ledger returns run, tool, inbound-message, and terminal outbound
   275	records. Nonterminal outbound actions use the separate progress owner and are
   276	projected as decision receipts by `--run ... --explain`; they are not placed in
   277	the released-reader-compatible activity ledger. Every returned activity record
   278	has a stable event id, a monotonically increasing ledger sequence, a lifecycle
   279	timestamp, actor, action, status, a `schemaVersion: 1` marker, source sequence,
   280	and `redaction: "metadata_only"`.
   281	Agent/session/run provenance and event-specific fields are present only when
   282	the trusted source provides them. Message records intentionally omit
   283	`sessionKey` and `sessionId`, so `--session` filters run and tool records only.
   284	
   285	Terminal run and tool records distinguish success, failure, cancellation,
   286	timeout, and policy blocks with closed status and error codes. `unknown` is an
   287	explicit non-success result when an upstream runtime does not expose an
   288	authoritative terminal outcome. Tool call ids are exported only as stable
   289	fingerprints. Tool names must match the compact model-facing name
   290	contract; other values become `unknown`.
   291	
   292	Message records add direction, channel, conversation kind, outcome, and
   293	optional delivery kind, failure stage, duration, result count, normalized
   294	reason code, and keyed account/conversation/message/target pseudonyms. The
   295	current inbound boundary covers accepted messages that reach core dispatch,
   296	including core duplicate and terminal processing outcomes. The outbound
   297	boundary writes replay-safe `queued` and `platform_started` progress records to
   298	its lazy companion plus one terminal activity row per original logical reply
   299	payload that reaches shared durable delivery. Chunking and adapter fan-out are
   300	aggregated in terminal `resultCount`.
   301	A terminal is `sent`, `suppressed`, `failed`, or `unknown` after acknowledgement,
   302	dead letter, or reconciliation makes that outcome known.
   303	Plugin-local and direct-send paths that bypass those shared boundaries are not
   304	yet covered; absence of a row does not prove that no message existed.
   305	
   306	The audit ledger does not replace transcripts, task history, cron run history,
   307	or logs. It provides a small cross-run index for operator questions without
   308	copying conversation content into another store.
   309	
   310	For inbound rows, `durationMs` measures core dispatch and `resultCount` counts
   311	finalized queued tool, block, and reply payloads. For outbound rows,
   312	`durationMs` includes delivery ownership through its terminal (and therefore
   313	queued wait time), while `resultCount` counts identified physical platform
   314	sends. `deliveryKind`, when present, describes the effective post-hook,
   315	post-render payload; suppressed and crash-ambiguous rows omit it.
   316	
   317	## Gateway RPC
   318	
   319	`audit.activity.list` requires `operator.read` and accepts the same filters. It
   320	returns the named V1 activity event union, including run, tool, inbound-message,
   321	and terminal outbound-message records.
   322	
   323	```bash
   324	openclaw gateway call audit.activity.list --params '{"channel":"telegram","limit":50}'
   325	```
   326	
   327	The result is `{ "events": AuditActivityEventV1[], "nextCursor"?: string }`.
   328	Results are newest first and limited to 500 records per request.
   329	
   330	`audit.run.inspect` also requires `operator.read`:
   331	
   332	```bash
   333	openclaw gateway call audit.run.inspect \
   334	  --params '{"runId":"8c69f72e-8b11-4c54-98d5-1a3dd67450c3","decisionLimit":50}'
   335	
   336	openclaw gateway call audit.run.inspect \
   337	  --params '{"executionId":"5da4c4c3-e1c9-4c95-a17d-6e5c10fd45cf","decisionLimit":50}'
   338	```
   339	
   340	Its result is `{ "schemaVersion": 1, "run": ..., "identity": ...,
   341	"decisionDisplays": ..., "coverage": ..., "nextDecisionCursor"?: ...,
   342	"nextExecutionCursor"?: ... }`. The required `decisionDisplays` array is the
   343	only receipt presentation field. Raw owner receipts and a `decisions` key never
   344	cross the Gateway boundary.
   345	The closed request accepts exactly one of `executionId` or `runId`.
   346	`decisionLimit` is 1–100 and `decisionCursor` is optional. Run discovery also
   347	accepts `executionLimit` from 1–50 and an optional `executionCursor`. A run
   348	with multiple retained executions returns the typed `ambiguous` identity state
   349	and no identity context; its required `decisionDisplays` array is empty until
   350	the caller selects an execution id.
   351	For one selected context, receipt paging starts with admission, then reads
   352	owner-native terminal approvals, merges outbound progress and terminal records,
   353	then reads generic facts and the cron, task, and flow lifecycle owners. The
   354	complete order is admission, approval, message, generic, cron, task, then flow.
   355	The merge is deterministic across restart and rejects a cursor whose exact
   356	owner row has expired. Approval and message selectors use the opaque
   357	`approval-decision:` and `message-decision:` namespaces minted from the same
   358	owner-query snapshot; raw receipt, resolution, and event identifiers never
   359	become selectors.
   360	Approval and delivery inspection never write generic duplicates. Generic fact
   361	writes and projections also require the full context, execution, and run tuple
   362	to match the immutable execution context.
   363	
   364	The activity ledger remains best-effort. By contrast, a returned approval
   365	receipt comes from the authoritative first-answer-wins approval row, and a
   366	returned generic receipt comes from the additive immutable decision-fact
   367	table. All three surfaces use 30-day retention, but absence from the activity
   368	ledger cannot prove that an approval or action did not occur. Generic fact
   369	delivery is also best-effort until its bounded queue write persists the row;
   370	owner-native approval persistence does not use that queue.
   371	
   372	The shipped `audit.list` RPC remains unchanged for older run/tool clients. When
   373	`audit.activity.list` is unavailable on an older Gateway, the CLI retries
   374	`audit.list` only if every requested filter is supported by that legacy method. `--kind message`,
   375	`--direction`, and `--channel` fail with an upgrade message on an older Gateway
   376	instead of being silently discarded.
   377	
   378	## Related
   379	
   380	- [Audit history](/gateway/audit)
   381	- [Gateway protocol](/gateway/protocol/ledgers#audit-ledger-rpc)
   382	- [Sessions](/cli/sessions)
   383	- [Tasks](/cli/tasks)
   384	- [Cron jobs](/automation/cron-jobs)
   385	---
   386	summary: "Camera capture and macOS physical PTZ control on paired nodes"
   387	read_when:
   388	  - Adding or modifying camera capture on node platforms
   389	  - Controlling a USB camera's physical pan, tilt, or zoom on macOS
   390	  - Extending agent-accessible MEDIA temp-file workflows
   391	title: "Camera capture"
   392	---
   393	
   394	OpenClaw supports camera capture for agent workflows on paired **iOS**, **Android**, **macOS**, and **Linux** nodes: capture a photo (`jpg`) or a short video clip (`mp4`, with optional audio) via Gateway `node.invoke`.
   395	
   396	When a capture request includes `deviceId`, the selected camera must match that ID exactly. An unknown ID fails instead of capturing from a different camera; run `camera.list` to refresh device IDs, which change when cameras are reconnected.
   397	
   398	The macOS app can also physically pan, tilt, and zoom supported USB UVC cameras. PTZ moves the camera hardware; it does not rotate, crop, or otherwise transform a captured image.
   399	
   400	All camera access is gated behind a user-controlled setting per platform.
   401	
   402	## iOS node
   403	
   404	### iOS user setting
   405	
   406	- iOS Settings tab → **Camera** → **Allow Camera** (`camera.enabled`).
   407	  - Default: **on** (missing key is treated as enabled).
   408	  - When off: `camera.*` commands return `CAMERA_DISABLED`.
   409	
   410	### iOS commands (via Gateway `node.invoke`)
   411	
   412	- `camera.list`
   413	  - Response payload: `devices` — array of `{ id, name, position, deviceType }`.
   414	
   415	- `camera.snap`
   416	  - Params:
   417	    - `facing`: `front|back` (default: `front`)
   418	    - `maxWidth`: number (optional; default `1600`)
   419	    - `quality`: `0..1` (optional; default `0.9`, clamped to `[0.05, 1.0]`)
   420	    - `format`: `jpg` (the only supported value)
   421	    - `delayMs`: number (optional; default `0`, internally capped at `10000`)
   422	    - `deviceId`: string (optional; from `camera.list`)
   423	  - Response payload: `format: "jpg"`, `base64`, `width`, `height`.
   424	  - Payload guard: photos are recompressed to keep the base64-encoded payload under 5MB.
   425	
   426	- `camera.clip`
   427	  - Params:
   428	    - `facing`: `front|back` (default: `front`)
   429	    - `durationMs`: number (default `3000`, clamped to `[250, 60000]`)
   430	    - `includeAudio`: boolean (default `true`)
   431	    - `format`: `mp4` (the only supported value)
   432	    - `deviceId`: string (optional; from `camera.list`)
   433	  - Response payload: `format: "mp4"`, `base64`, `durationMs`, `hasAudio`.
   434	
   435	### iOS foreground requirement
   436	
   437	The iOS node only allows `camera.*` commands in the **foreground**. Background invocations return `NODE_BACKGROUND_UNAVAILABLE`.
   438	
   439	### CLI helper
   440	
   441	The easiest way to get media files is via the CLI helper, which writes decoded media to a temp file and prints the saved path.
   442	
   443	```bash
   444	openclaw nodes camera snap --node <id>                 # default: one node-selected photo
   445	openclaw nodes camera snap --node <id> --facing front
   446	openclaw nodes camera snap --node <id> --facing both   # front then back (2 saved paths)
   447	openclaw nodes camera clip --node <id> --duration 10s
   448	openclaw nodes camera clip --node <id> --no-audio
   449	```
   450	
   451	`--duration` accepts a bare number of milliseconds (`3000`) or a unit suffix
   452	(`10s`, `1m`, `1h30m`). It defaults to `3000` (3 seconds).
   453	
   454	Without `--facing`, `nodes camera snap` captures one photo using the node's default camera and labels the saved artifact `unknown`. On non-Linux nodes, `--facing both` captures front then back and prints two saved paths. `--device-id` is valid without `--facing`; on non-Linux nodes, it cannot be combined with `--facing both`. Linux always sends one facing-less request and labels the artifact `unknown`, regardless of `--facing`. Output files are temporary (in the OS temp directory) unless you build your own wrapper.
   455	
   456	## Android node
   457	
   458	### Android user setting
   459	
   460	- Android Settings sheet → **Camera** → **Allow Camera** (`camera.enabled`).
   461	  - **Fresh installs default to off.** Existing installs that predate this setting are migrated to **on** so upgrades do not silently lose previously working camera access.
   462	  - When off: `camera.*` commands return `CAMERA_DISABLED: enable Camera in Settings`.
   463	
   464	### Permissions
   465	
   466	- `CAMERA` is required for both `camera.snap` and `camera.clip`; missing/denied permission returns `CAMERA_PERMISSION_REQUIRED`.
   467	- `RECORD_AUDIO` is required for `camera.clip` when `includeAudio` is `true`; missing/denied permission returns `MIC_PERMISSION_REQUIRED`.
   468	
   469	The app prompts for runtime permissions when possible.
   470	
   471	### Android foreground requirement
   472	
   473	The Android node only allows `camera.*` commands in the **foreground**. Background invocations return `NODE_BACKGROUND_UNAVAILABLE: command requires foreground`.
   474	
   475	### Android commands (via Gateway `node.invoke`)
   476	
   477	- `camera.list`
   478	  - Response payload: `devices` — array of `{ id, name, position, deviceType }`.
   479	
   480	- `camera.snap`
   481	  - Params: `facing` (`front|back`, default `front`), `quality` (default `0.95`, clamped to `[0.1, 1.0]`), `maxWidth` (default `1600`), `deviceId` (optional; unknown id fails with `INVALID_REQUEST`).
   482	  - Response payload: `format: "jpg"`, `base64`, `width`, `height`.
   483	  - Payload guard: recompressed to keep base64 under 5MB (same budget as iOS).
   484	
   485	- `camera.clip`
   486	  - Params: `facing` (default `front`), `durationMs` (default `3000`, clamped to `[200, 60000]`), `includeAudio` (default `true`), `deviceId` (optional).
   487	  - Response payload: `format: "mp4"`, `base64`, `durationMs`, `hasAudio`.
   488	  - Payload guard: raw MP4 is capped at 18MB before base64 encoding; oversize clips fail with `PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE` (reduce `durationMs` and retry).
   489	
   490	## macOS app
   491	
   492	### macOS user setting
   493	
   494	The macOS companion app exposes a checkbox:
   495	
   496	- **Settings → General → Allow Camera** (`openclaw.cameraEnabled`).
   497	  - Default: **off**.
   498	  - When off: camera requests return `CAMERA_DISABLED: enable Camera in Settings`.
   499	
   500	### CLI helper (node invoke)
   501	
   502	Use the main `openclaw` CLI to invoke camera commands on the macOS node.
   503	
   504	```bash
   505	openclaw nodes camera list --node <id>                     # list camera ids
   506	openclaw nodes camera snap --node <id>                     # prints saved path
   507	openclaw nodes camera snap --node <id> --max-width 1280
   508	openclaw nodes camera snap --node <id> --delay-ms 2000
   509	openclaw nodes camera snap --node <id> --device-id <id>
   510	openclaw nodes camera clip --node <id> --duration 10s       # prints saved path
   511	openclaw nodes camera clip --node <id> --device-id <id>
   512	openclaw nodes camera clip --node <id> --no-audio
   513	```
   514	
   515	- `openclaw nodes camera snap` defaults to `maxWidth=1600` unless overridden.
   516	- `camera.snap` waits `delayMs` (default 2000ms, clamped to `[0, 10000]`) after warm-up/exposure settle before capturing.
   517	- Photo payloads are recompressed to keep base64 under 5MB.
   518	
   519	If a macOS external camera starts a photo session in portrait, OpenClaw selects an advertised landscape format with transposed dimensions and the same encoding, when one exists. Already-landscape, built-in, and Continuity Camera formats are unchanged. Without an exact counterpart, or if the camera cannot be reconfigured, capture keeps the negotiated format. `--max-width` still only limits the returned JPEG width; it does not choose a camera format.
   520	
   521	### macOS physical PTZ
   522	
   523	Physical PTZ is implemented by the Mac app for USB cameras that expose standard UVC absolute pan/tilt or zoom controls. It uses the same **Allow Camera** setting as capture. Other node platforms do not advertise these commands.
   524	
   525	Always pass an explicit `deviceId` returned by `camera.list`. OpenClaw never chooses a default camera for physical movement.
   526	
   527	- `camera.ptz.status` reads the current position without moving the camera. Request: `{ "deviceId": "<camera-id>" }`.
   528	  - The response contains only executable `pan`, `tilt`, and `zoom` axes under `axes`.
   529	  - Pan and tilt values are degrees. Zoom values are percentages.
   530	  - Each axis reports `current`, `min`, `max`, `step`, `unit`, `canSet`, and `canMove`. `default` appears only when the camera successfully reports a device default.
   531	  - `canHome` is true only when every executable exposed axis has a real device-advertised default, so the complete home plan can be attempted.
   532	- `camera.ptz.control` changes the camera hardware. Its closed operations are:
   533	  - `{ "deviceId": "<camera-id>", "operation": "set", "target": { "panDegrees": 10, "tiltDegrees": -5, "zoomPercent": 40 } }`
   534	  - `{ "deviceId": "<camera-id>", "operation": "move", "delta": { "panDegrees": 2, "zoomPercent": -5 } }`
   535	  - `{ "deviceId": "<camera-id>", "operation": "home" }`
   536	
   537	`set` and `move` require at least one finite axis value. Omitted axes remain unchanged, and move deltas for zoom are percentage points. `home` restores the device-advertised defaults; it returns `CAMERA_PTZ_UNSUPPORTED` without moving the camera when `canHome` is false. The Mac app clamps and snaps requested values to the camera's range and resolution; the response returns the post-operation `state` and lists changed request fields in `adjusted`. Requesting an unsupported axis returns `CAMERA_PTZ_AXIS_UNSUPPORTED`.
   538	
   539	Both PTZ commands briefly open a live camera stream because supported cameras only service UVC controls while streaming. This activates the camera and its privacy indicator for the duration, including when `camera.ptz.status` only reads the position. Frames are not retained, and no photo, video, or file is produced.
   540	
   541	Pan/tilt and zoom use separate hardware writes and cannot be atomic. OpenClaw verifies the resulting position through a fresh control connection. If a later write or final status read fails, or an axis does not reach its requested position within the camera's reported resolution, `CAMERA_PTZ_PARTIAL` names the acknowledged control groups, includes the independently observed state when readable, and tells the caller to run `camera.ptz.status` before retrying. Position failures also report the requested and observed values; check that a video stream reaches the camera and disable on-camera AI framing or tracking that can override UVC controls.
   542	
   543	`camera.ptz.control` is dangerous and remains disarmed until the operator explicitly adds it to `gateway.nodes.commands.allow`:
   544	
   545	```json5
   546	{
   547	  gateway: {
   548	    nodes: {
   549	      commands: { allow: ["camera.ptz.control"] },
   550	    },
   551	  },
   552	}
   553	```
   554	
   555	The allow entry alone does not widen an existing node approval. After the updated Mac reconnects and declares PTZ control, run `openclaw nodes pending`, then approve the widened surface with `openclaw nodes approve <requestId>`.
   556	
   557	In the agent `nodes` tool, use `action: "camera_ptz"`, the selected Mac node, `deviceId`, and `ptzOperation: "status" | "set" | "move" | "home"`. Axis inputs are `panDegrees`, `tiltDegrees`, and `zoomPercent`.
   558	
   559	## Linux node host
   560	
   561	The bundled Linux Node plugin adds camera capture to the CLI `openclaw node` service. It works on a headless host and does not require the Linux desktop app.
   562	
   563	Camera access defaults to off. Enable it under the plugin entry, then restart the node service so its Gateway advertisement is rebuilt:
   564	
   565	```json5
   566	{
   567	  plugins: {
   568	    entries: {
   569	      "linux-node": {
   570	        config: {
   571	          camera: { enabled: true },
   572	        },
   573	      },
   574	    },
   575	  },
   576	}
   577	```
   578	
   579	Requirements:
   580	
   581	- FFmpeg with V4L2 input, `libx264`, and AAC support
   582	- a `/dev/video*` device readable by the node-service user; on common distributions, add that user to the `video` group
   583	- for clips with the default `includeAudio: true`, a working PulseAudio server or PipeWire PulseAudio compatibility layer with a default source
   584	
   585	Linux returns capture-capable, readable V4L2 device paths from `camera.list`; FFmpeg probes each `/dev/video*` candidate and omits metadata or output-only nodes. Device `position` is `unknown`, so facing requests without `deviceId` produce one `unknown`-position photo or clip instead of claiming a front or back camera. Use `deviceId` when a host has multiple cameras. `camera.snap` uses FFmpeg input warm-up for `delayMs` and preserves aspect ratio while limiting width. `camera.clip` records microphone audio as the MP4 audio track; OpenClaw deliberately exposes no standalone microphone command.
   586	
   587	The plugin uses `libx264` for MP4 video and does not silently change codecs. An FFmpeg build without the required input or encoders returns `CAMERA_UNAVAILABLE`. Photos and clips that would exceed the 25MB base64 payload budget fail with `PAYLOAD_TOO_LARGE`.
   588	
   589	`camera.snap` and `camera.clip` remain dangerous commands. Add them to `gateway.nodes.commands.allow` only when you intend to arm capture; enabling the plugin alone does not bypass Gateway policy.
   590	
   591	## Safety + practical limits
   592	
   593	- Camera and microphone access trigger the usual OS permission prompts (and require usage strings in `Info.plist`).
   594	- Video clips are capped at 60s to avoid oversized node payloads (base64 overhead plus message limits).
   595	
   596	## macOS screen video (OS-level)
   597	
   598	For _screen_ video (not camera), use the macOS companion:
   599	
   600	```bash
   601	openclaw nodes screen record --node <id> --duration 10s --fps 15   # prints saved path
   602	```
   603	
   604	Requires macOS **Screen Recording** permission (TCC).
   605	
   606	## Related
   607	
   608	- [Image and media support](/nodes/images)
   609	- [Media understanding](/nodes/media-understanding)
   610	- [Location command](/nodes/location-command)
   611	- [Computer use](/nodes/computer-use)
   612	- [Node troubleshooting](/nodes/troubleshooting)
