     1	---
     2	summary: "Which SQLite database holds what, and the tables behind individual features"
     3	read_when:
     4	  - "Locating the global state database or a per-agent database on disk"
     5	  - "Checking which table backs a feature such as the update ledger or meeting transcripts"
     6	title: "Database layout"
     7	---
     8	
     9	## Database layout
    10	
    11	| Scope                | Default path                                               | Contents                                                                                              |
    12	| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
    13	| Global control plane | `~/.openclaw/state/openclaw.sqlite`                        | Shared configuration state, registries, approvals, plugin state, and shared runtime state             |
    14	| Per-agent data plane | `~/.openclaw/agents/<agentId>/agent/openclaw-agent.sqlite` | Sessions, transcripts, memory indexes, auth state, conversation state, and agent-scoped runtime state |
    15	
    16	The task registry uses the shared state database. Runtime trajectory events live with their sessions in the per-agent database or a configured shared session SQLite store.
    17	
    18	In agent schema 23, `transcript_events` retains original event JSON as either
    19	`event_json` TEXT or `event_zstd` BLOB, with byte counts and bounded navigation
    20	metadata for compressed rows. Use the transcript accessor or supported exports
    21	to reconstruct history; selecting `event_json` alone omits compressed events.
    22	Memory chunk/cache embeddings are little-endian Float64 BLOBs. See
    23	[compact agent payload storage](/reference/database-schemas/agent-schema-history#compact-agent-payload-storage).
    24	
    25	Doctor normalizes historical task run and child-session identifiers together
    26	with their related subagent bindings, so scoped mutations can use the existing
    27	indexes. Legacy sidecar imports use the same transactional repair. Gateway
    28	restore and reads consume stored identifiers without repairing them. New task
    29	records and explicit identifier changes normalize before persistence and receipt
    30	publication; unrelated patches preserve the existing identity. Schema versions
    31	and retention are unchanged. `openclaw update` runs Doctor before activation;
    32	after a direct binary replacement or using an older writer, run
    33	`openclaw doctor --fix` before starting the new Gateway.
    34	
    35	### Activity session recaps
    36	
    37	[Activity](/web/control-ui/settings#activity-tab) stores one optional `activitySummary` object in the existing `session_nodes.entry_json` session metadata. This is a reconstructible cache; the transcript remains canonical. The [approved persistence design](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/147383) adds no SQL table, column, or database schema-version change. Current and `v2026.9.4` metadata serializers preserve unknown optional fields; unknown recap payload versions are treated as cache misses.
    38	
    39	Payload version 1 records the recap text, generation time, session ID and lifecycle revision, transcript generation and leaf, chronological coverage, and whether oversized message content was omitted. The optional `formatRevision` identifies the generated prose format; revision 2 uses one to three concise sentences. Missing or older format revisions retain their text and coverage while the existing queue refreshes the prose. This adds no SQL migration or payload-version bump. A rewind or replacement invalidates an incompatible source binding. The Gateway reads bounded transcript chunks outside the metadata write and rechecks the current lifecycle and transcript branch before committing. Recap writes preserve session activity timestamps and ordering.
    40	
    41	The latest recap survives restart and archival. Deleting the session removes it; reset or replacement makes the prior lifecycle's recap unusable. Incognito sessions do not persist or generate this cache. A shared, bounded Gateway queue deduplicates generation across viewers, retains the previous recap on failure, and uses only the configured utility route. Disabling that route stops new generation. Removing or ignoring the optional field is a rollback path that leaves session and transcript data intact; removing the feature does not require reversing a database migration.
    42	
    43	### Transcript search row ownership
    44	
    45	In agent schema 23, `session_transcript_fts_rows` maps each FTS `rowid` to its
    46	session and nullable message ID. `id` is the primary key; indexes on
    47	`session_id` and `(session_id, message_id)` support exact deletion and
    48	reconciliation. The transcript projection owner maintains these derived facts
    49	with their FTS rows. Migration preserves the FTS content and rowids while
    50	replacing schema 22's lazy mapping and completeness counter. See
    51	[compact agent payload storage](/reference/database-schemas/agent-schema-history#compact-agent-payload-storage)
    52	for migration, recovery and downgrade behavior.
    53	
    54	### Cold transcript archives
    55	
    56	The per-agent `session_transcript_cold_archives` table records cold transcript
    57	locations alongside `session_windows` and `transcript_events`. Each row belongs
    58	to a retained session window and identifies its generation, archive name, hash,
    59	counts, and sizes. The payload lives in an immutable compressed JSONL file, or
    60	in the row's blob when embedded by a supported backup.
    61	
    62	The default archive directory is
    63	`~/.openclaw/agents/<agentId>/sessions/cold/`, with filenames
    64	`<sha256>.jsonl.zst`. A database in a directory named `agent` uses its sibling
    65	`sessions/cold/` directory; other store layouts use `cold/` beside the database.
    66	These files contain authoritative history. See
    67	[cold transcript storage](/reference/session-management-compaction/maintenance#cold-transcript-storage)
    68	for retention and restoration, and
    69	[agent schema 20](/reference/database-schemas/agent-schema-history#cold-transcript-storage)
    70	for the schema and update contract.
    71	
    72	### Plugin state listing index
    73	
    74	Plugin keyed stores use the shared `plugin_state_entries` table. Its listing
    75	index includes `expires_at` after the existing plugin, namespace, creation-time,
    76	and entry-key columns, so live-row counts can read the index without fetching
    77	stored values. Quotas, TTL cutoffs, ordering, and row contents are unchanged.
    78	
    79	Writable startup and `openclaw doctor --fix` replace the older four-column
    80	definition through canonical index repair, without a schema-version bump. The
    81	repair builds temporary indexes and runs the existing table and full-file
    82	integrity checks; allow for extra disk space and work proportional to stored
    83	entries during the first repair.
    84	
    85	An older build can rebuild the same index back to its expected definition.
    86	Full-schema read-only validation rejects a mismatched definition until a
    87	writable owner repairs it; lightweight readers that validate only the numeric
    88	schema version may read either shape. See the
    89	[accepted index design](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/issues/142244) for
    90	upgrade, reverse-repair, and performance proof requirements.
    91	
    92	### Mentions Inbox
    93	
    94	The [mentions Inbox](/concepts/multi-user#temporary-mentions-inbox) uses existing
    95	`config_machine_state` rows in `state/openclaw.sqlite`.
    96	`notifications.mentions.source.*` records retain typed source identities,
    97	recipients, mention identifiers, expiry times, and dismissal bookkeeping;
    98	`notifications.mentions.head` records the revision and sequence. Writes use the
    99	existing table and primary key, with no new tables, columns, indexes, or schema
   100	version change.
   101	
   102	Retention remains seven days from creation, capped at 100 entries per profile,
   103	10,000 entries globally, and 10,000 source identities for duplicate suppression.
   104	Restarts preserve retained entries, dismissals, and their original expiry times.
   105	Loading stored state does not replay browser notifications or scan transcripts
   106	to reconstruct old mentions.
   107	
   108	### ACP replay accounting
   109	
   110	The shared `acp_replay_sessions` and `acp_replay_events` tables retain bridge
   111	replay history. Their `estimated_bytes` columns count the UTF-8 bytes of each
   112	persisted text field, plus 32 bytes per row. Session totals include their events.
   113	This is a retained-content estimate, not a limit on SQLite file, page, or WAL size.
   114	
   115	Older releases counted characters inconsistently, undercounting Unicode and
   116	allowing unchanged metadata writes to drift. Explicit Doctor shared-state
   117	repair rebuilds all derived totals
   118	atomically, preserving event JSON text, identifiers, timestamps, and sequence.
   119	Repair does not prune history. The next ordinary session write applies the
   120	existing caps and eviction order, so corrected Unicode history may trim sooner
   121	and use transcript fallback when loaded.
   122	
   123	Normal runtime opens and automatic startup schema preparation leave existing
   124	accounting columns unchanged, including after the application version changes. If
   125	the supported older shape lacks accounting columns, adding them also initializes
   126	their totals in the same transaction. Run
   127	`openclaw doctor --fix` during update maintenance to repair historical accounting.
   128	Supported older-schema upgrades still perform the content transformations needed
   129	to preserve data while changing its schema. Accounting repair cannot recover
   130	history already evicted by an older writer. See [ACP CLI](/cli/acp).
   131	
   132	### Meeting transcript tables
   133	
   134	Meeting captures use three `STRICT` tables in the shared
   135	`state/openclaw.sqlite` database, separate from per-agent conversation transcripts.
   136	The transcript store (`src/transcripts/store.ts`) owns their reads and writes;
   137	`src/transcripts/sqlite-schema.ts` ensures the tables on first use. Markdown and
   138	JSON files under the transcripts directory are explicit exports, not runtime
   139	storage. See [Transcripts CLI](/cli/transcripts).
   140	
   141	#### `meeting_transcript_sessions`
   142	
   143	One row per capture identity. The primary key is `(session_id, started_at)`;
   144	`selector` is unique. Indexes support start-time, session-ID, slug, and export-key
   145	lookups.
   146	
   147	| Columns                                  | Type                                        | Purpose                                                                 |
   148	| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   149	| `session_id`, `started_at`               | `TEXT NOT NULL`                             | Capture ID and original start time.                                     |
   150	| `selector`, `export_key`, `session_slug` | `TEXT NOT NULL`                             | Canonical selector and derived export identity.                         |
   151	| `provider_id`, `source_json`             | `TEXT NOT NULL`                             | Source provider and locator.                                            |
   152	| `title`, `stopped_at`, `metadata_json`   | Nullable `TEXT`                             | Display title, terminal time, and session metadata including ownership. |
   153	| `export_manifest_json`                   | `TEXT NOT NULL`, default `{}`               | Export artifact ownership manifest.                                     |
   154	| `export_pending_json`                    | `TEXT NOT NULL`, default `[]`               | Pending export artifacts.                                               |
   155	| `next_utterance_seq`                     | Nonnegative `INTEGER NOT NULL`, default `0` | Next append sequence.                                                   |
   156	| `created_at_ms`, `updated_at_ms`         | Nonnegative `INTEGER NOT NULL`              | Store timestamps.                                                       |
   157	
   158	Reopening an occupancy-driven capture clears `stopped_at` without changing the
   159	primary key, so the same meeting retains its utterances.
   160	New transcript admissions record `sessionIdOrigin` (`generated` or `supplied`)
   161	in `metadata_json`. The store preserves that value, including its absence or
   162	invalidity in legacy rows, on later writes to the same primary key. Occupancy
   163	reopening requires an explicitly generated origin; an unknown origin starts a
   164	fresh capture and leaves the old record intact. The existing newest-candidate
   165	query and ten-minute window are unchanged.
   166	
   167	This adds no schema, index, version, or backfill. Doctor metadata restoration
   168	preserves an explicitly recorded origin and leaves unknown origins unknown.
   169	Older runtimes do not enforce this rule, so downgrading also removes the fixed-ID
   170	history protection. See the [accepted ID-origin decision](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/130860).
   171	
   172	#### `meeting_transcript_utterances`
   173	
   174	Append-ordered speech records. The primary key is
   175	`(session_id, session_started_at, sequence)`; the session pair references
   176	`meeting_transcript_sessions(session_id, started_at)` with `ON DELETE CASCADE`.
   177	
   178	| Columns                                  | Type                           | Purpose                                          |
   179	| ---------------------------------------- | ------------------------------ | ------------------------------------------------ |
   180	| `session_id`, `session_started_at`       | `TEXT NOT NULL`                | Owning capture identity.                         |
   181	| `sequence`                               | Nonnegative `INTEGER NOT NULL` | Stable append order within the capture.          |
   182	| `utterance_id`, `started_at`, `ended_at` | Nullable `TEXT`                | Provider utterance identity and timing.          |
   183	| `speaker_id`, `speaker_label`            | Nullable `TEXT`                | Provider speaker identity and display label.     |
   184	| `text`                                   | `TEXT NOT NULL`                | Captured transcript text.                        |
   185	| `final`                                  | Nullable `INTEGER`, `0` or `1` | Whether the provider marked the utterance final. |
   186	| `metadata_json`                          | Nullable `TEXT`                | Provider utterance metadata.                     |
   187	
   188	#### `meeting_transcript_summaries`
   189	
   190	One current summary per capture. The primary key is
   191	`(session_id, session_started_at)` and references the session primary key with
   192	`ON DELETE CASCADE`. At least one of `summary_json` or `markdown` must be non-null.
   193	
   194	| Columns                            | Type                           | Purpose                                                                                                     |
   195	| ---------------------------------- | ------------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   196	| `session_id`, `session_started_at` | `TEXT NOT NULL`                | Owning capture identity.                                                                                    |
   197	| `generated_at`                     | Nullable `TEXT`                | Summary generation time.                                                                                    |
   198	| `summary_json`                     | Nullable `TEXT`                | Free-form summary, including participants, `source` (`model` or `heuristic`), and optional model reference. |
   199	| `markdown`                         | Nullable `TEXT`                | Rendered meeting notes.                                                                                     |
   200	| `utterance_count`                  | Nonnegative `INTEGER NOT NULL` | Number of utterances covered by the stored summary.                                                         |
   201	
   202	These are existing feature-local tables. Occupancy episodes and model-backed
   203	notes do not change their schema or database version.
   204	
   205	### Update run ledger
   206	
   207	`update_runs` stores one durable record per update in the shared
   208	`state/openclaw.sqlite` database. `src/infra/update-run-ledger.ts` owns writes
   209	from the admitting Gateway, orchestrator CLI, and restarted Gateway. The table
   210	is additive at shared schema version 15: the canonical schema declares it and
   211	first use ensures it inside the same write transaction. Existing tables and the
   212	schema version stay unchanged; older readers ignore the new table.
   213	
   214	`run_id` is the UUID primary key. Rows retain creation/update timestamps,
   215	trigger, phase, status, reason, origin, target, before/after versions, steps,
   216	verification facts, repair attempts, confirmation/finish timestamps, and known
   217	downtime. Each JSON column has a 16 KiB hard limit with deterministic truncation
   218	and redaction. The ledger stores bounded diagnostic summaries, not raw logs or
   219	credentials. There is no automatic history deletion.
   220	
   221	Candidate admission adds optional `origin.admission` metadata in the existing
   222	`origin_json` column: `owner` (`candidate` or `installed`), optional `protocol`,
   223	`candidateVersion`, `checks`, and `fallbackReason`. Reads expose the same metadata
   224	as `run.admission`. `origin.candidateAdmission` records the candidate's verdict,
   225	reasons, warnings, and facts within the existing redaction and byte limits.
   226	These observations do not grant execution authority. No column, table, or schema
   227	version changes; older records can omit them. See
   228	[candidate-owned admission](/cli/update#candidate-owned-admission).
   229	
   230	Asynchronous history lookup and listing run their queries and record decoding
   231	in the shared-state read worker. They preserve source artifacts and inherited
   232	snapshot or disposable-read scopes, and return empty history without creating
   233	a missing database or ledger table. Reconciliation and ledger writes retain
   234	their existing owners.
   235	
   236	New drivers store optional `origin.driver` fields `host` (the hostname), `pid`,
   237	and `startIdentity` (the operating system's process-start identity as a decimal
   238	string) in the existing `origin_json` column. Each adopter becomes the current
   239	driver and retains distinct earlier identities in `origin.previousDrivers`.
   240	There are at most eight identities in total. Only positively dead identities
   241	are pruned; adoption is refused rather than dropping a live or uninspectable
   242	driver at capacity. If local process identity cannot be captured, adoption
   243	continues with one warning and a retained `driver:identity-unavailable` step.
   244	That marker permanently excludes the run from automatic reconciliation, even
   245	if known parents later exit; existing recorded identities remain protected.
   246	A fresh run without identity follows the legacy explicit repair/supersession
   247	rules below.
   248	This is additive JSON metadata;
   249	there are no new columns, tables, or schema versions. The separate
   250	`verification.pid` still identifies the Gateway service, not the updater.
   251	Adoption records a retained `driver:adopted` step. Detached children can outlive
   252	their parent, so either lifetime can prevent reconciliation. Adopting a terminal
   253	run is refused. Long command and finalization phases renew `updated_at_ms`
   254	every 30 seconds; only current or retained identities may renew a row. Heartbeat
   255	write failures warn once per driver run and do not abort commands or finalization;
   256	step and outcome writes retain their existing failure behavior. Encoding
   257	reserves space for exact identity bytes before bounding and redacting other
   258	origin diagnostics.
   259	
   260	The ledger owns abandonment classification and terminalization. Automatic
   261	recovery requires more than 30 minutes since both `updated_at_ms` and the latest
   262	step timestamp, plus positive evidence that every recorded driver is dead on the
   263	same host: its PID is gone or its process-start identity differs. Unreadable and
   264	foreign-host identities are inconclusive. The Gateway performs reconciliation
   265	at startup and on active-run polls, rechecking the current row and process
   266	identity in the terminal write transaction. The shared 30-minute constant also
   267	owns the older-updater schema-publication bound described under
   268	[Schema bumps and older updaters](/reference/database-schemas/versioning#schema-bumps-and-older-updaters).
   269	
   270	Reconciliation writes status `failed`, reason `abandoned`, and a retained
   271	`reconcile:abandoned` step whose detail names `inactive-driver-dead` or
   272	`operator-reconciled-inactive-run`. All unfinished steps become terminal, and
   273	history is retained. Explicit `update repair` can reconcile inactive identityless
   274	rows when the current Gateway generation is healthy and no post-core repair is
   275	pending. When every recorded driver is positively dead and no
   276	`driver:identity-unavailable` marker exists, explicit recovery does not require
   277	the inactivity window. It cannot override a live or inconclusive recorded driver. The
   278	[2026.9.2 updater](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/blob/v2026.9.2/src/cli/update-cli/update-command.ts#L465)
   279	does not record adoption: package-manager and registry preflight can
   280	leave a live updater at its single `requested/in_progress` step. Older writers
   281	may drop unknown driver JSON fields; identityless rows normally require explicit recovery.
   282	
   283	An untouched legacy admission expires automatically after more than 24 hours:
   284	it is still `requested` / `running`, has identical creation and update timestamps,
   285	no finish timestamp, no recorded driver, and only its initial `requested` step.
   286	The ledger retains it as `failed` with reason `legacy-driver-expired` and a
   287	`reconcile:abandoned` step. Startup, `update status`, `status`, and the Control UI's
   288	update reads reconcile this shape through the same transaction. Status and failure
   289	reports explain that the update never progressed and recommend `openclaw update`
   290	to retry. Status retains the latest such advisory even after a newer update finishes.
   291	This fixed legacy expiry does not establish process death. It is the bounded
   292	recovery policy for 2026.9.2-era orphan admissions. Younger rows, progressed rows,
   293	recorded drivers, and retained recovery descriptors keep their existing protections.
   294	Recording `driver:identity-unavailable` is itself a mutation, so that adoption
   295	cannot match an untouched admission. All other `update status` history remains
   296	read-only; the existing 30-minute inactivity window is unchanged.
   297	Gateway update reads also run the existing automatic dead-driver reconciliation,
   298	so Control UI admission uses the same recovery decision as its startup watcher.
   299	
   300	Explicit new CLI update admission can supersede a legacy row only when it is
   301	the sole running row, has no current or previous driver identity, and exceeds
   302	the same inactivity bound. The transaction finishes it as `failed` with reason
   303	`superseded` and a retained `reconcile:superseded` step whose detail is
   304	`operator-started-update-supersedes-inactive-identityless-run`, then creates the
   305	new row. This includes dry-run admission, but excludes inherited continuations
   306	and campaigns. `abandoned` and `superseded` are additive values in the existing
   307	free-text reason contract. Neither recovery path deletes history.
   308	
   309	Successful ledger-only repair records a retained `reconcile:acknowledged` step.
   310	A terminal abandoned row can substitute for full repair only once, within
   311	30 minutes of its finish time; later repair invocations keep normal plugin
   312	convergence behavior.
   313	Repair also inspects newer failed/abandoned history for unacknowledged post-core
   314	work, regardless of its age. An older active row cannot hide that work. If the
   315	bounded history prefix does not reach the selected recovery rows, repair uses
   316	full finalization rather than claiming that no post-core work remains.
   317	When full finalization is required, the selected inactive rows are rechecked
   318	and reconciled only after successful convergence, before success output.
   319	Explicit recovery validates and commits its selected rows in one transaction;
   320	renewed activity in any selected run preserves the entire selection. Ledger-only
   321	repair also refuses the write if another active run falls outside that selection.
   322	Finalization (`finalize:*`) and post-update verification markers survive step-count
   323	and diagnostic-byte eviction because repair relies on that history. If retained
   324	metadata alone exceeds a hard limit, the write fails without changing the row.
   325	
   326	The CLI and Gateway share WAL-backed transactions, including while the Gateway
   327	is stopped. The first terminal outcome wins; subsequent verification can enrich
   328	its observed facts without rewriting success, failure, skip, or rollback status.
   329	Interrupted completion has one narrowly verified exception: a candidate records
   330	its installed version and build ID in the retained `finalize:installed-candidate`
   331	step before returning post-core completion to the installed updater. The Gateway
   332	watcher and Doctor share one ledger reconciliation owner, which may finish the
   333	latest interrupted verification or correct its `abandoned` result to `succeeded`
   334	only after all recorded drivers are positively dead and fresh installed-build,
   335	serving-build, readiness, and generation checks agree. Recovery descriptors and
   336	recorded repair, failure, or rollback evidence prevent that correction. The transaction
   337	rechecks the complete row and latest-run identity after probing, then records the
   338	verification, outcome, and an explanatory warning together. Older rows without
   339	the target identity remain unchanged, and Doctor explains the missing evidence.
   340	This uses existing step and verification fields; schemas and rollback readers
   341	remain unchanged.
   342	Explicit `update repair` can correct the older package-owner refusal
   343	misclassification to `skipped` once the installed version satisfies its resolved
   344	target. This exception requires the latest run to contain only the untouched
   345	request and optional driver-adoption metadata, with no recovery or active update.
   346	It preserves the refusal detail and finish time and records the existing
   347	acknowledgement marker; subsequent repairs use normal finalization.
   348	The restart sentinel carries `stats.runId` and remains the continuation owner;
   349	consuming it does not delete the run row. Chat, CLI, and status reports read that
   350	row. See [Run history and reports](/cli/update#run-history-and-reports).
   351	
   352	### Update installation control
   353	
   354	Managed update leases use a separate machine-local `managed-update-handoffs.sqlite`
   355	database under the secure OpenClaw temporary directory. This owner must remain
   356	available while an update replaces an installation or changes its runtime state.
   357	The update history above remains in the profile's shared state database.
   358	
   359	First creation exclusively creates a private file with one filesystem link.
   360	Concurrent initializers use that same file without replacing it. SQLite commits
   361	the existing schema atomically; ordinary lease inspection reports no lease while
   362	the first-use schema is empty. Reads of an absent database create no state.
   363	Existing lease rows, claim transactions, and the rule that one updater owns an
   364	installation are unchanged.
   365	
   366	The normal handoff parent prepares this database before launching its sealed
   367	helper. The helper receives the captured database identity and operates only on
   368	that existing database, without resolving installation packages or recreating
   369	missing or empty state.
   370	
   371	File creation applies private permissions before SQLite opens the file, including
   372	a protected ACL on Windows. Initialization follows the existing directory-durability
   373	policy and does not require the optional fs-safe native binding. Failure stops
   374	lease admission before its operation runs. After an interrupted first creation,
   375	the normal owner can finish initialization through its existing empty-database
   376	recovery path; committed rows remain governed by SQLite's normal transactions.
   377	This change requires no schema migration. See the
   378	[accepted initialization design](https://github.com/openclaw/openclaw/pull/144155).
   379	
   380	### Managed worktree acceleration templates
   381	
   382	[Managed worktree acceleration](/concepts/managed-worktrees#filesystem-acceleration) uses the first-use `worktree_templates` table in the shared state database. Each row records a reconstructible source template: repository and Git common directory, destination root, filesystem backend, artifact path, source commit, checkout content key, preparation status, and creation and last-use timestamps. The cache key allows one template per repository and destination root. The template contains no provisioned ignored files or repository setup output.
   383	
   384	The worktree service owns template creation, reuse, invalidation, and cleanup under its existing allocation lease. It reserves a `preparing` row before creating the artifact and publishes `ready` only after preparation completes. Durable mutations recheck the lease inside synchronous state transactions; filesystem work runs outside those transactions. Cleanup uses the reserved template ID so an old operation cannot delete its replacement. Templates are replaced when the commit or checkout policy changes and retired after seven days without use.
   385	
   386	The additive table is ensured on first use and does not change the numeric database schema version. Existing worktree and snapshot records retain their meaning; no existing checkout is migrated or moved. Template artifacts are reconstructible, while registered worktree contents and recovery snapshots retain their existing preservation rules.
   387	
   388	### Conversation environments
   389	
   390	Temporary desktops and app previews attached to a conversation use
   391	`worker_environment_session_attachments` in the shared state database. The worker
   392	environment store owns this additive companion table. One row binds an exact
   393	session ID and lifecycle revision to one environment, with an attachment
   394	generation, creation and last-use timestamps, and a nullable closed timestamp.
   395	The environment row continues to own provisioning, provider leases, transport
   396	identity, credentials, and teardown. Execution placement remains independent.
   397	
   398	Allocation intent and attachment reservation commit together before provisioning.
   399	Concurrent creation and retries reuse the owned allocation. Stop closes the
   400	relation before waiting for remote cleanup; cleanup failure retains the relation
   401	and prevents replacement until the old lease is confirmed destroyed. Session
   402	reset or deletion retires it, and startup checks the canonical session incarnation
   403	before allowing access. The configured profile's `suspendAfter` expires idle
   404	attachments; active agent runs and desktop observers keep them active. Provider
   405	lease lifetime limits continue to apply. Closing a sidebar panel only releases
   406	its viewer. Terminal attachment rows follow the environment owner's seven-day
   407	retention through a cascading foreign key.
   408	
   409	The table is ensured when the worker environment store opens and does not change
   410	the numeric schema version or the meaning of existing placement columns. Older
   411	builds ignore the relation and show these machines as ordinary unassigned
   412	environments; they do not maintain conversation attachment activity or cleanup.
   413	Stop attached machines before downgrading when they should not remain running.
   414	Existing environment destruction and provider lifetime limits remain available.
   415	Re-upgrading validates retained session identities and retries pending cleanup.
   416	Database backup and rollback include the companion table with the existing
   417	shared state database; no external attachment state needs reconstruction.
   418	
   419	### Cloud repository workspaces
   420	
   421	Repository-only [cloud sessions](/gateway/cloud-workers#dispatching-a-session) use the first-use `session_repository_workspaces` table in the shared state database. The existing session entry carries only `repositoryWorkspaceId`; the shared row owns the canonical agent/session key, repository URL, requested ref, session branch, setup intent, pinned base commit and manifest, accepted checkpoint pointer, and revision. Session reset preserves this owner; a fork receives a distinct owner.
   422	
   423	`github_repository_publication_requests` records shared and personal publication against an immutable accepted checkpoint and the session's admitted lifecycle revision. Reset preserves the session ID and repository checkpoint but invalidates publication authorized before that reset. Personal requests also retain the selected profile and connection generation and require same-owner confirmation after an interrupted publication. Pending publication keeps its original source even after an explicit move materializes a Gateway worktree.
   424	
   425	Both tables are additive, lazily ensured on first use, and leave the numeric database schema version unchanged. That is not a compatibility promise for older cloud-session implementations: run a build that understands repository-only sessions when using this state. Existing local managed-worktree sessions keep their existing representation.
   426	
   427	Checkpoint Git artifacts live under `state/repository-workspaces/<workspace-id>.git`, next to the shared database. These are bare repositories containing complete file manifests, cumulative changed-file blobs, and publication snapshots; they are not working checkouts or a backup of upstream Git history. Restoring an entire checkout still requires access to the pinned upstream commit. Back up these artifacts together with the shared and per-agent databases.
   428	
   429	Accepted checkpoint history and publication source artifacts remain until explicit session deletion, including after Stop, archive, reset, or Gateway restart. There is no timed checkpoint expiry. Deletion retires publication requests and source ownership before removing their artifact repository; failed cleanup is reported. The managed-worktree idle cleanup and snapshot retention rules do not apply to these checkpoints.
   430	
   431	## Sandbox runtime reservations
   432	
   433	The existing `sandbox_registry_entries` table owns runtime identity and cleanup.
   434	Backends that opt into reservation persist a generation before provider allocation.
   435	The entry payload retains the original `workspaceDir` and records `runtimeState`
   436	as `pending`, `ready`, `removing`, or `removing-pending`;
   437	no schema version, table, or column is added. Older entries are adopted on first
   438	use, and backends without reservation keep their existing registry behavior.
   439	Factories receive the reserved workspace on replay and shared-scope reuse, rather
   440	than the latest caller's local workspace. Provider execution and repository-scoped
   441	cleanup therefore use the same original owner.
   442	
   443	Reservation and publication use synchronous SQLite transactions. Provider work
   444	runs outside the transaction under a per-runtime file lock beside the shared
   445	database. Lock contenders wait up to 15 minutes, covering the backend's warmup
   446	and inspection budgets. Concurrent creators reuse the same generation. Failed
   447	provisioning retains its pending ID for replay after restart. Recreate and prune
   448	record removal intent before waiting for provisioning, then remove the provider runtime before
   449	deleting the row. Cleanup failures retain that intent for retry; stale handles
   450	cannot publish readiness or start new operations after removal begins.
   451	
   452	`removing-pending` preserves the fact that provisioning never published readiness.
   453	If ordinary cleanup fails, the Crabbox adapter can replay that same fixed ID from
   454	its original workspace and then release it. This also covers a failure before
   455	Crabbox recorded the request: recovery may allocate and immediately release the
   456	reserved runtime. Unknown outcomes retain the recovery row; an absent local claim
   457	or an error message is not proof that provider resources are absent.
   458	
   459	The reservation is canonical recovery state. Do not delete it to clear a provider
   460	error. Before downgrading to a version without reservation support, disable the
   461	backend and reconcile its pending leases using the current version. Older readers
   462	can open the database but do not implement this lifecycle.
