     1	---
     2	doc-schema-version: 1
     3	summary: "Continuing a failed Full Release Validation parent, attempt adoption rules, and the post-merge continuation proof"
     4	title: "Continue a failed validation"
     5	read_when:
     6	  - Rerunning failed child jobs on an existing parent
     7	  - Proving the failed-job rerun boundary after a merge
     8	---
     9	
    10	## Continue failed child jobs
    11	
    12	Full Release Validation can adopt monotonically newer attempts of the exact
    13	child runs recorded in its immutable plan. A newer attempt is accepted only
    14	when the run ID, workflow path, workflow ref, Tooling SHA, dispatch title, and
    15	event are unchanged. For each logical job, the newest observed attempt wins,
    16	including a newer failure; a job absent from a newer attempt carries forward
    17	from the last attempt that included it. Duplicate job names within one attempt,
    18	missing attempts, or provenance drift fail closed. During GitHub's rerun-attempt
    19	materialization window, `status` and `continue` re-read duplicate identities in
    20	the newest retry attempt for up to 60 seconds. The run identity and attempt stay
    21	pinned throughout; request timeouts, pagination, and transport backoff share
    22	the retry deadline. Persistent duplicates, duplicates in an earlier attempt, and
    23	changed identities still fail closed; ambiguous rows never become evidence.
    24	
    25	Inspect or continue an existing parent:
    26	
    27	```bash
    28	pnpm frv status --run <parent-run-id> --json
    29	pnpm frv rerun --run <parent-run-id> --job "normalCi:checks-node-agentic-control-plane-agent-chat"
    30	pnpm frv continue --failed --run <parent-run-id>
    31	pnpm frv verify --run <successful-parent-run-id>
    32	pnpm frv prioritize --restore <record> [--dry-run]
    33	```
    34	
    35	`prioritize --restore` recovers runs deferred by the former release-priority gate
    36	(see [Release priority](/reference/RELEASING#release-priority)). Active validation
    37	no longer pauses CI or supporting workflows.
    38	
    39	`rerun --job` selects an exact executed, terminal job name inside a child key shown by
    40	`status --json` (for example, `normalCi`, `pluginPrerelease`, or
    41	`releaseChecksIndependent`). It waits only for that child to become terminal,
    42	then uses GitHub's job-rerun API on the job's accepted attempt. GitHub also
    43	reruns dependent jobs. Other failures stay visible and require their own retry;
    44	a targeted retry never declares the parent recovered while blockers remain.
    45	
    46	`continue --failed` reruns each failed child's jobs as soon as that child is
    47	terminal, while sibling children and the original parent may still run. It
    48	adopts active attempts without duplicating them and leaves green child
    49	workflows untouched. After the resulting child evidence is green and the
    50	original parent finishes, it reruns the parent once. The original Decision and
    51	Drain may disagree after an early retry; the final parent attempt restores the
    52	same immutable execution plan and seals the updated evidence. Existing plan
    53	and attempt-binding checks remain mandatory.
    54	
    55	The final manifest records the planned and effective child attempt, accepted
    56	attempt for every logical job, and composite evidence digest. The controller's
    57	JSON result additionally lists requested reruns with their source and observed
    58	attempts; targeted entries include the job name and Actions job ID. Keep the
    59	command in a long-running shell: its default operation budget is 12 hours,
    60	including final collection and strict verification.
    61	
    62	Npm qualification participates in the same failed-job recovery: the controller
    63	retries its failed jobs on the original producer run, without rerunning
    64	successful diagnostic children.
    65	It waits for each producer attempt, adopts verified newer
    66	npm producer attempts, then reruns the parent collector once. Successful package
    67	preparation jobs and their exact artifact descriptors carry forward; a retry
    68	must not substitute rebuilt bytes for the candidate already tested.
    69	
    70	The parent authenticates the original dispatch identity and the latest successful
    71	producer receipt. Package and qualification jobs can come from different attempts;
    72	each must remain the effective successful job in that run's attempt history.
    73	Changed source, tooling, dispatch identity, superseded jobs, missing evidence,
    74	and expired receipts remain errors, not reasons to trust stale evidence.
    75	
    76	A failed npm producer is a retry target, not a reason to reject continuation.
    77	The controller uses GitHub's failed-job rerun operation on the same run, then
    78	collects its result. Frozen workflows still execute their original code:
    79	upgrading the local controller does not retrofit receipt adoption into an older
    80	workflow. Final verification must pass before recovery is reported successful.
    81	
    82	Each child or parent rerun mutation is sent exactly once. If GitHub returns an
    83	ambiguous transient error, the controller performs read-only reconciliation
    84	until the newer attempt becomes visible or the bounded reconciliation deadline
    85	expires. It never repeats the mutation, and provenance drift fails closed.
    86	After a timeout or an interrupted command, inspect `frv status` and the exact
    87	GitHub attempts before deciding on another retry; the local process cannot
    88	prove that an unobserved mutation was rejected.
    89	
    90	The command stores no continuation ledger or local journal. GitHub run
    91	attempts, the immutable execution plan, producer dispatch records and receipts,
    92	Decision/Drain artifacts, and the final manifest are the complete state model. It never tags, publishes, changes a
    93	registry, or prepares a new candidate.
    94	
    95	Parents whose immutable plan predates attempt-aware evidence cannot be
    96	continued. Start a fresh all-group Full Release Validation instead; the
    97	controller never reconstructs old state or dispatches a replacement parent.
    98	
    99	The helper creates a temporary `release-ci/*` ref pinned to the Tooling SHA,
   100	passes the Validation SHA as both the candidate ref and `expected_sha`, and
   101	deletes the temporary ref after successful validation and strict evidence
   102	verification. The helper reads Release Decision artifacts while the parent is
   103	active so blockers can surface while Diagnostic Drain collects failures. It
   104	checks parent status and exact-attempt decision metadata every two minutes,
   105	with full progress-job reads no more often than every 15 minutes. Each regular
   106	iteration makes at most two metadata requests; it downloads the decision only
   107	after its named artifact appears, retrying unavailable downloads on subsequent
   108	iterations. A validated passing decision is retained only for that attempt.
   109	Parent completion also triggers a decision download when none has been validated,
   110	so metadata lag cannot skip terminal handling. Discovery makes one immediate
   111	check and at most three retries, waiting 30, 60, then 120 seconds between checks.
   112	All reads use the normal cache-aware GitHub route; cache and request latency can
   113	add to these intervals. The helper retains its 12-hour wait deadline. Successful
   114	temporary-ref cleanup still requires parent completion and strict evidence
   115	verification. Failed validations retain both refs for reruns and diagnosis. The
   116	Validation SHA is the exact commit being qualified: the Code SHA, which can
   117	also be the Release SHA, or a later changelog-only Release SHA. It is not a
   118	third release identity. The workflow
   119	rejects malformed or mismatched expected SHAs before child dispatch. Every
   120	child must report the same Tooling SHA. Pass
   121	`-f reuse_evidence=false` to force a fresh run. Regular release-branch runs
   122	require `--workflow-sha` with the recorded full SHA, which must remain reachable
   123	from current `origin/main`. The helper rejects a pinned Tooling SHA that does
   124	not declare the current release-isolation contract or the `expected_sha`
   125	dispatch input; it never silently substitutes newer tooling. The workflow never
   126	creates or updates repository refs itself.
   127	
   128	### Automatic retries for declared flakes
   129	
   130	Automatic test retries are disabled. A failed or timed out child job remains a
   131	blocker; `known_flaky_jobs_json` is rejected on new dispatches. Inspect the
   132	original failure and fix its owner before requesting another execution. The
   133	explicit `frv rerun` and `frv continue --failed` commands remain operator recovery
   134	operations and never run as an automatic response to a test outcome.
   135	
   136	Published artifacts may contain empty `knownFlakyJobs` and `automaticRetries`
   137	fields. Readers retain their original plan digest and reject nonempty allowances
   138	or retry records. Historical advisory descriptions must match the recorded child
   139	jobs; current qualification still requires passing outcomes or the existing
   140	explicit operator waiver.
   141	
   142	### Read publication observations
   143	
   144	An optional publication selector adds a read-only view beside validation status:
   145	
   146	```bash
   147	pnpm frv status --run <parent-run-id> --publication-run <publish-parent-run-id>
   148	pnpm frv status --run <parent-run-id> --publication-run <publish-parent-run-id> --json
   149	```
   150	
   151	The JSON response keeps the compact validation summary and adds `publication`. The
   152	selector is valid only on `status`; it does not change `continue` or `verify`.
   153	The FRV root still needs an attempt-aware, all-group immutable plan.
   154	
   155	The reader pins the publication attempt observed at entry. It authenticates the
   156	FRV and publication workflow identities independently, then joins supported
   157	`release-postpublish-diagnostics` version 1 evidence to the exact validation
   158	manifest recorded by that publisher. An original plan from attempt 1 can bind a
   159	final validation manifest from attempt 2. The two attempts are reported
   160	separately; neither is silently replaced with the latest attempt.
   161	Linked children retain their own observed tooling SHA/ref. The recorded normal
   162	ClawHub ref can differ from an alpha publisher's ref.
   163	
   164	This view reports observations, **not release authorization or current registry
   165	visibility**. Writer selection, verification selection, job conclusions,
   166	registry readback, binding and asset checks remain separate. Failed publishers
   167	can retain successful readback and partial package results. Docker and VCR
   168	remain parent jobs; VCR copy, smoke and alias values are API step conclusions.
   169	Detached Windows acknowledgement, its pre-upload marker and the current child
   170	conclusion are separate observations. The normal ClawHub dispatch record is not
   171	a complete child inventory.
   172	
   173	Prepared-release activation remains unknown without an authenticated link to
   174	its external owner. A successful inner publisher or skipped finalize job does
   175	not prove activation. Supplied historical child IDs retain unknown recorded
   176	attempts where the diagnostic lacks them. Legacy success receipts without an
   177	exact publisher attempt, unsupported schemas and absent diagnostics do not
   178	become success by inference. The protected publication ref need not still
   179	exist for this historical observation; live privileged writers must still
   180	perform their own final authority checks.
   181	
   182	Exit 0 means collection completed, not that publication passed. Missing or
   183	expired historical evidence can produce exit 0 with an explicit unverified
   184	relationship. Contradictory identities, access/transport errors, incomplete
   185	pagination, truncated diagnostics and changing attempts produce exit 1 with
   186	classified partial output. There is no automatic restart or recovery action.
   187	An unrelated child failure leaves an already authenticated publisher/validation
   188	link verified while marking collection incomplete, provided final parent
   189	identity checks still pass. Changes to either joined parent invalidate that
   190	relationship; an unreadable final parent makes it unverified. These checks
   191	also run after a collection failure, within the original read budget.
   192	Final checks bind immutable run/workflow/repository/ref/SHA identity and attempt.
   193	Same-attempt lifecycle or display changes do not invalidate that relationship;
   194	reported lifecycle values remain observations from their individual reads.
   195	
   196	Reads use the selected GitHub CLI credential route, explicit authenticated
   197	GETs, exact artifact metadata/digests and bounded ZIP inspection. Limits are
   198	three minutes overall, twenty seconds per request, 256 requests, 32 observed
   199	runs, eight attempts per validation child, ten pages of 100 records, 2 MiB per
   200	JSON response/archive, 1 MiB per expanded artifact (128 KiB for diagnostics),
   201	32 MiB cumulative response bytes and 256 KiB output. A limit is an incomplete
   202	observation, never proof of absence. If output is oversized, the reader retains
   203	authenticated publisher/validation linkage and surface observations, limits each
   204	job/package list to four entries with explicit omission counts, and marks any
   205	omitted validation detail. It does not replace known results with unknowns.
   206	No registry reads, candidate execution, reruns, dispatches or release mutations
   207	occur.
   208	
   209	### Post-merge continuation proof
   210	
   211	For a registry-admitted publish parent, continuation authenticates the original
   212	attempt-one plan and its successful guarded upload before any rerun or dispatch.
   213	The retry restores cached bytes against the digest recorded after the original
   214	guarded upload, then re-uploads the same plan and admission for its consumers.
   215	It does not recollect public registry state, restamp admission time, or replace
   216	the observation artifact descriptor embedded in the admission. Historical
   217	registry-admitted parents without the digest-witness contract cannot continue;
   218	the controller refuses before rerunning children or the parent. Their surviving
   219	artifacts remain readable for strict verification. Updating the local controller
   220	cannot retrofit their frozen workflow. Historical parents keep their exact frozen contract;
   221	nonpublish parents carry no publication admission. Existing refusals for
   222	parent-owned artifacts and incomplete child identities still apply.
   223	
   224	Use the non-release `FRV Proof Broker` and `FRV Proof Fixture` workflows only
   225	after the reviewed SHA lands on protected `main`. The fixture contains one
   226	fixed no-op job that intentionally fails on attempt one and passes on attempt
   227	two. The broker validates the exact maintainer, merged pull request, protected
   228	main SHA, fixture workflow, and run tuple before selecting the exact failed Actions job ID and invoking
   229	GitHub's job-rerun endpoint.
   230	Supply the merged pull request number and its exact landed commit. The broker
   231	requires the pull request to be merged into `main`, requires its recorded merge
   232	commit to equal that landed commit, and requires the landed commit to be
   233	identical to or an ancestor of the trusted broker workflow SHA. It repeats the
   234	maintainer, merged pull request, and ancestry checks immediately before the
   235	fixture rerun.
   236	
   237	Accept the hosted mutation proof only when the exact fixture run advances to
   238	attempt two and passes. The broker emits a receipt and must create no release
   239	candidate, release artifact, publication, repository ref, replacement parent,
   240	or other workflow mutation. This proves the GitHub targeted-job rerun boundary;
   241	the focused controller tests prove plan eligibility, green-attempt
   242	preservation, same-parent collection, and strict-verifier invocation. Do not
   243	use a real Full Release Validation run for this proof.
   244	
   245	The main-lineage requirement above applies to the initial validation tooling
   246	selection. Once release publication binds that Tooling SHA to an exact protected
   247	lightweight `release-publish/<12sha>-<provenance-run>` tag, the live tag-to-SHA
   248	mapping remains authoritative even when `main` advances. The suffix records
   249	tag-creation provenance, not the current parent run id. Publication must re-read
   250	that exact tag and revalidate the exact parent run tuple immediately before each
   251	core or plugin npm publish or dist-tag mutation. A missing, moved, annotated, or
   252	wrong-SHA tag, parent mismatch, or disallowed parent state fails closed. Other
   253	privileged writers require their dependent enforcement changes before the
   254	protected-tag publication route is globally complete.
   255	---
   256	doc-schema-version: 1
   257	summary: "Dispatching Full Release Validation: Code SHA and Tooling SHA selection, helper inputs, and the immutable execution plan"
   258	title: "Dispatch a validation run"
   259	read_when:
   260	  - Starting a Full Release Validation run
   261	  - Selecting the Code SHA, Release SHA, and Tooling SHA
   262	---
   263	
   264	`Full Release Validation` is the release product-validation umbrella. Most work
   265	happens in child workflows so a failed box can be rerun without restarting the
   266	whole release. Run release preparation before freezing the Code SHA; it
   267	refreshes Control UI locale output when the background bot has not landed it
   268	yet, then enforces the same strict zero-fallback check used by release CI.
   269	
   270	Generated-locale drift is a warning before dispatch, not a reason to refuse
   271	validation. Source PRs and the serialized locale-refresh workflows land
   272	separately, so generated output can temporarily lag. Record any preflight drift
   273	against the frozen target SHA and continue dispatch. The normal-CI child still
   274	runs strict `control-ui-i18n` and `native-i18n` jobs; their failures remain visible
   275	in the run summary and fail validation. PR-side locale checks, release preparation,
   276	and publication requirements are unchanged.
   277	
   278	Selected Linux, Windows, and macOS fresh-install and upgrade lanes gate
   279	validation in every release profile. Their actual conclusions remain in the
   280	manifest and summary. Test failures require a fix or an explicit operator waiver;
   281	release profiles do not automatically downgrade failed tests to advisory results.
   282	Normal CI, npm qualification, Docker, Package Acceptance, and selected performance
   283	and soak checks retain their gates.
   284	
   285	Prepare the complete history manifest and substantive version-matched release
   286	notes before freezing the product-complete commit and its target context as the
   287	**Code SHA/ref**. Package source preflight requires a matching release section;
   288	an empty placeholder is not preparation. If the notes are final, this commit
   289	can also be the **Release SHA**. Select one trusted workflow commit and context
   290	as the **Tooling SHA/ref**, then run:
   291	
   292	```bash
   293	TOOLING_SHA="<recorded-full-main-ancestor-sha>"
   294	PUBLICATION_SELECTION='{"route":"normal","npmDistTag":"latest","publishOpenclawNpm":true,"pluginPublishScope":"all-publishable","plugins":[]}'
   295	pnpm ci:full-release \
   296	  --sha <code-sha> \
   297	  --target-ref release/YYYY.M.PATCH \
   298	  --workflow-sha "$TOOLING_SHA" \
   299	  -f validation_purpose=publish \
   300	  -f publication_selection_json="$PUBLICATION_SELECTION"
   301	```
   302	
   303	This example selects normal final-release publication. Select `npmDistTag=beta`
   304	for a beta, or `route=prepared` when the intended consumer is the prepared release
   305	button. Core publication requires `all-publishable` plugins. Plugin-only normal
   306	publication can set `publishOpenclawNpm=false`, `pluginPublishScope=selected`,
   307	and explicit canonical package names in `plugins`.
   308	
   309	Every fresh run requires an explicit `validation_purpose`. `publish` verifies
   310	complete committed source metadata before projecting the requested publication
   311	selection and before resolution can release any selected producer. Its retained
   312	source-admission fact is **source-only**, not registry eligibility, product-test
   313	success, or publication authority. `diagnostic`, `main-qualification`, and
   314	`postpublish-confidence` omit `publication_selection_json` and record publication
   315	source admission as not applicable. Coverage is selected independently.
   316	
   317	Fresh publish requests also require tooling with registry admission. After
   318	source verification, resolution collects bounded public npm and ClawHub
   319	observations for the selected packages, uploads them, then binds the immutable
   320	artifact and admission time before producers can start. Required read errors and
   321	unsupported bootstrap states block admission; latest-dependency drift is advisory.
   322	Supported first-package or trust-repair routes retain unresolved downstream owner
   323	authority, not permission to publish. The source fact remains source-only.
   324	Nonpublish requests do not collect registry observations.
   325	
   326	The SHA-pinned helper packs its semantic `-f validation_purpose` and
   327	`-f publication_selection_json` arguments into the existing
   328	`trusted_workflow_json` input. Raw workflow dispatch uses this closed envelope:
   329	`{"trustedWorkflow":{"ref":"main","fullRef":"refs/heads/main","sha":"<tooling-sha>"},"validationPurpose":"diagnostic","publicationSelection":null}`.
   330	For the existing direct branch route, `trustedWorkflow:null` lets the identity
   331	owner infer the executing identity; purpose is always explicit. Child workflows
   332	still receive only the resolved identity tuple. Existing request artifacts reopen
   333	without converting their inputs or witness digests.
   334	
   335	`pnpm release:candidate` defaults to the normal publication route. Choose
   336	`--publication-route prepared` before its first dispatch for the prepared button;
   337	merely supplying a protected tooling ref does not select that route. Saved state
   338	binds the choice and rejects contradictory resumes. Historical state without a
   339	route retains normal recovery semantics and gains no source-admission claim.
   340	For registry-admitted parents, the checklist reads authenticated retained planning
   341	summaries instead of repeating the two local registry sweeps. Preparation and
   342	publication compare that evidence with their actual selected operands; a normal
   343	parent cannot authorize the prepared route by changing the command afterward.
   344	
   345	Record the candidate SHA/ref and Tooling SHA/ref once for the release and reuse
   346	them for later Code-SHA, Release-SHA, and focused reruns. Main lineage
   347	authorizes the initial Tooling SHA selection; it does not authorize refreshing
   348	the tooling from moving `main`.
   349	
   350	## Exact frozen-target test omissions
   351	
   352	Declare narrowly justified omissions before dispatch with JSON arrays of exact
   353	repository-relative test paths. `plugin_prerelease_node_exclude_patterns_json`
   354	applies to the Plugin Prerelease Node lane, for example
   355	`["src/plugins/manifest-registry.test.ts"]`.
   356	`extension_test_exclude_patterns_json` applies to the Plugin Prerelease extension
   357	shards, for example
   358	`["extensions/codex/src/app-server/run-attempt.test.ts"]`. Both default to `[]`;
   359	there is no implicit Codex test omission. Normal CI keeps its own core lanes;
   360	Plugin Prerelease owns the full extension sweep.
   361	
   362	Pass them through the SHA-pinned helper as `-f name='["exact/path.test.ts"]'`.
   363	The helper packs the extension input into the existing trusted dispatch envelope
   364	to stay within GitHub's 25-input limit, and refuses tooling without the matching
   365	lane-input capability before creating remote refs or dispatching.
   366	
   367	Preflight rejects malformed, duplicate, nonexistent, and out-of-lane paths using
   368	the selected target's actual Vitest discovery. Globs and basenames are not
   369	accepted. Pinned tooling applies each exact omission to the executing config's
   370	inline projects, preserves the candidate's normal test runner and setup, and
   371	restores the original config bytes after the command. It does not depend on a
   372	new exclusion environment variable being supported by the frozen candidate.
   373	
   374	The immutable request, coverage identity, evidence reuse comparison, and final
   375	manifest retain both input values. Changing them requires a new validation
   376	request; continuation cannot widen an existing omission. Record the reason and
   377	owning fix for each omitted test in release evidence. An omission is untested
   378	coverage, not passing evidence.
   379	
   380	## Retain and reconcile the root request
   381	
   382	Before creating remote refs, the helper writes a private operator artifact at
   383	`.artifacts/full-release-validation/<request-id>.json` and prints its path.
   384	Use `--request-file <path>` to choose the artifact location. It retains the
   385	repository, workflow, frozen target/tooling identities, transport refs, complete
   386	typed/defaulted inputs, effective soak, and the first observed run and attempt.
   387	The helper records attempted intent before its single workflow dispatch POST.
   388	
   389	After a lost response or interruption, reuse that exact artifact:
   390	
   391	```bash
   392	node scripts/full-release-validation-at-sha.mjs \
   393	  --reconcile-request .artifacts/full-release-validation/<request-id>.json
   394	```
   395	
   396	An existing `--request-file` also enters read-only reconciliation; conflicting
   397	target, tooling, or input arguments are rejected. Recovery performs no ref
   398	creation/deletion, dispatch, rerun, cancellation, Git fetch, or request rewrite.
   399	`dispatch=observed` reports the exact run URL and attempt, not successful
   400	validation. A newer attempt cannot replace the retained attempt.
   401	
   402	Missing or ambiguous runs, incomplete pagination, unavailable or mismatched input
   403	witnesses, and exhausted discovery remain `dispatch=unknown`. A complete HTTP
   404	rejection is retained as `dispatch=rejected`; neither state permits redispatch.
   405	Keep the artifact and printed refs for investigation. There is no automatic
   406	retention expiry or cleanup for the local artifact; remove it only through
   407	deliberate operator cleanup. Losing or deleting it never proves non-execution.
   408	Independent requests and copies on other hosts are not globally deduplicated.
   409	
   410	New requests require `FULL_RELEASE_SOURCE_ADMISSION_CONTRACT=1` and
   411	`FULL_RELEASE_DISPATCH_WITNESS_CONTRACT=1` in the pinned
   412	workflow. Older frozen tooling fails before remote creation instead of starting
   413	work whose inputs cannot be proven. The helper never upgrades the Tooling SHA.
   414	Use `frv status` for already-running frozen validations; choosing different
   415	tooling for a new validation requires the release owner's explicit decision.
   416	The workflow's separate input witness is attempt-bound and retained for seven
   417	days. It reads the event file directly, without interpolating inputs into step
   418	environment variables or logs. Only safe GitHub context and a SHA-256 digest are
   419	uploaded: input keys sorted lexicographically, primitive values normalized to
   420	wire strings, then JSON serialization. The immutable workflow SHA binds the
   421	input types; the complete typed and wire maps remain in the private local
   422	artifact. Runner or artifact-service failure can leave the witness unavailable;
   423	it is never a release receipt or publication authority.
   424	
   425	## Select coverage
   426	
   427	`provider` also accepts `anthropic` or `minimax` for cross-OS onboarding and the
   428	end-to-end agent turn. Regular `release/*` targets accept the branch's final
   429	package version or a matching beta prerelease. For a correction, use
   430	`--target-ref release/YYYY.M.PATCH-N` to preserve the intended final tag before
   431	tagging. Its base package version is also accepted when `vYYYY.M.PATCH` resolves
   432	to the exact Code SHA; preparation retains the package version and seals both
   433	npm and Docker artifacts for `vYYYY.M.PATCH-N`. Tideclaw alpha validation uses
   434	its exact alpha tag and matching alpha branch. The helper maps beta releases and
   435	exact alpha tags to the `beta` profile and final versions to `stable`. Pass
   436	alternate workflow inputs with `-f key=value`; use `-f release_profile=full`
   437	only for the broad provider sweep.
   438	`fail_fast` defaults to `false`, so dispatched child workflows finish and expose
   439	independent failures together. In that mode, the parent makes no child
   440	cancellation calls. Pass `-f fail_fast=true` only when the shorter
   441	first-failure path is preferable; Release Decision then cancels only the exact
   442	still-active child that owns the blocking failure.
   443	Same-parent continuation requires the original root to have been dispatched
   444	with `fail_fast=false`. The controller verifies that exact logged input before
   445	any rerun mutation.
   446	Current runs dispatch standalone `Full Release Artifacts` producers for npm,
   447	Docker, and the validation candidate. Each producer owns its immutable dispatch
   448	record and output receipt. Parent retries recover those exact producer IDs and
   449	attempts, recheck their source and Tooling SHAs, and reuse the successful builds.
   450	Historical parents that produced their own candidate or publication artifacts
   451	cannot continue: keep both SHAs frozen and start a fresh all-group validation.
   452	
   453	Automatic test retries are disabled. Dispatch rejects `known_flaky_jobs_json`;
   454	remove that retired input and investigate the original job failure. Explicit
   455	operator recovery remains available after diagnosis through
   456	[continuation commands](/reference/full-release-validation/continuation).
   457	
   458	After dispatch, the parent writes one immutable
   459	`full-release-execution-plan-<run-id>` artifact and preserves the same bytes in
   460	an exact run-ID Actions cache. It records selected and
   461	required coverage, gate results, reuse identity, the original parent attempt,
   462	the fresh candidate request plus producer and publisher evidence when preparation ran, and
   463	every exact child run ID, attempt, title, workflow ref, and Tooling SHA.
   464	Decision, Drain, manifest generation, evidence verification, and the final
   465	verifier consume the artifact for their current attempt. After the original
   466	guarded upload succeeds, the sealer records the plan digest in its job log.
   467	Collector retries restore the exact run-ID cache before publication admission,
   468	authenticate its bytes against that original upload and digest, and re-upload
   469	the unchanged plan and admission for the current attempt. GitHub removes prior
   470	parent artifacts on a full rerun, so retain the cache and original job logs.
   471	If the cache is unavailable, an accessible plan artifact can supply the same
   472	authenticated bytes. Missing or invalid evidence fails closed; retries never
   473	rebuild the plan, recollect registry observations, or redispatch tests.
   474	Release Decision also repeats canonical reuse-chain validation before a reused
   475	run can pass. The sealed target SHA, evidence SHA, policy, changed-path set,
   476	selected run, root run, source manifest, trusted tooling identity, and child
   477	tuple must all still match.
   478	
   479	On a parent retry, final verification selects the newest available Release
   480	Decision and Diagnostic Drain artifacts independently. Both must bind the same
   481	immutable plan and exact child tuple; their source attempts remain recorded in
   482	the artifacts and may differ when only one collector needed a retry.
   483	---
   484	doc-schema-version: 1
   485	summary: "Which Full Release Validation evidence to retain, advisory lane handling, and the backing workflow files"
   486	title: "Evidence to keep"
   487	read_when:
   488	  - Recording release evidence after a validation pass
   489	---
   490	
   491	## Evidence to keep
   492	
   493	Keep the `Full Release Validation` summary as the release-level index. It links
   494	child run ids and includes slowest-job tables. Classify failures as product,
   495	harness/tooling/provenance, infrastructure/credential, or wrapper. Only a
   496	confirmed product failure changes the Code SHA. Use one diagnosis, one fix when
   497	needed, and one narrow retry, then reassess; do not automatically rerun `all`.
   498	Narrow evidence is not publish authorization by itself.
   499	
   500	Read any **advisory** entries in `release-ci-summary` alongside Release Decision.
   501	Only an explicit operator lane waiver can keep eligible failed jobs advisory;
   502	the manifest retains their actual conclusions and waiver reason. Keep those
   503	diagnostic artifacts for follow-up, and never report waived jobs as passed.
   504	Selected failures otherwise block validation across all platforms and profiles.
   505	
   506	For a regular release, record Code SHA and Release SHA even when they are the
   507	same commit. In that case, retain the successful full validation parent and
   508	its exact prepared publication artifacts for both roles. For a later
   509	changelog-only Release SHA using evidence reuse, also record the reuse policy,
   510	complete changed-path set, green Code SHA parent run, and Release SHA parent
   511	run. For extended-stable, record the canonical branch, exact release SHA,
   512	accepted producer identity, parent run id and attempt, workflow ref, every child run, and any
   513	frozen-target compatibility repair or intentional omission.
   514	
   515	Useful artifacts:
   516	
   517	- The attempt-one `full-release-execution-plan-<parent-run-id>` and current
   518	  `full-release-validation-<parent-run-id>-<attempt>` manifest retain source
   519	  facts and registry admission, including observations, exact selection,
   520	  immutable upload identity, and admission-time freshness. Reuse retains distinct
   521	  original and current records without restamping either. Authentication requires
   522	  the original successful admission and guarded upload, not an overall successful
   523	  sealer job: an interrupted sealer can still write and upload a complete plan.
   524	  Later attempts never replace that original artifact.
   525	- `release-package-under-test` from `OpenClaw Release Checks`
   526	- Docker release-path artifacts under `.artifacts/docker-tests/`
   527	- Package Acceptance `package-under-test` and Docker acceptance artifacts
   528	- Cross-OS release-check artifacts for each OS and suite
   529	- QA parity, runtime parity, and selected Matrix, Buzz, Telegram, Discord,
   530	  WhatsApp, or Slack artifacts
   531	
   532	Readers authenticate retained evidence; they do not refresh registry observations
   533	or expire it by the current clock. Registry admission is not publication authority
   534	or a promise that registry state is unchanged. Final writers keep their own trust,
   535	approval, content, and readback checks. Known enclosing evidence is budgeted before
   536	fanout, but later job/attempt growth can still exceed the unchanged final artifact
   537	limit and fail sealing; evidence is not truncated to fit.
   538	
   539	## Workflow files
   540	
   541	- `.github/workflows/full-release-validation.yml`
   542	- `.github/workflows/full-release-candidate.yml`
   543	- `.github/workflows/openclaw-release-checks.yml`
   544	- `.github/workflows/openclaw-live-and-e2e-checks-reusable.yml`
   545	- `.github/workflows/plugin-prerelease.yml`
   546	- `.github/workflows/install-smoke.yml`
   547	- `.github/workflows/install-smoke-reusable.yml`
   548	- `.github/workflows/openclaw-cross-os-release-checks-reusable.yml`
   549	- `.github/workflows/package-acceptance.yml`
   550	- `.github/workflows/openclaw-performance.yml`
   551	- `.github/workflows/npm-telegram-beta-e2e.yml`
   552	---
   553	doc-schema-version: 1
   554	summary: "Extended-stable validation dispatch, changelog-only Release SHA reuse, coverage policies, and Telegram deferrals and waivers"
   555	title: "Extended-stable and changelog-only validation"
   556	read_when:
   557	  - Validating an extended-stable candidate
   558	  - Reusing evidence for a changelog-only Release SHA
   559	  - Checking npm-beta-v1 and npm-stable-v1 coverage policies
   560	---
   561	
   562	## Extended-stable validation
   563	
   564	Extended-stable validation uses the same checked helper with an immutable
   565	trusted-main Tooling SHA. Keep the exact candidate, Tooling SHA, canonical
   566	context, and workflow transport separate:
   567	
   568	```bash
   569	VALIDATION_SHA="<exact-candidate-sha>"
   570	TOOLING_SHA="<recorded-full-main-ancestor-sha>"
   571	CONTEXT_REF="extended-stable/YYYY.M.33"
   572	pnpm ci:full-release \
   573	  --sha "$VALIDATION_SHA" \
   574	  --target-ref "$CONTEXT_REF" \
   575	  --workflow-sha "$TOOLING_SHA" \
   576	  -f validation_purpose=publish \
   577	  -f publication_selection_json='{"route":"extended-stable","npmDistTag":"extended-stable","publishOpenclawNpm":true,"pluginPublishScope":"all-publishable","plugins":[]}' \
   578	  -f release_profile=stable \
   579	  -f run_release_soak=true \
   580	  -f fail_fast=false \
   581	  -f rerun_group=all \
   582	  -f reuse_evidence=false \
   583	  -f dispatch_release_evidence=false
   584	```
   585	
   586	The helper creates `release-ci/<tooling-prefix>-<unique-id>` at the Tooling SHA,
   587	dispatches from that named branch, supplies the trusted-main identity, and uses
   588	the exact Validation SHA for both `ref` and `expected_sha` with the canonical
   589	branch in `target_context_ref`. GitHub workflow dispatch `--ref` accepts a branch
   590	or tag, not a raw SHA. Outside extended-stable validation, a direct
   591	canonical-branch dispatch is valid only when its head is also the trusted
   592	workflow implementation. Current extended-stable validation uses distinct
   593	trusted-main tooling and therefore requires the immutable helper.
   594	
   595	The shared publisher requires this canonical `release-ci/*` producer and binds
   596	its trusted workflow SHA separately from the exact candidate SHA. Its protected
   597	`release-publish/*` ref does not replace the canonical candidate branch. Retain
   598	the complete `rerun_group=all` manifest, exact run ID and successful attempt;
   599	reject direct canonical-branch/main producers, narrow runs, stale attempts, and
   600	mismatched targets. If a reviewed tooling repair changes the publication SHA,
   601	the validation tooling must remain reachable from current `main` and all
   602	candidate and evidence identities must still match.
   603	
   604	Backport product failures; make the smallest behavior-preserving repair for
   605	frozen-target tooling; retry provider, approval, or runner failures without a
   606	source change. Any branch change needs a complete new run. Do not omit required
   607	package, installer, update, channel, or live behavior because the target is old.
   608	
   609	For a regular release whose qualified Code SHA already contains final notes,
   610	use that same commit as the **Release SHA**. Retain its successful full
   611	validation parent and exact prepared publication artifacts; no extra commit or
   612	validation run is needed solely to separate those roles.
   613	
   614	If notes change after qualification, commit the selected release entry and its
   615	permitted record/index updates as a new
   616	Release SHA and run the same helper for that commit. Product evidence reuse is
   617	optional and requires GitHub to prove that the Release SHA descends from the
   618	green Code SHA. The complete changed path set must include
   619	`CHANGELOG/YYYY.M.PATCH.md` and may also include only `CHANGELOG.md` and
   620	`CHANGELOG/records/YYYY.M.PATCH.md`. Entry/record additions or modifications are
   621	allowed; the root index may only be modified. Renames, deletions, other release
   622	files, and docs source edits do not qualify. This path records
   623	`split-changelog-release-v1`; historical root-only receipts retain their
   624	`changelog-only-release-v1` contract. Reuse still qualifies the changed
   625	package and image bytes. Any other source change returns to full Code
   626	validation. See [Releasing](/reference/RELEASING) for the publication sequence.
   627	
   628	The conceptual phases map to current inputs:
   629	
   630	- `beta-publish`: `release_profile=beta`, `run_release_soak=false`
   631	- `postpublish-confidence`: `validation_purpose=postpublish-confidence`, no publication selection, exact published package plus
   632	  `run_release_soak=true` or explicit focused groups
   633	- `stable-publish`: `release_profile=stable`
   634	
   635	For an actual beta package on its matching canonical release branch or beta
   636	tag, `all` with `release_profile=beta` and no soak records
   637	`coveragePolicy=npm-beta-v1`. It retains Linux, macOS, and Windows Node checks,
   638	Control UI, plugins, package integrity, install/update acceptance, Linux cross-OS
   639	package checks, QA parity, core runtime-pair/restart proof, and runtime tool
   640	coverage. Native app qualification, product performance, and published-package
   641	Telegram confidence are deferred. Broad live/E2E and QA-live also remain outside
   642	this bounded gate.
   643	
   644	Run deferred confidence against the exact published beta with
   645	`run_release_soak=true`, or select `ci`, `performance`, `npm-telegram`,
   646	`package`, or the relevant QA/live group explicitly. Selected children must
   647	still finish and pass their existing policy; a deferred check is **not run**,
   648	never passed. Stable, full, soak-enabled, and focused validation retain their
   649	existing confidence coverage. `main`, alpha, and non-beta targets do not qualify
   650	for `npm-beta-v1`.
   651	
   652	For a regular final package on its matching release branch or tag, `all` with
   653	`release_profile=stable` records `coveragePolicy=npm-stable-v1` and uses CI's
   654	`npm-stable` scope. Numeric corrections qualify, including an unchanged base
   655	package whose base tag resolves to the same source SHA. This policy defers only
   656	macOS Swift/OpenClawKit, iOS/Watch, Android, and native i18n. Linux, macOS, and
   657	Windows Node coverage, Control UI, plugins, package and installer acceptance,
   658	QA, stable soak, and blocking product performance remain required. Extended-stable,
   659	`main` without release context, `full`, and explicit `ci` runs retain full CI.
   660	Evidence reuse requires the same coverage policy, exact package version, target
   661	context, and effective inputs; a beta or historical full receipt cannot silently
   662	replace stable npm qualification.
   663	
   664	Native publication owns the deferred qualification. For an npm-stable release,
   665	`OpenClaw Release Publish` starts an exact-source full CI run with Android enabled
   666	in parallel with core publication. Only successful native qualification and core
   667	publication permit the separate Android job to issue a v3 approval receipt and
   668	dispatch the tag-owned APK publisher. The receipt binds the exact native CI run,
   669	attempt, and tooling ref. The publisher rechecks that proof before writing approval,
   670	immediately before dispatch, before APK attestation, and before each asset upload.
   671	Frozen tags without the v3 consumer, including `v2026.8.2` and its same-source
   672	corrections, must use `release_profile=full`; publication rejects npm-only evidence
   673	for those targets before starting core publication. Their historical full-validation
   674	route retains the v2 approval contract. Native failures are
   675	recorded and block Android approval, while core npm publication and GitHub
   676	release finalization remain independent. The parent may remain active for native
   677	work after core publication completes. macOS retains its separate native
   678	validation, signing, notarization, and promotion gates. Stable npm publication
   679	still rejects evidence without soak and blocking product performance.
   680	
   681	macOS app signing, notarization, appcast publication, and Windows Hub asset
   682	promotion run in parallel with or after npm publication and never delay npm.
   683	Their own artifact validation and promotion gates still apply; Windows Hub
   684	assets remain required before the regular GitHub release leaves draft.
   685	
   686	Package Acceptance normally builds the candidate tarball from the resolved
   687	`ref`, including full-SHA runs dispatched with `pnpm ci:full-release`. After a
   688	beta publish, pass `release_package_spec=openclaw@YYYY.M.PATCH-beta.N` to reuse
   689	the shipped npm package across release checks, Package Acceptance, cross-OS,
   690	release-path Docker, and package Telegram. Use `package_acceptance_package_spec`
   691	only when Package Acceptance should intentionally prove a different package.
   692	The Codex plugin live package lane follows the same state: published
   693	`release_package_spec` values derive `codex_plugin_spec=npm:@openclaw/codex@<version>`;
   694	SHA/artifact runs pack `extensions/codex` from the selected ref; and operators
   695	can set `codex_plugin_spec` directly for `npm:`, `npm-pack:`, or `git:` plugin
   696	sources. The lane grants the explicit Codex CLI install approval required by
   697	that plugin, then runs Codex CLI preflight and same-session OpenAI agent turns.
   698	Its final zero-retry, medium-thinking turn sends visible progress with omitted
   699	Codex `final`, reads randomized workspace inputs, writes their exact artifact,
   700	and sends explicit completion. This catches the v2026.7.1 regression where an
   701	ordinary progress send terminated the turn.
   702	
   703	Telegram release tests are best effort in every release profile. Selected source
   704	and package lanes still attempt the real Test Server flow when a Convex credential
   705	is available. They use the canonical 90-second lease-acquisition retry budget;
   706	missing broker access, an exhausted pool, or failed tests remain visible as
   707	failures or skips in the job summaries and evidence, but never block release
   708	validation. Assertions, credential isolation, lease cleanup, and exact candidate
   709	identity checks remain unchanged. A successful release decision does not imply
   710	that Telegram passed; inspect the recorded Telegram outcome separately.
   711	
   712	Package Acceptance Telegram E2E is automatically deferred for every beta-profile
   713	`all` run without soak, including beta-profile checks of `main` or alpha targets.
   714	The effective `skip_package_telegram_e2e=true` is captured in the inputs and
   715	summary as **not run**. Soak-enabled runs and explicit `rerun_group=package`
   716	keep Telegram selected by default. The existing
   717	`-f skip_package_telegram_e2e=true` input remains available for an explicit beta
   718	deferral; it is rejected for `stable` and `full` and does not disable the focused
   719	`rerun_group=npm-telegram` workflow.
   720	
   721	Best effort is separate from an explicit omission. The reviewed exceptions are
   722	`-f telegram_waiver=2026.8.1-owner-approved` and
   723	`-f telegram_waiver=2026.9.1-owner-approved`. Any future exception requires a
   724	reviewed code change; a matching `<target-version>-owner-approved` string alone
   725	is not authorization. The value must name the validated target's actual
   726	`package.json` version, the sealed candidate version must match, and the profile
   727	must be `stable` or `full`. Beta, prerelease, and unlisted targets are rejected.
   728	Package-spec overrides must be exactly `openclaw@<target-version>`; blank specs
   729	select the sealed candidate.
   730	It omits source Telegram QA, Package Acceptance Telegram E2E, and the
   731	published-package Telegram E2E; their evidence states **waived / not run**,
   732	never passed. Telegram unit tests and every other selected gate remain active,
   733	including stable soak and performance checks. An explicit Telegram rerun or
   734	suite filter, including an aggregate such as `qa-live` or `qa-live-non-slack`
   735	that selects Telegram, conflicts with the waiver and is rejected. The declaration and
   736	target version bind the immutable execution plan, manifest, and reuse identity;
   737	the publisher carries the waiver into release verification notes. The beta-only
   738	package deferral above remains unchanged.
   739	
   740	Source Telegram QA uses the release checks' shared context check: an exact candidate
   741	SHA must remain an ancestor of its canonical branch, or equal its release tag.
   742	Both build and execution admission independently repeat that check and retain
   743	candidate-version, signature/merge-attribution, and live maintainer checks.
   744	Advancing a release branch does not select a new candidate or invalidate the old one.
   745	---
   746	doc-schema-version: 1
   747	summary: "Release profile coverage, full-only additions, and the focused rerun handles and suite filters"
   748	title: "Release profiles and focused reruns"
   749	read_when:
   750	  - Comparing beta, stable, and full profile coverage
   751	  - Picking a focused rerun_group or suite filter
   752	---
   753	
   754	## Release profiles
   755	
   756	`release_profile` controls live/provider breadth inside release checks. The
   757	bounded canonical beta gate described in [Extended-stable and changelog-only
   758	validation](/reference/full-release-validation/extended-stable) also selects npm-focused CI and
   759	defers performance and Telegram confidence. Plugin Prerelease, install smoke,
   760	package acceptance, and QA parity remain selected. Stable and full profiles always run exhaustive
   761	repo/live E2E, Docker release-path, and QA-live soak coverage. The beta profile
   762	adds those lanes only with `run_release_soak=true`, an explicit `qa-live`
   763	controller retry, or the direct child's manual `qa` aggregate. Package
   764	Acceptance supplies the canonical package Telegram E2E when selected; beta
   765	`all` without soak defers it to confidence work.
   766	
   767	| Profile  | Intended use                      | Included live/provider coverage                                                                                                                                                                            |
   768	| -------- | --------------------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   769	| `beta`   | Fastest release-critical smoke.   | OpenAI/core live path, Docker live models for OpenAI, native gateway core, native OpenAI gateway profile, native OpenAI plugin, and Docker live gateway OpenAI.                                            |
   770	| `stable` | Default release approval profile. | `beta` plus Anthropic smoke, Google, MiniMax, backend, native live test harness, Docker live CLI backend, Docker ACP bind, Docker Codex harness, Docker subagent-announce, and an OpenCode Go smoke shard. |
   771	| `full`   | Broad provider sweep.             | `stable` plus additional providers, plugin live shards, and media live shards.                                                                                                                             |
   772	
   773	## Full-only additions
   774	
   775	These suites are skipped by `stable` and included by `full`:
   776	
   777	| Area                             | Full-only coverage                                                                                                          |
   778	| -------------------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   779	| Docker live models               | OpenCode Go, OpenRouter, xAI, Z.ai, and Fireworks.                                                                          |
   780	| Docker live gateway              | Advisory providers split into DeepSeek/Fireworks, OpenCode Go/OpenRouter, and xAI/Z.ai shards.                              |
   781	| Native gateway provider profiles | Full Anthropic Opus and Sonnet/Haiku shards, Fireworks, DeepSeek, full OpenCode Go model shards, OpenRouter, xAI, and Z.ai. |
   782	| Native plugin live shards        | Plugins A-K, L-N, O-Z other, Moonshot, and xAI.                                                                             |
   783	| Native media live shards         | Audio, Google music, MiniMax music, and video groups A-D.                                                                   |
   784	
   785	`stable` includes `native-live-src-gateway-profiles-anthropic-smoke` and
   786	`native-live-src-gateway-profiles-opencode-go-smoke`; `full` uses the broader
   787	Anthropic and OpenCode Go model shards instead. Focused reruns can still use the
   788	aggregate `native-live-src-gateway-profiles-anthropic` or
   789	`native-live-src-gateway-profiles-opencode-go` handles.
   790	
   791	## Focused reruns
   792	
   793	Use `rerun_group` to avoid repeating unrelated release boxes:
   794	
   795	| Handle              | Scope                                                                                                                                               |
   796	| ------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   797	| `all`               | Profile-selected qualification; canonical beta without soak uses `npm-beta-v1`; regular stable uses `npm-stable-v1`, which defers native apps only. |
   798	| `ci`                | Manual full CI child only.                                                                                                                          |
   799	| `plugin-prerelease` | Plugin Prerelease child only.                                                                                                                       |
   800	| `install-smoke`     | Install Smoke through release checks.                                                                                                               |
   801	| `cross-os`          | Cross-OS release checks.                                                                                                                            |
   802	| `live-e2e`          | Repo/live E2E and Docker release-path validation.                                                                                                   |
   803	| `package`           | Package Acceptance.                                                                                                                                 |
   804	| `qa-parity`         | QA parity, runtime-pair/restart, and runtime tool coverage.                                                                                         |
   805	| `qa-live`           | QA live Matrix, Buzz, and Telegram plus gated Discord, WhatsApp, and Slack lanes when enabled.                                                      |
   806	| `npm-telegram`      | Published-package Telegram E2E; requires `release_package_spec` or `npm_telegram_package_spec`.                                                     |
   807	| `performance`       | Product performance evidence only.                                                                                                                  |
   808	
   809	Use `live_suite_filter` with `rerun_group=live-e2e` when one live suite failed.
   810	The former `release-checks` aggregate retry handle is invalid. It silently
   811	expanded to every release-check lane, including package and Docker setup. Pick
   812	one concrete group after classifying the failed surface.
   813	The umbrella/controller also rejects `qa`; direct `OpenClaw Release Checks`
   814	dispatches may use it only as a deliberate manual aggregate of `qa-parity` and
   815	`qa-live`. Live and QA-live filters must match their owning group; cross-OS
   816	filters are also accepted for `all`.
   817	Mismatches fail before scheduling and never widen to an unfiltered run.
   818	Valid filter ids are defined in the reusable live/E2E workflow, including
   819	`docker-live-models`, `live-gateway-docker`,
   820	`live-gateway-anthropic-docker`, `live-gateway-google-docker`,
   821	`live-gateway-minimax-docker`, `live-gateway-advisory-docker`,
   822	`live-cli-backend-docker`, `live-cli-cache-docker`, `live-acp-bind-docker`, and
   823	`live-codex-harness-docker`.
   824	
   825	For a focused QA transport rerun, set `rerun_group=qa-live` and use the
   826	canonical selector `qa-live-matrix`, `qa-live-buzz`, `qa-live-telegram`,
   827	`qa-live-discord`, `qa-live-whatsapp`, or `qa-live-slack`.
   828	
   829	The `live-gateway-advisory-docker` handle is an aggregate rerun handle for its
   830	three provider shards, so it still fans out to all additional Docker gateway jobs.
   831	
   832	Use `cross_os_suite_filter` with `rerun_group=cross-os` when one cross-OS lane
   833	failed. The filter accepts comma-separated OS ids, suite ids, or OS/suite pairs,
   834	for example `windows/packaged-upgrade`, `windows`, or `packaged-fresh`.
   835	All-group runs must keep every OS/suite pair: `-f cross_os_suite_filter=ubuntu,windows,macos`
   836	or `packaged-fresh,installer-fresh,packaged-upgrade` are accepted, while any all-group
   837	filter that omits one of the nine Linux/Windows/macOS install and upgrade pairs is
   838	rejected before scheduling. Windows/macOS outcomes are recorded as advisory; the Linux
   839	pairs are required proof.
   840	
   841	Selected QA, source and package Telegram, live-provider, Windows/macOS cross-OS,
   842	and performance failures are recorded as advisory during validation (Release
   843	Decision `- Advisory:` entries and `::warning` annotations); Linux Gateway
   844	cross-OS lanes and the other required proofs block. Publishing a stable with a
   845	recorded advisory failure, or without soak and blocking performance evidence,
   846	requires the operator waivers described in RELEASING.md "Publication modes";
   847	without them the publisher gates fail closed. Skipped or deferred attempts are
   848	never reported as passed. When
   849	`live_suite_filter` explicitly requests a gated QA live lane such as Discord,
   850	WhatsApp, or Slack, the matching `OPENCLAW_RELEASE_QA_*_LIVE_CI_ENABLED` repo
   851	variable must be enabled; otherwise input capture fails instead of silently skipping the lane.
   852	Use controller groups `qa-parity` or `qa-live` for fresh QA evidence. A direct
   853	manual `OpenClaw Release Checks` dispatch may use `qa` to aggregate both.
   854	---
   855	doc-schema-version: 1
   856	summary: "OpenClaw Release Checks stage matrix and the Docker release-path chunk list"
   857	title: "Release checks stages"
   858	read_when:
   859	  - Classifying a failed release-check surface
   860	  - Selecting a Docker release-path chunk
   861	---
   862	
   863	## Release checks stages
   864	
   865	`OpenClaw Release Checks` is the largest child workflow. It resolves the target
   866	once and validates the umbrella's shared package artifact when available. A
   867	direct or focused dispatch prepares its own `release-package-under-test`
   868	artifact when package or Docker-facing stages need it.
   869	
   870	| Stage                    | Details                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |
   871	| ------------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   872	| Release target           | **Job:** `Resolve target ref`<br />**Backing workflow:** none<br />**Tests:** selected ref, optional expected Validation SHA, profile, concrete release-check groups, and focused live suite filter.<br />**Rerun:** select the concrete group for the failed surface.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                      |
   873	| Package artifact         | **Job:** `Prepare release package artifact`<br />**Backing workflow:** none<br />**Tests:** validates the umbrella's immutable package tuple, or packs one candidate tarball for a direct/focused Release Checks dispatch, then exposes it to downstream package-facing checks.<br />**Rerun:** the affected package, cross-OS, or live/E2E group.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                          |
   874	| Install smoke            | **Job:** `Run install smoke`<br />**Backing workflow:** `Install Smoke`<br />**Tests:** full install path with root Dockerfile smoke image reuse, QR package install, root and gateway Docker smokes, installer Docker tests, and Bun global install plus CLI/local-agent/Gateway runtime smoke.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=install-smoke`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |
   875	| Cross-OS                 | **Job:** `cross_os_release_checks`<br />**Backing workflow:** `OpenClaw Cross-OS Release Checks (Reusable)`<br />**Tests:** fresh and upgrade lanes on Linux, Windows, and macOS for the selected provider and mode, using the candidate tarball plus a baseline package. Selected Linux, Windows, and macOS failures gate validation with recorded pass/fail conclusions.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=cross-os`.                                                                                                                                                                                          |
   876	| Repo and live E2E        | **Job:** `Run repo/live E2E validation`<br />**Backing workflow:** `OpenClaw Live And E2E Checks (Reusable)`<br />**Tests:** repository E2E, live cache, OpenAI websocket streaming, native live provider and plugin shards, and Docker-backed live model/backend/gateway harnesses selected by `release_profile`.<br />**Runs:** `run_release_soak=true`, `release_profile=full`, or focused `rerun_group=live-e2e`.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=live-e2e`, optionally with `live_suite_filter`.                                                                                                          |
   877	| Docker release path      | **Job:** `Run Docker release-path validation`<br />**Backing workflow:** `OpenClaw Live And E2E Checks (Reusable)`<br />**Tests:** release-path Docker chunks against the shared package artifact.<br />**Runs:** `run_release_soak=true`, `release_profile=full`, or focused `rerun_group=live-e2e`.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=live-e2e`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                               |
   878	| Package Acceptance       | **Job:** `Run package acceptance`<br />**Backing workflow:** `Package Acceptance`<br />**Tests:** offline plugin package fixtures, plugin update, and published-upgrade survivor checks against the same tarball. The canonical mock-OpenAI Telegram package E2E is deferred for beta `all` without soak; explicit `package` and soak select it by default. Blocking release checks use the default latest published baseline; soak checks (`run_release_soak=true`) resolve the latest stable baseline once and run the reported-issue upgrade fixtures against it.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=package`. |
   879	| Maturity scorecard       | **Job:** `Render maturity scorecard release docs`<br />**Backing workflow:** `maturity-scorecard.yml`<br />**Tests:** renders the advisory maturity scorecard docs against the target ref. Only runs when `run_maturity_scorecard=true` is passed.<br />**Rerun:** direct manual `rerun_group=qa` with `run_maturity_scorecard=true`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                       |
   880	| QA parity                | **Job:** `Run QA Lab parity lane` and `Run QA Lab parity report`<br />**Backing workflow:** direct jobs<br />**Tests:** candidate and baseline agentic parity packs, then the parity report.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=qa-parity`; direct manual child dispatch may aggregate with `qa`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |
   881	| QA runtime parity        | **Job:** `Verify QA Lab runtime-pair lanes`<br />**Backing workflow:** direct job<br />**Tests:** the canonical core `openclaw`/`codex` lane (`pnpm openclaw qa suite --runtime-pair openclaw,codex --runtime-pair-lane core`) and, with `run_release_soak=true`, the soak lane. Includes the OpenClaw core restart proof. The release verifier enforces the recorded lane status.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=qa-parity`; direct manual child dispatch may aggregate with `qa`.                                                                                                                           |
   882	| QA runtime tool coverage | **Job:** `Enforce QA Lab runtime tool coverage`<br />**Backing workflow:** direct job<br />**Tests:** dynamic tool drift between `openclaw` and `codex` in the canonical core runtime-pair lane (`pnpm openclaw qa coverage --tools`), using that lane's output. Blocking: this job is not advisory-overridable.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=qa-parity`; direct manual child dispatch may aggregate with `qa`.                                                                                                                                                                                             |
   883	| QA live Matrix           | **Job:** `Run QA Live Matrix catalog`<br />**Backing workflow:** `QA-Lab - All Lanes` reusable workflow<br />**Tests:** catalog-derived YAML scenarios through the shared Matrix live adapter in the `qa-live-shared` environment, distributed across deterministic shards.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=qa-live` with `live_suite_filter=qa-live-matrix`; direct manual child dispatch may aggregate with `qa`.                                                                                                                                                                                            |
   884	| QA live Buzz             | **Job:** `Run QA Lab live Buzz lane`<br />**Backing workflow:** `QA-Lab - All Lanes` reusable workflow<br />**Tests:** signed canary and mention-gating round trips through the real Buzz plugin using dedicated Convex-leased identities and a hosted relay room.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=qa-live` with `live_suite_filter=qa-live-buzz`; direct manual child dispatch may aggregate with `qa`.                                                                                                                                                                                                       |
   885	| QA live Telegram         | **Job:** `Run QA Lab live Telegram lane`<br />**Backing workflow:** trusted `OpenClaw Release Telegram QA` dispatch<br />**Tests:** live Telegram QA with Convex CI credential leases.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=qa-live`; direct manual child dispatch may aggregate with `qa`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |
   886	| QA live Discord          | **Job:** `Run QA Lab live Discord lane`<br />**Backing workflow:** direct job with recorded status enforced by the release verifier<br />**Tests:** live Discord QA with Convex CI credential leases when `OPENCLAW_RELEASE_QA_DISCORD_LIVE_CI_ENABLED` is enabled.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=qa-live` with `live_suite_filter=qa-live-discord`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |
   887	| QA live WhatsApp         | **Job:** `Run QA Lab live WhatsApp lane`<br />**Backing workflow:** direct job with recorded status enforced by the release verifier<br />**Tests:** live WhatsApp QA with Convex CI credential leases when `OPENCLAW_RELEASE_QA_WHATSAPP_LIVE_CI_ENABLED` is enabled.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=qa-live` with `live_suite_filter=qa-live-whatsapp`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                     |
   888	| QA live Slack            | **Job:** `Run QA Lab live Slack lane`<br />**Backing workflow:** direct job with recorded status enforced by the release verifier<br />**Tests:** live Slack QA with Convex CI credential leases when `OPENCLAW_RELEASE_QA_SLACK_LIVE_CI_ENABLED` is enabled.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=qa-live` with `live_suite_filter=qa-live-slack`.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                 |
   889	| Release verifier         | **Job:** `Verify release checks`<br />**Backing workflow:** none<br />**Tests:** required release-check jobs for the selected rerun group.<br />**Rerun:** rerun after focused child jobs pass.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                             |
   890	
   891	## Docker release-path chunks
   892	
   893	The Docker release-path stage runs these chunks when `live_suite_filter` is
   894	empty:
   895	
   896	| Chunk                                                           | Coverage                                                                                                                                    |
   897	| --------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
   898	| `core`                                                          | Core Docker release-path smoke lanes.                                                                                                       |
   899	| `package-update-openai`                                         | OpenAI package and tool-call proof, Codex on-demand install and live progress, root-managed VPS upgrades, and authenticated update restart. |
   900	| `package-update-onboarding`                                     | Channel onboarding, install switching, and skill installation.                                                                              |
   901	| `package-update-migrations`                                     | Channel switching and published-package upgrade survival.                                                                                   |
   902	| `package-update-self-upgrade`                                   | Local upgrade survival and compatibility with published September update drivers.                                                           |
   903	| `plugins-runtime-plugins`                                       | Plugin runtime lanes that exercise plugin behavior.                                                                                         |
   904	| `plugins-runtime-services`                                      | Service-backed and live plugin runtime lanes.                                                                                               |
   905	| `plugins-runtime-install-a` through `plugins-runtime-install-h` | Plugin install/runtime batches split for parallel release validation.                                                                       |
   906	| `openwebui`                                                     | OpenWebUI compatibility smoke isolated on a dedicated large-disk runner when requested.                                                     |
   907	
   908	All four package/update rows retain their coverage across every release profile.
   909	The provider-neutral checks are balanced across three runners, preserving each
   910	runner's npm limit. `package-update-core` and `package-update` remain aggregate
   911	manual chunk names. Root-managed VPS upgrade and authenticated restart checks
   912	run in the OpenAI row.
   913	Missing required credentials still fail the job; the diagnostic pool continues
   914	so independent non-live checks also report their results. Setup failures and
   915	cancellation do not start that pool.
   916	
   917	Expanded published-upgrade survivor and update-migration coverage runs in
   918	baseline-specific groups of at most three scenarios, with up to 32 targeted
   919	Docker jobs active per matrix. The grouping and execution planners share the
   920	same baseline compatibility rules; package identities, fresh scenario
   921	containers, per-runner npm limits, and failure reporting remain unchanged.
   922	
   923	Use targeted `docker_lanes=<lane[,lane]>` on the reusable live/E2E workflow when
   924	only one Docker lane failed. The release artifacts include per-lane rerun
   925	commands with package artifact and image reuse inputs when available.
   926	---
   927	doc-schema-version: 1
   928	summary: "The Full Release Validation umbrella stage matrix, evidence reuse, artifact producers, and decision states"
   929	title: "Top-level stages"
   930	read_when:
   931	  - Reading the umbrella stage matrix
   932	  - Debugging a Release Decision or Diagnostic Drain state
   933	---
   934	
   935	## Top-level stages
   936	
   937	For `rerun_group=all`, a `Check for reusable validation evidence` job runs
   938	first. It looks for the newest prior green full validation with the same release
   939	profile, coverage policy, effective soak setting, and validation inputs. Exact-target reruns use
   940	`exact-target-full-validation-v1`. A descendant whose complete delta includes
   941	the selected `CHANGELOG/YYYY.M.PATCH.md` and only that entry, its matching
   942	`CHANGELOG/records/YYYY.M.PATCH.md`, and root `CHANGELOG.md` uses
   943	`split-changelog-release-v1`. Entry/record additions or modifications and index
   944	modifications are allowed; renames, deletions, other releases, and docs source
   945	edits are rejected. Historical root-only receipts retain
   946	`changelog-only-release-v1`. Under either changelog policy, every product lane is skipped
   947	and the verifier independently rechecks the GitHub commit comparison, immutable
   948	parent artifact, child runs, and dispatch logs. Any other target change requires
   949	a fresh Code SHA validation. Pass `reuse_evidence=false` to force a fresh full
   950	run. Evidence reuse runs only from `main` or a canonical SHA-pinned
   951	`release-ci/*` ref whose workflow commit remains on trusted `main` lineage;
   952	other workflow refs run the selected lanes fresh.
   953	
   954	The reuse search checks each bound parent manifest for eligibility before
   955	loading its child runs, job logs, and execution plan. Incompatible profiles,
   956	inputs, targets, and non-root runs are rejected early. Eligible candidates
   957	still undergo complete provenance and attempt verification before reuse.
   958	The verifier reads independent children concurrently (at most seven), retains
   959	each attempt's job data for its policy checks, and waits for all reads before
   960	reporting success or failure. Attempts and pagination within each child remain
   961	sequential. Target resolution and reuse checkouts include only their tooling
   962	and release metadata; neither needs the complete source tree.
   963	
   964	Full validation starts independent npm and Docker producer runs through
   965	`full-release-artifacts.yml`. The read-only `openclaw-npm-preflight.yml` starts
   966	source, SDK, dependency, and package preparation together. Package-content and
   967	lifecycle checks start when the single root/core build and pack finishes. The
   968	early `openclaw-npm-package-descriptor-<run-id>-<attempt>` artifact also unblocks
   969	candidate preparation while qualification continues. Final qualification joins
   970	every successful exact-source proof and seals the same tarball bytes.
   971	For legacy candidates that ship `npm-shrinkwrap.json`, preparation checks the
   972	packed root manifest against both the shrinkwrap's root declarations and installed
   973	package entries before emitting the package descriptor. Tarball qualification
   974	repeats that check. Missing required runtime dependencies fail validation even if
   975	npm would exit successfully, a prepared dependency masks the omission in a local
   976	consumer, or a repacked Docker image works. Repair the candidate's shrinkwrap and
   977	produce fresh artifact evidence; qualification never repairs frozen package bytes.
   978	The SDK consumer install retains its smaller dependency context. The final manifest records its immutable descriptor in
   979	`publicationArtifacts.npmPreflight`. Regular final releases include separate
   980	SDK compatibility reports for the current npm `beta` and `latest` predecessors,
   981	sharing the target snapshot. Publication selects its channel's report and
   982	acknowledgement without rebuilding. Alpha, beta prerelease, and extended-stable
   983	targets keep their required channel.
   984	
   985	For directly dispatched `OpenClaw NPM Release` preflight-only runs, if qualification
   986	fails after source checks and package preparation succeed, rerun the failed
   987	qualification job. It reuses the exact successful producer jobs
   988	and package bytes from the earlier attempt, even if that attempt failed or was
   989	cancelled. Failed or unfinished producer jobs remain ineligible. Final npm
   990	publication still requires the current producer run to complete successfully
   991	and every retained proof job to remain the effective successful job. FRV-owned
   992	standalone producers support the same targeted recovery; the parent adopts
   993	the verified receipt without rebuilding successful package work.
   994	
   995	`docker-release-prepare.yml` builds both native architectures, retains OCI
   996	indexes and their SBOM/provenance, and runs image smoke checks before approval.
   997	OCI export uses gzip level 1 for new layers and reuses cached layers without
   998	forced recompression, preserving the image format used by smoke and promotion.
   999	
  1000	Default and browser images share the builder's local cache. Preparation does not
  1001	transfer a remote build cache: fresh provenance timestamps invalidate application
  1002	layers, and measured transfers cost more than reusing runtime setup saves.
  1003	Fresh runners rebuild that setup, including mutable Debian and npm updates;
  1004	the sealed OCI artifacts remain the reusable inputs for publication.
  1005	The hosted VM reclaims its local builder when the job ends, so builder-volume
  1006	deletion does not delay sealing after the artifact uploads.
  1007	The final manifest records `publicationArtifacts.docker`. Preparation has no
  1008	publication secrets or registry-write permission. After approval, `Docker
  1009	Release` verifies the source/tag, producer, artifact hashes, and image digests,
  1010	then promotes those bytes to GHCR and Docker Hub. The publication lock covers
  1011	registry writes and selector promotion. Historical evidence without prepared
  1012	images uses the same preparation workflow before promotion. Alpha targets
  1013	retain their existing npm-only preparation contract.
  1014	
  1015	If Docker preparation succeeds but publication fails in the same workflow run,
  1016	rerun the failed publication job. The new publisher attempt verifies the original
  1017	successful preparation job and sealed artifacts without rebuilding. A separate
  1018	publisher run still requires the original producer attempt to be active or
  1019	successful; it cannot adopt a failed producer attempt through this retry path.
  1020	
  1021	Fresh package-facing validation passes the prepared root/core bundle to a
  1022	standalone candidate producer that calls `Full Release Candidate`. Its registry carries the exact
  1023	unpublished core dependencies and selected plugins. Installers start that
  1024	registry before resolving the root package, including npm, pnpm, Bun, and
  1025	cross-OS lanes. Published baseline versions remain available through the
  1026	upstream registry. Plugin Prerelease and OpenClaw Release Checks each dispatch an
  1027	independent phase immediately, while their candidate phases wait for acquisition.
  1028	Both candidate phases verify the same package SHA, artifact IDs, service digests,
  1029	producer run attempt, and Docker archive digest before use. The package-independent
  1030	bare Docker layer uses a content-addressed GHCR cache; candidate-specific images
  1031	remain immutable GitHub artifacts. Focused runs with an explicit published
  1032	package spec keep the existing package path instead.
  1033	
  1034	Preparation also emits a canonical request digest and a seven-day
  1035	`full-release-candidate-v2-<request-sha256>` evidence artifact. Its bounded
  1036	manifest binds the exact target and Tooling SHAs, release and soak policy,
  1037	effective survivor baselines and scenarios, preparation-plan digest, sorted
  1038	plugin package set, producer and publisher workflow/job/run identities, and
  1039	package, registry, and image artifact identities and expiry timestamps. The
  1040	execution plan seals that evidence. Before preparing a candidate, the umbrella may reuse
  1041	the newest artifact with at least fourteen hours of remaining lifetime for the
  1042	same canonical request and exact prepared npm tarball digest only after it revalidates the exact workflow run,
  1043	publisher job identity, archive digest, manifest, producer attempt and job, and
  1044	live metadata for every package, registry, and image artifact.
  1045	A proven absence creates a fresh candidate. Bounded lookup uncertainty and
  1046	failures after selection are blocking so the run cannot silently switch
  1047	candidates. A different prepared tarball requires a fresh candidate even when
  1048	the source SHA is unchanged. Full validation succeeds only after package
  1049	qualification and Docker preparation also succeed; a passing product Release
  1050	Decision alone does not authorize publication.
  1051	
  1052	For alpha targets with `rerun_group=all`, a `Verify Docker runtime image assets`
  1053	job builds the `runtime-assets` Docker target with
  1054	`OPENCLAW_EXTENSIONS=diagnostics-otel,codex`. It runs in parallel with the other
  1055	stages and remains enforced by the umbrella verifier. Other release types
  1056	validate that same target inside mandatory Docker image preparation on both
  1057	native architectures, avoiding a duplicate build. A narrower `rerun_group`
  1058	skips the standalone preflight.
  1059	
  1060	| Stage                   | Details                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                         |
  1061	| ----------------------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
  1062	| Target resolution       | **Job:** `Resolve target ref`<br />**Child workflow:** none<br />**Proves:** resolves the release branch, tag, or full commit SHA and records selected inputs.<br />**Rerun:** rerun the umbrella if this fails.                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                                |
  1063	| Publication preparation | **Jobs:** `Prepare release npm artifacts`, `Qualify release npm artifacts`, and `Prepare release Docker artifacts`<br />**Child workflow:** separate npm and Docker `Full Release Artifacts` runs<br />**Proves:** qualifies the exact root/core npm tarballs and both native Docker architectures before publication. Parent retries recover the original producer records and receipts.<br />**Rerun:** retry failed npm qualification jobs on their original producer run, preserve successful preparation, and let the parent collect the verified receipt. |
  1064	| Shared candidate        | **Job:** `Acquire full release candidate`<br />**Child workflow:** `Full Release Artifacts` calls `Full Release Candidate`, which reuses a trusted candidate or prepares one on a proven miss<br />**Proves:** validates the exact npm tarball, registry, functional image, and producer/publisher binding. Preparation starts after raw npm bytes are ready, before qualification finishes.<br />**Rerun:** rerun the affected package, plugin-prerelease, cross-OS, or live/E2E group using the same candidate.                                               |
  1065	| Docker assets preflight | **Job:** `Verify Docker runtime image assets`<br />**Child workflow:** none<br />**Proves:** for alpha targets, the `runtime-assets` Docker build target succeeds in parallel with other stages and remains enforced by the umbrella verifier. Runs only for `rerun_group=all`; other release types cover this target in mandatory Docker image preparation.<br />**Rerun:** rerun the umbrella with `rerun_group=all`.                                                                                                                                         |
  1066	| Vitest and normal CI    | **Job:** `Run normal full CI`<br />**Child workflow:** `CI`<br />**Proves:** the selected CI graph against the target ref. `npm-beta-v1` and `npm-stable-v1` retain Linux/macOS/Windows Node, plugin and channel contracts, Node compatibility, checks, built-artifact smoke, docs, Python skills, and Control UI; they defer macOS Swift/OpenClawKit, iOS, Android, and native i18n. Other coverage policies use full CI.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=ci`.                                                                                                    |
  1067	| Plugin prerelease       | **Jobs:** `Run plugin prerelease independent validation` and `Run plugin prerelease candidate validation`<br />**Child workflow:** `Plugin Prerelease`<br />**Proves:** independent static and agentic coverage can start before acquisition, while candidate-dependent Docker lanes consume the sealed package and plugin registry identities.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=plugin-prerelease`.                                                                                                                                                                |
  1068	| Release checks          | **Jobs:** `Run release checks independent validation` and `Run release checks candidate validation`<br />**Child workflow:** `OpenClaw Release Checks`<br />**Proves:** independent install, QA, and live coverage can start before acquisition, while package, cross-OS, and candidate-dependent Docker lanes consume the sealed candidate. Stable and full profiles retain exhaustive live/E2E and release-path coverage.<br />**Rerun:** classify the failed surface and select one concrete release-check group.                                            |
  1069	| Package Telegram        | **Job:** `Run package Telegram E2E`<br />**Child workflow:** `NPM Telegram Beta E2E`<br />**Proves:** a focused published-package Telegram E2E when `release_package_spec` or `npm_telegram_package_spec` is set. `npm-beta-v1` defers this child; explicit `npm-telegram` and soak retain it. Package Acceptance owns Telegram proof for unpublished candidates when selected.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=npm-telegram` with `release_package_spec` or `npm_telegram_package_spec`.                                                                          |
  1070	| Product performance     | **Job:** `Run product performance evidence`<br />**Child workflow:** `OpenClaw Performance`<br />**Proves:** release-profile performance (`profile=release`, `repeat=3`, `publish_reports=false`) against the target SHA. Selected for `all` except `npm-beta-v1`, or explicit `performance`; selected regressions block in every profile. Selected children still finish and prove their report publisher was skipped.<br />**Rerun:** `rerun_group=performance`.                                                                                              |
  1071	| Release decision        | **Job:** `Release Decision`<br />**Child workflow:** none<br />**Proves:** polls the exact recorded child run IDs and attempts, enforces release policy, and publishes an attempt-bound decision artifact. A decisive failure becomes `blocked_diagnostics_running` while unrelated child diagnostics continue.<br />**Rerun:** fix or rerun only the blocking surface.                                                                                                                                                                                         |
  1072	| Diagnostic drain        | **Job:** `Diagnostic Drain`<br />**Child workflow:** none<br />**Proves:** with `fail_fast=false`, follows every selected exact child to terminal without cancellation and writes timing, failed-job, run-attempt, and Tooling-SHA evidence. Collector cancellation instead writes an immediate `cancelled_with_children` handoff containing active child identities.<br />**Rerun:** recover collection only for `orchestration_error`; product failures do not invalidate the drain.                                                                          |
  1073	| Execution plan          | **Job:** `Seal release execution plan`<br />**Child workflow:** none<br />**Proves:** persists the original parent attempt, exact child identities and titles, required coverage, gates, reuse identity, and fresh candidate request with exact producer and publisher binding in a stable run-bound artifact. Attempt-two collector recovery restores this artifact instead of redispatching.<br />**Rerun:** restore the existing plan only; a missing plan is an orchestration error.                                                                        |
  1074	| Umbrella verifier       | **Job:** `Verify full validation`<br />**Child workflow:** none<br />**Proves:** downloads the immutable execution plan plus the exact attempt-bound Release Decision and Diagnostic Drain artifacts, verifies their common digest and parent tuple, and accepts only a strict green decision plus terminal drain.<br />**Rerun:** recover the existing collectors or rerun only the failed product surface; the verifier never reclassifies or redispatches children.                                                                                          |
  1075	
  1076	The seven child-dispatch jobs own dispatch and exact identity capture only. They
  1077	emit the child run ID, run attempt, and URL, then finish. Release Decision owns
  1078	the blocking answer; Diagnostic Drain owns complete terminal evidence. The
  1079	immutable execution plan owns child identity across collector attempts. The
  1080	decision state is one of `qualifying`, `blocked_diagnostics_running`, `passed`,
  1081	`blocked_complete`, `orchestration_error`, or `cancelled_with_children`.
  1082	Persistent GitHub API failures are orchestration errors. A child whose workflow
  1083	path, display title, ref, Tooling SHA, or run ID changes is a distinct
  1084	provenance mismatch. A monotonically newer attempt is accepted only through the
  1085	composite-attempt rules in [Continue a failed
  1086	validation](/reference/full-release-validation/continuation).
  1087	
  1088	`blocked_diagnostics_running` is safe for immediate diagnosis but not for a
  1089	retry until Diagnostic Drain is terminal. `orchestration_error` authorizes
  1090	collector recovery against the same exact child identities, never test
  1091	redispatch. `blocked_complete` means diagnostics are complete; it does not
  1092	claim a drain is still running.
  1093	
  1094	When selected, the umbrella dispatches product performance in artifact-only mode.
  1095	`OpenClaw Performance` permits report publication only for scheduled runs or a
  1096	manual dispatch that explicitly sets `publish_reports=true`. The artifact-only
  1097	guard must complete successfully, proving the publisher job stayed skipped.
  1098	Evidence for a selected performance child records
  1099	`controls.performanceReportPublication=artifact-only`; the verifier and reuse
  1100	selector require the matching normalized performance-child proof whenever that
  1101	child is selected. `npm-beta-v1` records performance as deferred instead of
  1102	dispatching a child whose result would still require terminal evidence.
  1103	
  1104	The verifier uploads the canonical manifest as
  1105	`full-release-validation-<run-id>-<run-attempt>`. Evidence tooling validates
  1106	its artifact ID, digest, producer run, and attempt before downloading that exact
  1107	artifact ID. It caps the downloaded ZIP, verifies its bytes against the REST
  1108	`sha256:` digest, and streams the only allowed bounded manifest entry without
  1109	extracting the archive. A stable-name alias remains for older publish
  1110	consumers. The verifier always prefers the attempt-qualified artifact and
  1111	accepts the stable name only for an attempt-1 manifest v2 producer. It rejects
  1112	that stable name for later attempts and for manifest v3, so the alias stops
  1113	applying once a producer emits manifest v3.
  1114	
  1115	Concurrency is keyed by Validation SHA, Tooling SHA, rerun group, release
  1116	profile, and effective soak coverage, and does not cancel an older run. The
  1117	Release Checks child also separates profiles and effective soak, preserving
  1118	independent admission through both workflow levels. Stable/full normalize soak
  1119	to enabled, so explicitly enabling it does not admit a duplicate request.
  1120	Parent cancellation or timeout leaves adopted
  1121	identity-checked children running and records `cancelled_with_children` when
  1122	the state collector can complete its cancellation handoff. Cancel an exact
  1123	child explicitly when it is no longer useful. Do not run a second foreground
  1124	watcher when the SHA-pinned helper already owns the parent; use
  1125	`release-ci-summary --watch` only after the helper has returned or when the
  1126	parent was dispatched separately.
  1127	---
